// Generated by tools/build_content.py from content/*.json. Do not edit by hand.
// The Gospel events after the Baptism, in the order of A. T. Robertson's Harmony of the Gospels (1922), then the Acts of the Apostles.
const CONTENT_PLACES = {
  "aenon": {"name":"Aenon near Salim","ll":[35.53,32.43],"region":"Samaria / Jordan Valley","certainty":"Probable","note":"John 3:23 says John baptized at Aenon (\"springs\") near Salim \"because there was much water there.\" The site is uncertain. Eusebius (4th century) places it in the Jordan Valley about 8 Roman miles south of Scythopolis (Beth-shean), where many springs rise. Other proposals place it near the village of Salim east of Shechem, or east of the Jordan."},
  "cana": {"name":"Cana","ll":[35.34,32.75],"region":"Galilee","certainty":"Probable","note":"Cana of Galilee was Nathanael's home town (John 21:2). Two sites are proposed. Kafr Kanna, about 4 miles northeast of Nazareth, holds the Franciscan Wedding Church and the pilgrim tradition. Khirbet Qana, a ruined hilltop about 8 miles north of Nazareth, is favored by many archaeologists; excavations there found a Jewish village of Jesus' time and a cave venerated by early pilgrims."},
  "capernaum": {"name":"Capernaum","ll":[35.575,32.881],"region":"Galilee","certainty":"Certain","note":"A fishing town on the northwest shore of the Sea of Galilee, on the road toward Damascus. It became Jesus' home base in Galilee (Matthew 9:1 calls it \"his own city\"). The white limestone synagogue of the 4th–5th century stands on the black basalt foundations of an earlier one, and a modern church hovers over the house honored as Peter's."},
  "galilee": {"name":"Towns and villages of Galilee","ll":[35.42,32.8],"region":"Galilee","certainty":"Probable","note":"Lower Galilee, the hill country west of the Sea of Galilee ruled by Herod Antipas. It was dense with farming villages and small towns linked by roads. The point stands for Jesus' movements through the region rather than one place."},
  "jacobswell": {"name":"Jacob's Well, Sychar","ll":[35.285,32.209],"region":"Samaria","certainty":"Probable","note":"At the foot of Mount Gerizim, the Samaritans' holy mountain, near ancient Shechem (Tell Balata) in today's Nablus. The well is very deep and has been venerated since at least the 4th century. A Greek Orthodox church, completed in 2007, now stands over it. Sychar is usually placed at the village of Askar nearby."},
  "judeacountry": {"name":"Judean countryside","ll":[35.3,31.85],"region":"Judea","certainty":"Probable","note":"John 3:22 says only that Jesus and his disciples went \"into the land of Judea\" and stayed there baptizing. No town is named. The point marks the Judean countryside outside Jerusalem, toward the Jordan."},
  "judeanwild": {"name":"Judean Wilderness (Mount of Temptation)","ll":[35.43,31.87],"region":"Judea","certainty":"Traditional","note":"The Gospels say only \"the wilderness.\" The barren hills between Jerusalem and the Jordan fit the scene. Since at least the 4th century, Jabal Quruntul, the cliff just west of Jericho, has been honored as the Mount of Temptation; the Greek Orthodox Monastery of the Temptation clings to its face."},
  "machaerus": {"name":"Machaerus","ll":[35.64,31.56],"region":"Perea","certainty":"Certain","note":"A Herodian fortress-palace on a hilltop east of the Dead Sea, in today's Jordan. Josephus (Antiquities 18.5.2) names it as the place where Herod Antipas imprisoned and beheaded John the Baptist. Excavations have uncovered the palace courtyard, baths and a ritual bath."},
  "beatitudes": {"name":"Mount of Beatitudes","ll":[35.556,32.881],"region":"Galilee","certainty":"Traditional","note":"The hill above Tabgha, between Capernaum and the Plain of Gennesaret. Matthew says only \"the mountain\" and Luke \"a level place,\" which could be a level spot on a hillside. A Byzantine chapel stood on the lower slope; the present church (1938, by Antonio Barluzzi) is eight-sided for the eight Beatitudes."},
  "bethesda": {"name":"Pool of Bethesda","ll":[35.2359,31.7813],"region":"Jerusalem","certainty":"Certain","note":"A pool \"by the Sheep Gate\" north of the Temple, which John says had five porticoes. Excavations beside the Church of St Anne uncovered two large pools separated by a central wall, a good match for five porches, with later healing baths and a Byzantine church above them."},
  "kursi": {"name":"Kursi (country of the Gerasenes)","ll":[35.65,32.826],"region":"Decapolis","certainty":"Traditional","note":"Mark and Luke say \"the country of the Gerasenes,\" Matthew \"the country of the Gadarenes\"; some manuscripts read Gergesenes. Gerasa (Jerash) lies over 30 miles away and Gadara about 6 miles from the lake. Kursi, on the east shore where a steep slope comes close to the water, has a large 5th-century Byzantine monastery and a chapel marking the miracle, uncovered in 1970 during road building."},
  "nain": {"name":"Nain","ll":[35.35,32.63],"region":"Galilee","certainty":"Certain","note":"A village on the north slope of the Hill of Moreh, about 6 miles southeast of Nazareth and in sight of Mount Tabor. It is still called Nein. A small Franciscan church (1880) stands on older church remains, and rock-cut tombs lie east of the village."},
  "seagalilee": {"name":"Sea of Galilee (on the water)","ll":[35.59,32.83],"region":"Galilee","certainty":"Certain","note":"A freshwater lake about 13 miles long and 8 wide, nearly 700 feet below sea level, ringed by hills. Winds funnel down through the valleys and can raise sudden, violent storms. Fishing boats of the period held a crew of four or five and some passengers."},
  "tabgha": {"name":"Tabgha (Sea of Galilee shore)","ll":[35.549,32.873],"region":"Galilee","certainty":"Traditional","note":"Heptapegon, \"seven springs,\" about 2 miles west of Capernaum. Warm springs flow into the lake here and draw fish, so fishermen have long worked this shore. Nearby is a small bay, often called the Bay of Parables, whose slope forms a natural amphitheater: a voice from a boat carries to a crowd on the shore."},
  "bethany": {"name":"Bethany","ll":[35.262,31.771],"region":"Judea","certainty":"Certain","note":"A village on the eastern slope of the Mount of Olives, about 2 miles (15 stadia, John 11:18) from Jerusalem, home of Martha, Mary and Lazarus. Today it is al-Eizariya, a name that comes from Lazarus. A rock-cut tomb honored as Lazarus's since at least the 4th century lies in the village."},
  "bethsaida": {"name":"Bethsaida (Julias)","ll":[35.63,32.91],"region":"Gaulanitis","certainty":"Probable","note":"A fishing village just east of where the Jordan enters the lake, in the territory of Philip the tetrarch. Josephus says Philip raised it to a city and named it Julias. John 1:44 calls it the town of Philip, Andrew and Peter. Two sites are excavated: et-Tell, about 1.5 miles inland, and el-Araj near the shore."},
  "burqin": {"name":"Between Samaria and Galilee (Burqin)","ll":[35.263,32.457],"region":"Samaria / Galilee border","certainty":"Traditional","note":"Luke 17:11 places the healing of the ten lepers on the border between Samaria and Galilee, as Jesus entered an unnamed village. Local Christian tradition places it at Burqin, near Jenin, where the old Church of St George is said to stand over a cave where the lepers lived."},
  "caesareaphil": {"name":"Caesarea Philippi","ll":[35.69,33.25],"region":"Iturea / Gaulanitis","certainty":"Certain","note":"A town at one of the main springs of the Jordan, at the foot of Mount Hermon. Philip the tetrarch rebuilt old Paneas and named it for Caesar and himself. Beside the spring were a cave and shrines to the god Pan, and Herod the Great had built a temple to Augustus there. Today it is Banias."},
  "decapolis": {"name":"Decapolis","ll":[35.85,32.65],"region":"Decapolis","certainty":"Probable","note":"A league of about ten Greek cities, most of them east of the Jordan and the lake, such as Hippos, Gadara, Gerasa and Pella. The population was largely Gentile. Mark does not name the exact place where Jesus healed the deaf man or fed the four thousand."},
  "ephraim": {"name":"Ephraim (Taybeh)","ll":[35.3,31.95],"region":"Judea","certainty":"Probable","note":"John 11:54 says Jesus withdrew to \"a town called Ephraim\" near the wilderness. It is usually identified with et-Taiyiba (Taybeh), on a hilltop about 4 miles northeast of Bethel looking down toward the Jordan Valley, the Ophrah of Joshua 18:23."},
  "gennesaret": {"name":"Plain of Gennesaret","ll":[35.53,32.85],"region":"Galilee","certainty":"Certain","note":"A small, very fertile plain on the northwest shore, between Magdala and Capernaum. Josephus praised its mild climate and its walnut, palm, fig and olive trees."},
  "hermon": {"name":"Mount Hermon","ll":[35.86,33.42],"region":"Iturea","certainty":"Probable","note":"The snow-capped mountain, about 9,200 ft high, that rises above Caesarea Philippi. Robertson and many other scholars place the Transfiguration on one of its slopes because the Gospels set the scene a week after Peter's confession near Caesarea Philippi. A tradition since the 4th century names Mount Tabor instead."},
  "magdala": {"name":"Magdala (Magadan, Dalmanutha)","ll":[35.515,32.825],"region":"Galilee","certainty":"Probable","note":"Matthew 15:39 names Magadan and Mark 8:10 \"the district of Dalmanutha\"; neither name is known from any other source. Many copyists changed Magadan to the familiar Magdala, the fishing town on the west shore that was the home of Mary Magdalene. Excavations there since 2009 have uncovered a 1st-century synagogue and the carved \"Magdala stone.\""},
  "olives": {"name":"Mount of Olives","ll":[35.245,31.778],"region":"Jerusalem","certainty":"Certain","note":"The ridge east of Jerusalem across the Kidron Valley, about 2,700 ft high. Constantine built the Eleona church over a cave near the summit; the Pater Noster church there today recalls the teaching of the Lord's Prayer."},
  "perea": {"name":"Perea (beyond the Jordan)","ll":[35.65,32.05],"region":"Perea","certainty":"Probable","note":"The land east of the Jordan from Pella south to Machaerus, ruled with Galilee by Herod Antipas. Galilean pilgrims often went down this side of the river to avoid Samaria, crossing back at Jericho. The Gospels name no Perean towns for this ministry."},
  "samariavillage": {"name":"A Samaritan village","ll":[35.3,32.45],"region":"Samaria","certainty":"Probable","note":"Luke does not name the village. The direct road from Galilee to Jerusalem ran through Samaria, about a three-day walk; Ginae (modern Jenin), on the border where the Jezreel Valley meets the hills, was the first Samaritan town on it. Josephus tells of Galilean pilgrims being attacked there on the way to a feast."},
  "siloam": {"name":"Pool of Siloam","ll":[35.2353,31.7706],"region":"Jerusalem","certainty":"Probable","note":"A pool at the south end of the City of David, fed by Hezekiah's tunnel from the Gihon spring. In 2004 workers repairing a sewer uncovered the stepped edge of a large Second Temple period pool, widely identified as the pool of John 9. A Byzantine church once marked the older, smaller pool nearby."},
  "tyre": {"name":"Region of Tyre and Sidon","ll":[35.2,33.27],"region":"Phoenicia","certainty":"Certain","note":"The old Phoenician port cities on the coast, outside Jewish territory and outside Herod Antipas' rule. Tyre lies about 35 miles northwest of Capernaum, and Sidon about 22 miles farther north. People from this region had already come to hear Jesus (Mark 3:8)."},
  "akeldama": {"name":"Akeldama (Field of Blood)","ll":[35.232,31.7685],"region":"Jerusalem","certainty":"Traditional","note":"A potter's field bought with Judas' silver as a burial place for strangers. Since at least the 4th century it has been shown on the southern slope of the Hinnom Valley, where the Greek Orthodox Monastery of St Onuphrius stands among rock-cut tombs. Potters worked with the clay of this valley (Jeremiah 18–19)."},
  "bethphage": {"name":"Bethphage","ll":[35.255,31.78],"region":"Judea","certainty":"Probable","note":"A village near the top of the Mount of Olives, on the road from Bethany to Jerusalem. The name probably means \"house of unripe figs.\" A Franciscan chapel at et-Tur, built in 1883, marks the traditional place where the disciples fetched the colt; the Palm Sunday procession starts there every year."},
  "caiaphas": {"name":"House of Caiaphas","ll":[35.2315,31.7708],"region":"Jerusalem","certainty":"Traditional","note":"The high priest's palace stood in the wealthy Upper City. Two traditions compete: the Armenian site on Mount Zion near the Cenacle, and the Church of St Peter in Gallicantu on the eastern slope of Zion, built in 1931 over rock-cut cellars."},
  "gethsemane": {"name":"Gethsemane","ll":[35.2397,31.7794],"region":"Jerusalem","certainty":"Probable","note":"An olive grove at the foot of the Mount of Olives, across the Kidron Valley from the Temple. The name means \"oil press.\" The Church of All Nations (1924), built over the remains of Byzantine and Crusader churches, encloses the rock where Jesus is said to have prayed. Some of the olive trees in the garden are many centuries old."},
  "golgotha": {"name":"Golgotha (Calvary)","ll":[35.2296,31.7784],"region":"Jerusalem","certainty":"Probable","note":"\"The place of a skull\" (Aramaic Golgotha, Latin Calvaria), outside the city wall but near it, beside a road. The rock of Calvary is enclosed in the Church of the Holy Sepulchre, built by Constantine from AD 326 over a site the Jerusalem church remembered. In AD 30 this was an old quarry just outside the city's second wall; the third wall of the AD 40s brought it inside. Some Protestants since the 1880s have preferred \"Gordon's Calvary\", a skull-shaped hill north of the Damascus Gate."},
  "herodpalace": {"name":"Herod Antipas' quarters in Jerusalem","ll":[35.231,31.7765],"region":"Jerusalem","certainty":"Probable","note":"Luke says only that Antipas was in Jerusalem for the feast. He probably lodged in the old Hasmonean palace, which Josephus places on the western hill of the Upper City, overlooking the Temple. It has not been excavated."},
  "praetorium": {"name":"Praetorium (Pilate's headquarters)","ll":[35.2285,31.7762],"region":"Jerusalem","certainty":"Probable","note":"Where the Roman prefect judged when he came up for the feasts. Philo and Josephus show later governors using Herod the Great's palace on the western hill (by today's Jaffa Gate and Citadel), and most scholars now place Pilate's trial there. An older tradition puts it at the Antonia Fortress north of the Temple, where the Via Dolorosa begins; the Lithostrotos pavement and \"Ecce Homo\" arch shown there date from Hadrian's city in the 2nd century."},
  "tomb": {"name":"The Tomb (Holy Sepulchre)","ll":[35.2298,31.7786],"region":"Jerusalem","certainty":"Probable","note":"John says the tomb was new, in a garden near the place of crucifixion. The rock-cut tomb honored since the 4th century lies a few dozen yards west of Calvary inside the Church of the Holy Sepulchre, under the small shrine called the Edicule; other 1st-century tombs survive in the same quarry. The Garden Tomb near Gordon's Calvary is a peaceful place of prayer, but archaeologists date it to the Iron Age, centuries before Jesus."},
  "upperroom": {"name":"Upper Room (Cenacle)","ll":[35.229,31.7716],"region":"Jerusalem","certainty":"Traditional","note":"The Gospels name only \"a large upper room furnished\" in the city. Since at least the 4th century, Christians have honored a site on Mount Zion, in the southwest of the old city, as the room of the Last Supper and of Pentecost. The present Gothic hall was built by the Crusaders and Franciscans in the 12th to 14th centuries. Robertson suggests it may have been the house of John Mark's mother (Acts 12:12)."},
  "emmaus": {"name":"Emmaus","ll":[34.99,31.84],"region":"Judea","certainty":"Traditional","note":"Luke places Emmaus about seven miles (60 stadia) from Jerusalem; some early manuscripts read 160 stadia, about 19 miles. Emmaus Nicopolis (Imwas, near Latrun), honored since the 3rd and 4th centuries by Eusebius and Jerome, fits the longer figure. Abu Ghosh, el-Qubeibeh (a Crusader-era tradition kept by the Franciscans) and Motza (the Emmaus of Josephus, about 4 miles out) are the other candidates. The map marks Nicopolis."},
  "galileemount": {"name":"The mountain in Galilee","ll":[35.45,32.82],"region":"Galilee","certainty":"Traditional","note":"Matthew 28:16 says only \"the mountain to which Jesus had directed them\" and gives no name. Some traditions point to Mount Tabor, others to Mount Arbel above the Sea of Galilee or to the hill of the Beatitudes. The map marks the hills west of the lake as a general location."},
  "jerusalem": {"name":"Jerusalem (place not recorded)","ll":[35.232,31.776],"region":"Jerusalem","certainty":"Traditional","note":"Used for events the New Testament places in no definite spot. St Paul records an appearance to James (1 Corinthians 15:7) but says nothing of where or when. A tradition from the lost Gospel of the Hebrews, quoted by St Jerome, places it in Jerusalem soon after the Resurrection."},
  "jerusalemchurch": {"name":"Jerusalem (the city)","ll":[35.231,31.776],"region":"Judea","certainty":"Certain","note":"The first church met in the Temple courts and \"in their homes\" across the city (Acts 2:46; 5:42). Acts names no single meeting place, so events of the church's common life are mapped at the city itself."},
  "stephensite": {"name":"Place of Stephen's stoning","ll":[35.2296,31.7853],"region":"Jerusalem","certainty":"Traditional","note":"Acts says only that they \"cast him out of the city\" (7:58). The oldest tradition is north of the city, outside today's Damascus Gate, where the empress Eudocia built a basilica to Saint Stephen about AD 460; the Dominicans' Basilica of Saint-Etienne (École Biblique) stands on its ruins. A later, medieval tradition moved the site east, to the gate now called St Stephen's (Lions') Gate above the Kidron Valley. The map marks the northern site."},
  "antioch": {"name":"Antioch (Syria)","ll":[36.161,36.202],"region":"Syria","certainty":"Certain","note":"Antioch on the Orontes, the third city of the Roman Empire and capital of the province of Syria. Here the disciples were first called Christians (Acts 11:26), and from here Paul set out on each of his journeys. Today Antakya in southern Turkey; on the slope of Mount Staurin the cave church of St Peter is honored as an early meeting place of the church."},
  "arabia": {"name":"Arabia (Nabataea)","ll":[35.8,30.6],"region":"Nabataea","certainty":"Traditional","note":"Paul says only \"I went away into Arabia\" (Galatians 1:17). This was the Nabataean kingdom of Aretas IV, whose lands ran from the Hauran south of Damascus down through Petra, its capital, to the Hejaz. Where Paul went is unknown; the marker sits near Petra. Some think of the towns of the Hauran close to Damascus, others of Sinai, which Paul himself places \"in Arabia\" (Galatians 4:25)."},
  "damascus": {"name":"Damascus","ll":[36.306,33.511],"region":"Syria","certainty":"Certain","note":"One of the oldest continuously inhabited cities in the world, then a city of the Decapolis with a large Jewish community and many synagogues. The \"street called Straight\" (Acts 9:11), the Roman Via Recta, still runs east to west across the Old City to the Bab Sharqi gate; an underground chapel near it is honored as the House of Ananias, and St Paul's Chapel at Bab Kisan marks the traditional spot where he was let down over the wall."},
  "gazaroad": {"name":"The road down to Gaza","ll":[34.8,31.6],"region":"Judea","certainty":"Traditional","note":"\"The road that goes down from Jerusalem to Gaza. This is a desert road\" (Acts 8:26). The road ran southwest through the Judean hills toward the coast; the point marks its lower stretch toward Gaza. Where the eunuch was baptized is not known. Pilgrims honored a spring at Beth-zur near Hebron (the Ain ed-Dirweh, \"Philip's Fountain\"), and another tradition points to Ein Haniya near Jerusalem."},
  "joppa": {"name":"Joppa","ll":[34.752,32.054],"region":"Coastal plain","certainty":"Certain","note":"The ancient port of Jerusalem, from which Jonah sailed to flee from the Lord (Jonah 1:3). Today Jaffa, the old city of Tel Aviv-Yafo. A house near the lighthouse is shown as the House of Simon the Tanner, and the Franciscan church of St Peter stands above the harbor."},
  "kawkab": {"name":"Road to Damascus (Kawkab)","ll":[36.18,33.42],"region":"Syria","certainty":"Traditional","note":"Acts says only that it happened \"as he journeyed he approached Damascus\" (9:3). Tradition points to the hill of Kawkab, about 10 miles southwest of the city, where a Greek Orthodox monastery of St Paul now marks the place."},
  "lydda": {"name":"Lydda","ll":[34.895,31.951],"region":"Judea","certainty":"Certain","note":"A town on the edge of the coastal plain of Sharon, about 25 miles northwest of Jerusalem on the road to Joppa. Later called Diospolis, it is today Lod in Israel, where the Church of St George keeps the tomb honored as that of the martyr."},
  "sebaste": {"name":"Samaria (Sebaste)","ll":[35.192,32.276],"region":"Samaria","certainty":"Probable","note":"The old capital of the northern kingdom, rebuilt by Herod the Great and renamed Sebaste in honor of Augustus. Acts 8:5 says only \"a city of Samaria\" (some manuscripts read \"the city\"); Sebaste is the usual reading, but Shechem (near Sychar) and Gitta, Simon Magus' home town in Justin Martyr, have also been proposed. Today Sebastia, a village in the West Bank, keeps the ruins of Herod's temple of Augustus, a colonnaded street and a Crusader church of St John the Baptist."},
  "tarsus": {"name":"Tarsus","ll":[34.895,36.918],"region":"Cilicia","certainty":"Certain","note":"Paul's home town, \"no mean city\" (Acts 21:39): the capital of Cilicia on the river Cydnus, a seat of Greek learning, with a river harbor reached from the sea. Today Tarsus in southern Turkey shows a long stretch of Roman paved street, the Roman gate called Cleopatra's Gate, and \"St Paul's Well\" in the old quarter, honored by pilgrims as the site of his family home."},
  "attalia": {"name":"Attalia","ll":[30.7,36.886],"region":"Pamphylia","certainty":"Certain","note":"The main harbor of Pamphylia, founded by Attalus II of Pergamum; modern Antalya. The old harbor and Hadrian's Gate (AD 130) are in today's Kaleiçi quarter."},
  "derbe": {"name":"Derbe","ll":[33.36,37.35],"region":"Galatia","certainty":"Probable","note":"The easternmost city of the first journey, on the road toward the Cilician Gates. Inscriptions found in 1956 and 1964 point to the mound of Kerti Hüyük, about 60 miles from Lystra; some scholars prefer nearby Devri Şehri."},
  "iconium": {"name":"Iconium","ll":[32.48,37.87],"region":"Galatia","certainty":"Certain","note":"An old Phrygian city on the edge of the Lycaonian plain, today Konya, one of Turkey's largest cities. Little of the Roman city survives under the modern one."},
  "lystra": {"name":"Lystra","ll":[32.33,37.58],"region":"Galatia","certainty":"Probable","note":"A small Roman colony in Lycaonia, home of Timothy (Acts 16:1). An inscription naming Lystra, found in 1885, located it at the mound of Zoldera near Hatunsaray, about 20 miles south-southwest of Konya. The site is largely unexcavated."},
  "paphos": {"name":"Paphos","ll":[32.41,34.76],"region":"Cyprus","certainty":"Certain","note":"New Paphos, the Roman capital of Cyprus and seat of the proconsul, on the island's west coast. Its villas with mosaic floors are famous; a column at the church of Agia Kyriaki Chrysopolitissa is shown by tradition as \"St Paul's Pillar,\" where he is said to have been scourged."},
  "perga": {"name":"Perga","ll":[30.854,36.961],"region":"Pamphylia","certainty":"Certain","note":"A leading city of Pamphylia, a few miles inland from the coast near the Cestrus (Aksu) river, about 10 miles east of modern Antalya. Its theater, stadium, gate towers and colonnaded street survive."},
  "pisidianantioch": {"name":"Antioch of Pisidia","ll":[31.189,38.306],"region":"Galatia","certainty":"Certain","note":"A Roman colony (Colonia Caesarea) on the high plateau at about 3,600 ft, the chief city of southern Galatia. Its ruins, with a temple of Augustus, theater and a large early church, lie just outside Yalvaç, Turkey."},
  "salamis": {"name":"Salamis (Cyprus)","ll":[33.9,35.18],"region":"Cyprus","certainty":"Certain","note":"The chief port of eastern Cyprus, with a large Jewish community. Its ruins (gymnasium, theater, baths) lie north of Famagusta. Tradition holds that Barnabas was martyred and buried here."},
  "athens": {"name":"Athens (the Areopagus)","ll":[23.7225,37.9715],"region":"Achaia","certainty":"Certain","note":"In Paul's day Athens was a free city and a university town living on its past glory. The Areopagus, the rocky \"Hill of Ares\" just below the Acropolis, gave its name to the city's ancient council; the agora lay at its foot."},
  "beroea": {"name":"Beroea","ll":[22.2,40.52],"region":"Macedonia","certainty":"Certain","note":"A prosperous Macedonian town at the foot of Mount Vermion, south of the Via Egnatia. Today Veria in northern Greece."},
  "cenchreae": {"name":"Cenchreae","ll":[22.99,37.88],"region":"Achaia","certainty":"Certain","note":"Corinth's eastern port on the Saronic Gulf, about 7 miles from the city, facing Asia. The remains of its harbor moles and warehouses lie partly under water near modern Kechries."},
  "corinth": {"name":"Corinth","ll":[22.879,37.906],"region":"Achaia","certainty":"Certain","note":"Destroyed by Rome in 146 BC and refounded as a Roman colony by Julius Caesar in 44 BC, Corinth was the capital of the province of Achaia and a rich trading city on the isthmus between two seas, with ports at Lechaeum and Cenchreae. Excavations have uncovered its forum, the bema and the Erastus inscription."},
  "ephesus": {"name":"Ephesus","ll":[27.341,37.939],"region":"Asia","certainty":"Certain","note":"Capital of the Roman province of Asia and one of the largest cities of the empire, famous for the temple of Artemis. Its harbor has long since silted up; the ruins lie near Selçuk in western Turkey."},
  "philippi": {"name":"Philippi","ll":[24.287,41.013],"region":"Macedonia","certainty":"Certain","note":"Founded by Philip II of Macedon and refounded as a Roman colony after the battle of Philippi (42 BC), on the Via Egnatia about 10 miles inland from its port Neapolis. Its forum, theater and early churches are a UNESCO World Heritage Site."},
  "phrygia": {"name":"Phrygia and Galatia (the region)","ll":[31.5,39.2],"region":"Asia Minor","certainty":"Probable","note":"Acts names a region, not a town. Whether it means the Phrygian district of the province of Galatia (around Iconium and Pisidian Antioch) or the old Celtic Galatia around Ancyra is debated; the map marks a general point on the Phrygian uplands between the two, on the way toward Mysia and Bithynia."},
  "thessalonica": {"name":"Thessalonica","ll":[22.94,40.64],"region":"Macedonia","certainty":"Certain","note":"Capital of the Roman province of Macedonia and a free city governed by its own politarchs, on the Via Egnatia at the head of the Thermaic Gulf. Today Thessaloniki, Greece's second city."},
  "troas": {"name":"Troas","ll":[26.155,39.752],"region":"Asia","certainty":"Certain","note":"Alexandria Troas, a Roman colony and the main port for crossing from Asia to Macedonia, about 15 miles south of ancient Troy. Its ruins lie in the woods near Dalyan on the Turkish coast."},
  "assos": {"name":"Assos","ll":[26.34,39.49],"region":"Asia","certainty":"Certain","note":"A harbor city on the south coast of the Troad, on a steep volcanic hill crowned by a temple of Athena, facing the island of Lesbos."},
  "macedonia": {"name":"Macedonia and Greece (the region)","ll":[22.5,40.2],"region":"Macedonia","certainty":"Probable","note":"The Roman province of Macedonia, crossed by the Via Egnatia, with the churches Paul founded at Philippi, Thessalonica and Beroea. The marker stands for the region, not a town."},
  "miletus": {"name":"Miletus","ll":[27.278,37.53],"region":"Asia","certainty":"Certain","note":"An old Ionian harbor city near the mouth of the Maeander river, about 30 miles south of Ephesus. The river's silt has since left its ruins several miles inland."},
  "ptolemais": {"name":"Ptolemais (Acre)","ll":[35.07,32.92],"region":"Phoenicia","certainty":"Certain","note":"The old Canaanite port of Acco, renamed Ptolemais by the Ptolemies of Egypt, on the bay north of Mount Carmel. Under Claudius it became a Roman colony."},
  "adria": {"name":"The Sea of Adria (the storm)","ll":[19.0,35.45],"region":"Central Mediterranean","certainty":"Traditional","note":"In Paul's day \"Adria\" meant the whole central Mediterranean between Crete, Sicily and Greece, not only the Adriatic. Luke gives no position for the fourteen days of the storm. The map marks the open sea on the most likely line of drift, from Cauda toward Malta; the true course is unknown."},
  "antonia": {"name":"Antonia Fortress (the barracks)","ll":[35.2335,31.7803],"region":"Jerusalem","certainty":"Probable","note":"Herod the Great's fortress at the northwest corner of the Temple courts, named for Mark Antony. A Roman cohort was stationed there, and stairs led down into the Temple courts (Josephus, Jewish War 5.5.8). These are \"the steps\" of Acts 21:35 and 40 and \"the barracks\" where Paul was held. The fortress was razed by Titus in AD 70; its rock base lies under the Umariyya school on the Via Dolorosa."},
  "appiiforum": {"name":"Forum of Appius and Three Taverns","ll":[13.1,41.43],"region":"Italy","certainty":"Probable","note":"Two stopping places on the Appian Way south of Rome. The Forum of Appius, a market town by the Pontine marshes, lay 43 Roman miles from the city; Three Taverns, a road station near modern Cisterna, about 33. The map marks the Forum of Appius."},
  "fairhavens": {"name":"Fair Havens (Crete)","ll":[24.78,34.93],"region":"Crete","certainty":"Probable","note":"A small open bay on the south coast of Crete, near Lasea. The name survives as Kaloi Limenes, just east of Cape Lithino, where the coast turns north and the shelter ends; ruins of Lasea lie a few miles east. A small chapel of St Paul stands on the shore."},
  "malta": {"name":"Malta (St Paul's Bay)","ll":[14.4,35.95],"region":"Malta","certainty":"Traditional","note":"Luke names the island Melite. A long tradition, and the seamanship study of James Smith (1848), place the wreck at St Paul's Bay on Malta's northeast coast, where a sandbar off the small St Paul's Islands matches \"a place where two seas met\" (Acts 27:41). Other bays on Malta have been proposed, and a few scholars have argued for Mljet off Croatia or Cephalonia in Greece."},
  "myra": {"name":"Myra","ll":[29.98,36.26],"region":"Lycia","certainty":"Certain","note":"A chief city of Lycia, on the south coast of Asia Minor, with its port at Andriake. A regular stop for the grain ships from Alexandria to Rome, which sailed north to Lycia before turning west. Today Demre, Turkey, famous for its rock-cut tombs and for St Nicholas, its bishop in the 4th century."},
  "puteoli": {"name":"Puteoli","ll":[14.12,40.82],"region":"Italy","certainty":"Certain","note":"The main port of Rome for the Alexandrian grain fleet before the harbors at Ostia were built up, on the Bay of Naples. Paul found Christians here and stayed seven days. Today Pozzuoli; its Roman market hall (the \"Temple of Serapis\") and amphitheater survive."},
  "rome": {"name":"Rome","ll":[12.4964,41.9028],"region":"Italy","certainty":"Certain","note":"The capital of the empire, where a Christian community had existed for years before Paul came (his letter to the Romans was written about AD 57). Acts does not say where in the city Paul's rented lodging was. Later traditions honor sites such as San Paolo alla Regola and Santa Maria in Via Lata; the Mamertine prison is linked by tradition with a later imprisonment."},
  "sidon": {"name":"Sidon","ll":[35.37,33.56],"region":"Phoenicia","certainty":"Certain","note":"The old Phoenician port, a day's sail north of Caesarea. The first stop on Paul's voyage to Rome, where the centurion Julius let him visit his friends. Today Saida, Lebanon; the Crusader Sea Castle stands on an islet in the ancient harbor."},
  "syracuse": {"name":"Syracuse","ll":[15.29,37.07],"region":"Sicily","certainty":"Certain","note":"The great Greek city of eastern Sicily, with a fine harbor around the island of Ortygia. Paul's ship stayed three days. Tradition later linked the catacomb church of San Giovanni with Paul's preaching here."}
};
const CONTENT_EVENTS = [
  {"id":"temptation","harmony":"§25","lane":"jesus","place":"judeanwild","from":"bethabara","date":"Late AD 29","title":"The temptation in the wilderness","people":["Jesus","Satan (the devil)","Angels"],"gospels":{"Mt":"4:1–11","Mk":"1:12–13","Lk":"4:1–13"},"summary":"Straight after his baptism the Spirit leads Jesus into the wilderness. He fasts forty days and is tempted by the devil: to turn stones into bread, to throw himself down from the pinnacle of the Temple, and to worship Satan in return for all the kingdoms of the world. Each time he answers with a word from Deuteronomy. Mark adds that he was with the wild beasts, and angels ministered to him. Where Israel failed in its forty years in the desert, Jesus stays faithful.","stay":"Forty days alone in the wilderness, fasting.","journey":"About 8 miles west across the Jordan and past Jericho into the hills, if the traditional Mount of Temptation is the place.","route":[[35.556,31.84],[35.52,31.85],[35.47,31.86],[35.43,31.87]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 4:1","text":"Then Jesus was led up by the Spirit into the wilderness to be tempted by the devil."},{"ref":"Matthew 4:3–4","text":"And the tempter came and said to him, \"If you are the Son of God, command these stones to become loaves of bread.\" But he answered, \"It is written, 'Man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word that proceeds from the mouth of God.'\""},{"ref":"Matthew 4:10","text":"Then Jesus said to him, \"Begone, Satan! for it is written, 'You shall worship the Lord your God and him only shall you serve.'\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Deuteronomy 8:2–3","note":"Israel's forty years of testing in the wilderness, and the verse Jesus quotes: \"man does not live by bread alone.\""},{"ref":"Exodus 34:28","note":"Moses fasted forty days and forty nights on Sinai."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/temptation0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:05_Tentaciones_de_Cristo_(Botticelli).jpg","title":"The Temptations of Christ","artist":"Sandro Botticelli","year":"1480–82","medium":"Fresco","location":"Sistine Chapel, Vatican City","description":"The three temptations play out in the background: at top left the devil, dressed as a hermit, offers Christ stones; at the center they stand on the roof of the Temple; at top right Christ drives the unmasked devil off a mountain while angels set a table. In the foreground a high priest receives a sacrifice before a crowd.","significance":"One of the wall frescoes painted for Pope Sixtus IV by the leading painters of the day, decades before Michelangelo's ceiling. It faces Botticelli's scenes from the life of Moses on the opposite wall.","symbolism":"The Temple is modeled on the façade of the Hospital of Santo Spirito in Rome, rebuilt by Sixtus IV. The foreground sacrifice, often read as a leper's purification, sets the old law beside Christ, the new.","impact":"Seen by millions of visitors to the Sistine Chapel every year, though most look up at the ceiling and miss it."},{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/temptation1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ivan_Kramskoy_-_Христос_в_пустыне_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg","title":"Christ in the Wilderness","artist":"Ivan Kramskoi","year":"1872","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"State Tretyakov Gallery, Moscow","description":"Christ sits alone on a gray boulder in a stony desert at first light, worn and barefoot, his hands clenched together in his lap.","significance":"A founding work of Russian Realism, shown at the second exhibition of the Peredvizhniki (\"Wanderers\") in 1872 and bought by Pavel Tretyakov for his gallery.","symbolism":"There is no devil, angel or halo. The struggle is entirely inward: the knotted hands carry the weight of the choice Christ must make.","impact":"In his letters Kramskoi described it as the moment of decision every person faces, between serving God and serving oneself. It remains one of the best-known religious paintings in Russia."}],"tags":["conflict"],"orderNote":"Matthew gives the order bread, pinnacle, mountain; Luke has bread, mountain, pinnacle, perhaps so that the scene ends in Jerusalem, as his Gospel does. Matthew's \"then\" and \"again\" suggest he keeps the actual sequence. The second or third temptation takes Jesus to the Temple in Jerusalem, so not all of it happens on the mountain."},
  {"id":"johnwitness","harmony":"§26","lane":"john","place":"bethabara","date":"Late AD 29","title":"John's testimony to the delegation from Jerusalem","people":["John the Baptist","Priests and Levites from Jerusalem","Pharisees"],"gospels":{"Jn":"1:19–28"},"summary":"The Jewish leaders in Jerusalem send priests and Levites to ask John who he is. He says plainly that he is not the Christ, nor Elijah, nor the prophet. He is only the voice crying in the wilderness. He baptizes with water, but one already stands among them whose sandal he is not worthy to untie. Robertson notes that the priests and Levites were Sadducees, sent at the prompting of the Pharisees.","stay":"John was baptizing at Bethany beyond the Jordan.","scripture":[{"ref":"John 1:19–20","text":"And this is the testimony of John, when the Jews sent priests and Levites from Jerusalem to ask him, \"Who are you?\" He confessed, he did not deny, but confessed, \"I am not the Christ.\""},{"ref":"John 1:26–27","text":"John answered them, \"I baptize with water; but among you stands one whom you do not know, even he who comes after me, the thong of whose sandal I am not worthy to untie.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Malachi 4:5","note":"The promise that Elijah would return before the day of the Lord, behind the question \"Are you Elijah?\""},{"ref":"Deuteronomy 18:15","note":"Moses' promise of a prophet like himself: \"the prophet\" they ask about."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","img":"img/johnwitness0.jpg","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Saint_John_the_Baptist_and_the_Pharisees_(Saint_Jean-Baptiste_et_les_pharisiens)_-_James_Tissot_-_overall.jpg","title":"Saint John the Baptist and the Pharisees","description":"John, barefoot on a rocky ledge, stretches out his arm toward a crowd of listeners, soldiers and robed men who have come out to the wilderness. Two elders sit apart on the rocks, watching.","significance":"Part of Tissot's series of some 350 scenes from the life of Christ, painted after research trips to Palestine.","symbolism":"The rough desert prophet stands above the crowd, while the questioners from Jerusalem keep their distance.","impact":"Tissot's Life of Christ was a sensation in Paris, London and New York in the 1890s; the Brooklyn Museum bought the whole series in 1900."}],"tags":["prophecy"],"orderNote":"John's Gospel does not narrate the baptism or the temptation. Most harmonies, Robertson's among them, place this delegation at the end of the forty days, since the next day (John 1:29) John sees Jesus coming toward him. John denies being Elijah, while Jesus later says John is the Elijah who was to come (Matthew 11:14): John denies being Elijah in person, Jesus speaks of his role."},
  {"id":"lambofgod","harmony":"§27","lane":"jesus","place":"bethabara","from":"judeanwild","date":"Late AD 29","title":"\"Behold, the Lamb of God\"","people":["John the Baptist","Jesus"],"gospels":{"Jn":"1:29–34"},"summary":"The day after the delegation, John sees Jesus coming toward him and points him out to the crowd as the Lamb of God who takes away the sin of the world. He explains how he knew him: God had told him that the one on whom he saw the Spirit descend and remain would baptize with the Holy Spirit. John saw it happen at the baptism. Now he bears public witness that Jesus is the Son of God.","journey":"About 8 miles back from the wilderness to the Jordan.","route":[[35.43,31.87],[35.47,31.86],[35.52,31.85],[35.556,31.84]],"scripture":[{"ref":"John 1:32","text":"And John bore witness, \"I saw the Spirit descend as a dove from heaven, and it remained on him."},{"ref":"John 1:33–34","text":"I myself did not know him; but he who sent me to baptize with water said to me, 'He on whom you see the Spirit descend and remain, this is he who baptizes with the Holy Spirit.' And I have seen and have borne witness that this is the Son of God.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Isaiah 53:7","note":"The servant led \"like a lamb that is led to the slaughter.\" The Passover lamb (Exodus 12) is also in view."},{"ref":"Isaiah 11:2","note":"\"The Spirit of the Lord shall rest upon him\": the promised shoot from Jesse."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","img":"img/lambofgod0.jpg","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Saint_John_the_Baptist_Sees_Jesus_from_Afar_(Saint_Jean-Baptiste_voit_Jésus_de_loin)_-_James_Tissot_-_overall.jpg","title":"Saint John the Baptist Sees Jesus from Afar","description":"John stands above a rocky riverbed with a crowd sitting below him. He raises his arms toward a small white figure far off on the hillside: Jesus, coming toward him.","significance":"Tissot illustrates John 1:29 literally: \"he saw Jesus coming toward him.\"","symbolism":"The distance between the two figures marks the moment of recognition before John speaks.","impact":"Shows the river country beside the Jordan as Tissot observed it in the 1880s."}],"tags":["prophecy"]},
  {"id":"firstdisciples","harmony":"§28","lane":"jesus","place":"bethabara","date":"Late AD 29","title":"The first disciples","people":["Jesus","John the Baptist","Andrew","Simon Peter","Philip","Nathanael","An unnamed disciple (traditionally John)"],"gospels":{"Jn":"1:35–51"},"summary":"John again points out Jesus as the Lamb of God, and two of his own disciples follow Jesus and spend the day with him. One is Andrew, who finds his brother Simon and tells him \"We have found the Messiah\"; Jesus names Simon Cephas, Peter, \"Rock.\" The next day Jesus finds Philip of Bethsaida, and Philip brings Nathanael, who is won over when Jesus tells him he saw him under the fig tree. The unnamed second disciple is traditionally John, son of Zebedee, the Gospel's author.","stay":"The two disciples \"stayed with him that day,\" from about the tenth hour.","scripture":[{"ref":"John 1:41–42","text":"He first found his brother Simon, and said to him, \"We have found the Messiah\" (which means Christ). He brought him to Jesus. Jesus looked at him, and said, \"So you are Simon the son of John? You shall be called Cephas\" (which means Peter)."},{"ref":"John 1:45–46","text":"Philip found Nathanael, and said to him, \"We have found him of whom Moses in the law and also the prophets wrote, Jesus of Nazareth, the son of Joseph.\" Nathanael said to him, \"Can anything good come out of Nazareth?\" Philip said to him, \"Come and see.\""},{"ref":"John 1:51","text":"And he said to him, \"Truly, truly, I say to you, you will see heaven opened, and the angels of God ascending and descending upon the Son of man.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Genesis 28:12","note":"Jacob's dream of angels ascending and descending on a ladder: Jesus himself is now the meeting place of heaven and earth."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","img":"img/firstdisciples0.jpg","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Calling_of_Saint_John_and_Saint_Andrew_(Vocation_de_Saint_Jean_et_de_Saint_André)_-_James_Tissot_-_overall.jpg","title":"The Calling of Saint John and Saint Andrew","description":"Two young men follow Jesus along the riverbank as he turns to ask, \"What do you seek?\"","significance":"Tissot follows John's Gospel, where the first disciples come from John the Baptist's circle at the Jordan, not from the boats in Galilee.","symbolism":"Jesus turning back to them is the first invitation of the Gospel: \"Come and see.\"","impact":"One of few well-known images of this quiet scene, often overshadowed by the Synoptic call of the fishermen."},{"kind":"Descriptive","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","img":"img/firstdisciples1.jpg","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Nathaniel_Under_the_Fig_Tree_(Nathanaël_sous_le_figuier)_-_James_Tissot_-_overall.jpg","title":"Nathaniel Under the Fig Tree","description":"People rest on carpets in the shade of fig trees on a green slope above the water. Tissot sets Nathanael's fig tree in an everyday Galilean scene.","significance":"Shows the moment Jesus refers to when he tells Nathanael, \"when you were under the fig tree, I saw you.\"","symbolism":"Sitting under one's fig tree was a biblical image of peace and of study of the law (Micah 4:4).","impact":"Jesus' words about the fig tree convince Nathanael that Jesus knows him; Tissot imagines the ordinary place where Nathanael was seen."}],"tags":["call"],"orderNote":"These are the first meetings. The Synoptic call by the lake to leave the nets and follow him (Matthew 4:18–22) comes months later, in Galilee. Robertson notes the four successive days of John 1:19, 29, 35 and 43, and the \"third day\" of John 2:1, making a full first week."},
  {"id":"cana","harmony":"§29","lane":"jesus","place":"cana","from":"bethabara","date":"Early AD 30","title":"The wedding at Cana","people":["Jesus","Mary, his mother","The disciples","The steward of the feast","The bridegroom","The servants"],"gospels":{"Jn":"2:1–11"},"summary":"Jesus, his mother and his new disciples are guests at a wedding in Cana. When the wine runs out, Mary tells Jesus and then tells the servants to do whatever he says. He has them fill six stone jars, used for Jewish purification, with water, and it becomes wine better than what was served first. John calls it the first of his signs: it reveals his glory, and his disciples believe in him.","journey":"About 75 miles up the Jordan Valley and across into the Galilean hills, a hard walk of about three days.","route":[[35.556,31.84],[35.53,32.15],[35.51,32.45],[35.45,32.6],[35.38,32.68],[35.34,32.75]],"scripture":[{"ref":"John 2:3–5","text":"When the wine failed, the mother of Jesus said to him, \"They have no wine.\" And Jesus said to her, \"O woman, what have you to do with me? My hour has not yet come.\" His mother said to the servants, \"Do whatever he tells you.\""},{"ref":"John 2:10–11","text":"and said to him, \"Every man serves the good wine first; and when men have drunk freely, then the poor wine; but you have kept the good wine until now.\" This, the first of his signs, Jesus did at Cana in Galilee, and manifested his glory; and his disciples believed in him."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Isaiah 25:6","note":"The Lord's feast for all peoples, \"of wine on the lees well refined.\""},{"ref":"Genesis 41:55","note":"Pharaoh tells the Egyptians, \"Go to Joseph; what he says to you, do,\" which Mary's words echo."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/cana0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giotto_di_Bondone_-_No._24_Scenes_from_the_Life_of_Christ_-_8._Marriage_at_Cana_-_WGA09202.jpg","title":"The Marriage at Cana","artist":"Giotto di Bondone","year":"c. 1305","medium":"Fresco","location":"Scrovegni (Arena) Chapel, Padua","description":"The guests sit at a long table against striped hangings, beneath a carved wooden gallery. At right a stout steward drinks the new wine beside a row of great stone jars.","significance":"Part of the fresco cycle that broke from flat Byzantine style and gave figures weight and real space.","symbolism":"Christ blesses from the end of the table; Mary's raised hand recalls her words, \"Do whatever he tells you.\"","impact":"The portly, wine-tasting steward is one of Giotto's best-loved touches of everyday humor."},{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/cana1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Les_noces_de_Cana,_avec_Jean_de_Sedano,_son_fils_et_son_épouse_-_Gérard_David_-_Musée_du_Louvre_Peintures_INV_1995_;_MR_704.jpg","title":"The Marriage at Cana","artist":"Gerard David","year":"c. 1500","medium":"Oil on panel","location":"Musée du Louvre, Paris","description":"A calm wedding feast in a Flemish hall with a view of a city square. Christ blesses from the table beside Mary while servants pour from jugs over the stone jars; the donor Jean de Sedano and his wife kneel at the sides.","significance":"Painted in Bruges for a Spanish merchant, it shows how patrons had themselves painted into the Gospel scene.","symbolism":"The wine turned from water was read as a sign of the Eucharist; the donors kneel as if at Mass.","impact":"A leading work of Gerard David, the last great master of the Bruges school."}],"tags":["miracle","nature-miracle"],"orderNote":"Two sites claim Cana: Kafr Kanna, the traditional pilgrim site near Nazareth, and Khirbet Qana, a ruin about 5 miles further north, which many archaeologists prefer."},
  {"id":"capernaumstay","harmony":"§30","lane":"jesus","place":"capernaum","from":"cana","date":"Early AD 30","title":"A first stay in Capernaum","people":["Jesus","Mary, his mother","His brothers","The disciples"],"gospels":{"Jn":"2:12"},"summary":"After the wedding Jesus goes down to Capernaum on the Sea of Galilee with his mother, his brothers and his disciples. They stay only a few days before the Passover. Capernaum, the home of Peter and Andrew, will later become Jesus' base in Galilee.","stay":"\"There they stayed for a few days.\"","journey":"About 16 miles, going \"down\" from the hills to the lakeshore, nearly 700 feet below sea level.","route":[[35.34,32.75],[35.42,32.79],[35.5,32.83],[35.53,32.86],[35.575,32.881]],"scripture":[{"ref":"John 2:12","text":"After this he went down to Capernaum, with his mother and his brethren and his disciples; and there they stayed for a few days."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/capernaumstay0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jan_Ciągliński_-_Capernaum_on_the_Sea_of_Galilee._From_the_journey_to_Palestine_-_MP_1543_MNW_-_National_Museum_in_Warsaw.jpg","title":"Capernaum on the Sea of Galilee","artist":"Jan Ciągliński","year":"1901","medium":"Oil sketch","location":"National Museum in Warsaw","description":"A quick sketch of the lake at Capernaum: a low stone wall and a lone palm in the foreground, the pale blue water and the bare hills of the far shore beyond.","significance":"Painted on the Polish artist's journey to Palestine, before the modern churches were built over the site.","symbolism":"The empty shore recalls how small and ordinary the town was where Jesus made his home.","impact":"A rare painted view of Capernaum from the years when the synagogue ruins were first being explored."},{"kind":"Artifact","img":"img/capernaumstay1.jpg","credit":"Photo: Berthold Werner, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kafarnaum_BW_7.JPG","title":"The synagogue of Capernaum","artist":"Photo: Berthold Werner","year":"2008","medium":"Photograph","location":"Capernaum National Park, Sea of Galilee","description":"The white limestone walls and columns of the later synagogue rise behind palms, above dark basalt house foundations of the ancient town.","significance":"The standing synagogue dates from the 4th–5th century AD. Beneath it, excavators found basalt walls of an earlier building, which many identify with the synagogue where Jesus taught (Mark 1:21).","symbolism":"The white stone set on black basalt is a picture of the town's layers: the later Jewish community built on the place remembered from Jesus' day.","impact":"Excavated and partly rebuilt by Franciscan archaeologists in the 20th century; it is one of the most visited sites in Galilee."}],"tags":[],"orderNote":"\"His brethren\" are understood in Catholic and Orthodox tradition as relatives (cousins or Joseph's sons), and by many Protestants as later children of Mary and Joseph."},
  {"id":"templecleansing","harmony":"§31","lane":"jesus","place":"temple","from":"capernaum","date":"Passover (spring), AD 30","title":"The first cleansing of the Temple","people":["Jesus","The disciples","Merchants and money-changers","The Temple authorities"],"gospels":{"Jn":"2:13–22"},"summary":"At Passover Jesus goes up to Jerusalem. In the Temple courts he finds men selling oxen, sheep and pigeons, and money-changers at their tables. He makes a whip of cords, drives them out, overturns the tables and says, \"You shall not make my Father's house a house of trade.\" Asked for a sign, he says, \"Destroy this temple, and in three days I will raise it up.\" He spoke of his body, as the disciples understood after the Resurrection.","stay":"In Jerusalem for the week of the Passover feast.","journey":"About 100 miles, nearly a week on foot, probably down the Jordan Valley and up through Jericho with the Passover pilgrims.","route":[[35.575,32.881],[35.535,32.79],[35.56,32.68],[35.51,32.48],[35.53,32.15],[35.45,31.865],[35.3,31.8],[35.235,31.778]],"scripture":[{"ref":"John 2:15–16","text":"And making a whip of cords, he drove them all, with the sheep and oxen, out of the temple; and he poured out the coins of the money-changers and overturned their tables. And he told those who sold the pigeons, \"Take these things away; you shall not make my Father's house a house of trade.\""},{"ref":"John 2:19–21","text":"Jesus answered them, \"Destroy this temple, and in three days I will raise it up.\" The Jews then said, \"It has taken forty-six years to build this temple, and will you raise it up in three days?\" But he spoke of the temple of his body."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Psalm 69:9","note":"\"Zeal for your house has consumed me,\" which the disciples remembered (John 2:17)."},{"ref":"Zechariah 14:21","note":"\"There shall no longer be a trader in the house of the Lord of hosts on that day.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/templecleansing0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:El_Greco_-_The_Purification_of_the_Temple_-_WGA10541.jpg","title":"The Purification of the Temple","artist":"El Greco","year":"c. 1600","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"The Frick Collection, New York","description":"Christ swings a whip at the center of a crowded portico. Traders recoil on the left, while on the right the apostles watch and discuss.","significance":"El Greco painted this subject again and again over some forty years; it was one of his most personal themes.","symbolism":"The reliefs in the background show the expulsion from Eden and the sacrifice of Isaac: sin and its punishment, and the promise of redemption. In Counter-Reformation Spain the scene also stood for the purifying of the Church.","impact":"Among the most famous images of the cleansing; its twisting, elongated figures show El Greco's mature style."},{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/templecleansing1.jpg","credit":"National Gallery of Art, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rembrandt_van_Rijn,_Christ_Driving_the_Money_Changers_from_the_Temple,_1635,_NGA_9891.jpg","title":"Christ Driving the Money Changers from the Temple","artist":"Rembrandt van Rijn","year":"1635","medium":"Etching","location":"National Gallery of Art, Washington (this impression)","description":"Christ raises a whip of cords above his head as money-changers tumble over their tables and a frightened ox bolts.","significance":"Rembrandt borrowed the figure of Christ from a Dürer woodcut, but filled the scene with his own chaotic energy.","symbolism":"The whip of cords is the detail found only in John's account (John 2:15).","impact":"Prints like this spread Rembrandt's Bible scenes across Europe in his lifetime."}],"tags":["conflict","prophecy"],"orderNote":"John puts a cleansing at the first Passover; Matthew, Mark and Luke put one in Passion Week. Many scholars think there was one event, which John moved forward for theological reasons; Robertson, with many others, holds there were two, at the start and close of the ministry. Robertson dates this Passover AD 27; the date here follows this map's chronology (crucifixion AD 33)."},
  {"id":"nicodemus","harmony":"§32","lane":"jesus","place":"temple","date":"Passover (spring), AD 30","title":"Nicodemus comes by night","people":["Jesus","Nicodemus"],"gospels":{"Jn":"2:23–3:21"},"summary":"During the feast many believe in Jesus because of his signs, but he does not trust himself to them. Nicodemus, a Pharisee and a member of the ruling council, comes to him by night. Jesus tells him that no one can see the kingdom of God unless he is born anew, of water and the Spirit. The talk leads to the most quoted verse of the Gospel, John 3:16. Nicodemus will reappear defending Jesus (John 7:50) and helping to bury him (John 19:39).","stay":"Jesus was in Jerusalem for the Passover; the meeting place is not named.","scripture":[{"ref":"John 3:3","text":"Jesus answered him, \"Truly, truly, I say to you, unless one is born anew, he cannot see the kingdom of God.\""},{"ref":"John 3:8","text":"The wind blows where it wills, and you hear the sound of it, but you do not know whence it comes or whither it goes; so it is with every one who is born of the Spirit.\""},{"ref":"John 3:16","text":"For God so loved the world that he gave his only Son, that whoever believes in him should not perish but have eternal life."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Numbers 21:8–9","note":"Moses lifts up the bronze serpent so that those who look on it live; \"so must the Son of man be lifted up\" (John 3:14)."},{"ref":"Ezekiel 36:25–27","note":"God's promise to sprinkle clean water and put a new spirit within his people."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/nicodemus0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henry_Ossawa_Tanner_-_Jesus_and_nicodemus.jpg","title":"Nicodemus Visiting Jesus","artist":"Henry Ossawa Tanner","year":"1899","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Pennsylvania Academy of the Fine Arts, Philadelphia","description":"Jesus and the white-bearded Nicodemus sit facing each other on a flat rooftop under a deep blue night sky, the town and hills behind them. Warm light glows up from the stairway below.","significance":"Painted after Tanner's trip to Palestine, where he studied houses and rooftops like this one.","symbolism":"Nicodemus, in shadow, listens; Jesus, facing us, speaks. The night recalls that Nicodemus came \"by night,\" and John 3:19–21: the light has come into the world.","impact":"It won the Pennsylvania Academy's Lippincott Prize in 1900 and helped make Tanner the best-known African American painter of his day."},{"kind":"Descriptive","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","img":"img/nicodemus1.jpg","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Interview_between_Jesus_and_Nicodemus_(Entretien_de_Jésus_et_de_Nicodème)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"Interview between Jesus and Nicodemus","description":"Jesus and Nicodemus sit close together on a mat in a plain room, lit by a small lamp on a stand. Their sandals lie on the floor below.","significance":"Unlike Tanner, who chose a rooftop, Tissot sets the meeting indoors, as an intimate, whispered talk.","symbolism":"The night setting reflects John's note that Nicodemus came \"by night,\" perhaps for fear of others.","impact":"Gives a vivid sense of a quiet, private talk between a teacher of Israel and Jesus."}],"tags":["teaching"],"orderNote":"Ancient Greek had no quotation marks, so it is unclear where Jesus' words end and John's own reflection begins; many readers think the speech ends at verse 15 and verses 16–21 are the evangelist's comment."},
  {"id":"judeanbaptism","harmony":"§33a","lane":"jesus","place":"judeacountry","from":"temple","date":"Spring–summer, AD 30","title":"Jesus' disciples baptize in Judea","people":["Jesus","The disciples"],"gospels":{"Jn":"3:22"},"summary":"After the Passover Jesus goes out into the Judean countryside with his disciples. He stays there for some time, and they baptize the people who come; John 4:2 adds that Jesus himself did not baptize, only his disciples. Crowds begin to go to Jesus rather than John. This little-known Judean ministry may have lasted several months.","stay":"\"There he remained with them and baptized\": probably several months.","journey":"A short way out of Jerusalem into the Judean countryside; John names no town.","route":[[35.235,31.778],[35.27,31.81],[35.3,31.85]],"scripture":[{"ref":"John 3:22","text":"After this Jesus and his disciples went into the land of Judea; there he remained with them and baptized."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","img":"img/judeanbaptism0.jpg","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Disciples_of_Jesus_Baptize_(Les_disciples_de_Jésus_baptisent)_-_James_Tissot_-_overall.jpg","title":"The Disciples of Jesus Baptize","description":"At a pool reached by worn stone steps beneath an arch, men in white mantles guide people down into the water while a crowd watches from the wall above.","significance":"One of very few artworks of this brief scene from John 3:22 and 4:2.","symbolism":"The disciples, not Jesus, perform the baptisms, as John 4:2 is careful to note.","impact":"Brings to light a part of the ministry most readers overlook."}],"tags":[]},
  {"id":"johnaenon","harmony":"§33b","lane":"john","place":"aenon","date":"Spring–summer, AD 30","title":"\"He must increase, but I must decrease\"","people":["John the Baptist","John's disciples","A Jew who disputes about purifying"],"gospels":{"Jn":"3:23–36"},"summary":"John is now baptizing farther north at Aenon near Salim, where there is much water, since he has not yet been put in prison. His disciples complain that everyone is going to Jesus. John answers that he is only the friend of the bridegroom, who rejoices to hear the bridegroom's voice. His joy is now full: Jesus must increase and he must decrease.","stay":"John baptized at Aenon \"because there was much water there.\"","scripture":[{"ref":"John 3:23","text":"John also was baptizing at Aenon near Salim, because there was much water there; and people came and were baptized."},{"ref":"John 3:29–30","text":"He who has the bride is the bridegroom; the friend of the bridegroom, who stands and hears him, rejoices greatly at the bridegroom's voice; therefore this joy of mine is now full. He must increase, but I must decrease.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Isaiah 62:5","note":"\"As the bridegroom rejoices over the bride, so shall your God rejoice over you\": God as Israel's bridegroom."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/johnaenon0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leonardo_da_Vinci_-_Saint_John_the_Baptist_C2RMF_retouched.jpg","title":"Saint John the Baptist","artist":"Leonardo da Vinci","year":"c. 1513–16","medium":"Oil on walnut panel","location":"Musée du Louvre, Paris","description":"A smiling young John emerges from deep darkness, holding a slender reed cross and pointing upward.","significance":"Believed to be among Leonardo's last paintings; it was still with him in France at the end of his life.","symbolism":"The raised finger points away from himself toward heaven and the one to come: \"He must increase, but I must decrease.\"","impact":"Its soft modeling (sfumato) and enigmatic smile have fascinated viewers since the Renaissance."}],"tags":["prophecy"],"orderNote":"Aenon's site is uncertain: the Jordan Valley south of Beth-shean (Eusebius), the springs near Salim east of Shechem in Samaria, or east of the Jordan. Robertson notes John may have been preaching to Samaritans. Readers disagree whether verses 31–36 are still John's words or the evangelist's."},
  {"id":"leavejudea","harmony":"§34a","lane":"jesus","place":"judeacountry","date":"AD 30","title":"Jesus leaves Judea","people":["Jesus","The disciples","The Pharisees"],"gospels":{"Mt":"4:12","Mk":"1:14","Lk":"4:14","Jn":"4:1–4"},"summary":"The Pharisees hear that Jesus is making and baptizing more disciples than John. When Jesus learns this, and hears that John has been handed over to prison, he leaves Judea and returns to Galilee. Robertson reads both reasons together: the rising hostility of the Pharisees, and the danger shown by John's arrest. The shortest road north runs through Samaria.","scripture":[{"ref":"John 4:1–3","text":"Now when the Lord knew that the Pharisees had heard that Jesus was making and baptizing more disciples than John (although Jesus himself did not baptize, but only his disciples), he left Judea and departed again to Galilee."},{"ref":"Matthew 4:12","text":"Now when he heard that John had been arrested, he withdrew into Galilee;"}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Isaiah 9:1–2","note":"\"Galilee of the nations... the people who walked in darkness have seen a great light,\" which Matthew 4:14–16 quotes for Jesus' move to Galilee."}],"art":[],"tags":["conflict"],"orderNote":"John's Gospel says only that the Pharisees heard of his success; Matthew and Mark tie the return to Galilee to John's arrest. Mark and Matthew pass straight from the temptation to Galilee, so without John's Gospel we would not know of this earlier Judean ministry."},
  {"id":"johnarrested","harmony":"§34b","lane":"herod","place":"machaerus","date":"AD 30","title":"Herod Antipas imprisons John","people":["Herod Antipas","John the Baptist","Herodias"],"gospels":{"Lk":"3:19–20"},"summary":"John has rebuked Herod Antipas for taking Herodias, his brother's wife, and for all his other evil deeds. Herod adds this to the rest and shuts John up in prison. Josephus says the prison was the fortress of Machaerus east of the Dead Sea, and gives Herod's political motive: he feared John's influence over the people might lead to a revolt. John will remain there until his execution.","stay":"John was held in the fortress of Machaerus, by Josephus' account, for many months.","scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 3:19–20","text":"But Herod the tetrarch, who had been reproved by him for Herodias, his brother's wife, and for all the evil things that Herod had done, added this to them all, that he shut up John in prison."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"1 Kings 21:17–24","note":"Elijah confronts King Ahab, whose wife Jezebel then seeks his life; Herod and Herodias play the same roles against John, the new Elijah."},{"ref":"Leviticus 18:16","note":"The law forbidding a man to marry his brother's wife, the ground of John's rebuke."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","img":"img/johnarrested0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Machaerus_Panorama.jpg","title":"Machaerus","artist":"Unknown photographer","year":"Modern photograph","medium":"Photograph","location":"Mukawir, Jordan","description":"The cone-shaped hill of Machaerus rises from the dry hills east of the Dead Sea, which shines in the haze behind it. A few re-erected columns of Herod's palace stand on the flat summit.","significance":"Josephus names Machaerus as the place where Herod Antipas imprisoned and executed John the Baptist.","symbolism":"A remote desert stronghold, close to the Perean country where John preached, but cut off from the crowds.","impact":"Excavations by Franciscan and later Hungarian archaeologists uncovered the palace and re-erected some of its columns."}],"tags":["conflict"],"orderNote":"Matthew 14:3–5 and Mark 6:17–20 tell the arrest only later, as a flashback at John's death; Luke mentions it early, in Luke 3:19–20, to close John's story. Josephus (Antiquities 18.5.2) names Machaerus."},
  {"id":"samaritanwoman","harmony":"§35","lane":"jesus","place":"jacobswell","from":"judeacountry","date":"Late AD 30","title":"The Samaritan woman at Jacob's Well","people":["Jesus","A Samaritan woman","The disciples","The people of Sychar"],"gospels":{"Jn":"4:5–42"},"summary":"Tired from the journey, Jesus sits by Jacob's Well at noon and asks a Samaritan woman for a drink, breaking the custom that Jews and Samaritans had no dealings. He offers her living water, reveals that she has had five husbands, and tells her that true worshipers will worship the Father in spirit and truth. Then he tells her plainly that he is the Messiah. She runs to tell her town, many Samaritans believe, and they call him \"the Savior of the world.\"","stay":"At the Samaritans' request Jesus stayed two days in Sychar.","journey":"About 30 to 40 miles north along the ridge road through the hills of Samaria, a two-day walk; he reaches the well about noon.","route":[[35.3,31.85],[35.24,31.93],[35.26,32.03],[35.27,32.12],[35.285,32.209]],"scripture":[{"ref":"John 4:13–14","text":"Jesus said to her, \"Every one who drinks of this water will thirst again, but whoever drinks of the water that I shall give him will never thirst; the water that I shall give him will become in him a spring of water welling up to eternal life.\""},{"ref":"John 4:25–26","text":"The woman said to him, \"I know that Messiah is coming (he who is called Christ); when he comes, he will show us all things.\" Jesus said to her, \"I who speak to you am he.\""},{"ref":"John 4:42","text":"They said to the woman, \"It is no longer because of your words that we believe, for we have heard for ourselves, and we know that this is indeed the Savior of the world.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Joshua 24:32","note":"Joseph's bones were buried at Shechem, in the plot Jacob bought: the \"field that Jacob gave to his son Joseph\" (John 4:5)."},{"ref":"Genesis 29:9–11","note":"Jacob meets Rachel at a well, one of several biblical meetings at wells."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/samaritanwoman0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Duccio_di_Buoninsegna_-_Christ_and_the_Samaritan_Woman_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg","title":"Christ and the Samaritan Woman","artist":"Duccio di Buoninsegna","year":"1310–11","medium":"Tempera and gold on panel","location":"Museo Nacional Thyssen-Bornemisza, Madrid","description":"Christ sits on the edge of a six-sided well and speaks with the woman, who balances a red jar on her head. At right the disciples come out of the gate of the walled town of Sychar.","significance":"A panel from the back of the Maestà, Duccio's great altarpiece for Siena Cathedral, which was later dismantled and scattered among museums.","symbolism":"The gold background lifts the conversation out of time; the city gate behind recalls that the woman will go back to bring her town to Christ.","impact":"The Maestà was carried in procession from Duccio's workshop to the cathedral in 1311; its panels are among the treasures of early Italian art."},{"kind":"Descriptive","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","img":"img/samaritanwoman1.jpg","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Woman_of_Samaria_at_the_Well_(La_Samaritaine_à_la_fontaine)_-_James_Tissot_-_overall.jpg","title":"The Woman of Samaria at the Well","description":"Inside a vaulted stone well-house, Jesus sits wrapped in a white mantle while the woman, in bright striped clothes, lowers a small cup on a rope into the well.","significance":"Tissot pictures Jacob's Well as he found it in the 1880s, enclosed in the vaulted chamber of a ruined church.","symbolism":"The woman draws ordinary water while Jesus, seated at rest, speaks of the living water he gives.","impact":"Brings out the setting John stresses: \"the well is deep,\" and the woman has come alone at midday."}],"tags":["teaching"],"orderNote":"Robertson notes that Jesus openly says he is the Messiah here (John 4:26), to a Samaritan, while in Judea and Galilee he avoids the title until the end. Jesus' remark that there are \"yet four months\" to harvest (John 4:35) could date this to about December or January, if it is not a proverb; Robertson thinks it is probably a proverb."},
  {"id":"galileearrival","harmony":"§36","lane":"jesus","place":"galilee","from":"jacobswell","date":"Late AD 30","title":"Jesus arrives in Galilee","people":["Jesus","The disciples","The Galileans"],"gospels":{"Jn":"4:43–45"},"summary":"After two days in Sychar Jesus goes on to Galilee. Though he had said that a prophet has no honor in his own country, the Galileans welcome him. Many had been in Jerusalem at the Passover and seen what he did there. This begins the great Galilean ministry.","journey":"About 40 to 50 miles north through Samaria and across the Jezreel Valley into Galilee, two or three days on foot.","route":[[35.285,32.209],[35.3,32.33],[35.3,32.46],[35.33,32.6],[35.38,32.72],[35.42,32.8]],"scripture":[{"ref":"John 4:44–45","text":"For Jesus himself testified that a prophet has no honor in his own country. So when he came to Galilee, the Galileans welcomed him, having seen all that he had done in Jerusalem at the feast, for they too had gone to the feast."}],"art":[],"tags":[]},
  {"id":"galileeteach","harmony":"§37","lane":"jesus","place":"galilee","date":"Late AD 30","title":"Jesus preaches in Galilee","people":["Jesus","The people of Galilee"],"gospels":{"Mt":"4:17","Mk":"1:14–15","Lk":"4:14–15"},"summary":"After John is arrested, Jesus returns to Galilee in the power of the Spirit. He teaches in the synagogues and announces that the time is fulfilled and the kingdom of God is at hand. His fame spreads through the whole region. This opens the great Galilean ministry, the longest and busiest period of his public life.","scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 1:14–15","text":"Now after John was arrested, Jesus came into Galilee, preaching the gospel of God, and saying, \"The time is fulfilled, and the kingdom of God is at hand; repent, and believe in the gospel.\""},{"ref":"Luke 4:14–15","text":"And Jesus returned in the power of the Spirit into Galilee, and a report concerning him went out through all the surrounding country. And he taught in their synagogues, being glorified by all."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Daniel 2:44","note":"The God of heaven will set up a kingdom that shall never be destroyed."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/galileeteach0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Jesus_Teaches_in_the_Synagogues_(Jésus_enseigne_dans_les_synagogues)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"Jesus Teaches in the Synagogues","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Wrapped in a prayer shawl, Jesus stands at the reading desk of a synagogue hung with lamps, a scroll before him.","significance":"Tissot made three long trips to the Holy Land to study landscapes, buildings and dress for his Life of Christ.","symbolism":"The plain room and listening elders stress that Jesus' ministry began in ordinary village synagogues.","impact":"The series of about 350 pictures drew huge crowds in Paris, London and New York in the 1890s."}],"tags":["teaching"],"orderNote":"Robertson treats this as a summary heading for the whole Galilean ministry. How long that ministry lasted is uncertain: about a year and a half if the feast of John 5:1 was a Passover, otherwise perhaps six to eight months."},
  {"id":"courtier","harmony":"§38","lane":"jesus","place":"cana","from":"galilee","date":"Late AD 30","title":"The official's son healed from Cana","people":["Jesus","A royal official of Capernaum","His son"],"gospels":{"Jn":"4:46–54"},"summary":"Jesus comes again to Cana, where he had turned water into wine. An official in Herod Antipas' service, whose son lies dying in Capernaum, begs him to come down. Jesus tells him to go: his son will live. On the road home his servants meet him with the news that the fever left the boy at the very hour Jesus spoke. John calls it the second sign Jesus did in Galilee.","journey":"A few miles across the hills of Lower Galilee to Cana.","route":[[35.42,32.8],[35.38,32.775],[35.34,32.75]],"scripture":[{"ref":"John 4:49–50","text":"The official said to him, \"Sir, come down before my child dies.\" Jesus said to him, \"Go; your son will live.\" The man believed the word that Jesus spoke to him and went his way."},{"ref":"John 4:53","text":"The father knew that was the hour when Jesus had said to him, \"Your son will live\"; and he himself believed, and all his household."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/courtier0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Healing_of_the_Officer's_Son_(La_guérison_du_fils_de_l'officier)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"The Healing of the Officer's Son","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"In a busy street under market awnings, the richly dressed official pleads with Jesus, who answers that his son will live.","significance":"One of the few paintings of this scene from John 4, rarely shown in art.","symbolism":"The healing happens by a word spoken miles away; the picture centers on the father's faith, not on the sick boy.","impact":"The Brooklyn Museum bought the whole series by public subscription in 1900."}],"tags":["miracle","healing"],"orderNote":"Cana to Capernaum is about 16 to 20 miles, depending on which site is Cana, so the healing happens at a distance. Some link this official with Chuza, Herod's steward (Luke 8:3), but that is only a guess."},
  {"id":"nazarethreject","harmony":"§39","lane":"jesus","place":"nazareth","from":"cana","date":"Late AD 30","title":"Rejected at Nazareth","people":["Jesus","The synagogue of Nazareth"],"gospels":{"Lk":"4:16–31"},"summary":"On the Sabbath Jesus stands up in the synagogue of his home town and reads from Isaiah: the Spirit of the Lord has anointed him to bring good news to the poor and release to captives. He tells them the scripture is fulfilled today. The hearers first wonder at him, then grow angry when he recalls how Elijah and Elisha were sent to foreigners. They drive him to the brow of the hill to throw him down, but he passes through their midst.","journey":"About 4 to 9 miles from Cana, depending on which site is Cana, over the hills of Lower Galilee.","route":[[35.34,32.75],[35.32,32.725],[35.3,32.7]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 4:18–19","text":"\"The Spirit of the Lord is upon me, because he has anointed me to preach good news to the poor. He has sent me to proclaim release to the captives and recovering of sight to the blind, to set at liberty those who are oppressed, to proclaim the acceptable year of the Lord.\""},{"ref":"Luke 4:21","text":"And he began to say to them, \"Today this scripture has been fulfilled in your hearing.\""},{"ref":"Luke 4:29–30","text":"And they rose up and put him out of the city, and led him to the brow of the hill on which their city was built, that they might throw him down headlong. But passing through the midst of them he went away."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Isaiah 61:1–2","note":"The passage Jesus reads: the anointed one sent with good news to the poor."},{"ref":"1 Kings 17:8–9; 2 Kings 5:14","note":"The widow of Zarephath and Naaman the Syrian, foreigners helped by Elijah and Elisha."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/nazarethreject0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Jesus_Unrolls_the_Book_in_the_Synagogue_(Jésus_dans_la_synagogue_déroule_le_livre)_-_James_Tissot_-_overall.jpg","title":"Jesus Unrolls the Book in the Synagogue","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Jesus stands on the raised reading platform of a synagogue hung with lamps, among men wrapped in prayer shawls.","significance":"Tissot shows a scroll and synagogue furnishings based on what he saw of Jewish worship in his own day.","symbolism":"The open scroll marks the moment Jesus declares, \"Today this scripture has been fulfilled.\"","impact":"Helps readers picture Luke's account, the only Gospel scene of Jesus reading aloud."},{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/nazarethreject1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Brow_of_the_Hill_near_Nazareth_(L'escarpement_de_Nazareth)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"The Brow of the Hill near Nazareth","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"An angry crowd surges along a path at the edge of a cliff, while Jesus walks calmly back through the middle of them.","significance":"Tissot based the cliff on the steep drops he saw around Nazareth.","symbolism":"Jesus alone is still amid the shouting crowd: \"passing through the midst of them he went away.\"","impact":"A vivid picture of a scene few artists have attempted."}],"tags":["teaching","conflict"],"orderNote":"Luke places this visit at the start of the ministry; Mark 6:1–6 and Matthew 13:54–58 tell of a visit much later. Robertson takes them as two visits (§39 and §69); many scholars think Luke has moved the one visit forward as a program for the whole ministry. A cliff south of the town, the Mount of the Precipice, is the traditional site."},
  {"id":"capernaumhome","harmony":"§40","lane":"jesus","place":"capernaum","from":"nazareth","date":"Late AD 30","title":"A new home in Capernaum","people":["Jesus"],"gospels":{"Mt":"4:13–16"},"summary":"Leaving Nazareth, Jesus settles in Capernaum, a lakeside town in the old tribal lands of Zebulun and Naphtali. Matthew sees in this the fulfilment of Isaiah's promise of a great light for \"Galilee of the Gentiles.\" From now on Capernaum is his base, though much of his time is spent on the road.","stay":"Capernaum was his home base for the rest of the Galilean ministry, probably in Peter's house.","journey":"About 28 miles from Nazareth, down through the hills and the Arbel pass to the lake shore, a long day's walk.","route":[[35.3,32.7],[35.34,32.75],[35.42,32.79],[35.47,32.81],[35.515,32.825],[35.549,32.873],[35.575,32.881]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 4:13","text":"and leaving Nazareth he went and dwelt in Capernaum by the sea, in the territory of Zebulun and Naphtali,"},{"ref":"Matthew 4:16","text":"the people who sat in darkness have seen a great light, and for those who sat in the region and shadow of death light has dawned.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Isaiah 9:1–2","note":"The people who walked in darkness have seen a great light, in Zebulun and Naphtali."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/capernaumhome0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Sea_of_Tiberias_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"Sea of Tiberias","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Drawing on paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"A quiet stretch of the lake shore, with reeds, boulders and the hills of the far side.","significance":"A landscape study Tissot made on site during his Holy Land travels.","symbolism":"The lake was the setting for most of Jesus' Galilean ministry, from Capernaum on its northern shore.","impact":"Tissot's landscapes gave Western viewers a first look at the real setting of the Gospels."}],"tags":["prophecy"]},
  {"id":"fishers","harmony":"§41","lane":"jesus","place":"tabgha","from":"capernaum","date":"Late AD 30","title":"The call of the four fishermen","people":["Jesus","Simon Peter","Andrew","James","John","Zebedee"],"gospels":{"Mt":"4:18–22","Mk":"1:16–20","Lk":"5:1–11"},"summary":"By the lake Jesus calls two pairs of brothers, Simon and Andrew and James and John, to follow him and become fishers of men. Luke tells how Jesus first teaches the crowd from Simon's boat, then sends him out to let down the nets after a fruitless night; the catch is so great the nets begin to break. Simon falls at his knees. They leave everything and follow him. Several of them had already met Jesus at the Jordan.","journey":"About 2 miles along the shore west of Capernaum.","route":[[35.575,32.881],[35.56,32.877],[35.549,32.873]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 5:5","text":"And Simon answered, \"Master, we toiled all night and took nothing! But at your word I will let down the nets.\""},{"ref":"Luke 5:8","text":"But when Simon Peter saw it, he fell down at Jesus' knees, saying, \"Depart from me, for I am a sinful man, O Lord.\""},{"ref":"Mark 1:17","text":"And Jesus said to them, \"Follow me and I will make you become fishers of men.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Jeremiah 16:16","note":"\"I am sending for many fishers, says the Lord, and they shall catch them.\""},{"ref":"1 Kings 19:19–21","note":"Elisha leaves his plowing at once to follow Elijah."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/fishers0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Duccio_di_Buoninsegna_036.jpg","title":"The Calling of the Apostles Peter and Andrew","artist":"Duccio di Buoninsegna","year":"1308–1311","medium":"Tempera on panel","location":"National Gallery of Art, Washington","description":"Christ stands on the shore and beckons to Peter and Andrew, who sit in their boat with a net full of fish on a gold background.","significance":"A panel from the back predella of the Maestà, Duccio's great altarpiece for Siena Cathedral, later broken up and sold.","symbolism":"The gold sky turns a fishing scene into a sacred moment; the brothers' turning toward Christ is the whole story.","impact":"One of the founding works of Sienese painting; parts of the Maestà are now spread among museums in Europe and America."},{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/fishers1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ghirlandaio,_Domenico_-_Calling_of_the_Apostles_-_1481.jpg","title":"The Calling of the First Apostles","artist":"Domenico Ghirlandaio","year":"1481–1482","medium":"Fresco","location":"Sistine Chapel, Vatican City","description":"Peter and Andrew kneel before Christ by the lake, while in the background the call of the fishermen is told again in smaller scenes.","significance":"Part of the cycle of the life of Christ that Pope Sixtus IV commissioned for the side walls of the Sistine Chapel, decades before Michelangelo's ceiling.","symbolism":"Florentine onlookers in contemporary dress stand around the scene, as if the call reaches into the painter's own city.","impact":"Seen by millions of visitors to the Sistine Chapel, and by the cardinals at every papal conclave."}],"tags":["call","miracle","nature-miracle"],"orderNote":"Robertson takes Luke's miraculous catch as the same call told in Mark and Matthew; some scholars see two occasions. Mark and Matthew do not name the place beyond \"the Sea of Galilee\"; the shore at Tabgha is the traditional site."},
  {"id":"unclean","harmony":"§42","lane":"jesus","place":"capernaum","from":"tabgha","date":"Late AD 30","title":"A demoniac healed in the synagogue","people":["Jesus","A man with an unclean spirit","The synagogue at Capernaum"],"gospels":{"Mk":"1:21–28","Lk":"4:31–37"},"summary":"On the Sabbath Jesus teaches in the synagogue at Capernaum, and the people are astonished, for he teaches with authority, not like the scribes. A man with an unclean spirit cries out that he knows who Jesus is, the Holy One of God. Jesus silences the spirit and commands it to come out. His fame spreads at once through all Galilee.","journey":"About 2 miles back along the shore to Capernaum.","route":[[35.549,32.873],[35.56,32.877],[35.575,32.881]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 1:22","text":"And they were astonished at his teaching, for he taught them as one who had authority, and not as the scribes."},{"ref":"Mark 1:27","text":"And they were all amazed, so that they questioned among themselves, saying, \"What is this? A new teaching! With authority he commands even the unclean spirits, and they obey him.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/unclean0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Possessed_Man_in_the_Synagogue_(Le_possédé_dans_la_Synagogue)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"The Possessed Man in the Synagogue","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"In the synagogue a possessed man is thrown to the floor among the startled worshipers in prayer shawls.","significance":"Tissot shows the fear and confusion that Mark describes.","symbolism":"Christ's calm authority contrasts with the man's frenzy.","impact":"A rare picture of Jesus' first recorded exorcism."}],"tags":["exorcism","teaching"]},
  {"id":"petersmother","harmony":"§43","lane":"jesus","place":"capernaum","date":"Late AD 30","title":"Peter's mother-in-law and many others healed","people":["Jesus","Simon Peter","Andrew","James","John","Peter's mother-in-law"],"gospels":{"Mt":"8:14–17","Mk":"1:29–34","Lk":"4:38–41"},"summary":"Leaving the synagogue, Jesus goes to the house of Simon and Andrew, where Simon's mother-in-law lies sick with a fever. He takes her by the hand and lifts her up; the fever leaves her and she serves them. At sundown, when the Sabbath ends, the whole town gathers at the door, and he heals many sick and casts out many demons.","stay":"In the house of Simon and Andrew at Capernaum.","scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 1:31","text":"And he came and took her by the hand and lifted her up, and the fever left her; and she served them."},{"ref":"Matthew 8:17","text":"This was to fulfil what was spoken by the prophet Isaiah, \"He took our infirmities and bore our diseases.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Isaiah 53:4","note":"\"He took our infirmities and bore our diseases,\" as Matthew quotes it."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/petersmother0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Healing_of_Peter's_Mother-in-law_(La_guérison_de_la_belle-mère_de_Pierre)_-_James_Tissot_-_overall.jpg","title":"The Healing of Peter's Mother-in-law","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Outside a stone house, Jesus bends to raise the sick woman by the hand while Peter looks on.","significance":"Tissot sets the scene at a modest Galilean house rather than in a grand interior.","symbolism":"The touch of the hand, which Mark mentions, is the center of the picture.","impact":"One of very few paintings of this short Gospel scene."}],"tags":["miracle","healing","exorcism"]},
  {"id":"firsttour","harmony":"§44","lane":"jesus","place":"galilee","from":"capernaum","date":"Late AD 30","title":"The first tour of Galilee","people":["Jesus","Simon Peter","Andrew","James","John"],"gospels":{"Mt":"4:23–25","Mk":"1:35–39","Lk":"4:42–44"},"summary":"Before dawn Jesus goes out to a lonely place to pray. When Simon and the others find him and say everyone is looking for him, he answers that he must preach in the other towns too, for that is why he came. He goes throughout Galilee teaching in the synagogues, preaching the kingdom and healing every disease. Crowds come from Galilee, the Decapolis, Jerusalem, Judea and beyond the Jordan.","journey":"A circuit of the towns and villages of Galilee; the Gospels give no route or length.","route":[[35.575,32.881],[35.549,32.873],[35.53,32.85],[35.515,32.825],[35.47,32.81],[35.42,32.8]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 1:35","text":"And in the morning, a great while before day, he rose and went out to a lonely place, and there he prayed."},{"ref":"Mark 1:38","text":"And he said to them, \"Let us go on to the next towns, that I may preach there also; for that is why I came out.\""},{"ref":"Matthew 4:23","text":"And he went about all Galilee, teaching in their synagogues and preaching the gospel of the kingdom and healing every disease and every infirmity among the people."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/firsttour0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Jesus_Went_Out_into_a_Desert_Place_(Jésus_va_dans_un_endroit_désert)_-_James_Tissot_-_overall.jpg","title":"Jesus Went Out into a Desert Place","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Jesus sits alone on a rocky slope among olive trees, while below the disciples come looking for him.","significance":"Tissot often painted Jesus' solitary prayer, which the Gospels mention again and again.","symbolism":"The solitary figure apart from the searching crowd shows prayer as the source of his ministry.","impact":"These quiet scenes balance the crowded miracle pictures of the series."}],"tags":["teaching","prayer","healing"],"orderNote":"Robertson counts three tours of Galilee: this one with the four fishermen, a second in Luke 8:1–3 (§60), and a third after the sending of the Twelve (§70)."},
  {"id":"leper","harmony":"§45","lane":"jesus","place":"galilee","date":"Late AD 30","title":"A leper healed","people":["Jesus","A man full of leprosy"],"gospels":{"Mt":"8:2–4","Mk":"1:40–45","Lk":"5:12–16"},"summary":"In one of the towns a leper kneels before Jesus and says, \"If you will, you can make me clean.\" Moved with pity, Jesus touches him, something the Law treated as making a person unclean, and he is cleansed. Jesus tells him to tell no one but to show himself to the priest as Moses commanded. The man spreads the news everywhere, so that Jesus can no longer openly enter a town.","scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 1:40–41","text":"And a leper came to him beseeching him, and kneeling said to him, \"If you will, you can make me clean.\" Moved with pity, he stretched out his hand and touched him, and said to him, \"I will; be clean.\""},{"ref":"Mark 1:45","text":"But he went out and began to talk freely about it, and to spread the news, so that Jesus could no longer openly enter a town, but was out in the country; and people came to him from every quarter."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Leviticus 14:2–4","note":"The law for the cleansing of a leper, shown to the priest."},{"ref":"2 Kings 5:14","note":"Naaman the Syrian cleansed of leprosy in the Jordan."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/leper0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Healing_of_the_Lepers_at_Capernaum_(Guérison_des_lépreux_à_Capernaum)_-_James_Tissot_-_overall.jpg","title":"Healing of the Lepers at Capernaum","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"A leper in rags kneels in a village street and raises his arms toward Jesus.","significance":"Tissot shows the outcast alone in the street, with townspeople keeping their distance.","symbolism":"Jesus touches a man whom the Law kept apart.","impact":"A widely reproduced image from the series."}],"tags":["miracle","healing"],"orderNote":"Luke says only \"in one of the cities\"; Matthew places the healing as Jesus comes down from the mountain after the Sermon. Robertson follows Mark's order."},
  {"id":"paralytic","harmony":"§46","lane":"jesus","place":"capernaum","from":"galilee","date":"Late AD 30","title":"The paralytic let down through the roof","people":["Jesus","A paralyzed man","Four friends","Scribes and Pharisees"],"gospels":{"Mt":"9:1–8","Mk":"2:1–12","Lk":"5:17–26"},"summary":"Back in Capernaum, the house where Jesus is staying is so crowded that no one can reach the door. Four men carry a paralyzed friend onto the roof, open it up, and let him down on his pallet. Seeing their faith, Jesus tells the man his sins are forgiven. When scribes object that only God can forgive sins, Jesus heals him to show that the Son of man has authority to forgive. The man walks out carrying his pallet.","journey":"Back to Capernaum after the tour.","route":[[35.42,32.8],[35.47,32.81],[35.515,32.825],[35.53,32.85],[35.549,32.873],[35.575,32.881]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 2:4–5","text":"And when they could not get near him because of the crowd, they removed the roof above him; and when they had made an opening, they let down the pallet on which the paralytic lay. And when Jesus saw their faith, he said to the paralytic, \"My son, your sins are forgiven.\""},{"ref":"Mark 2:11–12","text":"\"I say to you, rise, take up your pallet and go home.\" And he rose, and immediately took up the pallet and went out before them all; so that they were all amazed and glorified God, saying, \"We never saw anything like this!\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/paralytic0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Palsied_Man_Let_Down_through_the_Roof_(Le_paralytique_descendu_du_toit)_-_James_Tissot_-_overall.jpg","title":"The Palsied Man Let Down through the Roof","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"A paralyzed man on his mat is lowered by ropes through an opening in the roof into a crowded room.","significance":"Tissot builds the scene around a flat-roofed house like those he saw in Palestine.","symbolism":"The opening above lets light fall on the man as faith brings him to Jesus.","impact":"One of the best-known images in the series."},{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/paralytic1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anthony_van_Dyck_-_Christ_healing_the_paralytic.jpg","title":"Christ Healing the Paralytic","artist":"Anthony van Dyck","year":"c. 1619","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Royal Collection, United Kingdom","description":"Christ, in a red robe and blue mantle, turns toward a frail old man with a bandaged head, while a young man looks on.","significance":"An early work painted while van Dyck was in Rubens' studio in Antwerp.","symbolism":"Christ's raised hand both forgives and heals; the close half-length figures place us right beside the bed.","impact":"Shows the young van Dyck already rivaling his master in color and feeling."}],"tags":["miracle","healing","conflict"],"orderNote":"Robertson takes the house to be Peter's. A flat roof of beams, branches and packed earth, as in Galilean houses of basalt, could be dug through; Luke speaks of tiles."},
  {"id":"levi","harmony":"§47","lane":"jesus","place":"capernaum","date":"Late AD 30","title":"The call of Matthew (Levi)","people":["Jesus","Matthew (Levi), son of Alphaeus","Tax collectors and sinners","Pharisees"],"gospels":{"Mt":"9:9–13","Mk":"2:13–17","Lk":"5:27–32"},"summary":"Walking by the lake, Jesus sees Levi, also called Matthew, sitting at the tax office and says, \"Follow me.\" He leaves everything and follows. Levi then holds a great feast in his house for Jesus, with many tax collectors and sinners at table. When the Pharisees complain, Jesus answers that he came to call not the righteous but sinners.","scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 9:9","text":"As Jesus passed on from there, he saw a man called Matthew sitting at the tax office; and he said to him, \"Follow me.\" And he rose and followed him."},{"ref":"Mark 2:17","text":"And when Jesus heard it, he said to them, \"Those who are well have no need of a physician, but those who are sick; I came not to call the righteous, but sinners.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Hosea 6:6","note":"\"I desire mercy, and not sacrifice,\" which Jesus quotes in Matthew 9:13."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/levi0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Gleb Simonov, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caravaggio_—_The_Calling_of_Saint_Matthew.jpg","title":"The Calling of Saint Matthew","artist":"Caravaggio","year":"1599–1600","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Contarelli Chapel, San Luigi dei Francesi, Rome","description":"Christ and Peter step into a dim counting room and Christ points at Matthew, who sits at a table with coins and well-dressed companions.","significance":"Caravaggio's first major public commission; it made him famous in Rome overnight.","symbolism":"A beam of light follows Christ's pointing hand, which echoes Adam's hand in Michelangelo's Creation: a new creation. Scholars still debate which figure is Matthew.","impact":"One of the most influential paintings of the Baroque; its dramatic light set a style followed across Europe."},{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/levi1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Calling_of_Saint_Matthew_(Vocation_de_Saint_Mathieu)_-_James_Tissot_-_overall.jpg","title":"The Calling of Saint Matthew","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Jesus stops at the window of a customs booth beside resting camels and calls the tax collector inside.","significance":"Tissot sets the call in a busy street, as a real customs post on the road by the lake.","symbolism":"The booth's barred window stands for the trade Matthew is about to leave.","impact":"Gives a plain, historical view of the same scene Caravaggio set in a Roman tavern."}],"tags":["call","conflict"],"orderNote":"Mark and Luke call him Levi; the first Gospel calls him Matthew, and all three lists of the Twelve include a Matthew. Most take them as one man with two names."},
  {"id":"newwine","harmony":"§48","lane":"jesus","place":"capernaum","date":"Late AD 30","title":"New wine in fresh wineskins","people":["Jesus","Disciples of John","Pharisees"],"gospels":{"Mt":"9:14–17","Mk":"2:18–22","Lk":"5:33–39"},"summary":"John's disciples and the Pharisees ask why Jesus' disciples do not fast. He answers with three short parables: wedding guests do not fast while the bridegroom is with them; no one sews new cloth on an old garment; no one puts new wine into old wineskins. The days will come, he says, when the bridegroom is taken away, and then they will fast.","scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 2:19","text":"And Jesus said to them, \"Can the wedding guests fast while the bridegroom is with them? As long as they have the bridegroom with them, they cannot fast."},{"ref":"Mark 2:22","text":"And no one puts new wine into old wineskins; if he does, the wine will burst the skins, and the wine is lost, and so are the skins; but new wine is for fresh skins.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Hosea 2:19–20","note":"God as the bridegroom of his people."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/newwine0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Meal_in_the_House_of_Matthew_(Le_repas_chez_Mathieu)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"The Meal in the House of Matthew","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Guests gather in the shaded courtyard of Matthew's house, while critics stand in the foreground watching.","significance":"Tissot shows the custom of reclining at table and the mixed company the Pharisees objected to.","symbolism":"The feast is the setting for the question about fasting and Jesus' sayings about the bridegroom and new wine.","impact":"Helps readers picture how meals were eaten in Jesus' world."}],"tags":["parable","teaching","conflict"],"orderNote":"Robertson thinks the question was probably prompted by the disciples feasting at Matthew's house on a Jewish fast day."},
  {"id":"bethesda","harmony":"§49","lane":"jesus","place":"bethesda","from":"capernaum","date":"AD 31 (an unnamed feast)","title":"The healing at the Pool of Bethesda","people":["Jesus","A man ill for 38 years","The Jewish authorities"],"gospels":{"Jn":"5:1–47"},"summary":"At a feast in Jerusalem, Jesus finds a man who has been ill for thirty-eight years lying by the Pool of Bethesda among many sick people. He asks, \"Do you want to be healed?\" and tells him to rise, take up his pallet and walk. Because it is the Sabbath, the authorities object, and Jesus replies that his Father is working still and so is he. They seek all the more to kill him, for he calls God his own Father. A long discourse follows on the Son's authority and the witnesses to him.","journey":"About 90 to 100 miles from Capernaum down the Jordan Valley and up from Jericho to Jerusalem, about four or five days.","route":[[35.575,32.881],[35.515,32.825],[35.535,32.79],[35.57,32.7],[35.55,32.5],[35.53,32.15],[35.47,31.88],[35.3,31.8],[35.2359,31.7813]],"scripture":[{"ref":"John 5:6","text":"When Jesus saw him and knew that he had been lying there a long time, he said to him, \"Do you want to be healed?\""},{"ref":"John 5:8–9","text":"Jesus said to him, \"Rise, take up your pallet, and walk.\" And at once the man was healed, and he took up his pallet and walked. Now that day was the sabbath."},{"ref":"John 5:17","text":"But Jesus answered them, \"My Father is working still, and I am working.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/bethesda0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Piscina_Probatica_or_Pool_of_Bethesda_(La_piscine_probatique_ou_de_Bethesda)_-_James_Tissot_-_overall.jpg","title":"The Piscina Probatica or Pool of Bethesda","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Sick people wait under stone arches around the pool; two hands reach into the water to stir it.","significance":"Tissot drew on the excavations and descriptions of the pool known in his day.","symbolism":"The hands recall the old belief, added in some manuscripts at John 5:4, that an angel stirred the water and the first one in was healed.","impact":"An early attempt to show the pool as an actual place."}],"tags":["miracle","healing","conflict","teaching"],"orderNote":"John does not name the feast, and the manuscripts differ on \"a feast\" or \"the feast.\" Robertson thinks it most probably a Passover, which would make the ministry about three years long; others propose Purim, Pentecost or Tabernacles. Some scholars think John 5 and 6 have been transposed."},
  {"id":"grainfields","harmony":"§50","lane":"jesus","place":"galilee","from":"bethesda","date":"AD 31","title":"Plucking grain on the Sabbath","people":["Jesus","The disciples","Pharisees"],"gospels":{"Mt":"12:1–8","Mk":"2:23–28","Lk":"6:1–5"},"summary":"Walking through grainfields on a Sabbath, the disciples pluck heads of grain and rub them in their hands to eat. The Pharisees call this unlawful work. Jesus points to David eating the holy bread when he was hungry and to the priests who work in the Temple on the Sabbath. The Sabbath was made for man, he says, and the Son of man is lord even of the Sabbath.","journey":"About 85 to 95 miles back north to Galilee by the Jordan Valley.","route":[[35.2359,31.7813],[35.3,31.8],[35.47,31.88],[35.53,32.15],[35.55,32.5],[35.5,32.65],[35.42,32.8]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 2:23","text":"One sabbath he was going through the grainfields; and as they made their way his disciples began to pluck ears of grain."},{"ref":"Mark 2:27–28","text":"And he said to them, \"The sabbath was made for man, not man for the sabbath; so the Son of man is lord even of the sabbath.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"1 Samuel 21:1–6","note":"David and his men eat the holy bread at Nob."},{"ref":"Hosea 6:6","note":"\"I desire mercy, and not sacrifice,\" quoted in Matthew 12:7."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/grainfields0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Disciples_Eat_Wheat_on_the_Sabbath_(Les_disciples_mangent_du_blé_au_sabbat)_-_James_Tissot_-_overall.jpg","title":"The Disciples Eat Wheat on the Sabbath","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Pharisees lean on a stone wall to watch as Jesus and the disciples walk through the ripe fields below a hill town.","significance":"Tissot shows the terraced grainfields of the hill country.","symbolism":"The ripe grain marks the season and the small, ordinary act that sparked the dispute.","impact":"A rare picture of this Sabbath controversy."}],"tags":["conflict","teaching"],"orderNote":"Robertson places this \"probably in Galilee on the way back from Jerusalem\"; the Gospels name no place. Ripe grain points to spring, between Passover and Pentecost."},
  {"id":"witheredhand","harmony":"§51","lane":"jesus","place":"galilee","date":"AD 31","title":"The man with the withered hand","people":["Jesus","A man with a withered hand","Pharisees","Herodians"],"gospels":{"Mt":"12:9–14","Mk":"3:1–6","Lk":"6:6–11"},"summary":"On another Sabbath, in a synagogue, the Pharisees watch to see whether Jesus will heal a man with a withered hand. Jesus asks whether it is lawful on the Sabbath to do good or to do harm, to save life or to kill. Grieved at their hardness of heart, he tells the man to stretch out his hand, and it is restored. The Pharisees go out and plot with the Herodians how to destroy him.","scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 3:4","text":"And he said to them, \"Is it lawful on the sabbath to do good or to do harm, to save life or to kill?\" But they were silent."},{"ref":"Mark 3:5","text":"And he looked around at them with anger, grieved at their hardness of heart, and said to the man, \"Stretch out your hand.\" He stretched it out, and his hand was restored."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/witheredhand0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Man_with_the_Withered_Hand_(L'homme_à_la_main_desséchée)_-_James_Tissot_-_overall.jpg","title":"The Man with the Withered Hand","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"In a crowded synagogue, the man in a striped robe holds out his hand as the worshipers watch.","significance":"Tissot captures the tension of a healing done under hostile eyes.","symbolism":"The outstretched hand is both the man's obedience and the sign of restoration.","impact":"One of few paintings of this scene."},{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/witheredhand1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Pharisees_and_the_Herodians_Conspire_Against_Jesus_(Les_pharisiens_et_les_hérodiens_conspirent_contre_Jésus)_-_James_Tissot_-_overall.jpg","title":"The Pharisees and the Herodians Conspire Against Jesus","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Pharisees and Herodians sit together on a low wall beneath the trees, deep in talk.","significance":"Tissot gives the opponents of Jesus their own scene, following Mark 3:6.","symbolism":"Religious and political rivals unite against him.","impact":"Marks the turn toward the Passion early in the Gospel story."}],"tags":["miracle","healing","conflict"],"orderNote":"The synagogue is not named. It may have been at Capernaum, but Robertson says only \"in Galilee.\""},
  {"id":"multitudes","harmony":"§52","lane":"jesus","place":"tabgha","from":"galilee","date":"AD 31","title":"Crowds by the Sea of Galilee","people":["Jesus","The disciples","Crowds from Galilee, Judea, Idumea, Perea, Tyre and Sidon"],"gospels":{"Mt":"12:15–21","Mk":"3:7–12"},"summary":"Jesus withdraws with his disciples to the lake, and a great multitude follows from all over the land, from Idumea in the south to Tyre and Sidon in the north. He has a boat kept ready so the crowd will not crush him. All who have diseases press to touch him, and unclean spirits fall down crying, \"You are the Son of God,\" but he orders them not to make him known. Matthew sees here Isaiah's gentle Servant.","journey":"Down to the lake shore, about 10 miles from central Galilee.","route":[[35.42,32.8],[35.47,32.81],[35.515,32.825],[35.53,32.85],[35.549,32.873]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 3:9–10","text":"And he told his disciples to have a boat ready for him because of the crowd, lest they should crush him; for he had healed many, so that all who had diseases pressed upon him to touch him."},{"ref":"Matthew 12:18","text":"\"Behold, my servant whom I have chosen, my beloved with whom my soul is well pleased. I will put my Spirit upon him, and he shall proclaim justice to the Gentiles."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Isaiah 42:1–4","note":"The chosen Servant who will not break a bruised reed, quoted in full by Matthew."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/multitudes0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Jesus_Preaches_in_a_Ship_(Jésus_prèche_dans_une_barque)_-_James_Tissot_-_overall.jpg","title":"Jesus Preaches in a Ship","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Jesus speaks from a boat drawn close to the shore, with the crowd gathered on the beach.","significance":"Tissot shows how the water carried his voice to a large crowd.","symbolism":"The boat kept ready for him, as Mark says, becomes his pulpit.","impact":"A popular image of Jesus as teacher by the lake."}],"tags":["healing","exorcism","prophecy"]},
  {"id":"twelve","harmony":"§53","lane":"jesus","place":"beatitudes","from":"tabgha","date":"AD 31","title":"The choosing of the Twelve","people":["Jesus","Simon Peter","Andrew","James and John, sons of Zebedee","Philip","Bartholomew","Matthew","Thomas","James son of Alphaeus","Thaddaeus (Judas son of James)","Simon the Zealot","Judas Iscariot"],"gospels":{"Mk":"3:13–19","Lk":"6:12–16"},"summary":"Jesus goes up into the hills and spends the whole night in prayer. In the morning he calls his disciples and chooses twelve of them, whom he names apostles, to be with him, to be sent out to preach, and to have authority over demons. The list begins with Simon, whom he names Peter, and ends with Judas Iscariot, who betrayed him. The number twelve recalls the twelve tribes of Israel.","journey":"Up into the hills above the lake, about a mile.","route":[[35.549,32.873],[35.553,32.878],[35.556,32.881]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 6:12–13","text":"In these days he went out into the hills to pray; and all night he continued in prayer to God. And when it was day, he called his disciples, and chose from them twelve, whom he named apostles;"},{"ref":"Mark 3:14–15","text":"And he appointed twelve, to be with him, and to be sent out to preach and have authority to cast out demons:"}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Genesis 49:28","note":"The twelve tribes of Israel; the Twelve point to a renewed Israel."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/twelve0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Ordaining_of_the_Twelve_Apostles_(Election_des_douze_apôtres)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"Ordaining of the Twelve Apostles","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"On a rocky hillside above the lake, Jesus calls out to the disciples as they come toward him one by one.","significance":"Tissot sets the choosing on the hills above the Sea of Galilee, following Luke's \"he went out into the hills.\"","symbolism":"Twelve men for the twelve tribes of a renewed Israel.","impact":"A rare depiction of the choosing of the Twelve."}],"tags":["call","prayer"],"orderNote":"Matthew gives the names only when the Twelve are sent out (Matthew 10:2–4, §70). The four lists (with Acts 1:13) agree on the names except Thaddaeus, whom Luke calls Judas son of James. The hill is not named; tradition places it near the Mount of Beatitudes."},
  {"id":"sermon","harmony":"§54","lane":"jesus","place":"beatitudes","date":"AD 31","title":"The Sermon on the Mount","people":["Jesus","The disciples","Great crowds"],"gospels":{"Mt":"5:1–7:29","Lk":"6:17–49"},"summary":"Seeing the crowds, Jesus sits down on the mountain and teaches his disciples. The sermon sets out the righteousness of the kingdom, deeper than that of the scribes and Pharisees: the Law fulfilled, not abolished; anger and lust judged in the heart; love of enemies; prayer, almsgiving and fasting done in secret; trust in the Father instead of anxiety; and the Golden Rule. The crowds are astonished, for he teaches as one who has authority.","scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 5:1–2","text":"Seeing the crowds, he went up on the mountain, and when he sat down his disciples came to him. And he opened his mouth and taught them, saying:"},{"ref":"Matthew 5:14","text":"\"You are the light of the world. A city set on a hill cannot be hid."},{"ref":"Matthew 7:28–29","text":"And when Jesus finished these sayings, the crowds were astonished at his teaching, for he taught them as one who had authority, and not as their scribes."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Exodus 19–20","note":"Moses receives the Law on Sinai; Matthew presents Jesus teaching the Law's fulfilment on a mountain."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/sermon0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fra_Angelico_-_Die_Bergpredigt.jpeg","title":"The Sermon on the Mount","artist":"Fra Angelico","year":"c. 1438–1445","medium":"Fresco","location":"Convent of San Marco, Florence","description":"Christ sits on a rocky height with his hand raised, teaching the apostles, who sit in a circle around him on the bare hillside.","significance":"One of the frescoes Fra Angelico and his workshop painted in the friars' cells of San Marco, as aids to prayer.","symbolism":"The pale, simplified rocks and quiet figures turn the scene into a meditation: only Christ and those who listen.","impact":"The San Marco frescoes are among the most loved works of the early Renaissance."},{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/sermon1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cosimo_Rosselli_Sermone_della_Montagna.jpg","title":"Sermon on the Mount and Healing of the Leper","artist":"Cosimo Rosselli (landscape by Piero di Cosimo)","year":"1481–1482","medium":"Fresco","location":"Sistine Chapel, Vatican City","description":"Christ stands on a hillside preaching to a great crowd; the fresco also shows the healing of a leper that follows the sermon in Matthew.","significance":"Part of the cycle of the life of Christ on the side walls of the Sistine Chapel.","symbolism":"Two moments from Matthew 5–8 appear in one picture: the teaching and then the healing that follows it.","impact":"Seen by millions in the Sistine Chapel; it shows how Renaissance painters told several episodes in one frame."}],"tags":["sermon","teaching"],"orderNote":"Matthew sets the sermon \"on the mountain,\" Luke \"on a level place\" and in shorter form. Robertson takes them as one discourse, perhaps on a level spot on a hillside; others think Matthew has gathered sayings from many occasions."},
  {"id":"beatitudes","harmony":"§54a","lane":"jesus","place":"beatitudes","date":"AD 31","title":"The Beatitudes","people":["Jesus","The disciples","Great crowds"],"gospels":{"Mt":"5:3–12","Lk":"6:20–26"},"summary":"The sermon opens with blessings on those the world overlooks: the poor in spirit, those who mourn, the meek, those who hunger for righteousness, the merciful, the pure in heart, the peacemakers and the persecuted. Each is promised a share in the kingdom. Matthew gives eight or nine blessings; Luke gives four, followed by four matching woes on the rich and the satisfied.","scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 5:3","text":"\"Blessed are the poor in spirit, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven."},{"ref":"Matthew 5:9","text":"\"Blessed are the peacemakers, for they shall be called sons of God."},{"ref":"Luke 6:20","text":"And he lifted up his eyes on his disciples, and said: \"Blessed are you poor, for yours is the kingdom of God."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Psalm 37:11","note":"\"The meek shall possess the land.\""},{"ref":"Isaiah 61:1–3","note":"Good news to the poor and comfort for those who mourn."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/beatitudes0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Sermon_of_the_Beatitudes_(La_sermon_des_béatitudes)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"The Sermon of the Beatitudes","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Jesus stands on a rocky height above the lake and speaks to a crowd spread across the hillside below.","significance":"Tissot shows the open-air setting and the size of the crowd.","symbolism":"The sloping ground forms a natural amphitheater, as on the hills above the lake.","impact":"One of the most reproduced images of the Sermon on the Mount."}],"tags":["sermon","teaching"]},
  {"id":"ourfather","harmony":"§54b","lane":"jesus","place":"beatitudes","date":"AD 31","title":"The Lord's Prayer","people":["Jesus","The disciples"],"gospels":{"Mt":"6:9–13"},"summary":"Warning against praying to be seen or heaping up empty phrases, Jesus gives his disciples a model prayer. It addresses God as Father, asks first for his name, kingdom and will, then for daily bread, forgiveness as we forgive, and deliverance from evil. It became the central prayer of Christians everywhere.","scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 6:9–10","text":"Pray then like this: Our Father who are in heaven, Hallowed be your name. Your kingdom come, Your will be done, On earth as it is in heaven."},{"ref":"Matthew 6:11–13","text":"Give us this day our daily bread; And forgive us our debts, As we also have forgiven our debtors; And lead us not into temptation, But deliver us from evil."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/ourfather0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Lord's_Prayer_(Le_Pater_Noster)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"The Lord's Prayer","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Jesus stands with open arms among the disciples seated on the ground around him, teaching them to pray.","significance":"Tissot gives the prayer its own scene, a moment of private instruction.","symbolism":"The circle of disciples shows that this is a prayer for a community, \"Our Father.\"","impact":"A widely used image for teaching the Lord's Prayer."}],"tags":["prayer","sermon","teaching"],"orderNote":"Luke gives a shorter form of the prayer on a later occasion, when a disciple asks Jesus to teach them to pray (Luke 11:1–4). Robertson treats that as a separate teaching."},
  {"id":"builders","harmony":"§54c","lane":"jesus","place":"beatitudes","date":"AD 31","title":"The wise and foolish builders","people":["Jesus","The disciples","Great crowds"],"gospels":{"Mt":"7:24–27","Lk":"6:46–49"},"summary":"Jesus closes the sermon with a parable. Whoever hears his words and does them is like a wise man who built his house on the rock: rain, floods and wind beat on it, and it stands. Whoever hears and does not act is like a foolish man who built on sand, and great is its fall. The point is not hearing but doing.","scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 7:24–25","text":"\"Every one then who hears these words of mine and does them will be like a wise man who built his house upon the rock; and the rain fell, and the floods came, and the winds blew and beat upon that house, but it did not fall, because it had been founded on the rock."},{"ref":"Matthew 7:26","text":"And every one who hears these words of mine and does not do them will be like a foolish man who built his house upon the sand;"}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Proverbs 10:25","note":"\"When the tempest passes, the wicked is no more, but the righteous is established for ever.\""}],"art":[],"tags":["parable","sermon","teaching"]},
  {"id":"centurion","harmony":"§55","lane":"jesus","place":"capernaum","from":"beatitudes","date":"AD 31","title":"The centurion's servant healed","people":["Jesus","A centurion of Capernaum","His servant","Jewish elders"],"gospels":{"Mt":"8:5–13","Lk":"7:1–10"},"summary":"Entering Capernaum, Jesus is asked to heal the servant of a centurion, a Gentile officer who, the Jewish elders say, built their synagogue. The centurion sends word that he is not worthy for Jesus to come under his roof: just say the word, and the servant will be healed. Jesus marvels and says he has not found such faith in Israel. The servant is healed at that hour.","journey":"About 2 miles down from the hill to Capernaum.","route":[[35.556,32.881],[35.565,32.882],[35.575,32.881]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 8:8","text":"But the centurion answered him, \"Lord, I am not worthy to have you come under my roof; but only say the word, and my servant will be healed."},{"ref":"Matthew 8:10","text":"When Jesus heard him, he marveled, and said to those who followed him, \"Truly, I say to you, not even in Israel have I found such faith."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/centurion0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jesús_y_el_centurión_(El_Veronés).jpg","title":"Christ and the Centurion","artist":"Paolo Veronese","year":"c. 1571","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Museo del Prado, Madrid","description":"The armored centurion kneels before Christ with his soldiers behind him, while the apostles crowd around Jesus.","significance":"Veronese painted this subject several times; it suited his love of rich costume and grand settings.","symbolism":"A Roman officer kneels to a Jewish teacher: power bowing to faith.","impact":"The centurion's words, \"Lord, I am not worthy,\" are prayed at every Mass, which made the scene especially popular in Catholic art."}],"tags":["miracle","healing"],"orderNote":"Matthew has the centurion come himself; Luke has him send Jewish elders and then friends. The centurion's words are echoed in the Mass before Communion."},
  {"id":"nain","harmony":"§56","lane":"jesus","place":"nain","from":"capernaum","date":"AD 31","title":"The widow's son raised at Nain","people":["Jesus","A widow of Nain","Her only son","The disciples","A great crowd"],"gospels":{"Lk":"7:11–17"},"summary":"As Jesus comes near the gate of Nain with his disciples and a crowd, a funeral procession comes out: the only son of a widow. Seeing her, Jesus has compassion and says, \"Do not weep.\" He touches the bier and tells the young man to arise, and he sits up and begins to speak. Fear seizes everyone, and they glorify God, saying a great prophet has arisen among them.","journey":"About 25 to 30 miles southwest from Capernaum, past Mount Tabor, a day and a half's walk.","route":[[35.575,32.881],[35.549,32.873],[35.515,32.825],[35.47,32.81],[35.42,32.76],[35.39,32.69],[35.35,32.63]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 7:13–14","text":"And when the Lord saw her, he had compassion on her and said to her, \"Do not weep.\" And he came and touched the bier, and the bearers stood still. And he said, \"Young man, I say to you, arise.\""},{"ref":"Luke 7:15–16","text":"And the dead man sat up, and began to speak. And he gave him to his mother. Fear seized them all; and they glorified God, saying, \"A great prophet has arisen among us!\" and \"God has visited his people!\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"1 Kings 17:17–24","note":"Elijah raises the son of the widow of Zarephath and gives him back to his mother."},{"ref":"2 Kings 4:32–37","note":"Elisha raises the Shunammite's son at Shunem, on the other side of the same hill."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/nain0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Resurrection_of_the_Widow's_Son_at_Nain_(La_résurrection_du_fils_de_la_veuve_de_Naïm)_-_James_Tissot_-_overall.jpg","title":"The Resurrection of the Widow's Son at Nain","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"At the town gate Jesus stops the funeral procession, and the young man rises on his bier amid the crowd.","significance":"Tissot shows the open bier used for burial in Palestine.","symbolism":"The mother's grief and her son's return to life sit side by side.","impact":"One of the best-known images of this miracle, which Luke alone records."}],"tags":["miracle","raising"]},
  {"id":"johnsquestion","harmony":"§57","lane":"jesus","place":"nain","date":"AD 31","title":"John's question from prison","people":["Jesus","Two disciples of John","John the Baptist (in prison)","The crowds"],"gospels":{"Mt":"11:2–19","Lk":"7:18–35"},"summary":"John, in prison, hears what Jesus is doing and sends two disciples to ask, \"Are you he who is to come, or shall we look for another?\" Jesus tells them to report what they see: the blind see, lepers are cleansed, the dead are raised, the poor hear good news. Then he praises John to the crowds as more than a prophet, the messenger sent before him. Yet he compares his generation to children who will dance to neither tune.","scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 11:3","text":"and said to him, \"Are you he who is to come, or shall we look for another?\""},{"ref":"Matthew 11:4–5","text":"And Jesus answered them, \"Go and tell John what you hear and see: the blind receive their sight and the lame walk, lepers are cleansed and the deaf hear, and the dead are raised up, and the poor have good news preached to them."},{"ref":"Matthew 11:11","text":"Truly, I say to you, among those born of women there has risen no one greater than John the Baptist; yet he who is least in the kingdom of heaven is greater than he."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Isaiah 35:5–6; 61:1","note":"The eyes of the blind opened and good news preached to the poor."},{"ref":"Malachi 3:1","note":"\"Behold, I send my messenger to prepare the way before me.\""}],"art":[],"tags":["teaching","prophecy"],"orderNote":"Robertson places Jesus \"probably near Nain\" when the messengers come, since Luke ties the question to news of the raising there. John was held at Machaerus, east of the Dead Sea, about 75 miles away."},
  {"id":"cityswoes","harmony":"§58","lane":"jesus","place":"galilee","date":"AD 31","title":"Woes on the lake towns, and \"Come to me\"","people":["Jesus","The crowds"],"gospels":{"Mt":"11:20–30"},"summary":"Jesus reproaches Chorazin, Bethsaida and Capernaum, the towns where most of his mighty works were done, because they did not repent; Tyre, Sidon and even Sodom would have. Then he thanks the Father for revealing these things to babes and says that no one knows the Father except the Son. He ends with an invitation to all who labor and are heavy laden: his yoke is easy and his burden light.","scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 11:21","text":"\"Woe to you, Chorazin! woe to you, Bethsaida! for if the mighty works done in you had been done in Tyre and Sidon, they would have repented long ago in sackcloth and ashes."},{"ref":"Matthew 11:28–30","text":"Come to me, all who labor and are heavy laden, and I will give you rest. Take my yoke upon you, and learn from me; for I am gentle and lowly in heart, and you will find rest for your souls. For my yoke is easy, and my burden is light.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Jeremiah 6:16","note":"\"Walk in it, and find rest for your souls.\""}],"art":[],"tags":["teaching","prophecy","prayer"],"orderNote":"Luke gives the woes later, when the Seventy are sent out (Luke 10:13–15, 21–22). Robertson follows Matthew's setting here and says only \"Galilee.\""},
  {"id":"sinfulwoman","harmony":"§59","lane":"jesus","place":"galilee","date":"AD 31","title":"A sinful woman anoints his feet","people":["Jesus","Simon the Pharisee","A woman of the city, a sinner"],"gospels":{"Lk":"7:36–50"},"summary":"At dinner in the house of Simon, a Pharisee, a woman known as a sinner comes in with an alabaster flask of ointment. Weeping, she wets Jesus' feet with her tears, wipes them with her hair, kisses them and anoints them. Simon thinks a prophet would know what sort of woman she is. Jesus tells the parable of two debtors forgiven, one much and one little, and declares her many sins forgiven, for she loved much.","scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 7:37–38","text":"And behold, a woman of the city, who was a sinner, when she learned that he was sitting at table in the Pharisee's house, brought an alabaster flask of ointment, and standing behind him at his feet, weeping, she began to wet his feet with her tears, and wiped them with the hair of her head, and kissed his feet, and anointed them with the ointment."},{"ref":"Luke 7:47","text":"Therefore I tell you, her sins, which are many, are forgiven, for she loved much; but he who is forgiven little, loves little.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/sinfulwoman0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rubens-Feast_of_Simon_the_Pharisee.jpg","title":"The Feast in the House of Simon the Pharisee","artist":"Peter Paul Rubens","year":"c. 1618–1620","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"State Hermitage Museum, Saint Petersburg","description":"The woman kneels at Christ's feet and wipes them with her hair, while Simon and his guests react with shock and disapproval.","significance":"A large banquet piece by Rubens and his workshop, later bought for Catherine the Great.","symbolism":"The penitent's humility contrasts with the stiff, staring Pharisees; Christ calmly defends her.","impact":"Shows the Counter-Reformation theme of penitence and forgiveness."},{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/sinfulwoman1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Meal_in_the_House_of_the_Pharisee_(Le_repas_chez_le_pharisien)_-_James_Tissot_-_overall.jpg","title":"The Meal in the House of the Pharisee","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"The woman bends over Jesus' feet as he reclines at table, while Simon and the guests look on.","significance":"Tissot shows the custom of reclining at meals, which explains how she could reach his feet.","symbolism":"Her tears and loosened hair stand against the formal setting of the feast.","impact":"Helps readers picture Luke's scene accurately."}],"tags":["parable","teaching"],"orderNote":"Robertson holds this to be distinct from the anointing at Bethany a year later. Western tradition since Gregory the Great identified the woman with Mary Magdalene, but Luke introduces Mary Magdalene as a new figure in the next paragraph; the town is not named."},
  {"id":"secondtour","harmony":"§60","lane":"jesus","place":"galilee","date":"AD 31","title":"The second tour, with the Twelve and the women","people":["Jesus","The Twelve","Mary Magdalene","Joanna, wife of Chuza","Susanna","Many other women"],"gospels":{"Lk":"8:1–3"},"summary":"Jesus goes through cities and villages preaching the kingdom of God, now with the Twelve. Luke notes that women also travel with them: Mary called Magdalene, from whom seven demons had gone out; Joanna, wife of Herod's steward Chuza; Susanna; and many others, who provide for them out of their own means. Several of these women will stand by the cross and come to the empty tomb.","scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 8:1–3","text":"Soon afterward he went on through cities and villages, preaching and bringing the good news of the kingdom of God. And the twelve were with him, and also some women who had been healed of evil spirits and infirmities: Mary, called Magdalene, from whom seven demons had gone out, and Joanna, the wife of Chuza, Herod's steward, and Susanna, and many others, who provided for them out of their means."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/secondtour0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Women_of_Galilee_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"Women of Galilee","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1889","medium":"Drawing on paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Three Galilean women carry water jars on their heads and shoulders.","significance":"Tissot made studies of local people to ground his Gospel scenes in the life of the land.","symbolism":"Recalls the Galilean women who traveled with Jesus and provided for him.","impact":"Tissot's costume studies record village dress of 19th-century Palestine."}],"tags":["teaching"]},
  {"id":"beelzebub","harmony":"§61","lane":"jesus","place":"capernaum","from":"galilee","date":"AD 31","title":"The Beelzebul accusation","people":["Jesus","A blind and mute demoniac","Scribes from Jerusalem","Pharisees","Jesus' family"],"gospels":{"Mt":"12:22–37","Mk":"3:19–30"},"summary":"Jesus heals a blind and mute demoniac, and the crowds ask whether he can be the Son of David. Scribes from Jerusalem say he casts out demons by Beelzebul, the prince of demons. Jesus answers that a kingdom divided against itself cannot stand: if he casts out demons by the Spirit of God, the kingdom has come upon them. He warns that blasphemy against the Holy Spirit will not be forgiven. Meanwhile his family set out to seize him, saying he is beside himself.","journey":"Back to Capernaum; Mark says \"he went home.\"","route":[[35.42,32.8],[35.47,32.81],[35.515,32.825],[35.53,32.85],[35.549,32.873],[35.575,32.881]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 3:21","text":"And when his friends heard it, they went out to seize him, for they said, \"He is beside himself.\""},{"ref":"Matthew 12:28","text":"But if it is by the Spirit of God that I cast out demons, then the kingdom of God has come upon you."},{"ref":"Matthew 12:30","text":"He who is not with me is against me, and he who does not gather with me scatters."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/beelzebub0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Blind_and_Mute_Man_Possessed_by_Devils_(Le_démoniaque_aveugle_et_muet)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"The Blind and Mute Man Possessed by Devils","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"On the steps of a colonnaded building Jesus lays his hand on the blind and mute man, while onlookers crowd around.","significance":"Tissot illustrates the healing that led to the Beelzebul controversy.","symbolism":"The healing is a sign that the kingdom of God has come.","impact":"Part of Tissot's effort to paint every scene of the Gospels."}],"tags":["exorcism","conflict","teaching"],"orderNote":"Luke gives a similar controversy later (Luke 11:14–23); Robertson treats it as a separate occasion. He calls this the start of a \"busy day\" that runs through the parables by the sea and the storm on the lake."},
  {"id":"signjonah","harmony":"§62","lane":"jesus","place":"capernaum","date":"AD 31","title":"The sign of Jonah","people":["Jesus","Scribes and Pharisees"],"gospels":{"Mt":"12:38–45"},"summary":"The same day, scribes and Pharisees ask Jesus for a sign. He answers that an evil and adulterous generation will be given no sign but the sign of Jonah: as Jonah was three days in the belly of the whale, so the Son of man will be three days in the heart of the earth. The men of Nineveh repented at Jonah's preaching, and something greater than Jonah is here.","scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 12:39–40","text":"But he answered them, \"An evil and adulterous generation seeks for a sign; but no sign shall be given to it except the sign of the prophet Jonah. For as Jonah was three days and three nights in the belly of the whale, so will the Son of man be three days and three nights in the heart of the earth."},{"ref":"Matthew 12:41","text":"The men of Nineveh will arise at the judgment with this generation and condemn it; for they repented at the preaching of Jonah, and behold, something greater than Jonah is here."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Jonah 1:17","note":"Jonah in the belly of the fish three days and three nights."},{"ref":"Jonah 3:5","note":"The people of Nineveh believe God and repent."}],"art":[],"tags":["conflict","prophecy","teaching"]},
  {"id":"truekin","harmony":"§63","lane":"jesus","place":"capernaum","date":"AD 31","title":"His mother and brothers","people":["Jesus","Mary his mother","His brothers","The crowd"],"gospels":{"Mt":"12:46–50","Mk":"3:31–35","Lk":"8:19–21"},"summary":"While Jesus is teaching, his mother and brothers come and stand outside, asking for him. He looks around at those seated about him and says, \"Here are my mother and my brothers!\" Whoever does the will of God is his brother and sister and mother. The saying does not reject his family; it opens it to all who do God's will, and Luke presents Mary as the first who heard the word and kept it.","scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 3:33–35","text":"And he replied, \"Who are my mother and my brethren?\" And looking around on those who sat about him, he said, \"Here are my mother and my brethren! Whoever does the will of God is my brother, and sister, and mother.\""}],"art":[],"tags":["teaching"],"orderNote":"Who the \"brothers\" were has been debated since early times: Catholic and Orthodox tradition holds that they were cousins or children of Joseph by an earlier marriage, while many Protestants take them as later children of Mary and Joseph."},
  {"id":"sower","harmony":"§64a","lane":"jesus","place":"tabgha","from":"capernaum","date":"AD 31","title":"The parable of the sower","people":["Jesus","The disciples","A great crowd on the shore"],"gospels":{"Mt":"13:1–23","Mk":"4:1–29","Lk":"8:4–18"},"summary":"So great a crowd gathers by the lake that Jesus sits in a boat and teaches them in parables. A sower's seed falls on the path, on rocky ground, among thorns and on good soil, which yields thirty, sixty and a hundredfold. Later he explains it to the disciples: the seed is the word, and the soils are the ways people hear it. Mark adds a companion parable of seed that grows by itself while the farmer sleeps.","journey":"Out of the house and down to the shore, a short walk.","route":[[35.575,32.881],[35.56,32.877],[35.549,32.873]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 13:3","text":"And he told them many things in parables, saying: \"A sower went out to sow."},{"ref":"Matthew 13:8","text":"Other seeds fell on good soil and brought forth grain, some a hundredfold, some sixty, some thirty."},{"ref":"Mark 4:26–27","text":"And he said, \"The kingdom of God is as if a man should scatter seed upon the ground, and should sleep and rise night and day, and the seed should sprout and grow, he knows not how."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Isaiah 6:9–10","note":"Hearing but not understanding, quoted to explain why he teaches in parables."},{"ref":"Isaiah 55:10–11","note":"Like rain making the earth sprout, God's word does not return empty."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/sower0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pieter_Bruegel_d._Ä._030.jpg","title":"Landscape with the Parable of the Sower","artist":"Pieter Bruegel the Elder","year":"1557","medium":"Oil on panel","location":"Timken Museum of Art, San Diego","description":"A small sower strides across a hillside field in the foreground of a vast landscape of river, towns and mountains.","significance":"One of Bruegel's earliest dated paintings; it shows his gift for the panoramic \"world landscape.\"","symbolism":"The sower is almost lost in the wide world around him, as the word is scattered over every kind of ground.","impact":"The only painting by Bruegel in a public collection in the western United States."},{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/sower1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Sower_(Le_semeur)_-_James_Tissot_-_overall.jpg","title":"The Sower","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"A farmer scatters seed by hand over stony ground.","significance":"Tissot illustrates the parable itself, not only the scene of Jesus telling it.","symbolism":"Seed falls on every kind of ground, as in the parable.","impact":"Part of the parable series within Tissot's Life of Christ."}],"tags":["parable","teaching"],"orderNote":"Robertson calls this the first great group of at least ten parables, all spoken the same day. A cove between Tabgha and Capernaum, sometimes called the Bay of Parables or Sower's Cove, is the traditional setting."},
  {"id":"tares","harmony":"§64b","lane":"jesus","place":"tabgha","date":"AD 31","title":"The parable of the wheat and the tares","people":["Jesus","The disciples","The crowd"],"gospels":{"Mt":"13:24–30, 36–43"},"summary":"An enemy sows weeds among a man's wheat by night. The servants want to pull them up, but the householder says to let both grow together until the harvest, lest the wheat be uprooted too; then the reapers will separate them. In the house, Jesus explains it to the disciples: the field is the world, the good seed the sons of the kingdom, and the harvest the close of the age.","scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 13:24–25","text":"Another parable he put before them, saying, \"The kingdom of heaven may be compared to a man who sowed good seed in his field; but while men were sleeping, his enemy came and sowed weeds among the wheat, and went away."},{"ref":"Matthew 13:30","text":"Let both grow together until the harvest; and at harvest time I will tell the reapers, Gather the weeds first and bind them in bundles to be burned, but gather the wheat into my barn.'\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/tares0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abraham_Bloemaert_-_Parable_of_the_Wheat_and_the_Tares_-_Walters_372505.jpg","title":"Parable of the Wheat and the Tares","artist":"Abraham Bloemaert","year":"1624","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Walters Art Museum, Baltimore","description":"Peasants sleep in the foreground beneath a hut on stilts while the devil, with horns and tail, sows weeds in the field.","significance":"Bloemaert, a leading Utrecht painter, turns the parable into a Dutch peasant scene.","symbolism":"The sleepers are the \"men sleeping\" of the parable: evil spreads while no one watches.","impact":"An example of how Dutch painters set the parables in everyday life."}],"tags":["parable","teaching"],"orderNote":"The \"tares\" are probably darnel, a weed that looks like wheat until it ripens."},
  {"id":"mustardseed","harmony":"§64c","lane":"jesus","place":"tabgha","date":"AD 31","title":"The mustard seed and the leaven","people":["Jesus","The crowd"],"gospels":{"Mt":"13:31–35","Mk":"4:30–34"},"summary":"Two short parables show the kingdom's small beginning and great end. A mustard seed, smallest of seeds, grows into a shrub big enough for birds to nest in its branches. A little leaven hidden in three measures of flour leavens the whole batch. Mark notes that Jesus spoke the word in many such parables, explaining everything privately to his disciples.","scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 13:31–32","text":"Another parable he put before them, saying, \"The kingdom of heaven is like a grain of mustard seed which a man took and sowed in his field; it is the smallest of all seeds, but when it has grown it is the greatest of shrubs and becomes a tree, so that the birds of the air come and make nests in its branches.\""},{"ref":"Matthew 13:33","text":"He told them another parable. \"The kingdom of heaven is like leaven which a woman took and hid in three measures of meal, till it was all leavened.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Ezekiel 17:23; Daniel 4:12","note":"A great tree where birds of every sort nest in its branches."},{"ref":"Psalm 78:2","note":"\"I will open my mouth in a parable,\" quoted in Matthew 13:35."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/mustardseed0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Jesus_Sits_by_the_Seashore_and_Preaches_(Jésus_s'assied_au_bord_de_la_mer_et_prêche)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"Jesus Sits by the Seashore and Preaches","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Jesus sits by the shore of the lake and teaches the crowd gathered around him.","significance":"Tissot shows the setting Matthew gives for the day of parables.","symbolism":"Jesus teaches the crowd with images drawn from fields, kitchens and the lake around them.","impact":"Helps readers place the parables in their lakeside setting."}],"tags":["parable","teaching"]},
  {"id":"treasure","harmony":"§64d","lane":"jesus","place":"capernaum","from":"tabgha","date":"AD 31","title":"The hidden treasure and the pearl","people":["Jesus","The disciples"],"gospels":{"Mt":"13:44–46"},"summary":"Back in the house, Jesus tells the disciples two twin parables. A man finds treasure hidden in a field, hides it again, and in his joy sells all he has to buy that field. A merchant seeking fine pearls finds one of great value and sells everything to buy it. The kingdom is worth everything, and finding it brings joy.","journey":"From the shore back to the house in Capernaum.","route":[[35.549,32.873],[35.56,32.877],[35.575,32.881]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 13:44","text":"\"The kingdom of heaven is like treasure hidden in a field, which a man found and covered up; then in his joy he goes and sells all that he has and buys that field."},{"ref":"Matthew 13:45–46","text":"\"Again, the kingdom of heaven is like a merchant in search of fine pearls, who, on finding one pearl of great value, went and sold all that he had and bought it."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Proverbs 2:4–5","note":"Seek wisdom like silver and search for it as for hidden treasures."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/treasure0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Yelkrokoyade, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Parable_of_the_hidden_treasure_Rembrandt_-_Gerard_Dou.jpg","title":"The Parable of the Hidden Treasure","artist":"Gerrit Dou (formerly attributed to Rembrandt)","year":"c. 1630","medium":"Oil on panel","location":"Museum of Fine Arts, Budapest","description":"A man kneels with his spade beside the hole he has dug, where jars and vessels of treasure lie uncovered.","significance":"Painted when Dou was Rembrandt's pupil in Leiden; it long passed as Rembrandt's own work.","symbolism":"The ordinary laborer and his spade show that the treasure is found in the middle of daily work.","impact":"One of very few paintings of this parable."},{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/treasure1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Pearl_of_Great_Price_(The_Parables_of_Our_Lord_and_Saviour_Jesus_Christ)_MET_DP835752.jpg","title":"The Pearl of Great Price","artist":"John Everett Millais, engraved by the Dalziel Brothers","year":"1864","medium":"Wood engraving","location":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York","description":"A merchant, surrounded by traders, holds a single pearl in his hand.","significance":"From The Parables of Our Lord, a set of twenty illustrations Millais worked on for years.","symbolism":"The merchant's whole attention on one small pearl shows the kingdom outweighing everything else.","impact":"The series is counted among the finest Victorian wood engravings."}],"tags":["parable","teaching"]},
  {"id":"dragnet","harmony":"§64e","lane":"jesus","place":"capernaum","date":"AD 31","title":"The parable of the net","people":["Jesus","The disciples"],"gospels":{"Mt":"13:47–53"},"summary":"The kingdom is like a net thrown into the sea that gathers fish of every kind; when it is full, the fishermen sort the good into vessels and throw away the bad. So it will be at the close of the age. Jesus asks if they have understood, and ends with the householder who brings out of his treasure what is new and what is old.","scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 13:47–48","text":"\"Again, the kingdom of heaven is like a net which was thrown into the sea and gathered fish of every kind; when it was full, men drew it ashore and sat down and sorted the good into vessels but threw away the bad."},{"ref":"Matthew 13:52","text":"And he said to them, \"Therefore every scribe who has been trained for the kingdom of heaven is like a householder who brings out of his treasure what is new and what is old.\""}],"art":[],"tags":["parable","teaching"],"orderNote":"A dragnet, or seine, was pulled between boats or from the shore and caught everything in its path; it was still used on the lake in modern times."},
  {"id":"storm","harmony":"§65","lane":"jesus","place":"seagalilee","from":"capernaum","date":"AD 31","title":"Jesus stills the storm","people":["Jesus","The disciples"],"gospels":{"Mt":"8:18, 23–27","Mk":"4:35–41","Lk":"8:22–25"},"summary":"That evening Jesus says, \"Let us go across to the other side.\" A great storm of wind arises, and the waves break into the boat while he sleeps on a cushion in the stern. The terrified disciples wake him: \"Teacher, do you not care if we perish?\" He rebukes the wind and says to the sea, \"Peace! Be still!\" There is a great calm, and they ask one another, \"Who then is this, that even wind and sea obey him?\"","journey":"Out from Capernaum onto the lake toward the eastern shore, a crossing of about 6 miles.","route":[[35.575,32.881],[35.59,32.865],[35.59,32.83]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 4:38","text":"But he was in the stern, asleep on the cushion; and they woke him and said to him, \"Teacher, do you not care if we perish?\""},{"ref":"Mark 4:39","text":"And he awoke and rebuked the wind, and said to the sea, \"Peace! Be still!\" And the wind ceased, and there was a great calm."},{"ref":"Mark 4:41","text":"And they were filled with awe, and said to one another, \"Who then is this, that even wind and sea obey him?\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Psalm 107:28–29","note":"\"He made the storm be still, and the waves of the sea were hushed.\""},{"ref":"Jonah 1:4–6","note":"Jonah asleep in a ship in a great storm."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/storm0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rembrandt_Christ_in_the_Storm_on_the_Lake_of_Galilee.jpg","title":"The Storm on the Sea of Galilee","artist":"Rembrandt","year":"1633","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Isabella Stewart Gardner Museum, Boston (stolen in 1990)","description":"A fishing boat heels over in a huge wave; some disciples fight the sail and rigging while others wake Christ in the dark stern.","significance":"Rembrandt's only seascape. He includes himself among the passengers, looking out at the viewer.","symbolism":"Light and storm split the canvas in two: chaos at the bow, and Christ calm in the shadow.","impact":"Stolen in the 1990 Gardner Museum heist, the largest unsolved art theft in history; its empty frame still hangs in the museum."},{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/storm1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eugène_Delacroix_-_Christ_on_the_Sea_of_Galilee_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg","title":"Christ on the Sea of Galilee","artist":"Eugène Delacroix","year":"1841","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Nelson-Atkins Museum of Art, Kansas City","description":"A small boat is tossed in a dark green sea; the disciples struggle and plead while Christ sleeps in a pool of light.","significance":"Delacroix painted this subject at least six times, a sign of how much it meant to him.","symbolism":"Christ's peaceful sleep contrasts with the fear and motion of the disciples.","impact":"The Romantic painter's storms influenced later artists, including Van Gogh, who copied Delacroix."}],"tags":["miracle","nature-miracle"],"orderNote":"Matthew places the storm earlier, after the Sermon on the Mount, in a collection of miracles; Robertson follows Mark and Luke, who tie it to the evening of the day of parables."},
  {"id":"gerasene","harmony":"§66","lane":"jesus","place":"kursi","from":"seagalilee","date":"AD 31","title":"The Gerasene demoniac","people":["Jesus","The disciples","A man possessed by \"Legion\"","Swineherds","People of the region"],"gospels":{"Mt":"8:28–34","Mk":"5:1–20","Lk":"8:26–39"},"summary":"On the eastern shore, in Gentile country, a man possessed by many demons comes out from the tombs. No chain can hold him; night and day he cries out and cuts himself with stones. Asked his name, he answers \"Legion.\" Jesus lets the demons enter a great herd of swine, which rush down the steep bank into the lake. The people beg Jesus to leave, but he sends the healed man home to tell how much the Lord has done for him, and he proclaims it in the Decapolis.","journey":"Landing on the eastern shore after the storm.","route":[[35.59,32.83],[35.62,32.828],[35.65,32.826]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 5:9","text":"And Jesus asked him, \"What is your name?\" He replied, \"My name is Legion; for we are many.\""},{"ref":"Mark 5:13","text":"So he gave them leave. And the unclean spirits came out, and entered the swine; and the herd, numbering about two thousand, rushed down the steep bank into the sea, and were drowned in the sea."},{"ref":"Mark 5:19","text":"But he refused, and said to him, \"Go home to your friends, and tell them how much the Lord has done for you, and how he has had mercy on you.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Isaiah 65:4","note":"A rebellious people \"who sit in tombs\" and eat swine's flesh."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/gerasene0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Swine_Driven_into_the_Sea_(Les_porcs_précipités_dans_la_mer)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"The Swine Driven into the Sea","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"In the foreground Jesus holds the healed man, while the herd of swine runs down the rocky slope toward the lake.","significance":"Tissot shows the steep bank at the water's edge that the Gospels describe.","symbolism":"The rushing herd shows the destructive power of \"Legion,\" now gone from the man.","impact":"A dramatic image from the series."}],"tags":["miracle","exorcism"],"orderNote":"The manuscripts read Gerasenes, Gadarenes or Gergesenes. Robertson, following W. M. Thomson, identifies the place with Khersa (Kursi) on the east shore, within the district of Gadara. Matthew speaks of two demoniacs; Mark and Luke tell of one, perhaps the more prominent."},
  {"id":"jairus","harmony":"§67","lane":"jesus","place":"capernaum","from":"kursi","date":"AD 31","title":"Jairus' daughter and the woman who touched his cloak","people":["Jesus","Jairus, a ruler of the synagogue","His twelve-year-old daughter","A woman with a flow of blood","Peter, James and John"],"gospels":{"Mt":"9:18–26","Mk":"5:21–43","Lk":"8:40–56"},"summary":"Back across the lake, Jairus, a ruler of the synagogue, begs Jesus to come and lay hands on his dying daughter. On the way a woman who has suffered from bleeding for twelve years touches his garment and is healed; Jesus turns, finds her and says, \"Your faith has made you well.\" News comes that the girl has died. Jesus takes Peter, James and John and her parents into the room, takes her by the hand and says, \"Talitha cumi,\" and she gets up and walks.","journey":"Back across the lake by boat, about 6 miles.","route":[[35.65,32.826],[35.62,32.85],[35.6,32.87],[35.575,32.881]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 5:28","text":"For she said, \"If I touch even his garments, I shall be made well.\""},{"ref":"Mark 5:34","text":"And he said to her, \"Daughter, your faith has made you well; go in peace, and be healed of your disease.\""},{"ref":"Mark 5:41–42","text":"Taking her by the hand he said to her, \"Talitha cumi\"; which means, \"Little girl, I say to you, arise.\" And immediately the girl got up and walked; for she was twelve years old. And immediately they were overcome with amazement."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Leviticus 15:25","note":"A woman with a long discharge of blood was ritually unclean, and so was whatever she touched."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/jairus0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Voskreshenie_docheri_Iaira_(1871)_by_Vasiliy_Polenov.jpg","title":"The Raising of Jairus' Daughter","artist":"Vasily Polenov","year":"1871","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Research Museum of the Russian Academy of Arts, Saint Petersburg","description":"In a dim room Christ, in a red robe, stretches out his hand toward the girl on her bed, while her mother kneels and others watch from the doorway.","significance":"Polenov's graduation work at the Imperial Academy of Arts, which won him the gold medal and a study trip abroad.","symbolism":"The light falls on the girl and on Christ's hand, the moment of \"Talitha cumi.\"","impact":"Started Polenov's lifelong work on the life of Christ, later seen in his \"Christ and the Sinner\" and Gospel cycle."},{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/jairus1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Woman_with_an_Issue_of_Blood_(L'hémoroïsse)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"The Woman with an Issue of Blood","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"In a narrow street packed with people, a woman stoops low in the crowd to touch the edge of Jesus' cloak.","significance":"Tissot shows the tassels (tzitzit) that Jewish men wore on their garments, which Matthew and Luke mention.","symbolism":"Her hidden touch and Jesus' turning to find her show faith rewarded in public.","impact":"One of the most reproduced images of the series."}],"tags":["miracle","healing","raising"],"orderNote":"Matthew sets this after the feast at Matthew's house; Robertson follows Mark and Luke, judging Matthew not chronological in this part of his Gospel. The town is not named; Capernaum is likely."},
  {"id":"twoblind","harmony":"§68","lane":"jesus","place":"capernaum","date":"AD 31","title":"Two blind men and a mute demoniac","people":["Jesus","Two blind men","A mute man possessed by a demon","Pharisees"],"gospels":{"Mt":"9:27–34"},"summary":"As Jesus goes on, two blind men follow him crying, \"Have mercy on us, Son of David.\" In the house he asks whether they believe he can do this, touches their eyes and says, \"According to your faith be it done to you,\" and their eyes are opened. Then a mute demoniac is brought; the demon is cast out and the man speaks. The crowds marvel, but the Pharisees say he casts out demons by the prince of demons.","scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 9:28–29","text":"When he entered the house, the blind men came to him; and Jesus said to them, \"Do you believe that I am able to do this?\" They said to him, \"Yes, Lord.\" Then he touched their eyes, saying, \"According to your faith be it done to you.\""},{"ref":"Matthew 9:33","text":"And when the demon had been cast out, the dumb man spoke; and the crowds marveled, saying, \"Never was anything like this seen in Israel.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Isaiah 35:5–6","note":"\"Then the eyes of the blind shall be opened... and the tongue of the dumb sing for joy.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/twoblind0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Blind_of_Capernaum_(Les_aveugles_de_Capharnum)_-_James_Tissot_-_overall.jpg","title":"The Blind of Capernaum","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Two blind men kneel with arms raised before Jesus in a stone courtyard.","significance":"Tissot shows the blind beggars common in the towns he visited.","symbolism":"Their cry \"Son of David\" names Jesus as Messiah before others do.","impact":"Part of Tissot's series of healing scenes."},{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/twoblind1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Jesus_Heals_a_Mute_Possessed_Man_(Jésus_guérit_un_possédé_muet)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"Jesus Heals a Mute Possessed Man","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Jesus touches his own lips as he faces the mute man, who begins to speak.","significance":"Tissot illustrates the healing that ends Matthew 9.","symbolism":"The man's restored speech is answered by the crowd's wonder and the Pharisees' accusation.","impact":"Part of Tissot's series of healing scenes."}],"tags":["miracle","healing","exorcism","conflict"]},
  {"id":"nazarethlast","harmony":"§69","lane":"jesus","place":"nazareth","from":"capernaum","date":"AD 31","title":"The last visit to Nazareth","people":["Jesus","The disciples","The people of Nazareth"],"gospels":{"Mt":"13:54–58","Mk":"6:1–6"},"summary":"Jesus returns with his disciples to his own country and teaches in the synagogue on the Sabbath. The people are astonished but take offense: is not this the carpenter, the son of Mary, whose brothers and sisters live among them? Jesus says a prophet is not without honor except in his own country. He could do no mighty work there, except heal a few sick people, and he marveled at their unbelief.","journey":"About 28 miles from Capernaum up through the Arbel pass to Nazareth.","route":[[35.575,32.881],[35.549,32.873],[35.515,32.825],[35.47,32.81],[35.42,32.79],[35.34,32.75],[35.3,32.7]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 6:3","text":"Is not this the carpenter, the son of Mary and brother of James and Joses and Judas and Simon, and are not his sisters here with us?\" And they took offense at him."},{"ref":"Mark 6:5–6","text":"And he could do no mighty work there, except that he laid his hands upon a few sick people and healed them. And he marveled because of their unbelief. And he went about among the villages teaching."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/nazarethlast0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_View_of_Nazareth_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"View of Nazareth","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1889","medium":"Drawing on paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"The small hill town of Nazareth seen across its valley.","significance":"A landscape study made during Tissot's Holy Land travels.","symbolism":"The modest town where Jesus grew up and where he was not honored.","impact":"Records Nazareth as it looked in the 1880s, before modern growth."}],"tags":["teaching","conflict"],"orderNote":"Robertson sees no reason to identify this with the visit in Luke 4:16–30 (§39): that was at the start of the ministry, this near its close, and the details differ. Many scholars take them as one visit."},
  {"id":"twelvesent","harmony":"§70","lane":"jesus","place":"galilee","from":"nazareth","date":"AD 31","title":"The Twelve sent out two by two","people":["Jesus","The Twelve"],"gospels":{"Mt":"9:35–11:1","Mk":"6:6–13","Lk":"9:1–6"},"summary":"Seeing the crowds harassed and helpless like sheep without a shepherd, Jesus sends out the Twelve two by two with authority over unclean spirits. They are to preach that the kingdom of heaven is at hand, heal the sick, take no money or bag, and stay in whatever house welcomes them. He warns them of persecution: he sends them as sheep among wolves. They go out preaching repentance, casting out demons and anointing the sick with oil, while Jesus himself tours the villages.","journey":"A third circuit of the villages of Galilee, while the Twelve go out in pairs.","route":[[35.3,32.7],[35.34,32.75],[35.42,32.8]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 10:7–8","text":"And preach as you go, saying, 'The kingdom of heaven is at hand.' Heal the sick, raise the dead, cleanse lepers, cast out demons. You received without pay, give without pay."},{"ref":"Matthew 10:16","text":"\"Behold, I send you out as sheep in the midst of wolves; so be wise as serpents and innocent as doves."},{"ref":"Mark 6:12–13","text":"So they went out and preached that men should repent. And they cast out many demons, and anointed with oil many that were sick and healed them."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Numbers 27:17","note":"Moses prays that God's people may not be \"as sheep which have no shepherd.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/twelvesent0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Exhortation_to_the_Apostles_(Recommandation_aux_apôtres)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"The Exhortation to the Apostles","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Seated under a tree, Jesus speaks to the Twelve gathered on the ground around him.","significance":"Tissot illustrates the mission discourse of Matthew 10.","symbolism":"The close circle of listeners is about to be scattered two by two.","impact":"Part of Tissot's series on the training of the Twelve."},{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/twelvesent1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_On_Entering_the_House,_Salute_It_(En_entrant_la_maison_salue-la)_-_James_Tissot_-_overall.jpg","title":"On Entering the House, Salute It","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"An apostle stands in an open doorway and touches his forehead in greeting, a companion behind him.","significance":"Tissot illustrates one instruction from the mission charge.","symbolism":"The greeting of peace is the apostles' first gift to each household.","impact":"Shows the Gospel's instructions as everyday actions."}],"tags":["call","teaching","healing"],"orderNote":"Matthew gathers into this charge sayings about persecution that Mark and Luke place later (e.g. Mark 13:9–13). Robertson counts this as the third tour of Galilee."},
  {"id":"herodfears","harmony":"§71a","lane":"herod","place":"tiberias","date":"AD 31","title":"Herod Antipas hears of Jesus","people":["Herod Antipas","His courtiers"],"gospels":{"Mt":"14:1–2","Mk":"6:14–16","Lk":"9:7–9"},"summary":"News of Jesus and of the mighty works done by the Twelve reaches Herod Antipas. Some say John the Baptist has been raised from the dead, others that Jesus is Elijah or one of the prophets. Herod, haunted by his guilt, says, \"John, whom I beheaded, has been raised.\" Luke adds that he sought to see Jesus, a wish fulfilled only at the trial.","stay":"Antipas ruled from his new capital at Tiberias.","scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 6:14","text":"King Herod heard of it; for Jesus' name had become known. Some said, \"John the baptizer has been raised from the dead; that is why these powers are at work in him.\""},{"ref":"Mark 6:16","text":"But when Herod heard of it he said, \"John, whom I beheaded, has been raised.\""},{"ref":"Luke 9:9","text":"Herod said, \"John I beheaded; but who is this about whom I hear such things?\" And he sought to see him."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/herodfears0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Herod_(Hérode)_-_James_Tissot_-_overall.jpg","title":"Herod","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Herod Antipas, in rich robes and a tall headdress, stands with folded arms and a hard stare.","significance":"Tissot gives the tetrarch a portrait of his own, as a figure of worldly power.","symbolism":"His guarded, suspicious look fits the guilty fear of Mark 6:16.","impact":"Part of Tissot's gallery of Gospel characters."}],"tags":["prophecy"],"orderNote":"Robertson places Herod in Tiberias; the Gospels do not say where he was. Jesus soon withdraws beyond Antipas' territory, partly, Robertson thinks, to avoid his jealousy."},
  {"id":"johnbeheaded","harmony":"§71b","lane":"john","place":"machaerus","date":"AD 31","title":"The beheading of John the Baptist","people":["John the Baptist","Herod Antipas","Herodias","The daughter of Herodias (Salome, per Josephus)","Guests of Galilee's court"],"gospels":{"Mt":"14:3–12","Mk":"6:17–29"},"summary":"Mark tells the story in flashback. Herod had arrested John because John told him it was not lawful to have his brother's wife, Herodias. Herod feared John as a holy man and liked to hear him, but Herodias wanted him dead. At Herod's birthday banquet her daughter dances and pleases him, and he swears to give her whatever she asks. Prompted by her mother, she asks for John's head on a platter, and the king, though sorry, sends a guard to behead him in prison. John's disciples take his body and bury it, then go and tell Jesus.","stay":"John was held in prison, at Machaerus according to Josephus, for some months before his death.","scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 6:18","text":"For John said to Herod, \"It is not lawful for you to have your brother's wife.\""},{"ref":"Mark 6:25","text":"And she came in immediately with haste to the king, and asked, saying, \"I want you to give me at once the head of John the Baptist on a platter.\""},{"ref":"Mark 6:27–28","text":"And immediately the king sent a soldier of the guard and gave orders to bring his head. He went and beheaded him in the prison, and brought his head on a platter, and gave it to the girl; and the girl gave it to her mother."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"1 Kings 19:2","note":"Queen Jezebel vows to kill the prophet Elijah, as Herodias seeks John's life."},{"ref":"Esther 5:3","note":"\"It shall be given you, even to the half of my kingdom.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/johnbeheaded0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_decapitación_de_San_Juan_Bautista,_por_Caravaggio.jpg","title":"The Beheading of Saint John the Baptist","artist":"Caravaggio","year":"1608","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Oratory of St John's Co-Cathedral, Valletta, Malta","description":"In a prison courtyard the executioner kneels over John's body and draws a knife, while a young woman holds out a golden platter and two prisoners watch through a barred window.","significance":"Caravaggio's largest painting and the only one he signed, in the blood flowing from John's neck.","symbolism":"The vast empty wall and dim light make the killing feel bleak and ordinary; the prisoners at the window are witnesses for us.","impact":"Painted for the Knights of Malta while Caravaggio was a member of the order; it is considered one of the masterpieces of Western painting."},{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/johnbeheaded1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Daughter_of_Herodias_Dancing_(Hérodiade_dansant)_-_James_Tissot_-_overall.jpg","title":"The Daughter of Herodias Dancing","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Herodias' daughter bends backward in an acrobatic dance on the banquet table before Herod and his guests, while harpists play below and armed guards stand by.","significance":"Tissot imagines the court of Antipas from his study of Eastern palaces and dress.","symbolism":"The dance that pleases the king leads to the oath and John's death.","impact":"One of the most striking images in Tissot's series, often reproduced."}],"tags":[],"orderNote":"Mark places the story as a flashback; John probably died some time before Herod heard of the Twelve's mission. Josephus (Antiquities 18.5.2) names Machaerus and says Herod killed John fearing an uprising; he names Herodias' daughter as Salome. Some think the banquet was held at Tiberias, though the head was then brought from Machaerus, a long way off."},
  {"id":"feed5000","harmony":"§72","lane":"jesus","place":"bethsaida","from":"capernaum","date":"Spring AD 32, near Passover","title":"The feeding of the five thousand","people":["Jesus","The Twelve","Philip","Andrew","A boy with five loaves and two fish","About 5,000 men, besides women and children"],"gospels":{"Mt":"14:13–21","Mk":"6:30–44","Lk":"9:10–17","Jn":"6:1–14"},"summary":"The Twelve return from their mission, and Jesus takes them by boat to a lonely place near Bethsaida to rest. The crowds run ahead around the shore. Jesus has compassion on them, \"like sheep without a shepherd,\" and teaches them. Toward evening he takes five barley loaves and two fish, gives thanks, and the disciples feed about five thousand men; twelve baskets of pieces are left over. It is the only miracle, apart from the Resurrection, told in all four Gospels.","stay":"A rest away from the crowds was the purpose of the trip; Jesus and the Twelve spent the day on the grassy hillside with the crowd.","journey":"About 4 to 5 miles by boat across the north end of the lake; the crowds walked around the shore, about 8 to 10 miles.","route":[[35.575,32.881],[35.6,32.875],[35.625,32.885],[35.63,32.91]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 6:34","text":"As he landed he saw a great throng, and he had compassion on them, because they were like sheep without a shepherd; and he began to teach them many things."},{"ref":"John 6:9","text":"\"There is a lad here who has five barley loaves and two fish; but what are they among so many?\""},{"ref":"Matthew 14:19–20","text":"Then he ordered the crowds to sit down on the grass; and taking the five loaves and the two fish he looked up to heaven, and blessed, and broke and gave the loaves to the disciples, and the disciples gave them to the crowds. And they all ate and were satisfied. And they took up twelve baskets full of the broken pieces left over."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"2 Kings 4:42–44","note":"Elisha feeds a hundred men with twenty barley loaves, and some is left over."},{"ref":"Exodus 16","note":"God feeds Israel with manna in the wilderness."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","img":"img/feed50000.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mosaic_of_loaves_%26_fishes_at_Tabgha_LOC_matpc.04154.jpg","title":"Loaves and fishes mosaic, Church of the Multiplication","artist":"Byzantine mosaicist (photographed by the Matson Photo Service)","year":"5th century (photo c. 1934–1939)","medium":"Stone mosaic floor; black-and-white photograph","location":"Church of the Multiplication, Tabgha, Sea of Galilee (photo: Library of Congress)","description":"A basket of loaves flanked by two fish, set in the floor before the altar, photographed soon after the mosaic was uncovered in the 1930s.","significance":"The church at Tabgha marks the pilgrim tradition of the feeding of the five thousand; the pilgrim Egeria described a stone altar here in the late 4th century.","symbolism":"Only four loaves are visible in the basket: the fifth, many suggest, is the Eucharistic bread on the altar above it.","impact":"Now one of the best-known images of Christian art in the Holy Land, reproduced on countless souvenirs and pottery from Galilee."},{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/feed50001.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Miracle_of_the_Loaves_and_Fishes_(La_multiplication_des_pains)_by_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"The Miracle of the Loaves and Fishes","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Thousands sit in rows along the green terraces of a steep hillside above a valley, while the disciples carry baskets of bread down the lines.","significance":"Shows the scale of the miracle: Mark 6:39–40 says the people sat down \"in groups, by hundreds and by fifties\" on the green grass.","symbolism":"The ordered ranks recall Israel's camp in the wilderness, fed with manna; the baskets moving through the crowd point to the twelve baskets left over.","impact":"One of Tissot's grandest panoramas, and a widely reproduced image of the feeding."}],"tags":["miracle","nature-miracle"],"orderNote":"Luke 9:10 sets the miracle near Bethsaida, which Robertson takes as Bethsaida Julias on the northeast shore; Mark 6:45 then sends the disciples \"to Bethsaida,\" which Robertson thinks was a second, western Bethsaida near Capernaum (others read it as the same town). Pilgrim tradition since the 4th century has honored the miracle at Tabgha on the northwest shore, where the Church of the Multiplication stands."},
  {"id":"notking","harmony":"§73","lane":"jesus","place":"bethsaida","date":"Spring AD 32","title":"Jesus refuses to be made king","people":["Jesus","The Twelve","The crowd"],"gospels":{"Mt":"14:22–23","Mk":"6:45–46","Jn":"6:14–15"},"summary":"After the meal the crowd hails Jesus as \"the prophet who is to come\" and wants to seize him and make him king. He makes the disciples get into the boat at once and sends the crowd away. Then he goes up into the hills alone to pray. He will not be the political Messiah they hoped for.","stay":"Jesus spent the evening and much of the night alone on the hillside in prayer.","scripture":[{"ref":"John 6:14–15","text":"When the people saw the sign which he had done, they said, \"This is indeed the prophet who is to come into the world!\" Perceiving then that they were about to come and take him by force to make him king, Jesus withdrew again to the hills by himself."},{"ref":"Mark 6:46","text":"And after he had taken leave of them, he went up into the hills to pray."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Deuteronomy 18:15","note":"Moses promises that God will raise up a prophet like him; the crowd thinks of this \"prophet who is to come.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/notking0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_People_Seek_Jesus_to_Make_Him_King_(On_cherche_J%C3%A9sus_pour_le_faire_roi)_-_James_Tissot_-_overall.jpg","title":"The People Seek Jesus to Make Him King","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Crowds clamber over the rocky hillside above the lake, searching for Jesus. The Sea of Galilee and the plain stretch out far below.","significance":"Tissot traveled in Palestine in 1886–87 and again in 1889 to sketch landscapes, and the wide view of the lake comes from those studies.","symbolism":"Jesus himself is absent from the picture: the crowd hunts for a king they cannot find.","impact":"Part of Tissot's 350-scene Life of Christ, a sensation when first shown in Paris in 1894 and widely reproduced in Bibles."},{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/notking1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Jesus_Goes_Up_Alone_onto_a_Mountain_to_Pray_(J%C3%A9sus_monte_seul_sur_une_montagne_pour_prier)_-_James_Tissot_-_overall.jpg","title":"Jesus Goes Up Alone onto a Mountain to Pray","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Jesus stands alone on a rocky summit under a starry night sky and a crescent moon, arms spread wide in prayer.","significance":"Illustrates Matthew 14:23 and Mark 6:46: after sending the crowd away, he goes up alone to pray.","symbolism":"The open arms and the empty dark around him show the solitude he sought after refusing a worldly crown.","impact":"One of the most reproduced images of Tissot's series for its simple, striking composition."}],"tags":["prayer"]},
  {"id":"walkonwater","harmony":"§74","lane":"jesus","place":"seagalilee","from":"bethsaida","date":"Spring AD 32, night","title":"Jesus walks on the water","people":["Jesus","Peter","The Twelve"],"gospels":{"Mt":"14:24–33","Mk":"6:47–52","Jn":"6:16–21"},"summary":"The disciples row against a strong wind for hours. In the fourth watch of the night, between 3 and 6 a.m., Jesus comes to them walking on the sea, and they cry out in fear, thinking he is a ghost. He says, \"Take heart, it is I.\" Matthew adds that Peter walks on the water toward him, begins to sink when he sees the wind, and is caught by Jesus' hand. When Jesus gets into the boat the wind stops, and they worship him as the Son of God.","journey":"Jesus came out to the boat in the middle of the lake; John says they had rowed about three or four miles.","route":[[35.63,32.91],[35.625,32.88],[35.61,32.855],[35.59,32.83]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 14:27","text":"But immediately he spoke to them, saying, \"Take heart, it is I; have no fear.\""},{"ref":"Matthew 14:28–31","text":"And Peter answered him, \"Lord, if it is you, bid me come to you on the water.\" He said, \"Come.\" So Peter got out of the boat and walked on the water and came to Jesus; but when he saw the wind, he was afraid, and beginning to sink he cried out, \"Lord, save me.\" Jesus immediately reached out his hand and caught him, saying to him, \"O man of little faith, why did you doubt?\""},{"ref":"Matthew 14:33","text":"And those in the boat worshiped him, saying, \"Truly you are the Son of God.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Job 9:8","note":"God alone \"trampled the waves of the sea.\""},{"ref":"Exodus 3:14","note":"\"It is I\" (literally \"I am\") echoes the divine name revealed to Moses."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/walkonwater0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Jesus_Walks_on_the_Sea_(J%C3%A9sus_marche_sur_la_mer)_-_James_Tissot_-_overall.jpg","title":"Jesus Walks on the Sea","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Jesus, glowing white against the night, walks barefoot toward the viewer across dark, rippling water under a crescent moon, his hands raised.","significance":"Illustrates Matthew 14:25–27 from the disciples' point of view in the boat: a figure coming toward them across the waves.","symbolism":"The ghostly halo of light explains why the disciples cried out in fear, thinking he was a ghost; the raised hands echo his words \"Take heart, it is I.\"","impact":"One of Tissot's most haunting images, a companion to his picture of Peter sinking."},{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/walkonwater1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Saint_Peter_Walks_on_the_Sea_(Saint_Pierre_marche_sur_la_mer)_-_James_Tissot_-_overall.jpg","title":"Saint Peter Walks on the Sea","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Peter, sinking to his waist in the dark water, throws up his hands. Jesus, in white, stands on the waves and reaches out to him; the disciples watch from the boat by lantern light under the moon.","significance":"Illustrates Matthew 14:30–31, the moment Peter begins to sink and cries, \"Lord, save me.\"","symbolism":"The glowing white figure of Christ against the black night stands for faith's anchor in the storm; Peter's raised hands are a picture of every desperate prayer.","impact":"Tissot's night scenes on the lake are among the most dramatic of his Life of Christ series."}],"tags":["miracle","nature-miracle"]},
  {"id":"gennesaretheal","harmony":"§75","lane":"jesus","place":"gennesaret","from":"seagalilee","date":"Spring AD 32","title":"Healings at Gennesaret","people":["Jesus","The Twelve","The sick of the region"],"gospels":{"Mt":"14:34–36","Mk":"6:53–56"},"summary":"The boat comes to land at Gennesaret, and people recognize Jesus at once. They carry the sick on mats to wherever they hear he is. In villages, cities and countryside they lay the sick in the market places and beg to touch even the fringe of his garment, and all who touch it are made well.","journey":"About 3 to 4 miles across the water to the western shore.","route":[[35.59,32.83],[35.56,32.84],[35.53,32.85]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 6:55–56","text":"and ran about the whole neighborhood and began to bring sick people on their pallets to any place where they heard he was. And wherever he came, in villages, cities, or country, they laid the sick in the market places, and besought him that they might touch even the fringe of his garment; and as many as touched it were made well."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Numbers 15:38–40","note":"The fringes (tassels) on the garment that every Israelite wore, which the sick reached out to touch."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/gennesaretheal0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_In_the_Villages_the_Sick_Were_Presented_to_Him_(Dans_les_villages_on_lui_pr%C3%A9sentait_des_malades)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"In the Villages the Sick Were Presented to Him","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Jesus walks down a narrow stepped street under a stone arch. The sick, the old and mothers with children crowd the steps on both sides, and he bends to touch a man leaning on a staff.","significance":"Matches Mark 6:56: \"wherever he came, in villages, cities, or country, they laid the sick in the market places.\"","symbolism":"The steep, enclosed street funnels every sufferer toward Christ; his stooping posture shows a healer who comes down to meet them.","impact":"Tissot drew such streets from life in the old towns of Palestine, so the setting feels like a real village rather than an idealized one."}],"tags":["healing"]},
  {"id":"breadoflife","harmony":"§76","lane":"jesus","place":"capernaum","from":"gennesaret","date":"Spring AD 32, near Passover","title":"The Bread of Life discourse","people":["Jesus","The crowd","\"The Jews\" in the synagogue","Peter","The Twelve","Judas Iscariot"],"gospels":{"Jn":"6:22–71"},"summary":"The crowd finds Jesus in Capernaum the next day, hoping for more bread. In the synagogue he tells them he is the bread of life come down from heaven, and that whoever eats his flesh and drinks his blood has eternal life. Many disciples find this a hard saying and stop following him. Jesus asks the Twelve whether they too will go, and Peter answers, \"Lord, to whom shall we go? You have the words of eternal life.\" Jesus warns that one of them is a devil, meaning Judas.","journey":"About 3 miles along the shore from Gennesaret to Capernaum.","route":[[35.53,32.85],[35.55,32.87],[35.575,32.881]],"scripture":[{"ref":"John 6:35","text":"Jesus said to them, \"I am the bread of life; he who comes to me shall not hunger, and he who believes in me shall never thirst."},{"ref":"John 6:53","text":"So Jesus said to them, \"Truly, truly, I say to you, unless you eat the flesh of the Son of man and drink his blood, you have no life in you;"},{"ref":"John 6:66–68","text":"After this many of his disciples drew back and no longer went about with him. Jesus said to the twelve, \"Will you also go away?\" Simon Peter answered him, \"Lord, to whom shall we go? You have the words of eternal life;"}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Exodus 16:4","note":"\"I will rain bread from heaven for you\": the manna the crowd raises (John 6:31)."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/breadoflife0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_You_Follow_Me_for_the_Miracles_(Vous_me_suivez_pour_des_miracles)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"You Follow Me for the Miracles","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Jesus stands on a rocky, flowered slope above the lake and speaks to people who have just come ashore by boat to find him.","significance":"Illustrates John 6:26: \"you seek me, not because you saw signs, but because you ate your fill of the loaves.\"","symbolism":"The boats pulled up below show how hard the crowd worked to reach him; his raised hands turn them from bread that perishes to the bread of life.","impact":"One of the few images of the opening of the Bread of Life discourse, a scene painters rarely attempted."}],"tags":["teaching","conflict"],"orderNote":"John 6:59 places the discourse in the synagogue at Capernaum. Robertson calls this the collapse of the Galilean campaign: from here the crowds fall away and Jesus turns to training the Twelve."},
  {"id":"traditions","harmony":"§77","lane":"jesus","place":"capernaum","date":"Spring AD 32, after Passover","title":"The tradition of the elders: what defiles","people":["Jesus","Pharisees and scribes from Jerusalem","The disciples","Peter"],"gospels":{"Mt":"15:1–20","Mk":"7:1–23","Jn":"7:1"},"summary":"Pharisees and scribes come from Jerusalem and complain that Jesus' disciples eat without the ritual washing of hands required by the tradition of the elders. Jesus answers with Isaiah's words about honoring God with the lips while the heart is far away, and shows how their tradition can set aside God's commandment to honor father and mother. Then he tells the crowd that nothing going into a person defiles him, but what comes out of the heart. Mark adds, \"Thus he declared all foods clean.\"","stay":"Robertson places this probably in Capernaum. John 7:1 notes that Jesus now stayed in Galilee, because the Judeans sought to kill him.","scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 7:6–7","text":"And he said to them, \"Well did Isaiah prophesy of you hypocrites, as it is written, 'This people honors me with their lips, but their heart is far from me; in vain do they worship me, teaching as doctrines the precepts of men.'"},{"ref":"Mark 7:15","text":"there is nothing outside a man which by going into him can defile him; but the things which come out of a man are what defile him.\""},{"ref":"Mark 7:20–21","text":"And he said, \"What comes out of a man is what defiles a man. For from within, out of the heart of man, come evil thoughts, fornication, theft, murder, adultery,"}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Isaiah 29:13","note":"\"This people draw near with their mouth and honor me with their lips, while their hearts are far from me.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/traditions0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Pharisees_Question_Jesus_(Les_pharisiens_questionnent_J%C3%A9sus)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"The Pharisees Question Jesus","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"In a colonnaded court, Jesus stands with arm outstretched, answering a knot of bearded Pharisees and scribes in rich robes, while ordinary people sit on mats around them.","significance":"A general scene of Jesus disputing with the Pharisees, fitting the challenge over the tradition of the elders in Mark 7 and Matthew 15.","symbolism":"The plain white robe of Jesus stands out against the layered, ornate dress of the religious experts, echoing his point that what matters is the heart, not outward show.","impact":"Tissot's crowded debate scenes helped generations of readers picture the arguments of the Gospels as real, public encounters."}],"tags":["conflict","teaching"]},
  {"id":"syrophoenician","harmony":"§78","lane":"jesus","place":"tyre","from":"capernaum","date":"Summer AD 32","title":"The Syrophoenician woman's daughter","people":["Jesus","The disciples","A Greek woman of Syrophoenician birth (\"a Canaanite woman\")","Her daughter"],"gospels":{"Mt":"15:21–28","Mk":"7:24–30"},"summary":"Jesus withdraws to the region of Tyre and Sidon and tries to stay hidden in a house. A Gentile woman begs him to drive a demon out of her daughter. He tests her: it is not right to take the children's bread and throw it to the dogs. She answers that even the dogs eat the crumbs that fall from their master's table. Jesus praises her great faith, and her daughter is healed from a distance.","stay":"Jesus stayed in a house in the region of Tyre and did not want anyone to know (Mark 7:24).","journey":"About 35 to 40 miles northwest through the hills of Upper Galilee to the coast.","route":[[35.575,32.881],[35.55,32.96],[35.49,33.05],[35.4,33.13],[35.3,33.2],[35.2,33.27]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 7:28–29","text":"But she answered him, \"Yes, Lord; yet even the dogs under the table eat the children's crumbs.\" And he said to her, \"For this saying you may go your way; the demon has left your daughter.\""},{"ref":"Matthew 15:27–28","text":"She said, \"Yes, Lord, yet even the dogs eat the crumbs that fall from their master's table.\" Then Jesus answered her, \"O woman, great is your faith! Be it done for you as you desire.\" And her daughter was healed instantly."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"1 Kings 17:8–24","note":"Elijah is sent to a widow of Zarephath, near Sidon, and raises her son: Jesus himself cited it (Luke 4:26)."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/syrophoenician0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jean-Germain_Drouais_-_The_Woman_of_Canaan_at_the_Feet_of_Christ_-_WGA06696.jpg","title":"The Woman of Canaan at the Feet of Christ","artist":"Jean-Germain Drouais","year":"1784","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Musée du Louvre, Paris","description":"The woman kneels with clasped hands before Jesus, who reaches toward her, while an older apostle presses forward to send her away. A Gentile city with a pyramid-like tower rises behind.","significance":"Drouais, a 20-year-old pupil of Jacques-Louis David, won the Prix de Rome with this painting in 1784.","symbolism":"The apostle's gesture of dismissal (Matthew 15:23: \"Send her away\") contrasts with Christ's open hand; the foreign architecture marks her as an outsider.","impact":"A celebrated example of early Neoclassical painting. Drouais died in Rome only four years later, aged 24."},{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/syrophoenician1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Canaanite's_Daughter_(La_Chanan%C3%A9enne)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"The Canaanite's Daughter","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Jesus stands beneath a stone arch and stretches out his hand toward a group of women in red robes and jeweled headdresses who kneel and plead behind a low wall.","significance":"Illustrates Matthew 15:21–28. Tissot dressed the women in the costume he observed among local women of the region.","symbolism":"The low wall between them stands for the barrier between Jew and Gentile that the woman's faith crosses.","impact":"Shows the scene in a real Near Eastern street rather than a classical setting, in contrast to Drouais."}],"tags":["healing","exorcism","miracle"],"orderNote":"Robertson notes it was once questioned whether Jesus actually left the land of Israel; Matthew's wording and the best text of Mark 7:31 (\"through Sidon\") show that he did. This is the only time the Gospels record him outside the Jewish homeland, apart from the flight to Egypt."},
  {"id":"ephphatha","harmony":"§79a","lane":"jesus","place":"decapolis","from":"tyre","date":"Summer AD 32","title":"\"Ephphatha\": the deaf man in the Decapolis","people":["Jesus","The disciples","A deaf man with a speech impediment","Crowds with the lame, blind and maimed"],"gospels":{"Mt":"15:29–31","Mk":"7:31–37"},"summary":"Jesus goes north through Sidon and then around into the Decapolis, east of the lake. People bring him a deaf man who can hardly speak. Jesus takes him aside, puts his fingers in his ears, touches his tongue, looks up to heaven, sighs, and says \"Ephphatha,\" Aramaic for \"Be opened.\" Matthew tells of great crowds bringing the lame, blind and maimed to him on a mountain, and they glorify the God of Israel.","journey":"A long loop of about 100 miles: north up the coast to Sidon, east across the hills toward Hermon, and south through Philip's territory into the Decapolis, keeping out of Herod Antipas' land.","route":[[35.2,33.27],[35.3,33.45],[35.37,33.56],[35.5,33.45],[35.6,33.33],[35.69,33.25],[35.7,33.05],[35.67,32.85],[35.75,32.74],[35.85,32.65]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 7:33–35","text":"And taking him aside from the multitude privately, he put his fingers into his ears, and he spat and touched his tongue; and looking up to heaven, he sighed, and said to him, \"Ephphatha,\" that is, \"Be opened.\" And his ears were opened, his tongue was released, and he spoke plainly."},{"ref":"Mark 7:37","text":"And they were astonished beyond measure, saying, \"He has done all things well; he even makes the deaf hear and the dumb speak.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Isaiah 35:5–6","note":"\"The ears of the deaf unstopped... and the tongue of the dumb sing for joy.\" Mark's rare Greek word for the man's speech defect (7:32) appears elsewhere only in the Greek of this verse."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/ephphatha0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Jesus_Heals_the_Blind_and_Lame_on_the_Mountain_(Sur_la_montagne_J%C3%A9sus_gu%C3%A9rit_les_aveugles_et_les_boiteux)_-_James_Tissot_-_overall.jpg","title":"Jesus Heals the Blind and Lame on the Mountain","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"On a wooded slope high above the lake, people lead the sick and lame up the path to Jesus, who lays his hands on a kneeling man while the disciples stand by.","significance":"Illustrates Matthew 15:29–31, Matthew's account of this visit: great crowds bring the lame, maimed, blind and dumb to him on the mountain by the sea.","symbolism":"The long line of people winding up from the shore shows how far the news had spread among the Gentile towns.","impact":"Tissot's view from the eastern heights over the water reflects his own sketches made by the lake."}],"tags":["healing","miracle"],"orderNote":"Robertson stresses that each of the withdrawals keeps out of Herod Antipas' territory and goes into Gentile, Greek-speaking districts."},
  {"id":"feed4000","harmony":"§79b","lane":"jesus","place":"decapolis","date":"Summer AD 32","title":"The feeding of the four thousand","people":["Jesus","The disciples","About 4,000 people, besides women and children"],"gospels":{"Mt":"15:32–38","Mk":"8:1–9"},"summary":"A great crowd has been with Jesus three days in a lonely place and has nothing left to eat. He has compassion on them, for some have come a long way. With seven loaves and a few small fish he feeds about four thousand, and seven large baskets of pieces are left. Many of this crowd were probably Gentiles of the Decapolis.","stay":"The crowd stayed with Jesus for three days (Mark 8:2).","scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 8:2","text":"\"I have compassion on the crowd, because they have been with me now three days, and have nothing to eat;"},{"ref":"Mark 8:6","text":"And he commanded the crowd to sit down on the ground; and he took the seven loaves, and having given thanks he broke them and gave them to his disciples to set before the people; and they set them before the crowd."},{"ref":"Mark 8:8","text":"And they ate, and were satisfied; and they took up the broken pieces left over, seven baskets full."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/feed40000.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jacopo_Tintoretto_-_The_Miracle_of_the_Loaves_and_Fishes_-_WGA22566.jpg","title":"The Miracle of the Loaves and Fishes","artist":"Jacopo Tintoretto","year":"1579–1581","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Scuola Grande di San Rocco, Venice (upper hall)","description":"On a dark hillside Christ hands bread to a disciple while a youth holds a basket. Below, a crowd of men, women and children reclines and reaches for food under a stormy, glowing sky.","significance":"Part of Tintoretto's vast cycle for the confraternity of San Rocco, which cared for the poor and sick. The painting does not specify which of the two feedings it shows; it stands here for the meal in the Decapolis.","symbolism":"The ceiling cycle above shows God feeding Israel in the wilderness, so this picture of bread for a hungry crowd answers the manna overhead.","impact":"The San Rocco cycle, painted over more than twenty years, is often called Venice's answer to the Sistine Chapel."}],"tags":["miracle","nature-miracle"],"orderNote":"Some scholars think this is a second telling of the feeding of the five thousand. Robertson rejects that: Mark and Matthew report both, and both report Jesus himself referring to the two feedings (Mark 8:19–20; Matthew 16:9–10). Some pilgrim traditions set it at Tabgha, but Mark's order places it in the Decapolis."},
  {"id":"magadan","harmony":"§80","lane":"jesus","place":"magdala","from":"decapolis","date":"Summer AD 32","title":"The Pharisees and Sadducees demand a sign","people":["Jesus","The disciples","Pharisees","Sadducees"],"gospels":{"Mt":"15:39–16:4","Mk":"8:10–12"},"summary":"Jesus crosses by boat to Magadan (Mark: Dalmanutha) on the Galilean side. At once Pharisees, now joined by Sadducees, come to test him by asking for a sign from heaven. He sighs deeply and tells them they can read the sky for the weather but not the signs of the times. No sign will be given to this generation except the sign of Jonah. Then he leaves them.","journey":"About 12 to 15 miles from the Decapolis hills down to the eastern shore and across the lake by boat.","route":[[35.85,32.65],[35.72,32.74],[35.64,32.8],[35.58,32.82],[35.515,32.825]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 16:2–4","text":"He answered them, \"When it is evening, you say, 'It will be fair weather; for the sky is red.' And in the morning, 'It will be stormy today, for the sky is red and threatening.' You know how to interpret the appearance of the sky, but you cannot interpret the signs of the times. An evil and adulterous generation seeks for a sign, but no sign shall be given to it except the sign of Jonah.\" So he left them and departed."},{"ref":"Mark 8:12","text":"And he sighed deeply in his spirit, and said, \"Why does this generation seek a sign? Truly, I say to you, no sign shall be given to this generation.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Jonah 1:17","note":"Jonah three days in the belly of the fish, which Jesus elsewhere links to his own death and rising (Matthew 12:40)."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/magadan0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Pharisees_and_the_Saduccees_Come_to_Tempt_Jesus_(Les_pharisiens_et_les_saduc%C3%A9ens_viennent_pour_tenter_J%C3%A9sus)_-_James_Tissot_-_overall.jpg","title":"The Pharisees and the Saduccees Come to Tempt Jesus","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Jesus sits with his disciples on a stone bench under a great spreading tree. A richly robed group of Pharisees and Sadducees stands before him, one with hands on hips.","significance":"Illustrates Matthew 16:1, the first time the Gospel shows the two rival parties joining forces against Jesus.","symbolism":"The calm, seated Christ, palm open, contrasts with the stiff, demanding posture of the questioners who want a sign.","impact":"Tissot took care to dress the two groups differently, a sign of the archaeological detail he aimed for throughout the series."},{"kind":"Artifact","img":"img/magadan1.jpg","credit":"Photo: Hanay, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Magdala_Stone.jpg","title":"The Magdala stone","artist":"Unknown Jewish stone carver","year":"1st century AD (photo 2012)","medium":"Carved limestone block","location":"Found in the synagogue at Magdala; photographed on display at the site","description":"A low, footed limestone block carved with arches, rosettes and other decoration, shown in a glass case.","significance":"Found in 2009 in the 1st-century synagogue at Magdala, a town where Jesus may well have taught. One face bears one of the earliest known carvings of the Temple menorah.","symbolism":"Scholars read its carvings as a miniature of the Jerusalem Temple, linking this lakeside synagogue with worship in Jerusalem.","impact":"One of the most important finds for picturing the Galilean synagogues of Jesus' day."}],"tags":["conflict"],"orderNote":"The site of Magadan and Dalmanutha is unknown. Robertson notes that early copyists changed it to the familiar Magdala; he also notes this is the first time the Sadducees appear with the Pharisees against Jesus."},
  {"id":"bethsaidablind","harmony":"§81","lane":"jesus","place":"bethsaida","from":"magdala","date":"Summer AD 32","title":"The leaven of the Pharisees; the blind man of Bethsaida","people":["Jesus","The disciples","A blind man of Bethsaida"],"gospels":{"Mt":"16:5–12","Mk":"8:13–26"},"summary":"Crossing the lake, the disciples find they have only one loaf. Jesus warns them against the leaven of the Pharisees and of Herod, and they think he means bread. He rebukes their dullness: have they forgotten the five thousand and the four thousand? At Bethsaida people bring him a blind man. Jesus leads him out of the village, spits on his eyes and lays hands on him; at first the man sees people \"like trees, walking,\" and after a second touch he sees clearly. It is the only miracle in the Gospels done in two stages.","journey":"About 8 miles by boat across the north end of the lake to Philip's territory.","route":[[35.515,32.825],[35.55,32.85],[35.6,32.875],[35.63,32.91]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 8:15","text":"And he cautioned them, saying, \"Take heed, beware of the leaven of the Pharisees and the leaven of Herod.\""},{"ref":"Mark 8:23–25","text":"And he took the blind man by the hand, and led him out of the village; and when he had spit on his eyes and laid his hands upon him, he asked him, \"Do you see anything?\" And he looked up and said, \"I see men; but they look like trees, walking.\" Then again he laid his hands upon his eyes; and he looked intently and was restored, and saw everything clearly."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/bethsaidablind0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Lake_of_Gennesaret_near_the_Site_of_Bethsaida_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"The Lake of Gennesaret near the Site of Bethsaida","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Pen and ink over graphite on paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"A pen sketch of the lake shore: rocks and reeds in the shallows, and a low hill with a few huts and trees rising behind.","significance":"Tissot drew it on his travels in the 1880s to record the setting of Bethsaida, where Jesus led the blind man out of the village (Mark 8:23).","symbolism":"The empty shore reminds us that Jesus, under Antipas' threat, was now working away from the crowds of Galilee.","impact":"Tissot's landscape studies gave his Life of Christ its sense of real places; the site of Bethsaida is still debated today."}],"tags":["healing","miracle","teaching"]},
  {"id":"confession","harmony":"§82","lane":"jesus","place":"caesareaphil","from":"bethsaida","date":"Summer AD 32","title":"Peter's confession at Caesarea Philippi","people":["Jesus","Simon Peter","The Twelve"],"gospels":{"Mt":"16:13–20","Mk":"8:27–30","Lk":"9:18–21"},"summary":"In the villages of Caesarea Philippi, after praying alone, Jesus asks the disciples who people say he is: John the Baptist, Elijah, Jeremiah or one of the prophets. Then he asks, \"But who do you say that I am?\" Peter answers, \"You are the Christ, the Son of the living God.\" Jesus calls him blessed, names him Peter, the rock on which he will build his Church, and gives him the keys of the kingdom of heaven. He tells them to tell no one that he is the Christ.","stay":"Robertson thinks Jesus spent several months in the region of Caesarea Philippi, quietly teaching the Twelve.","journey":"About 25 miles north up the Jordan Valley, past the Huleh basin, to the springs of the Jordan.","route":[[35.63,32.91],[35.64,32.98],[35.65,33.06],[35.66,33.14],[35.69,33.25]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 16:15–16","text":"He said to them, \"But who do you say that I am?\" Simon Peter replied, \"You are the Christ, the Son of the living God.\""},{"ref":"Matthew 16:17–18","text":"And Jesus answered him, \"Blessed are you, Simon Bar-Jona! For flesh and blood has not revealed this to you, but my Father who is in heaven. And I tell you, you are Peter, and on this rock I will build my church, and the powers of death shall not prevail against it."},{"ref":"Matthew 16:19","text":"I will give you the keys of the kingdom of heaven, and whatever you bind on earth shall be bound in heaven, and whatever you loose on earth shall be loosed in heaven.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Isaiah 22:22","note":"The key of the house of David given to the king's steward: \"he shall open, and none shall shut.\""},{"ref":"Psalm 89","note":"Robertson notes that the imagery here (build, rock, anointed, the power of Sheol, an everlasting throne) all appears in this psalm of David's line."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/confession0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Christ_Handing_the_Keys_to_St._Peter_by_Pietro_Perugino.jpg","title":"Delivery of the Keys","artist":"Pietro Perugino","year":"1481–1482","medium":"Fresco","location":"Sistine Chapel, Vatican City","description":"Christ hands the keys to the kneeling Peter, surrounded by the apostles and contemporary onlookers, on a vast paved square before a domed temple flanked by triumphal arches.","significance":"Part of the wall cycle commissioned by Pope Sixtus IV for his new chapel, decades before Michelangelo painted its ceiling.","symbolism":"The gold and silver keys stand for the power to bind and loose in heaven and on earth (Matthew 16:19); the central temple stands for the Church built on Peter.","impact":"A landmark of Renaissance perspective; the young Raphael, Perugino's pupil, echoed its design in his Marriage of the Virgin."},{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/confession1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Primacy_of_Saint_Peter_(La_primaut%C3%A9_de_Saint-Pierre)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"The Primacy of Saint Peter","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"The disciples sit together on a rocky ledge beside a path under olive trees. Peter stands among them, hand raised to his head, as Jesus speaks to him.","significance":"Illustrates Matthew 16:18–19, where Jesus names Simon the rock and promises him the keys.","symbolism":"Tissot sets the scene against a wall of bare rock, recalling the word-play on Peter (Petros) and rock (petra).","impact":"A quiet, informal version of a scene that churches usually show with great ceremony."}],"tags":["teaching","call"]},
  {"id":"firstprediction","harmony":"§83","lane":"jesus","place":"caesareaphil","date":"Summer AD 32","title":"The first prediction of the Passion: \"Get behind me, Satan\"","people":["Jesus","Peter","The Twelve","The crowd"],"gospels":{"Mt":"16:21–26","Mk":"8:31–37","Lk":"9:22–25"},"summary":"From this time Jesus begins to teach plainly that the Son of man must suffer many things, be rejected by the elders, chief priests and scribes, be killed, and rise on the third day. Peter takes him aside and rebukes him. Jesus turns and rebukes Peter: \"Get behind me, Satan!\" Then he calls the crowd and tells them that anyone who would follow him must deny himself and take up his cross.","scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 16:21","text":"From that time Jesus began to show his disciples that he must go to Jerusalem and suffer many things from the elders and chief priests and scribes, and be killed, and on the third day be raised."},{"ref":"Matthew 16:23","text":"But he turned and said to Peter, \"Get behind me, Satan! You are a hindrance to me; for you are not on the side of God, but of men.\""},{"ref":"Mark 8:34–35","text":"And he called to him the multitude with his disciples, and said to them, \"If any man would come after me, let him deny himself and take up his cross and follow me. For whoever would save his life will lose it; and whoever loses his life for my sake and the gospel's will save it."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Isaiah 53","note":"The suffering servant who is rejected, bears the sins of many, and is vindicated."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/firstprediction0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Get_Thee_Behind_Me_Satan_(R%C3%A9tire-toi_Satan)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"Get Thee Behind Me, Satan","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"On a stony road through barren hills, Jesus turns sharply on Peter with both hands raised, while the other disciples look on in surprise.","significance":"Illustrates Matthew 16:23 and Mark 8:33, Jesus' rebuke when Peter tries to talk him out of the cross.","symbolism":"The road behind them leads on toward Jerusalem: Peter is literally standing in the way.","impact":"One of the few paintings of this sharp moment, which artists usually passed over in favor of the giving of the keys."}],"tags":["prophecy","teaching"]},
  {"id":"comingkingdom","harmony":"§84","lane":"jesus","place":"caesareaphil","date":"Summer AD 32","title":"The Son of man coming in his kingdom","people":["Jesus","The Twelve","The crowd"],"gospels":{"Mt":"16:27–28","Mk":"8:38–9:1","Lk":"9:26–27"},"summary":"Jesus warns that whoever is ashamed of him and his words, the Son of man will be ashamed of when he comes in the glory of his Father with the holy angels. He adds that some standing there will not taste death before they see the kingdom of God come with power. Each of the three Gospels places the Transfiguration immediately after this saying.","scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 8:38","text":"For whoever is ashamed of me and of my words in this adulterous and sinful generation, of him will the Son of man also be ashamed, when he comes in the glory of his Father with the holy angels.\""},{"ref":"Mark 9:1","text":"And he said to them, \"Truly, I say to you, there are some standing here who will not taste death before they see the kingdom of God come with power.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Daniel 7:13–14","note":"One like a son of man comes with the clouds and receives everlasting dominion."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","img":"img/comingkingdom0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banias,_Ancient_Caesarea_Philippi.jpg","title":"Banias, ancient Caesarea Philippi","artist":"Unknown photographer (lantern slide, Oregon State University Special Collections)","year":"c. 1910","medium":"Glass lantern slide","location":"Oregon State University Special Collections & Archives","description":"A traveler and a local guide look down from a spur of Mount Hermon over the stone houses and orchards of Banias and the plain beyond.","significance":"Banias is the site of Caesarea Philippi, where Jesus gave these sayings about the cross and the coming kingdom.","symbolism":"The view from the slopes of Hermon looks toward the spring-fed town and the land of Israel stretching south.","impact":"Lantern slides like this one brought the landscapes of the Bible to church halls and lecture rooms in the early 20th century."}],"tags":["prophecy","teaching"],"orderNote":"Interpreters differ on what Jesus meant by the kingdom coming \"with power\" within that generation: the Transfiguration that follows, the Resurrection and Pentecost, or the fall of Jerusalem in AD 70."},
  {"id":"transfiguration","harmony":"§85","lane":"jesus","place":"hermon","from":"caesareaphil","date":"Summer AD 32, \"after six days\"","title":"The Transfiguration","people":["Jesus","Peter","James","John","Moses","Elijah","The Father's voice"],"gospels":{"Mt":"17:1–8","Mk":"9:2–8","Lk":"9:28–36"},"summary":"Six days later (Luke: about eight) Jesus takes Peter, James and John up a high mountain to pray. His face shines like the sun and his clothes become dazzling white. Moses and Elijah appear and speak with him about his \"departure\" (exodus) that he is to accomplish at Jerusalem. Peter offers to build three booths. A bright cloud overshadows them, and a voice says, \"This is my beloved Son, with whom I am well pleased; listen to him.\" When they look up, they see only Jesus.","stay":"Luke implies they spent the night on the mountain: the disciples were heavy with sleep, and they came down \"on the next day\" (Luke 9:32, 37).","journey":"A climb from Caesarea Philippi onto the slopes of Mount Hermon, which rises directly above the town; the summit is about 12 to 14 miles away.","route":[[35.69,33.25],[35.75,33.3],[35.8,33.36],[35.86,33.42]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 17:1–2","text":"And after six days Jesus took with him Peter and James and John his brother, and led them up a high mountain apart. And he was transfigured before them, and his face shone like the sun, and his garments became white as light."},{"ref":"Luke 9:30–31","text":"And behold, two men talked with him, Moses and Elijah, who appeared in glory and spoke of his departure, which he was to accomplish at Jerusalem."},{"ref":"Matthew 17:5","text":"He was still speaking, when lo, a bright cloud overshadowed them, and a voice from the cloud said, \"This is my beloved Son, with whom I am well pleased; listen to him.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Exodus 24:15–18; 34:29–35","note":"Moses on Sinai in the cloud of glory, his face shining when he came down."},{"ref":"Deuteronomy 18:15","note":"\"Him you shall heed\": the voice's \"listen to him\" points to the prophet like Moses."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/transfiguration0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Transfiguration_Raphael.jpg","title":"The Transfiguration","artist":"Raphael","year":"1516–1520","medium":"Oil on panel","location":"Pinacoteca Vaticana, Vatican Museums, Rome","description":"Above, Christ floats in a blaze of light between Moses and Elijah, while Peter, James and John fall dazzled on the mountaintop. Below, the other disciples are helpless before the possessed boy, whose family points upward to Christ.","significance":"Raphael's last painting, nearly finished when he died in 1520 at the age of 37; it was hung above his body as he lay in state.","symbolism":"The two halves join the Transfiguration with the next day's healing (Matthew 17:14–18): glory above, human need and failure below, and the boy's upturned eyes link the two.","impact":"For centuries it was often called the greatest painting in the world. Napoleon carried it to Paris in 1797; it returned to Rome in 1816."},{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/transfiguration1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giovanni_Bellini_-_Transfiguration_of_Christ_-_WGA1690.jpg","title":"Transfiguration","artist":"Giovanni Bellini","year":"c. 1480","medium":"Oil on panel","location":"Museo di Capodimonte, Naples","description":"Christ stands calmly in white on a low hilltop between Moses and Elijah, while Peter, James and John crouch or sit startled at their feet. A wide Italian countryside of farms and towns stretches behind.","significance":"A late masterpiece of the Venetian master. Bellini sets the event in broad daylight on an ordinary hill rather than in a blaze of heavenly glory.","symbolism":"The fence and rocky ledge in the foreground mark the mountain as holy ground set apart; the farmers and ox in the distance carry on, unaware.","impact":"Its natural light and atmosphere were a turning point for Venetian landscape painting, opening the way for Giorgione and Titian."}],"tags":["miracle","prayer"],"orderNote":"The Gospels say only \"a high mountain.\" A tradition from the 4th century (Cyril of Jerusalem, Jerome) names Mount Tabor, where basilicas stand today; Robertson calls that tradition \"beyond question false,\" since Tabor lies far to the south, in Galilee. He prefers a spur of Hermon; others suggest Mount Meron."},
  {"id":"elijahcome","harmony":"§86","lane":"jesus","place":"hermon","date":"Summer AD 32","title":"Coming down the mountain: Elijah has come","people":["Jesus","Peter","James","John"],"gospels":{"Mt":"17:9–13","Mk":"9:9–13","Lk":"9:36"},"summary":"On the way down, Jesus tells the three to tell no one what they have seen until the Son of man is raised from the dead. They puzzle over what rising from the dead means, and ask why the scribes say Elijah must come first. Jesus answers that Elijah has already come and they did to him whatever they pleased, and so the Son of man will suffer at their hands. The disciples understand he is speaking of John the Baptist.","scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 9:9","text":"And as they were coming down the mountain, he charged them to tell no one what they had seen, until the Son of man should have risen from the dead."},{"ref":"Matthew 17:12–13","text":"but I tell you that Elijah has already come, and they did not know him, but did to him whatever they pleased. So also the Son of man will suffer at their hands.\" Then the disciples understood that he was speaking to them of John the Baptist."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Malachi 4:5","note":"\"I will send you Elijah the prophet before the great and terrible day of the Lord comes.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/elijahcome0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Transfiguration_(La_transfiguration)_-_James_Tissot_-_overall.jpg","title":"The Transfiguration","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Christ, robed in brilliant white, rises in a burst of light above the mountaintop, with Moses and Elijah on either side and the three disciples cowering below.","significance":"The vision the three disciples were ordered to keep secret \"until the Son of man should have risen from the dead\" (Mark 9:9).","symbolism":"Elijah's presence in the vision is the key to the disciples' question on the way down: the Elijah who was to come had already come, in John the Baptist.","impact":"Tissot renders the glory almost as pure light, a striking contrast to the earthy realism of most of his series."},{"kind":"Artifact","img":"img/elijahcome1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Hermon_and_vicinity._Pharpar_and_Hermon._Snow_clad_Hermon_from_Nahar_Barbar_LOC_matpc.00224.jpg","title":"Snow-clad Hermon from the Pharpar","artist":"American Colony Photo Department (Matson Collection)","year":"c. 1920","medium":"Glass negative","location":"Library of Congress, Washington, D.C.","description":"Snow-covered Mount Hermon rises on the horizon beyond a river meadow, with donkeys grazing by the water.","significance":"Shows the long snowy ridge that Robertson and many others think was the \"high mountain\" of the Transfiguration.","symbolism":"Hermon's snows feed the springs of the Jordan; in Psalm 133 its dew is a picture of God's blessing.","impact":"Part of the Matson Collection, thousands of photographs of the Holy Land taken in the first half of the 20th century."}],"tags":["teaching","prophecy"]},
  {"id":"demoniacboy","harmony":"§87","lane":"jesus","place":"caesareaphil","from":"hermon","date":"Summer AD 32","title":"The boy the disciples could not heal","people":["Jesus","Peter, James and John","The other disciples","A father","His only son, possessed","Scribes and the crowd"],"gospels":{"Mt":"17:14–20","Mk":"9:14–29","Lk":"9:37–43"},"summary":"At the foot of the mountain Jesus finds the other disciples arguing with scribes. A father has brought his only son, who is thrown into fits by a spirit, and the disciples could not cast it out. The father cries, \"I believe; help my unbelief!\" Jesus rebukes the spirit, and the boy lies like a corpse until Jesus takes his hand and lifts him up. Privately he tells the disciples that this kind can be driven out only by prayer and fasting, and that faith as small as a mustard seed could move mountains.","journey":"Back down from the mountain to the villages around Caesarea Philippi, the next day.","route":[[35.86,33.42],[35.8,33.36],[35.75,33.3],[35.69,33.25]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 9:23–24","text":"And Jesus said to him, \"If you can! All things are possible to him who believes.\" Immediately the father of the child cried out and said, \"I believe; help my unbelief!\""},{"ref":"Mark 9:29","text":"And he said to them, \"This kind cannot be driven out by anything but prayer and fasting.\""},{"ref":"Matthew 17:20","text":"He said to them, \"Because of your little faith. For truly, I say to you, if you have faith as a grain of mustard seed, you will say to this mountain, 'Move from hence to yonder place,' and it will move; and nothing will be impossible to you.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/demoniacboy0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Le_poss%C3%A9d%C3%A9_au_pied_du_Thabor_(The_Possessed_Boy_at_the_Foot_of_Mount_Tabor)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"Le possédé au pied du Thabor (The Possessed Boy at the Foot of Mount Tabor)","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"The boy lies twisted on his back on the hillside, mouth open, as his father kneels behind him. Jesus stands over them, one hand raised, while villagers watch from a town in the valley.","significance":"Illustrates Mark 9:20–27. Tissot followed the old tradition that set the Transfiguration on Mount Tabor, so he placed this scene at its foot.","symbolism":"Jesus' calm, upright figure in white contrasts with the boy's convulsed body and the anxious faces of the crowd.","impact":"Brings out the raw physical drama of the healing that Raphael set beneath his Transfiguration."}],"tags":["exorcism","healing","miracle"]},
  {"id":"secondprediction","harmony":"§88","lane":"jesus","place":"galilee","from":"caesareaphil","date":"Late summer AD 32","title":"The second prediction of the Passion","people":["Jesus","The Twelve"],"gospels":{"Mt":"17:22–23","Mk":"9:30–32","Lk":"9:43–45"},"summary":"Jesus passes through Galilee and does not want anyone to know, because he is teaching his disciples. He tells them again that the Son of man will be delivered into the hands of men, who will kill him, and that he will rise on the third day. They do not understand, and are afraid to ask him. Matthew says they were greatly distressed.","journey":"About 30 to 35 miles south from Caesarea Philippi down the Jordan Valley into Galilee, traveling privately.","route":[[35.69,33.25],[35.63,33.1],[35.59,32.98],[35.5,32.88],[35.42,32.8]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 9:31","text":"for he was teaching his disciples, saying to them, \"The Son of man will be delivered into the hands of men, and they will kill him; and when he is killed, after three days he will rise.\""},{"ref":"Luke 9:44–45","text":"\"Let these words sink into your ears; for the Son of man is to be delivered into the hands of men.\" But they did not understand this saying, and it was concealed from them, that they should not perceive it; and they were afraid to ask him about this saying."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/secondprediction0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Jesus_Discourses_with_His_Disciples_(J%C3%A9sus_s'entretient_avec_ses_disciples)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"Jesus Discourses with His Disciples","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Jesus walks along a rocky valley road, deep in conversation with a disciple, while the others follow in small groups up the winding path.","significance":"Matches Mark 9:30–31: they passed through Galilee and he did not want anyone to know, \"for he was teaching his disciples.\"","symbolism":"The quiet road away from the crowds shows the private training of the Twelve that marks this whole period.","impact":"Tissot's scenes of Jesus on the road give a sense of how much of the Gospels' teaching happened on foot."}],"tags":["prophecy","teaching"],"orderNote":"Robertson ends the season of retirement from Galilee here (§§72–88), about six months from spring to autumn, and thinks the events of §§89–96 took only a few days."},
  {"id":"templetax","harmony":"§89","lane":"jesus","place":"capernaum","from":"galilee","date":"Autumn AD 32","title":"The Temple tax and the coin in the fish","people":["Jesus","Peter","The collectors of the half-shekel tax"],"gospels":{"Mt":"17:24–27"},"summary":"Back in Capernaum, the collectors of the half-shekel Temple tax ask Peter whether his teacher pays it. Jesus asks Peter whether kings take tribute from their own sons or from others: the sons are free. Yet so as not to give offense, he sends Peter to cast a hook into the lake. In the mouth of the first fish he will find a shekel, enough to pay for both of them.","journey":"About 10 to 15 miles back to Capernaum.","route":[[35.42,32.8],[35.5,32.84],[35.575,32.881]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 17:24","text":"When they came to Capernaum, the collectors of the half-shekel tax went up to Peter and said, \"Does not your teacher pay the tax?\""},{"ref":"Matthew 17:27","text":"However, not to give offense to them, go to the sea and cast a hook, and take the first fish that comes up, and when you open its mouth you will find a shekel; take that and give it to them for me and for yourself.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Exodus 30:11–16","note":"Every Israelite man was to pay half a shekel as an offering for the sanctuary."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/templetax0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Masaccio_-_Tribute_Money_-_WGA14194.jpg","title":"The Tribute Money","artist":"Masaccio","year":"c. 1425–1427","medium":"Fresco","location":"Brancacci Chapel, Santa Maria del Carmine, Florence","description":"Three moments in one scene: in the center the tax collector confronts Jesus and the apostles; at left Peter kneels by the lake to take the coin from the fish's mouth; at right he pays the collector.","significance":"Painted for the Brancacci family's chapel of Saint Peter. It is the main painting of Matthew 17:24–27 in Western art.","symbolism":"The circle of apostles around Christ suggests the Church gathered around him; Peter's two appearances show his obedience to Jesus' word.","impact":"A founding work of Renaissance painting, with solid, sculptural figures, consistent light and perspective. Michelangelo studied and copied these frescoes as a young man."}],"tags":["miracle"]},
  {"id":"greatest","harmony":"§90","lane":"jesus","place":"capernaum","date":"Autumn AD 32","title":"Who is the greatest? A little child","people":["Jesus","The Twelve","A child"],"gospels":{"Mt":"18:1–5","Mk":"9:33–37","Lk":"9:46–48"},"summary":"In the house at Capernaum Jesus asks the disciples what they were arguing about on the road. They are silent, for they had argued about who was the greatest. He sits down and tells them that whoever would be first must be last of all and servant of all. Then he puts a child in their midst, takes it in his arms, and says that whoever receives such a child in his name receives him.","stay":"Jesus was in \"the house\" in Capernaum (Mark 9:33), probably Peter's home.","scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 9:35","text":"And he sat down and called the twelve; and he said to them, \"If any one would be first, he must be last of all and servant of all.\""},{"ref":"Matthew 18:3–4","text":"and said, \"Truly, I say to you, unless you turn and become like children, you will never enter the kingdom of heaven. Whoever humbles himself like this child, he is the greatest in the kingdom of heaven."},{"ref":"Mark 9:36–37","text":"And he took a child, and put him in the midst of them; and taking him in his arms, he said to them, \"Whoever receives one such child in my name receives me; and whoever receives me, receives not me but him who sent me.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/greatest0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Jesus_and_the_Little_Child_(J%C3%A9sus_et_le_petit_enfant)_-_James_Tissot_-_overall.jpg","title":"Jesus and the Little Child","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Jesus sits on stone steps in a town courtyard, holding a child close in his arms. The disciples stand before him in a silent, chastened row.","significance":"Illustrates Mark 9:36: \"taking him in his arms, he said to them...\"","symbolism":"The disciples, who had argued about greatness, stand while a child rests in the place of honor.","impact":"A tender image often used in children's Bibles and catechisms."}],"tags":["teaching"]},
  {"id":"johnszeal","harmony":"§91","lane":"jesus","place":"capernaum","date":"Autumn AD 32","title":"\"He that is not against us\"; the little ones and the lost sheep","people":["Jesus","John son of Zebedee","The Twelve"],"gospels":{"Mt":"18:6–14","Mk":"9:38–50","Lk":"9:49–50"},"summary":"John tells Jesus they stopped a man casting out demons in his name because he was not one of them. Jesus says not to forbid him: whoever is not against us is for us, and even a cup of water given in his name will be rewarded. He warns against causing one of these little ones to sin: better a millstone around the neck. In Matthew he tells of the shepherd who leaves the ninety-nine to look for one lost sheep, for it is not the Father's will that one of these little ones should perish.","scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 9:38–40","text":"John said to him, \"Teacher, we saw a man casting out demons in your name, and we forbade him, because he was not following us.\" But Jesus said, \"Do not forbid him; for no one who does a mighty work in my name will be able soon after to speak evil of me. For he that is not against us is for us."},{"ref":"Matthew 18:6","text":"but whoever causes one of these little ones who believe in me to sin, it would be better for him to have a great millstone fastened round his neck and to be drowned in the depth of the sea."},{"ref":"Matthew 18:12–14","text":"What do you think? If a man has a hundred sheep, and one of them has gone astray, does he not leave the ninety-nine on the hills and go in search of the one that went astray? And if he finds it, truly, I say to you, he rejoices over it more than over the ninety-nine that never went astray. So it is not the will of my Father who is in heaven that one of these little ones should perish."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Numbers 11:26–29","note":"Joshua wants Moses to stop Eldad and Medad prophesying in the camp; Moses answers, \"Would that all the Lord's people were prophets.\""},{"ref":"Ezekiel 34:11–16","note":"God himself will seek out his lost sheep."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/johnszeal0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Lost_Sheep_(The_Parables_of_Our_Lord_and_Saviour_Jesus_Christ)_MET_DP835793.jpg","title":"The Lost Sheep","artist":"John Everett Millais (engraved by the Dalziel Brothers)","year":"1864","medium":"Wood engraving","location":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York (this impression)","description":"A shepherd struggles up a steep, rocky hillside with the lost sheep slung across his shoulders, while a bird of prey circles overhead.","significance":"From Millais' The Parables of Our Lord, twenty wood engravings made over several years and counted among the finest Victorian book illustrations.","symbolism":"The bird of prey shows the danger the sheep was in; the shepherd's bent back shows the cost of the search.","impact":"Millais, a founder of the Pre-Raphaelite Brotherhood, set the parable in a real, rugged landscape rather than an idealized one."}],"tags":["teaching","parable"],"orderNote":"Luke tells the parable of the lost sheep again, more fully, to Pharisees and scribes later in the ministry (Luke 15:3–7). Robertson treats them as two occasions."},
  {"id":"unmerciful","harmony":"§92","lane":"jesus","place":"capernaum","date":"Autumn AD 32","title":"Forgiving a brother: the parable of the unmerciful servant","people":["Jesus","Peter","The disciples"],"gospels":{"Mt":"18:15–35"},"summary":"Jesus teaches how to correct a brother who sins: first alone, then with witnesses, then before the Church. Where two or three are gathered in his name, he is among them. Peter asks whether he should forgive as many as seven times, and Jesus answers, \"seventy times seven.\" He tells of a servant forgiven a debt of ten thousand talents who then throttles a fellow servant over a hundred denarii. The king hands him over to the jailers, \"so also my heavenly Father will do to every one of you, if you do not forgive your brother from your heart.\"","scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 18:20","text":"For where two or three are gathered in my name, there am I in the midst of them.\""},{"ref":"Matthew 18:21–22","text":"Then Peter came up and said to him, \"Lord, how often shall my brother sin against me, and I forgive him? As many as seven times?\" Jesus said to him, \"I do not say to you seven times, but seventy times seven."},{"ref":"Matthew 18:32–33","text":"Then his lord summoned him and said to him, 'You wicked servant! I forgave you all that debt because you besought me; and should not you have had mercy on your fellow servant, as I had mercy on you?'"}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Genesis 4:24","note":"Lamech's boast of vengeance \"seventy-sevenfold,\" which Jesus turns into a measure of forgiveness."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/unmerciful0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Claude_Vignon_-_Parable_of_the_Unforgiving_Servant.jpg","title":"Parable of the Unforgiving Servant","artist":"Claude Vignon","year":"1629","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Musée des Beaux-Arts, Tours","description":"A crowned king, chin in hand, sits at a table of ledgers and gold coins, pointing at an old man in debt who is held by two young attendants.","significance":"Long known as \"Croesus Receiving Tribute from a Lydian Peasant,\" it is now identified as the king of Jesus' parable settling accounts with his servants (Matthew 18:23–25).","symbolism":"The scattered coins and the open account books recall the vast debt; the king's thoughtful gesture hints at the mercy to come.","impact":"A showpiece of Vignon's rich, colorful style, which blended the influence of Caravaggio with the Venetian painters."}],"tags":["parable","teaching"],"orderNote":"Robertson's note puts the gap in modern terms: the king forgave about a million, and the servant would not forgive about ten. A talent was worth about 6,000 denarii, a laborer's wage for nearly 20 years."},
  {"id":"costfollow","harmony":"§93","lane":"jesus","place":"capernaum","date":"Autumn AD 32","title":"The cost of following Jesus","people":["Jesus","A scribe","Would-be disciples"],"gospels":{"Mt":"8:19–22","Lk":"9:57–62"},"summary":"A scribe offers to follow Jesus wherever he goes. Jesus answers that foxes have holes and birds have nests, but the Son of man has nowhere to lay his head. To one who wants first to bury his father, he says, \"Leave the dead to bury their own dead.\" To another who wants first to say farewell at home, he says that no one who puts his hand to the plow and looks back is fit for the kingdom of God.","scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 9:58","text":"And Jesus said to him, \"Foxes have holes, and birds of the air have nests; but the Son of man has nowhere to lay his head.\""},{"ref":"Luke 9:59–60","text":"To another he said, \"Follow me.\" But he said, \"Lord, let me first go and bury my father.\" But he said to him, \"Leave the dead to bury their own dead; but as for you, go and proclaim the kingdom of God.\""},{"ref":"Luke 9:62","text":"Jesus said to him, \"No one who puts his hand to the plow and looks back is fit for the kingdom of God.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"1 Kings 19:19–21","note":"Elisha leaves his plow and his oxen to follow Elijah, after first saying farewell to his parents."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/costfollow0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Man_at_the_Plough_(L'homme_%C3%A0_la_charrue)_-_James_Tissot_-_overall.jpg","title":"The Man at the Plough","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"An old farmer guides a wooden plow pulled by an ox and a donkey across a stony hillside field, looking back over his shoulder.","significance":"Illustrates Luke 9:62: \"No one who puts his hand to the plow and looks back is fit for the kingdom of God.\"","symbolism":"The plowman's backward glance, while his furrow runs crooked, is the very image Jesus used for divided loyalty.","impact":"Tissot painted the light plows and mixed teams he saw in use in Palestine in the 1880s."}],"tags":["call","teaching"],"orderNote":"Matthew sets these sayings early, before the crossing to the Gadarenes; Luke sets them on the road after the Samaritan village. Robertson places them here, just before Jesus leaves Galilee, and notes that such sayings may have been repeated."},
  {"id":"brothers","harmony":"§94","lane":"jesus","place":"capernaum","date":"Autumn AD 32, before Tabernacles","title":"The unbelieving brothers of Jesus","people":["Jesus","His brothers"],"gospels":{"Jn":"7:2–9"},"summary":"The Feast of Tabernacles is near. Jesus' brothers urge him to go up to Judea and show his works to the world, for no one works in secret if he wants to be known. John explains that even his brothers did not believe in him. Jesus answers that his time has not yet come, though their time is always here, and he stays in Galilee.","stay":"Jesus remained in Galilee for a few more days while his brothers went up to the feast (John 7:9).","scripture":[{"ref":"John 7:3–5","text":"So his brethren said to him, \"Leave here and go to Judea, that your disciples may see the works you are doing. For no man works in secret if he seeks to be known openly. If you do these things, show yourself to the world.\" For even his brethren did not believe in him."},{"ref":"John 7:6","text":"Jesus said to them, \"My time has not yet come, but your time is always here."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/brothers0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Jesus_Traveling_(J%C3%A9sus_en_voyage)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"Jesus Traveling","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Jesus walks with a few disciples across a wide, stony hillside, while families with donkeys travel in small groups along the slope behind them.","significance":"The caravans of pilgrims suggest the crowds going up to the feasts at Jerusalem, as Jesus' brothers did at Tabernacles (John 7:10).","symbolism":"Jesus stands a little apart from the flow of travelers: his time, he told his brothers, had not yet come.","impact":"Tissot's wide landscapes from his trips to Palestine give the long walks of the Gospels their real scale."}],"tags":["conflict"],"orderNote":"Who the \"brothers\" were is debated: Catholic and Orthodox tradition holds they were cousins or Joseph's sons by an earlier marriage; many Protestants take them as later children of Mary and Joseph. James, one of them, later led the church in Jerusalem."},
  {"id":"samaritanvillage","harmony":"§95","lane":"jesus","place":"samariavillage","from":"capernaum","date":"Autumn AD 32, Tabernacles","title":"Rejected by a Samaritan village","people":["Jesus","James and John","Messengers","The Samaritan villagers"],"gospels":{"Lk":"9:51–56","Jn":"7:10"},"summary":"When the days drew near for him to be received up, Jesus sets his face to go to Jerusalem. After his brothers have gone up to the feast, he goes too, privately, by the road through Samaria. He sends messengers ahead, but a Samaritan village will not receive him because he is going to Jerusalem. James and John offer to call down fire from heaven to consume them. Jesus turns and rebukes them, and they go on to another village.","journey":"About 45 miles south from Capernaum through the Jezreel Valley to the edge of Samaria; Jerusalem lay about 70 miles farther, some three days on foot.","route":[[35.575,32.881],[35.515,32.825],[35.45,32.74],[35.4,32.65],[35.34,32.55],[35.3,32.45]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 9:51","text":"When the days drew near for him to be received up, he set his face to go to Jerusalem."},{"ref":"Luke 9:53–55","text":"but the people would not receive him, because his face was set toward Jerusalem. And when his disciples James and John saw it, they said, \"Lord, do you want us to bid fire come down from heaven and consume them?\" But he turned and rebuked them."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"2 Kings 1:10–12","note":"Elijah calls down fire from heaven on the king's soldiers, the precedent James and John have in mind."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/samaritanvillage0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_He_Went_Through_the_Villages_on_the_Way_to_Jerusalem_(Il_allait_par_les_villages_en_route_pour_J%C3%A9rusalem)_-_James_Tissot_-_overall.jpg","title":"He Went Through the Villages on the Way to Jerusalem","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Jesus and his followers climb toward a hill town of stone houses and palm trees, as villagers come out or sit and watch by the road.","significance":"Tissot made it for Luke's long account of the journey to Jerusalem, which begins at Luke 9:51 with Jesus setting his face toward the city.","symbolism":"The road climbing toward the town suggests the long ascent to Jerusalem that Luke describes.","impact":"Shows the kind of hill village, packed with flat-roofed houses, where Jesus' messengers sought lodging."}],"tags":["conflict"],"orderNote":"Luke 9:51 opens his long travel narrative (9:51–19:27). Robertson, joining Luke and John, takes this first journey as the secret one to the Feast of Tabernacles in John 7:10, not the final journey to the Passover; others read Luke's whole section as the last journey."},
  {"id":"tabernacles","harmony":"§96","lane":"jesus","place":"temple","from":"samariavillage","date":"Autumn AD 32 (Feast of Tabernacles)","title":"At the Feast of Tabernacles","people":["Jesus","The crowds","The chief priests and Pharisees","The Temple officers","Nicodemus"],"gospels":{"Jn":"7:11–52"},"summary":"Jesus arrives in the middle of the week-long feast and teaches openly in the Temple. The crowds argue over whether he is a good man, a deceiver, or the Christ. On the last and greatest day he cries out that whoever thirsts should come to him and drink. Officers sent to arrest him come back empty-handed, and Nicodemus reminds the council that the Law does not condemn a man without a hearing.","stay":"Pilgrims lived for the week of the feast in booths of branches in and around Jerusalem.","journey":"About 45 to 50 miles, some three days on foot, by the ridge road through Samaria past Sychar and Bethel.","route":[[35.3,32.45],[35.29,32.3],[35.285,32.209],[35.27,32.05],[35.24,31.93],[35.23,31.85],[35.235,31.778]],"scripture":[{"ref":"John 7:37–38","text":"On the last day of the feast, the great day, Jesus stood up and proclaimed, \"If any one thirst, let him come to me and drink. He who believes in me, as the scripture has said, 'Out of his heart shall flow rivers of living water.'\""},{"ref":"John 7:46","text":"The officers answered, \"No man ever spoke like this man!\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Zechariah 14:16","note":"The nations going up year by year to keep the feast of booths."},{"ref":"Isaiah 12:3","note":"\"With joy you will draw water from the wells of salvation\", a verse linked with the feast's daily water rite."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"Reconstruction of Jerusalem and the Temple of Herod","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"A bird's-eye view of Jerusalem from the east: Herod's Temple with its vast colonnaded courts rises above the city wall, smoke climbing from the altar, with pilgrims and flocks on the slope below.","significance":"Tissot made this reconstruction for his Life of Christ after studying the site and the archaeology of his day, to show the setting of Jesus' Temple teaching.","symbolism":"The smoke of sacrifice at the heart of the city recalls that the great feasts brought all Israel up to the Temple.","impact":"Helps the reader picture the scale of the courts where crowds argued about Jesus during the feast.","img":"img/tabernacles0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Reconstruction_of_Jerusalem_and_the_Temple_of_Herod_(Réconstitution_de_Jérusalem_et_du_temple_d'Hérode)_-_James_Tissot.jpg"},{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"But No Man Laid Hands Upon Him","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Jesus, in white, teaches a seated crowd in a Temple colonnade while armed officers lean on the low wall nearby, watching but not moving.","significance":"Illustrates John 7:30–46, where the officers sent to arrest Jesus return without him.","symbolism":"The officers sit idle, weapons at rest: no one can touch Jesus before \"his hour\" has come.","impact":"Shows how openly Jesus taught at the feast, under the eyes of those sent to seize him.","img":"img/tabernacles1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_But_No_Man_Laid_Hands_Upon_Him_(Les_satellites_ne_prirent_point_Jésus)_-_James_Tissot.jpg"}],"tags":["teaching","conflict"],"orderNote":"Robertson dates this Tabernacles to October AD 29, six months before the crucifixion. The water-pouring and lamp-lighting rites of the feast, which John's sayings about living water and light seem to echo, are known from the Mishnah (Sukkah 4–5), written down about AD 200."},
  {"id":"adulteress","harmony":"§97","lane":"jesus","place":"temple","date":"Autumn AD 32","title":"The woman caught in adultery","people":["Jesus","A woman caught in adultery","The scribes and Pharisees"],"gospels":{"Jn":"7:53–8:11"},"summary":"Early in the morning Jesus comes from the Mount of Olives to teach in the Temple. Scribes and Pharisees bring a woman caught in adultery and ask whether she should be stoned, as Moses commanded, hoping to trap him. Jesus bends down and writes on the ground, then says that the one without sin should throw the first stone. They leave one by one, and he sends her away: \"Go, and do not sin again.\"","stay":"He spent the night on the Mount of Olives and came back to the Temple at dawn.","scripture":[{"ref":"John 8:7","text":"And as they continued to ask him, he stood up and said to them, \"Let him who is without sin among you be the first to throw a stone at her.\""},{"ref":"John 8:10–11","text":"Jesus looked up and said to her, \"Woman, where are they? Has no one condemned you?\" She said, \"No one, Lord.\" And Jesus said, \"Neither do I condemn you; go, and do not sin again.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Leviticus 20:10","note":"The Law's death penalty for adultery, for both the man and the woman."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"The Woman Taken in Adultery","artist":"Rembrandt","year":"1644","medium":"Oil on oak panel","location":"National Gallery, London","description":"In a towering, shadowy Temple interior, a shaft of light falls on the kneeling woman in white and on the tall, calm figure of Christ, while her accusers crowd around. A golden high altar glitters in the gloom behind.","significance":"One of Rembrandt's finest small history paintings, painted with the care of a miniature.","symbolism":"The light picks out only the woman and Christ: mercy and the sinner meet, while the accusers stay in half-shadow.","impact":"A treasured work of the National Gallery's Dutch collection and an often-reproduced image of this scene.","img":"img/adulteress0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rembrandt_-_The_Woman_taken_in_Adultery_(1644).jpg"},{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"Christ and the Woman Taken in Adultery","artist":"Pieter Bruegel the Elder","year":"1565","medium":"Oil on panel (grisaille)","location":"The Courtauld Gallery, London","description":"Painted in shades of grey, Christ kneels and writes on the ground while the woman stands quietly behind him and the scribes and Pharisees lean in to read.","significance":"A rare small grisaille, apparently painted for Bruegel's own circle rather than for sale; it was copied many times, including in an engraving of 1579.","symbolism":"The words Christ writes are legible, in Dutch: \"He that is without sin...\". Painted in years of religious persecution in the Netherlands, it is often read as a plea for mercy and tolerance.","impact":"One of the most admired works of the Courtauld collection, and an unusual image that shows the moment of writing rather than the woman's shame.","img":"img/adulteress1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pieter_Bruegel_(I)_-_Christ_and_the_woman_taken_in_adultery.jpg"}],"tags":["teaching","conflict"],"orderNote":"Most of the oldest manuscripts do not have this passage, and some place it elsewhere (after John 7:36 or Luke 21:38). Robertson thinks it is very probably a true story of Jesus, perhaps preserved by Papias, and notes that without it John 8:12 follows straight on from John 7:52. The Catholic Church receives it as canonical Scripture."},
  {"id":"lightworld","harmony":"§98","lane":"jesus","place":"temple","date":"Autumn AD 32","title":"\"I am the light of the world\"","people":["Jesus","The Pharisees"],"gospels":{"Jn":"8:12–20"},"summary":"Speaking in the treasury of the Temple, Jesus declares that he is the light of the world. The Pharisees object that he bears witness to himself. He answers that the Father who sent him bears witness too. No one arrests him, \"because his hour had not yet come.\"","scripture":[{"ref":"John 8:12","text":"Again Jesus spoke to them, saying, \"I am the light of the world; he who follows me will not walk in darkness, but will have the light of life.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Isaiah 49:6","note":"The Servant given \"as a light to the nations.\""},{"ref":"Exodus 13:21","note":"The pillar of fire that gave Israel light in the wilderness."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"Jesus Speaks Near the Treasury","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Jesus, seen from behind in a white mantle, sits on the pavement of a Temple court and speaks to a ring of seated listeners, while robed elders stand watching at the back.","significance":"Illustrates John 8:20: \"These words he spoke in the treasury, as he taught in the temple.\"","symbolism":"The bright figure of Jesus at the center of the gathering echoes his claim to be the light of the world.","impact":"Places the saying in a real setting: the busy Court of the Women, where the offering chests stood.","img":"img/lightworld0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Jesus_Speaks_Near_the_Treasury_(Jésus_parle_près_du_trésor)_-_James_Tissot.jpg"}],"tags":["teaching","conflict"]},
  {"id":"beforeabraham","harmony":"§99","lane":"jesus","place":"temple","date":"Autumn AD 32","title":"\"Before Abraham was, I am\"","people":["Jesus","The Jews who had believed in him","The Pharisees"],"gospels":{"Jn":"8:21–59"},"summary":"Jesus warns that those who do not believe will die in their sins, and promises that the truth will make his followers free. His hearers claim Abraham as their father, and the argument grows sharp. When Jesus says, \"Before Abraham was, I am,\" they pick up stones to throw at him. He hides himself and goes out of the Temple.","scripture":[{"ref":"John 8:31–32","text":"Jesus then said to the Jews who had believed in him, \"If you continue in my word, you are truly my disciples, and you will know the truth, and the truth will make you free.\""},{"ref":"John 8:58","text":"Jesus said to them, \"Truly, truly, I say to you, before Abraham was, I am.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Exodus 3:14","note":"God's name revealed to Moses: \"I AM WHO I AM.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"The Jews Took Up Rocks to Stone Jesus","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Among the timber scaffolding of the unfinished Temple buildings, an angry crowd bends to snatch up stones, while Jesus walks calmly away out of the scene.","significance":"Illustrates the end of John 8, when Jesus \"hid himself, and went out of the temple.\"","symbolism":"The building works recall that Herod's Temple was still being finished in Jesus' day (John 2:20); the loose stones become weapons.","impact":"A vivid reminder of how close Jesus came to death in Jerusalem months before the Passion.","img":"img/beforeabraham0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Jews_Took_Up_Rocks_to_Stone_Jesus_(Les_juifs_prirent_des_pierres_pour_lapider_Jésus)_-_James_Tissot.jpg"},{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"He Who is of God Hears the Word of God","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Jesus stands in a colonnade, one hand raised, speaking to a group seated along a low wall; some listen, others turn away.","significance":"Illustrates John 8:47: \"He who is of God hears the words of God.\"","symbolism":"The divided audience mirrors the division John describes: some believe, others reject him.","impact":"One of the many quiet teaching scenes in Tissot's cycle that slow the reader down to listen.","img":"img/beforeabraham1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_He_Who_is_of_God_Hears_the_Word_of_God_(Celui_de_Dieu_entend_la_parole_de_Dieu)_-_James_Tissot.jpg"}],"tags":["teaching","conflict"]},
  {"id":"bornblind","harmony":"§100","lane":"jesus","place":"siloam","from":"temple","date":"Autumn AD 32","title":"The man born blind","people":["Jesus","The disciples","A man blind from birth","His parents","The Pharisees"],"gospels":{"Jn":"9:1–41"},"summary":"The disciples ask whose sin caused a man to be born blind. Jesus says neither his nor his parents'; it is so that God's works may be shown. He makes clay with spittle, anoints the man's eyes and sends him to wash in the Pool of Siloam, and he comes back seeing. Because it is a sabbath, the Pharisees question him and his parents and finally cast him out. Jesus finds him, and the man believes and worships him.","journey":"Under a mile, from the Temple down to the pool at the south end of the City of David.","route":[[35.235,31.778],[35.2345,31.774],[35.2353,31.7706]],"scripture":[{"ref":"John 9:6–7","text":"As he said this, he spat on the ground and made clay of the spittle and anointed the man's eyes with the clay, saying to him, \"Go, wash in the pool of Siloam\" (which means Sent). So he went and washed and came back seeing."},{"ref":"John 9:25","text":"He answered, \"Whether he is a sinner, I do not know; one thing I know, that though I was blind, now I see.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Isaiah 35:5","note":"\"Then the eyes of the blind shall be opened.\""},{"ref":"Isaiah 8:6","note":"\"The waters of Shiloah that go softly\", the same spring-fed water system."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"Healing of the Man Born Blind","artist":"El Greco","year":"c. 1570–75","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Galleria Nazionale, Parma","description":"Christ, in red and blue, touches the eyes of the kneeling blind man, while disciples and onlookers react around them in a broad piazza of classical buildings.","significance":"Painted in Italy by the young El Greco, who treated this subject in several versions; the Venetian colors and deep perspective show what he learned from Titian and Tintoretto.","symbolism":"The long, empty street behind the figures leads the eye into the distance, as sight is given; the arguing groups recall the debate that follows the miracle.","impact":"A key work for understanding El Greco before his move to Spain.","img":"img/bornblind0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:El_Greco_14.jpg"},{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"The Blind Man Washes in the Pool of Siloam","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Under a great stone arch, a man kneels at the edge of a pool and splashes water on his face, while people draw water nearby.","significance":"Tissot sketched the pool as it looked in the 1880s, long before the Second Temple pool was found in 2004.","symbolism":"The water of Siloam, which John translates as \"Sent\", points to Jesus, the one sent by the Father.","impact":"Shows the obedient act that brings the healing: \"So he went and washed and came back seeing.\"","img":"img/bornblind1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Blind_Man_Washes_in_the_Pool_of_Siloam_(Le_aveugle-né_se_lave_à_la_piscine_de_Siloë)_-_James_Tissot.jpg"}],"tags":["miracle","healing","conflict"],"orderNote":"John does not say where Jesus met the man, only that he washed in Siloam. A stepped pool of the Second Temple period was uncovered in 2004 a little south of the older Byzantine pool, and is widely identified as the Pool of Siloam of Jesus' day."},
  {"id":"goodshepherd","harmony":"§101","lane":"jesus","place":"temple","from":"siloam","date":"Autumn AD 32","title":"The Good Shepherd","people":["Jesus","The Pharisees"],"gospels":{"Jn":"10:1–21"},"summary":"Still speaking to the Pharisees after the healing of the blind man, Jesus contrasts the shepherd who enters by the door with thieves and hirelings. He is the door of the sheep and the good shepherd who knows his own. He will lay down his life for the sheep, and take it up again, by his own will. Once more his hearers are divided.","journey":"Under a mile, back up to the Temple area.","route":[[35.2353,31.7706],[35.2345,31.774],[35.235,31.778]],"scripture":[{"ref":"John 10:11","text":"I am the good shepherd. The good shepherd lays down his life for the sheep."},{"ref":"John 10:14–15","text":"I am the good shepherd; I know my own and my own know me, as the Father knows me and I know the Father; and I lay down my life for the sheep."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Ezekiel 34:11–16","note":"God promises to search for his scattered sheep and shepherd them himself."},{"ref":"Psalm 23:1","note":"\"The Lord is my shepherd.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"The Parable of the Good Shepherd","artist":"Philip Galle after Pieter Bruegel the Elder","year":"1565","medium":"Engraving","location":"Musea Brugge, Bruges","description":"Christ stands in the doorway of a sheepfold carrying a lamb on his shoulders, sheep at his feet. All around, thieves break through the roof and walls and wolves attack the flock.","significance":"A print published after a design by Bruegel. The Latin over the door reads \"Ego sum ostium ovium\", \"I am the door of the sheep\" (John 10:7).","symbolism":"The shepherd who enters by the door is set against those who climb in \"by another way\"; the image is often read as a comment on the religious conflicts of Bruegel's day.","impact":"Spread widely as a print, it is one of the fullest pictures of John 10 ever made.","img":"img/goodshepherd0.jpg","credit":"Musea Brugge, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Musea_Brugge,_HUB,_2014_GRO0023_III.jpg"}],"tags":["teaching","parable"],"orderNote":"John gives no place; Robertson simply says Jerusalem. Some scholars join John 9–10:21 to the Feast of Dedication, but Robertson thinks John 10:24 implies a gap of time."},
  {"id":"seventy","harmony":"§102","lane":"jesus","place":"judeacountry","from":"temple","date":"Autumn AD 32","title":"The mission of the Seventy","people":["Jesus","The seventy (or seventy-two) disciples"],"gospels":{"Lk":"10:1–24"},"summary":"Jesus sends out seventy others two by two into every town where he himself is about to come. They are to travel light, bring peace to each house, heal the sick and announce that the kingdom of God has come near. They return full of joy that even the demons submit to them. Jesus rejoices in the Holy Spirit and thanks the Father for revealing these things to little ones.","journey":"Luke names no towns. Robertson places this ministry in the Judean countryside, a few miles out from Jerusalem.","route":[[35.235,31.778],[35.25,31.8],[35.27,31.83],[35.3,31.85]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 10:2","text":"And he said to them, \"The harvest is plentiful, but the laborers are few; pray therefore the Lord of the harvest to send out laborers into his harvest."},{"ref":"Luke 10:17–18","text":"The seventy returned with joy, saying, \"Lord, even the demons are subject to us in your name!\" And he said to them, \"I saw Satan fall like lightning from heaven."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Numbers 11:16–17","note":"Moses chooses seventy elders to share his spirit and his work."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"He Sent Them Out Two by Two","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Pairs of disciples with staffs set off in different directions across rolling hills, while Jesus stands among them, sending them on their way.","significance":"Tissot's title comes from Mark's sending of the Twelve, but Luke uses the same words for the Seventy: \"two by two, into every town and place.\"","symbolism":"Going in pairs gave each witness a companion, as the Law required two witnesses.","impact":"Shows how far and wide the mission spread over the countryside.","img":"img/seventy0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_He_Sent_them_out_Two_by_Two_(Il_les_envoya_deux_à_deux)_-_James_Tissot_-_overall.jpg"}],"tags":["call","teaching"],"orderNote":"Manuscripts differ between seventy and seventy-two (the RSV-2CE reads seventy). Robertson places Luke 10–13:21 in Judea between Tabernacles and Dedication, while many scholars read it as part of the long journey to Jerusalem that begins in Luke 9:51."},
  {"id":"goodsamaritan","harmony":"§103","lane":"jesus","place":"judeacountry","date":"Autumn AD 32","title":"The Good Samaritan","people":["Jesus","A lawyer"],"gospels":{"Lk":"10:25–37"},"summary":"A lawyer asks what he must do to inherit eternal life, and answers his own question: love God, and love your neighbor as yourself. \"And who is my neighbor?\" Jesus tells of a man beaten by robbers on the road down from Jerusalem to Jericho. A priest and a Levite pass by, but a Samaritan, a despised outsider, binds his wounds and pays for his care. \"Go and do likewise.\"","scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 10:27","text":"And he answered, \"You shall love the Lord your God with all your heart, and with all your soul, and with all your strength, and with all your mind; and your neighbor as yourself.\""},{"ref":"Luke 10:33–34","text":"But a Samaritan, as he journeyed, came to where he was; and when he saw him, he had compassion, and went to him and bound up his wounds, pouring on oil and wine; then he set him on his own beast and brought him to an inn, and took care of him."},{"ref":"Luke 10:36–37","text":"Which of these three, do you think, proved neighbor to the man who fell among the robbers?\" He said, \"The one who showed mercy on him.\" And Jesus said to him, \"Go and do likewise.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Deuteronomy 6:5","note":"The Shema: love the Lord your God with all your heart."},{"ref":"Leviticus 19:18","note":"\"You shall love your neighbor as yourself.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"The Good Samaritan (after Delacroix)","artist":"Vincent van Gogh","year":"1890","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Kröller-Müller Museum, Otterlo","description":"In swirling yellows and blues, the Samaritan heaves the wounded man onto his horse, while the priest and the Levite walk away along the road in the distance.","significance":"Painted in May 1890 at the asylum in Saint-Rémy, from a print of Eugène Delacroix's painting of the scene. Van Gogh often made such \"translations\" into color.","symbolism":"The straining body of the Samaritan shows love as hard physical work; the empty box at his feet hints at the robbery.","impact":"One of the best known religious images of modern art, painted only two months before Van Gogh's death.","img":"img/goodsamaritan0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Van_Gogh_-_Der_barmherzige_Samariter.jpeg"},{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"The Good Samaritan","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"In a rocky, barren gully, the Samaritan crouches to lift the injured man, his saddled animals waiting on the bank above.","significance":"Tissot set the scene in the real desert landscape of the road from Jerusalem down to Jericho, which he had traveled.","symbolism":"The empty hills stress how alone the victim was, and how freely the stranger chose to stop.","impact":"Helps the reader see why the road was so dangerous.","img":"img/goodsamaritan1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Good_Samaritan_(Le_bon_samaritain)_-_James_Tissot.jpg"}],"tags":["parable","teaching"],"orderNote":"The parable is set on the steep, lonely road from Jerusalem down to Jericho, which falls about 3,300 feet in some 17 miles. The \"Inn of the Good Samaritan\" on that road is a later tradition; Luke does not say where the parable itself was told."},
  {"id":"marthamary","harmony":"§104","lane":"jesus","place":"bethany","from":"judeacountry","date":"Autumn AD 32","title":"Martha and Mary","people":["Jesus","Martha","Mary"],"gospels":{"Lk":"10:38–42"},"summary":"Jesus is welcomed into the home of Martha. Her sister Mary sits at his feet and listens, while Martha is busy with much serving and asks Jesus to tell Mary to help. Jesus answers gently that Martha is anxious about many things, but one thing is needful. Mary has chosen the good portion, which will not be taken from her.","stay":"A guest in Martha's house. Robertson notes that Bethany became his Jerusalem home in Passion Week.","journey":"A few miles across the Judean hills.","route":[[35.3,31.85],[35.29,31.82],[35.275,31.79],[35.262,31.771]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 10:41–42","text":"But the Lord answered her, \"Martha, Martha, you are anxious and troubled about many things; one thing is needful. Mary has chosen the good portion, which shall not be taken away from her.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"Christ in the House of Martha and Mary","artist":"Johannes Vermeer","year":"c. 1654–55","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Scottish National Gallery, Edinburgh","description":"Christ sits at a table, turning to Mary, who rests her head on her hand at his feet, while Martha leans in with a basket of bread.","significance":"One of Vermeer's earliest known works and his largest painting, made before he turned to the quiet interiors for which he is famous.","symbolism":"Christ's gesture links the two sisters: his hand points to Mary, but his face turns toward Martha, holding both service and listening together.","impact":"Rediscovered in 1901; its signature helped scholars trace Vermeer's early career.","img":"img/marthamary0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johannes_(Jan)_Vermeer_-_Christ_in_the_House_of_Martha_and_Mary_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg"},{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"Kitchen Scene with Christ in the House of Martha and Mary","artist":"Diego Velázquez","year":"1618","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"National Gallery, London","description":"In a Spanish kitchen, a sulking young cook pounds garlic beside fish and eggs while an old woman points her toward a small scene of Christ with Martha and Mary in the background.","significance":"Painted by Velázquez at about nineteen in Seville. It is a bodegón, a kitchen still life, with the Gospel story seen through an opening.","symbolism":"The young cook is a Martha of Seville, busy with \"much serving\"; the old woman seems to remind her of the \"one thing needful.\"","impact":"A celebrated early masterpiece, and a striking example of a Gospel scene placed inside everyday life.","img":"img/marthamary1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cristo_en_casa_de_Marta_y_María,_by_Diego_Velázquez.jpg"}],"tags":["teaching"],"orderNote":"Luke says only \"a village\". John 11:1 names Bethany as the village of Mary and Martha, so Robertson places the visit there, near Jerusalem, and not at Bethany beyond the Jordan."},
  {"id":"lordsprayer","harmony":"§105","lane":"jesus","place":"olives","from":"bethany","date":"Autumn AD 32","title":"\"Lord, teach us to pray\"","people":["Jesus","The disciples"],"gospels":{"Lk":"11:1–13"},"summary":"After Jesus has been praying, a disciple asks him to teach them to pray, as John taught his disciples. Jesus gives them a short form of the Lord's Prayer. He adds the parable of a friend who knocks at midnight to borrow bread and is answered because he persists. \"Ask, and it will be given you\"; how much more will the Father give the Holy Spirit to those who ask him.","journey":"About 1 to 2 miles, from Bethany up over the Mount of Olives.","route":[[35.262,31.771],[35.255,31.775],[35.245,31.778]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 11:2–4","text":"And he said to them, \"When you pray, say: \"Father, hallowed be your name. Your kingdom come. Give us each day our daily bread; and forgive us our sins, for we ourselves forgive every one who is indebted to us; and lead us not into temptation.\""},{"ref":"Luke 11:9","text":"And I tell you, Ask, and it will be given you; seek, and you will find; knock, and it will be opened to you."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"Jerusalem Seen from the Mount of Olives","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Drawing on paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"A panoramic study of the walled city across the Kidron Valley, drawn from the slope of the Mount of Olives with olive trees and terraces in the foreground.","significance":"One of the landscape studies Tissot made in Palestine as material for his Life of Christ.","symbolism":"The view takes in the city Jesus prayed over and taught near; the Mount of Olives was a place he often went to pray (Luke 22:39).","impact":"Shows the setting tradition gives to the teaching of the Lord's Prayer, just above the Garden of Gethsemane.","img":"img/lordsprayer0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Jerusalem_Seen_from_the_Mount_of_Olives_-_James_Tissot.jpg"}],"tags":["prayer","teaching","parable"],"orderNote":"Luke says only \"in a certain place.\" Robertson suggests Judea. Since the Middle Ages, tradition has placed the teaching of the Lord's Prayer at a cave on the Mount of Olives, under the ruins of Constantine's 4th-century Eleona church; the Pater Noster church stands there today. Matthew gives a longer form in the Sermon on the Mount; Robertson takes this as a second teaching."},
  {"id":"blessedwomb","harmony":"§106","lane":"jesus","place":"judeacountry","from":"olives","date":"Autumn AD 32","title":"By the finger of God","people":["Jesus","A man who had been mute","The crowds","A woman in the crowd"],"gospels":{"Lk":"11:14–36"},"summary":"Jesus casts out a demon that had made a man mute. Some say he does it by Beelzebul, the prince of demons. He answers that a divided kingdom cannot stand, and that if he casts out demons by the finger of God, then the kingdom of God has come. A woman calls out a blessing on his mother; he answers, \"Blessed rather are those who hear the word of God and keep it!\" He warns that this generation will get no sign but the sign of Jonah.","journey":"A few miles back into the Judean countryside.","route":[[35.245,31.778],[35.26,31.8],[35.28,31.83],[35.3,31.85]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 11:20","text":"But if it is by the finger of God that I cast out demons, then the kingdom of God has come upon you."},{"ref":"Luke 11:27–28","text":"As he said this, a woman in the crowd raised her voice and said to him, \"Blessed is the womb that bore you, and the breasts that you sucked!\" But he said, \"Blessed rather are those who hear the word of God and keep it!\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Exodus 8:19","note":"Pharaoh's magicians: \"This is the finger of God.\""},{"ref":"Jonah 3:5","note":"The people of Nineveh repent at Jonah's preaching."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"A Woman Cries Out in a Crowd","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"In a crowded street of tall stone houses, a woman lifts her arms and calls out toward Jesus over the heads of the throng.","significance":"Illustrates Luke 11:27, a moment found only in Luke.","symbolism":"The woman praises Mary's motherhood; Jesus answers that hearing and keeping God's word is the greater blessing, which Mary herself did.","impact":"Catches the noisy, spontaneous mood of Jesus' teaching among ordinary people.","img":"img/blessedwomb0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_A_Woman_Cries_Out_in_a_Crowd_(Une_femme_crie_dans_en_foule)_-_James_Tissot.jpg"}],"tags":["miracle","exorcism","conflict","teaching"],"orderNote":"Matthew and Mark tell a similar charge of league with Beelzebul in Galilee (§61). Robertson thinks it natural that the accusation was repeated a year or so later in Judea; others take Luke's account as the same event placed by theme."},
  {"id":"woesmeal","harmony":"§107","lane":"jesus","place":"judeacountry","date":"Autumn AD 32","title":"Woes at a Pharisee's table","people":["Jesus","A Pharisee","The lawyers"],"gospels":{"Lk":"11:37–54"},"summary":"A Pharisee invites Jesus to a meal and is astonished that he does not first wash. Jesus says they clean the outside of the cup while inside they are full of greed. He pronounces woes on Pharisees who tithe herbs but neglect justice and the love of God, and on lawyers who load others with burdens. From then on the scribes and Pharisees press him hard, lying in wait to catch him in something he might say.","stay":"A guest at a Pharisee's midday meal.","scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 11:42","text":"\"But woe to you Pharisees! for you tithe mint and rue and every herb, and neglect justice and the love of God; these you ought to have done, without neglecting the others."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Micah 6:8","note":"\"To do justice, and to love kindness, and to walk humbly with your God.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"Curses Against the Pharisees","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Jesus rises at a banquet table under a colonnade and stretches out his arm in rebuke, while the reclining guests recoil and argue.","significance":"Tissot sets the woes at a meal, as Luke does in chapter 11.","symbolism":"The laden table and the angry faces set outward piety against inward greed, the theme of Jesus' words about cup and dish.","impact":"A dramatic picture of the growing break between Jesus and the Pharisees.","img":"img/woesmeal0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Curses_Against_the_Pharisees_(Imprécations_contre_les_pharisiens)_-_James_Tissot.jpg"}],"tags":["teaching","conflict"]},
  {"id":"richfool","harmony":"§108","lane":"jesus","place":"judeacountry","date":"Autumn AD 32","title":"The rich fool, and do not be anxious","people":["Jesus","The disciples","A man in the crowd","Peter","A vast crowd"],"gospels":{"Lk":"12:1–59"},"summary":"With thousands crowding around, Jesus warns his disciples against the leaven of the Pharisees and tells them not to fear. When a man asks him to settle an inheritance, he tells of a rich fool who builds bigger barns and dies that very night. He tells them not to be anxious, like the ravens and the lilies, and to be like servants waiting for their master's return. He speaks of the baptism he must undergo, and of the division his coming will bring.","scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 12:20–21","text":"But God said to him, 'Fool! This night your soul is required of you; and the things you have prepared, whose will they be?' So is he who lays up treasure for himself, and is not rich toward God.\""},{"ref":"Luke 12:27","text":"Consider the lilies, how they grow; they neither toil nor spin; yet I tell you, even Solomon in all his glory was not arrayed like one of these."},{"ref":"Luke 12:32","text":"\"Fear not, little flock, for it is your Father's good pleasure to give you the kingdom."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Sirach 11:18–19","note":"A man grows rich and says, \"I have found rest\", not knowing his time will pass."},{"ref":"Psalm 49:16–17","note":"When the rich man dies he will carry nothing away."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"The Parable of the Rich Fool","artist":"Rembrandt","year":"1627","medium":"Oil on oak panel","location":"Gemäldegalerie, Berlin","description":"An old man in a cluttered room full of ledgers, money bags and papers holds a coin up to a candle and peers at it through his spectacles.","significance":"An early work painted in Leiden when Rembrandt was about 21. It is also known as \"The Money Changer.\"","symbolism":"The candle, hidden by his hand, lights only the coin and his face: the man's whole world has shrunk to his money, with the dark pressing in around him.","impact":"One of Rembrandt's first experiments with dramatic candlelight, a hallmark of his later art.","img":"img/richfool0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Der_Geldwechsler_-_Gemäldegalerie_Berlin_-_5210540.jpg"},{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"The Man Who Hoards","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"A rich man sits brooding among bulging sacks of grain, while a shadowy figure with a blade looms in the gloom above him.","significance":"Illustrates the parable of Luke 12:16–21.","symbolism":"The hidden figure behind him stands for the death he has not planned for: \"This night your soul is required of you.\"","impact":"Turns the parable into a chilling image that is easy to remember.","img":"img/richfool1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Man_Who_Hoards_(L'homme_qui_thésaurise)_-_James_Tissot.jpg"}],"tags":["parable","teaching"],"orderNote":"Much of this teaching also appears in Matthew's Sermon on the Mount and other discourses. Robertson notes that Jesus often repeated his favorite sayings on different occasions."},
  {"id":"figtree","harmony":"§109","lane":"jesus","place":"judeacountry","date":"Autumn AD 32","title":"Repent or perish: the barren fig tree","people":["Jesus","Some who told him of the Galileans"],"gospels":{"Lk":"13:1–9"},"summary":"Some tell Jesus of Galileans whose blood Pilate mingled with their sacrifices. He asks whether they, or the eighteen killed when the tower in Siloam fell, were worse sinners than others, and answers: \"Unless you repent you will all likewise perish.\" Then he tells of a fig tree that has borne no fruit for three years. The vinedresser begs for one more year to dig around it and manure it.","scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 13:3","text":"I tell you, No; but unless you repent you will all likewise perish."},{"ref":"Luke 13:8–9","text":"And he answered him, 'Let it alone, sir, this year also, till I dig about it and put on manure. And if it bears fruit next year, well and good; but if not, you can cut it down.'\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Isaiah 5:1–7","note":"The song of the vineyard that yields wild grapes."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"The Vine Dresser and the Fig Tree","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"A vinedresser crouches with his spade at the roots of a gnarled, leafless fig tree, while the owner stands beside him looking at it.","significance":"Illustrates the parable of Luke 13:6–9.","symbolism":"The bare tree stands for a people given time to bear fruit; the digging and manure are the patient mercy that pleads for one more year.","impact":"Draws attention to the vinedresser, whose plea many readers see as an image of Christ interceding.","img":"img/figtree0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Vine_Dresser_and_the_Fig_Tree_(Le_vigneron_et_le_figuier)_-_James_Tissot.jpg"},{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"The Tower of Siloam","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"A stone tower collapses in a cloud of dust, its beams and blocks crashing down as people run for their lives from the archway below.","significance":"Illustrates Luke 13:4, the disaster Jesus mentions, known only from this verse.","symbolism":"The sudden fall makes Jesus' point: death can come to anyone without warning, so repent now.","impact":"Turns a passing remark into a vivid scene.","img":"img/figtree1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Tower_of_Siloam_(Le_tour_de_Siloë)_-_James_Tissot.jpg"}],"tags":["parable","teaching"],"orderNote":"Neither tragedy is recorded outside Luke. Pilate's harshness is known from Josephus, and the tower of Siloam probably stood on the walls near the pool."},
  {"id":"crippledwoman","harmony":"§110","lane":"jesus","place":"judeacountry","date":"Autumn AD 32","title":"The bent woman healed on the sabbath","people":["Jesus","A woman bent over for eighteen years","The ruler of the synagogue"],"gospels":{"Lk":"13:10–21"},"summary":"Teaching in a synagogue on the sabbath, Jesus sees a woman who has been bent double for eighteen years. He lays his hands on her and she stands up straight and praises God. The ruler of the synagogue protests; Jesus answers that they untie an ox or donkey on the sabbath, so surely this daughter of Abraham should be set free. He repeats the parables of the mustard seed and the leaven.","scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 13:12–13","text":"And when Jesus saw her, he called her and said to her, \"Woman, you are freed from your infirmity.\" And he laid his hands upon her, and immediately she was made straight, and she praised God."},{"ref":"Luke 13:16","text":"And ought not this woman, a daughter of Abraham whom Satan bound for eighteen years, be loosed from this bond on the sabbath day?\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Deuteronomy 5:15","note":"The sabbath as a remembrance of Israel's release from bondage."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"The Woman with an Infirmity of Eighteen Years","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"In a synagogue hung with lamps, a woman bent almost double stands before Jesus as he reaches toward her; the elders on the benches look on.","significance":"Illustrates Luke 13:10–17, a healing found only in Luke.","symbolism":"Her bent body pictures bondage; Jesus calls her \"a daughter of Abraham\" and sets her free on the day of rest.","impact":"Shows the public, sabbath setting that sparked the ruler's protest.","img":"img/crippledwoman0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Woman_with_an_Infirmity_of_Eighteen_Years_(La_femme_malade_depuis_dix-huit_ans)_-_James_Tissot.jpg"}],"tags":["miracle","healing","conflict","parable"]},
  {"id":"dedication","harmony":"§111","lane":"jesus","place":"temple","from":"judeacountry","date":"Winter AD 32 (Feast of Dedication)","title":"At the Feast of Dedication","people":["Jesus","The Jews"],"gospels":{"Jn":"10:22–39"},"summary":"It is winter, the feast of Hanukkah, and Jesus is walking in Solomon's portico in the Temple. The Jews gather round and demand that he tell them plainly whether he is the Christ. He points to his works, says his sheep hear his voice, and declares, \"I and the Father are one.\" They take up stones again and try to arrest him, but he escapes from their hands.","journey":"A few miles back to Jerusalem.","route":[[35.3,31.85],[35.27,31.83],[35.25,31.8],[35.235,31.778]],"scripture":[{"ref":"John 10:27–28","text":"My sheep hear my voice, and I know them, and they follow me; and I give them eternal life, and they shall never perish, and no one shall snatch them out of my hand."},{"ref":"John 10:30","text":"I and the Father are one.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"1 Maccabees 4:52–59","note":"Judas Maccabeus rededicates the altar in 164 BC and decrees the yearly eight-day feast."},{"ref":"Psalm 82:6","note":"\"I say, 'You are gods'\", which Jesus quotes in his defense."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"Jesus Walks in the Portico of Solomon","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Jesus, in white, walks with his disciples through a long colonnade of the Temple, while people sit and talk in the shade of the columns.","significance":"Illustrates John 10:23, set in winter at the Feast of Dedication.","symbolism":"Solomon's portico on the east side of the Temple recalls the king who built the first Temple, where Jesus now claims to be one with the Father.","impact":"Later the apostles also gathered in Solomon's portico (Acts 3:11; 5:12).","img":"img/dedication0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Jesus_Walks_in_the_Portico_of_Solomon_(Jésus_se_promène_dans_le_portique_de_Salomon)_-_James_Tissot.jpg"}],"tags":["teaching","conflict"],"orderNote":"Robertson dates this to December AD 29, about two months after Tabernacles and three to four months before the crucifixion."},
  {"id":"beyondjordan","harmony":"§112","lane":"jesus","place":"bethabara","from":"temple","date":"Winter AD 32–33","title":"Withdrawal beyond the Jordan","people":["Jesus","The disciples","The people who came to him"],"gospels":{"Jn":"10:40–42"},"summary":"Jesus leaves Jerusalem and goes back across the Jordan to the place where John had first baptized, and stays there. Many come to him. They say that John did no sign, but everything John said about this man was true. Many believe in him there.","stay":"He stayed some time at the place where John had baptized, in Perea, under Herod Antipas.","journey":"About 25 miles, down the desert road to Jericho and across the Jordan, a long day or two on foot.","route":[[35.235,31.778],[35.262,31.771],[35.35,31.82],[35.45,31.865],[35.5,31.85],[35.556,31.84]],"scripture":[{"ref":"John 10:41–42","text":"And many came to him; and they said, \"John did no sign, but everything that John said about this man was true.\" And many believed in him there."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"Al-Maghtas, Bethany beyond the Jordan","artist":"Producer (Wikimedia Commons)","year":"2009","medium":"Photograph","location":"Al-Maghtas, Jordan","description":"Excavated pools and stone steps at the baptism site east of the Jordan, under modern shelters, beside the wooded river valley.","significance":"Al-Maghtas has been a UNESCO World Heritage Site since 2015. Excavations found churches, pools and hermit caves from the Roman and Byzantine periods.","symbolism":"Jesus returns to the place where John had first pointed him out, and finds that John's witness has borne fruit.","impact":"Helps the reader picture the quiet refuge across the river, out of reach of the Jerusalem authorities.","img":"img/beyondjordan0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Producer, CC BY 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bethany_(5).JPG"}],"tags":["teaching"],"orderNote":"Robertson places Luke 13:22 to 17:10 in this Perean period between Dedication and the raising of Lazarus. Many scholars instead read Luke's material as one long final journey to Jerusalem."},
  {"id":"herodfox","harmony":"§113","lane":"jesus","place":"perea","from":"bethabara","date":"Winter AD 32–33","title":"\"Go and tell that fox\"","people":["Jesus","Someone who asked about the saved","Some Pharisees"],"gospels":{"Lk":"13:22–35"},"summary":"Jesus goes through towns and villages teaching, journeying toward Jerusalem. Asked whether few will be saved, he says to strive to enter by the narrow door. Some Pharisees warn him that Herod Antipas wants to kill him. He sends word to \"that fox\" that he will finish his work, for a prophet cannot perish away from Jerusalem, and he laments over the city that kills the prophets.","journey":"Luke says only \"through towns and villages\"; Perea lay along the east side of the Jordan Valley.","route":[[35.556,31.84],[35.6,31.92],[35.63,32.0],[35.65,32.05]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 13:32","text":"And he said to them, \"Go and tell that fox, 'Behold, I cast out demons and perform cures today and tomorrow, and the third day I finish my course."},{"ref":"Luke 13:34","text":"O Jerusalem, Jerusalem, killing the prophets and stoning those who are sent to you! How often would I have gathered your children together as a hen gathers her brood under her wings, and you would not!"}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Psalm 118:26","note":"\"Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord\", quoted at the end of the lament."},{"ref":"Jeremiah 22:5","note":"\"This house shall become a desolation.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"He Went Through the Villages on the Way to Jerusalem","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Jesus and his disciples pass a hillside village of flat-roofed stone houses, as people come out from the houses to see him.","significance":"Illustrates Luke 13:22: \"He went on his way through towns and villages, teaching, and journeying toward Jerusalem.\"","symbolism":"The road keeps leading on toward Jerusalem, where Jesus says a prophet must die.","impact":"Captures the slow, village-by-village movement of this part of the ministry.","img":"img/herodfox0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_He_Went_Through_the_Villages_on_the_Way_to_Jerusalem_(Il_allait_par_les_villages_en_route_pour_Jérusalem)_-_James_Tissot_-_overall.jpg"}],"tags":["teaching","prophecy","conflict"],"orderNote":"Matthew places a nearly identical lament over Jerusalem in the Temple during Holy Week (Matthew 23:37–39). Robertson treats them as two occasions."},
  {"id":"greatbanquet","harmony":"§114","lane":"jesus","place":"perea","date":"Winter AD 32–33","title":"Dropsy healed, and the great banquet","people":["Jesus","A ruler of the Pharisees","A man with dropsy","The lawyers and Pharisees","The guests"],"gospels":{"Lk":"14:1–24"},"summary":"Dining on the sabbath at the house of a leading Pharisee, Jesus heals a man with dropsy and silences his critics. Seeing guests choose the places of honor, he tells them to take the lowest place. He tells his host to invite the poor, the maimed, the lame and the blind. Then he tells of a great banquet whose invited guests all make excuses, so the master fills his house with the poor and with people from the highways and hedges.","stay":"A guest at a sabbath meal in a Pharisee's house.","scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 14:11","text":"For every one who exalts himself will be humbled, and he who humbles himself will be exalted.\""},{"ref":"Luke 14:13–14","text":"But when you give a feast, invite the poor, the maimed, the lame, the blind, and you will be blessed, because they cannot repay you. You will be repaid at the resurrection of the just.\""},{"ref":"Luke 14:23","text":"And the master said to the servant, 'Go out to the highways and hedges, and compel people to come in, that my house may be filled."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Proverbs 25:6–7","note":"Do not put yourself forward; better to be told, \"Come up here.\""},{"ref":"Isaiah 25:6","note":"The Lord's feast of rich food for all peoples on his mountain."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"The Parable of the Great Banquet","artist":"Brunswick Monogrammist","year":"c. 1525","medium":"Oil on panel","location":"National Museum, Warsaw","description":"A crowded Renaissance palace: in the foreground the poor and the crippled are brought in from the street, while the feast goes on at tables in the halls beyond.","significance":"By an unidentified Antwerp master named after a monogram on a painting in Brunswick. He set Gospel scenes among the busy street life of his own day.","symbolism":"The people of the highways and hedges fill the master's house, while the invited guests are nowhere to be seen.","impact":"A rare large painting of this parable, packed with lively details of 16th-century Flemish life.","img":"img/greatbanquet0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brunswick_Monogrammist_Great_Banquet.jpg"}],"tags":["miracle","healing","parable","teaching"],"orderNote":"Matthew's parable of the wedding feast (Matthew 22:1–14), told in Holy Week, is similar. Robertson treats them as two different parables."},
  {"id":"costdisciple","harmony":"§115","lane":"jesus","place":"perea","date":"Winter AD 32–33","title":"Count the cost","people":["Jesus","Great crowds"],"gospels":{"Lk":"14:25–35"},"summary":"Great crowds are following him. Jesus turns and tells them that anyone who does not bear his own cross and come after him cannot be his disciple. A man who builds a tower first sits down and counts the cost; a king going to war first weighs his strength. So whoever does not renounce all that he has cannot be his disciple. Salt that has lost its taste is thrown away.","scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 14:27","text":"Whoever does not bear his own cross and come after me, cannot be my disciple."},{"ref":"Luke 14:28","text":"For which of you, desiring to build a tower, does not first sit down and count the cost, whether he has enough to complete it?"}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"Jesus Traveling","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Jesus and his disciples walk along a broad hillside track, followed at a distance by other travelers and pack animals.","significance":"Pictures the scene of Luke 14:25: \"Now great multitudes accompanied him; and he turned and said to them...\"","symbolism":"The long road and the following crowd frame Jesus' warning: to follow him all the way means carrying the cross.","impact":"Helps the reader picture the long walks between the places on this map.","img":"img/costdisciple0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Jesus_Traveling_(Jésus_en_voyage)_-_James_Tissot.jpg"}],"tags":["teaching","parable"]},
  {"id":"lostsheep","harmony":"§116a","lane":"jesus","place":"perea","date":"Winter AD 32–33","title":"The lost sheep and the lost coin","people":["Jesus","Tax collectors and sinners","The Pharisees and scribes"],"gospels":{"Lk":"15:1–10"},"summary":"Tax collectors and sinners come near to hear Jesus, and the Pharisees and scribes grumble that he receives sinners and eats with them. He answers with parables. A shepherd leaves the ninety-nine to find one lost sheep and carries it home rejoicing. A woman lights a lamp and sweeps the house until she finds her lost silver coin. So there is joy in heaven over one sinner who repents.","scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 15:4","text":"\"What man of you, having a hundred sheep, if he has lost one of them, does not leave the ninety-nine in the wilderness, and go after the one which is lost, until he finds it?"},{"ref":"Luke 15:10","text":"Just so, I tell you, there is joy before the angels of God over one sinner who repents.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Ezekiel 34:16","note":"\"I will seek the lost, and I will bring back the strayed.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"The Lost Drachma","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"In a dark house, a woman crouches on the stone floor beside a small oil lamp, searching every corner for her lost coin.","significance":"Illustrates the parable of Luke 15:8–10.","symbolism":"The lamp and the sweeping show God's careful, personal search for each one who is lost.","impact":"Brings out the homely detail of the parable: a poor woman for whom one silver coin truly matters.","img":"img/lostsheep0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Lost_Drachma_(La_drachme_perdue)_-_James_Tissot_-_overall.jpg"}],"tags":["parable","teaching"],"orderNote":"Matthew gives the lost sheep in a different setting, a lesson to the disciples about little ones (Matthew 18:12–14, §91)."},
  {"id":"prodigal","harmony":"§116b","lane":"jesus","place":"perea","date":"Winter AD 32–33","title":"The prodigal son","people":["Jesus","The Pharisees and scribes","Tax collectors and sinners"],"gospels":{"Lk":"15:11–32"},"summary":"A younger son takes his share of his father's property and squanders it in a far country. Starving and feeding pigs, he comes to himself and goes home to confess. While he is still far off his father runs to embrace him, clothes him and kills the fatted calf. The elder son refuses to join the feast, and the father pleads with him: his brother was dead and is alive again, was lost and is found.","scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 15:20","text":"And he arose and came to his father. But while he was yet at a distance, his father saw him and had compassion, and ran and embraced him and kissed him."},{"ref":"Luke 15:31–32","text":"And he said to him, 'Son, you are always with me, and all that is mine is yours. It was fitting to make merry and be glad, for this your brother was dead, and is alive; he was lost, and is found.'\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Genesis 33:4","note":"Esau runs to meet Jacob, embraces him and kisses him."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"The Return of the Prodigal Son","artist":"Rembrandt","year":"c. 1668","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"State Hermitage Museum, St Petersburg","description":"The ragged son kneels with his shaven head pressed against his father's chest; the old father, in a red cloak, lays both hands on his back. The elder brother stands to the right, stiff and apart.","significance":"One of Rembrandt's last works, painted near the end of his life.","symbolism":"The father's two hands are painted differently, one broad and strong, one gentle, often read as a father's and a mother's love together.","impact":"Among the most loved religious paintings in the world; Henri Nouwen's book on it (1992) made it famous to millions.","img":"img/prodigal0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rembrandt_Harmensz_van_Rijn_-_Return_of_the_Prodigal_Son_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg"},{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"The Prodigal Son Begging","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"The younger son, thin and in rags, stands alone on a hillside with a shepherd's crook, the pigs he tends rooting behind him.","significance":"Illustrates Luke 15:15–16, the son's lowest point.","symbolism":"Feeding pigs, unclean animals to a Jew, shows how far he has fallen before he \"came to himself.\"","impact":"Makes the son's misery concrete, so the welcome home feels all the greater.","img":"img/prodigal1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Prodigal_Son_Begging_(L'enfant_prodigue_mendiant)_-_James_Tissot.jpg"}],"tags":["parable","teaching"]},
  {"id":"unjuststeward","harmony":"§117a","lane":"jesus","place":"perea","date":"Winter AD 32–33","title":"The dishonest steward","people":["Jesus","The disciples","The Pharisees"],"gospels":{"Lk":"16:1–18"},"summary":"A rich man's steward is about to be dismissed for wasting his goods. He quickly calls in his master's debtors and reduces their bills, so that they will welcome him later. The master commends him for his shrewdness. Jesus tells his disciples to use worldly wealth to make friends for eternity, for no one can serve two masters. The Pharisees, lovers of money, scoff at him.","scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 16:10","text":"\"He who is faithful in a very little is faithful also in much; and he who is dishonest in a very little is dishonest also in much."},{"ref":"Luke 16:13","text":"No servant can serve two masters; for either he will hate the one and love the other, or he will be devoted to the one and despise the other. You cannot serve God and mammon.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"The Parable of the Unjust Steward","artist":"Follower of Marinus van Reymerswaele","year":"16th century","medium":"Oil on panel","location":"National Museum, Warsaw","description":"A rich master in a fur-trimmed coat sits at a desk crowded with ledgers and papers, raising his hand as his steward, in red, looks up anxiously.","significance":"Marinus van Reymerswaele and his followers specialized in sharp-eyed scenes of bankers, tax collectors and stewards at their accounts.","symbolism":"The account books stand for the reckoning: \"Turn in the account of your stewardship.\"","impact":"Shows how Netherlandish painters used the parable to comment on money and business in their own day.","img":"img/unjuststeward0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marinus_Claeszoon_van_Reymerswaele_-_Parable_of_the_shrewd_manager_(Luke_16-1-13)_-_M.Ob.593_MNW_-_National_Museum_in_Warsaw.jpg"}],"tags":["parable","teaching"]},
  {"id":"richmanlazarus","harmony":"§117b","lane":"jesus","place":"perea","date":"Winter AD 32–33","title":"The rich man and Lazarus","people":["Jesus","The Pharisees"],"gospels":{"Lk":"16:19–31"},"summary":"A rich man feasts every day in purple and fine linen, while a poor man named Lazarus lies at his gate covered with sores. Both die. Lazarus is carried by angels to Abraham's bosom; the rich man is in torment in Hades. Abraham tells him a great chasm is fixed between them, and that if his brothers do not hear Moses and the prophets, they will not be convinced even if someone rises from the dead.","scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 16:25","text":"But Abraham said, 'Son, remember that you in your lifetime received your good things, and Lazarus in like manner evil things; but now he is comforted here, and you are in anguish."},{"ref":"Luke 16:31","text":"He said to him, 'If they do not hear Moses and the prophets, neither will they be convinced if some one should rise from the dead.'\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Amos 6:4–7","note":"Woe to those who lie on beds of ivory and feast, but do not grieve over the ruin of Joseph."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"The Poor Lazarus at the Rich Man's Door","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Lazarus, wrapped in rags, sits on the cobbles by the steps of a great stone house while stray dogs lick his sores.","significance":"Illustrates Luke 16:20–21.","symbolism":"The closed door and the dogs set the rich man's indifference against the poor man's need; only the dogs show him any care.","impact":"Tissot also painted the sequel, the rich man in torment.","img":"img/richmanlazarus0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Poor_Lazarus_at_the_Rich_Man's_Door_(Le_pauvre_Lazare_à_la_porte_du_riche)_-_James_Tissot.jpg"}],"tags":["parable","teaching"],"orderNote":"This is the only parable in which a character is given a name. Medieval tradition called the rich man \"Dives\", Latin for \"rich\"."},
  {"id":"unworthyservants","harmony":"§117c","lane":"jesus","place":"perea","date":"Winter AD 32–33","title":"Faith, forgiveness and unworthy servants","people":["Jesus","The disciples","The apostles"],"gospels":{"Lk":"17:1–10"},"summary":"Jesus warns his disciples not to cause little ones to sin, and tells them to forgive a brother who repents, even seven times a day. The apostles say, \"Increase our faith!\" He answers that faith as small as a grain of mustard seed could uproot a tree. A servant who has done all he was told has only done his duty, and should say, \"We are unworthy servants.\"","scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 17:6","text":"And the Lord said, \"If you had faith as a grain of mustard seed, you could say to this sycamine tree, 'Be rooted up, and be planted in the sea,' and it would obey you."},{"ref":"Luke 17:10","text":"So you also, when you have done all that is commanded you, say, 'We are unworthy servants; we have only done what was our duty.'\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"The Man at the Plough","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"A farmer drives a plough pulled by a pair of donkeys across a stony hillside field.","significance":"A scene of everyday farm work, the kind of labor Jesus uses in Luke 17:7: \"Will any one of you, who has a servant plowing...\"","symbolism":"The servant who has plowed all day still serves his master's supper: duty done is not a claim on reward.","impact":"Shows the hard, ordinary work behind Jesus' images of service.","img":"img/unworthyservants0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Man_at_the_Plough_(L'homme_à_la_charrue)_-_James_Tissot_-_overall.jpg"}],"tags":["teaching","parable"]},
  {"id":"lazarus","harmony":"§118","lane":"jesus","place":"bethany","from":"perea","date":"Early AD 33","title":"The raising of Lazarus","people":["Jesus","Lazarus","Martha","Mary","Thomas","The disciples","Mourners from Jerusalem"],"gospels":{"Jn":"11:1–44"},"summary":"Martha and Mary send word that their brother Lazarus is ill. Jesus waits two days, then goes back to Judea, though the disciples fear he will be stoned. Lazarus has been in the tomb four days. Jesus tells Martha, \"I am the resurrection and the life\", and she confesses him as the Christ. Jesus weeps, has the stone taken away, and cries, \"Lazarus, come out.\" The dead man walks out, still bound in his grave clothes.","stay":"He stayed outside the village at first; Martha and then Mary came out to meet him.","journey":"Probably 25 to 30 miles from Perea, across the Jordan, through Jericho and up the desert road, a journey of a day or two.","route":[[35.65,32.05],[35.63,32.0],[35.6,31.92],[35.556,31.84],[35.5,31.85],[35.45,31.865],[35.35,31.82],[35.262,31.771]],"scripture":[{"ref":"John 11:25–26","text":"Jesus said to her, \"I am the resurrection and the life; he who believes in me, though he die, yet shall he live, and whoever lives and believes in me shall never die. Do you believe this?\""},{"ref":"John 11:35","text":"Jesus wept."},{"ref":"John 11:43–44","text":"When he had said this, he cried with a loud voice, \"Lazarus, come out.\" The dead man came out, his hands and feet bound with bandages, and his face wrapped with a cloth. Jesus said to them, \"Unbind him, and let him go.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Ezekiel 37:12–13","note":"\"I will open your graves, and raise you from your graves, O my people.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"The Raising of Lazarus","artist":"Giotto","year":"c. 1304–06","medium":"Fresco","location":"Scrovegni (Arena) Chapel, Padua","description":"Christ raises his hand toward Lazarus, who stands in the mouth of the tomb wrapped like a mummy. Martha and Mary kneel at Jesus' feet, and onlookers cover their noses.","significance":"Part of Giotto's cycle of the lives of the Virgin and Christ, a turning point in Western painting.","symbolism":"The grave clothes, still grey with death, face the living gesture of Christ; it foreshadows Christ's own resurrection, shown in the same chapel.","impact":"Giotto's solid, human figures and real emotion opened the way to the Renaissance.","img":"img/lazarus0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giotto_di_Bondone_-_No._25_Scenes_from_the_Life_of_Christ_-_9._Raising_of_Lazarus_-_WGA09204.jpg"},{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"The Raising of Lazarus","artist":"Caravaggio","year":"1609","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Museo Regionale, Messina","description":"In a dark, bare room, men lift the stiff body of Lazarus from the tomb as Christ, at the left, points toward him. A beam of light falls across Lazarus, whose arms open in the shape of a cross.","significance":"Painted in Sicily while Caravaggio was on the run, for a merchant family named Lazzari.","symbolism":"Lazarus is caught between death and life: one hand falls toward a skull, the other opens toward the light.","impact":"One of the great works of Caravaggio's last years.","img":"img/lazarus1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Michelangelo_Caravaggio_006.jpg"}],"tags":["miracle","raising"],"orderNote":"Robertson notes that Jesus seems to have been two or three days' journey away (John 11:6, 17), probably in Perea. A tomb cut in the rock at al-Eizariya (the town's Arabic name comes from \"Lazarus\") has been honored as Lazarus's tomb since at least the 4th century."},
  {"id":"ephraim","harmony":"§119","lane":"jesus","place":"ephraim","from":"bethany","date":"Early AD 33","title":"Caiaphas's counsel; retreat to Ephraim","people":["Jesus","The disciples","Caiaphas","The chief priests and Pharisees"],"gospels":{"Jn":"11:45–54"},"summary":"Many who saw Lazarus raised believe in Jesus, but some report it to the Pharisees. The council meets, afraid that the Romans will come and destroy the Temple and the nation. Caiaphas, high priest that year, says it is better that one man should die for the people; John sees in this an unwitting prophecy. From that day they plan to put him to death, so Jesus withdraws with his disciples to a town called Ephraim, near the wilderness.","stay":"He stayed at Ephraim with his disciples until shortly before the Passover.","journey":"About 14 miles north from Bethany into the hills near the wilderness.","route":[[35.262,31.771],[35.25,31.82],[35.245,31.88],[35.27,31.93],[35.3,31.95]],"scripture":[{"ref":"John 11:49–50","text":"But one of them, Caiaphas, who was high priest that year, said to them, \"You know nothing at all; you do not understand that it is expedient for you that one man should die for the people, and that the whole nation should not perish.\""},{"ref":"John 11:54","text":"Jesus therefore no longer went about openly among the Jews, but went from there to the country near the wilderness, to a town called Ephraim; and there he stayed with the disciples."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"He Went on His Way to Ephraim","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Jesus and a few disciples walk down a rocky path among olive trees and steep banks, away from Jerusalem.","significance":"Illustrates John 11:54.","symbolism":"The narrow, lonely path pictures his withdrawal after the council's decision to put him to death.","impact":"A rare image of this little-known retreat before the last Passover.","img":"img/ephraim0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_He_Went_on_His_Way_to_Ephraim_(Il_s'en_alla_à_Ephrem)_-_James_Tissot.jpg"}],"tags":["conflict","passion","prophecy"],"orderNote":"Ephraim is usually identified with et-Taiyiba (Taybeh), about 4 miles northeast of Bethel, the Ophrah of the Old Testament. Robertson thinks it lay in the north of Judea."},
  {"id":"tenlepers","harmony":"§120","lane":"jesus","place":"burqin","from":"ephraim","date":"Spring AD 33","title":"The ten lepers","people":["Jesus","Ten lepers","A Samaritan","The Pharisees","The disciples"],"gospels":{"Lk":"17:11–37"},"summary":"On the way to Jerusalem, passing between Samaria and Galilee, Jesus is met by ten lepers who stand far off and cry for mercy. He sends them to show themselves to the priests, and on the way they are made clean. Only one, a Samaritan, comes back praising God and falls at Jesus' feet. Asked by the Pharisees when the kingdom of God will come, Jesus says it is \"in the midst of you\", and warns the disciples of the sudden day of the Son of man.","journey":"About 40 miles north from Ephraim along the hill road through Samaria, past Sychar, to the edge of the Jezreel Valley.","route":[[35.3,31.95],[35.25,32.02],[35.27,32.12],[35.285,32.209],[35.27,32.33],[35.263,32.457]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 17:15–16","text":"Then one of them, when he saw that he was healed, turned back, praising God with a loud voice; and he fell on his face at Jesus' feet, giving him thanks. Now he was a Samaritan."},{"ref":"Luke 17:20–21","text":"Being asked by the Pharisees when the kingdom of God was coming, he answered them, \"The kingdom of God is not coming with signs to be observed; nor will they say, 'Lo, here it is!' or 'There!' for behold, the kingdom of God is in the midst of you.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Leviticus 13:45–46","note":"The leper must live apart and cry, \"Unclean, unclean.\""},{"ref":"2 Kings 5:14–15","note":"Naaman the Syrian, a foreigner, is cleansed and returns to give thanks."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"The Healing of Ten Lepers","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"On a rocky hillside outside a town, ten lepers wrapped in cloths kneel and raise their arms toward Jesus, who stands at a distance in white.","significance":"Illustrates Luke 17:11–19.","symbolism":"The lepers keep their distance as the Law required; the healing that follows lets them return to their families.","impact":"Shows the scene from far off, as Luke says they \"stood at a distance.\"","img":"img/tenlepers0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Healing_of_Ten_Lepers_(Guérison_de_dix_lépreux)_-_James_Tissot_-_overall.jpg"}],"tags":["miracle","healing","teaching","prophecy"],"orderNote":"Robertson follows Wieseler: from Ephraim Jesus went north through Samaria to the edge of Galilee, to join the Galilean pilgrims going down through Perea. That explains why Luke names Samaria first. The village is not named; a tradition places it at Burqin near Jenin."},
  {"id":"prayerparables","harmony":"§121","lane":"jesus","place":"burqin","date":"Spring AD 33","title":"The widow and the judge; the Pharisee and the tax collector","people":["Jesus","The disciples","Some who trusted in themselves"],"gospels":{"Lk":"18:1–14"},"summary":"To teach that they ought always to pray and not lose heart, Jesus tells of a widow who keeps coming to an unjust judge until he gives her justice. How much more will God vindicate his elect who cry to him day and night. Then he tells of a Pharisee who thanks God he is not like other men, and a tax collector who stands far off and beats his breast. It is the tax collector who goes home justified.","scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 18:7","text":"And will not God vindicate his elect, who cry to him day and night? Will he delay long over them?"},{"ref":"Luke 18:13–14","text":"But the tax collector, standing far off, would not even lift up his eyes to heaven, but beat his breast, saying, 'God, be merciful to me a sinner!' I tell you, this man went down to his house justified rather than the other; for every one who exalts himself will be humbled, but he who humbles himself will be exalted.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Sirach 35:17","note":"\"The prayer of the humble pierces the clouds.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"The Pharisee and the Publican","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"In a richly decorated Temple hall, a Pharisee in a prayer shawl stands upright in the foreground, while the tax collector hides by a pillar in the back, head bowed.","significance":"Illustrates Luke 18:10–14.","symbolism":"Distance and posture tell the story: the proud man stands forward, the humble one stays far off and will not lift his eyes.","impact":"A clear picture of the reversal at the heart of the parable.","img":"img/prayerparables0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tissot_The_Pharisee_and_the_publican_Brooklyn.jpg"}],"tags":["parable","prayer","teaching"],"orderNote":"Luke gives no place for these parables. Robertson places them on the last journey, after the healing of the ten lepers and before Jesus crosses into Perea."},
  {"id":"divorce","harmony":"§122","lane":"jesus","place":"perea","from":"burqin","date":"Spring AD 33","title":"On marriage and divorce","people":["Jesus","The Pharisees","The disciples","Crowds"],"gospels":{"Mt":"19:1–12","Mk":"10:1–12"},"summary":"Jesus leaves Galilee and comes into the region of Judea beyond the Jordan, and crowds gather again. Pharisees test him: is it lawful for a man to divorce his wife? He points back to creation: God made them male and female, and the two become one flesh. What God has joined together, no one must separate; Moses allowed divorce because of their hardness of heart. In the house the disciples ask again, and he speaks of those who renounce marriage for the kingdom.","journey":"About 45 miles, east through the Jezreel Valley to Scythopolis, across the Jordan and south through Perea with the Galilean pilgrims.","route":[[35.263,32.457],[35.38,32.48],[35.5,32.5],[35.57,32.45],[35.6,32.3],[35.63,32.15],[35.65,32.05]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 10:9","text":"What therefore God has joined together, let not man put asunder.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Genesis 2:24","note":"\"A man leaves his father and his mother and cleaves to his wife, and they become one flesh.\""},{"ref":"Deuteronomy 24:1","note":"The certificate of divorce the Pharisees cite."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"The Scribe Stood to Tempt Jesus","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"On a stony hillside, a man in a striped robe stands and questions Jesus, who sits among his disciples; the Judean hills stretch away behind.","significance":"Tissot made it for the lawyer's test in Luke 10:25. It fits this scene too, where the Pharisees come \"to test him\" with a question of the Law.","symbolism":"The standing questioner and the seated teacher set a challenge against calm authority.","impact":"Shows the open-air setting in which such debates took place along the road.","img":"img/divorce0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Scribe_Stood_to_Tempt_Jesus_(Le_scribe_se_leva_pour_tenter_Jésus)_-_James_Tissot_-_overall.jpg"}],"tags":["teaching","conflict"],"orderNote":"Here Matthew and Mark rejoin the story after a long gap. Matthew 19:1 links this departure to the end of the Galilean teaching (§92), and Robertson notes that we must suppose a break in their narrative. Matthew alone includes the exception \"except for unchastity\"."},
  {"id":"children","harmony":"§123","lane":"jesus","place":"perea","date":"Spring AD 33","title":"Let the children come to me","people":["Jesus","The disciples","Parents and their children"],"gospels":{"Mt":"19:13–15","Mk":"10:13–16","Lk":"18:15–17"},"summary":"People bring little children, even infants, to Jesus so that he might touch them and pray for them. The disciples rebuke them. Jesus is indignant: the kingdom of God belongs to such as these, and whoever does not receive it like a child shall not enter it. He takes the children in his arms and blesses them.","scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 10:14–15","text":"But when Jesus saw it he was indignant, and said to them, \"Let the children come to me, do not hinder them; for to such belongs the kingdom of God. Truly, I say to you, whoever does not receive the kingdom of God like a child shall not enter it.\""},{"ref":"Mark 10:16","text":"And he took them in his arms and blessed them, laying his hands upon them."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"Christ Blessing the Children","artist":"Lucas Cranach the Elder","year":"after 1537","medium":"Oil on panel","location":"Statens Museum for Kunst, Copenhagen","description":"Christ holds a baby and lays his hand on others as mothers press around him with their infants; the apostles look on from the left. A German inscription runs across the top.","significance":"Cranach's workshop painted many versions of this subject, which became popular in Lutheran Germany.","symbolism":"The infants in their mothers' arms were read as an argument for infant baptism, against the Anabaptists of the day.","impact":"One of the most recognizable images of the Reformation era.","img":"img/children0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lucas_Cranach_d.Ä._-_Christ_Blessing_the_Children_-_KMS2087_-_Statens_Museum_for_Kunst.jpg"}],"tags":["teaching"]},
  {"id":"richruler","harmony":"§124a","lane":"jesus","place":"perea","date":"Spring AD 33","title":"The rich young ruler","people":["Jesus","A rich young ruler","Peter","The disciples"],"gospels":{"Mt":"19:16–30","Mk":"10:17–31","Lk":"18:18–30"},"summary":"A man runs up and kneels, asking what he must do to inherit eternal life. He has kept the commandments from his youth. Jesus looks at him, loves him, and tells him to sell what he has, give to the poor, and follow him; the man goes away sorrowful, for he has great possessions. Jesus says it is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle than for a rich man to enter the kingdom, but with God all things are possible. Those who have left everything will receive a hundredfold and eternal life.","scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 10:21","text":"And Jesus looking upon him loved him, and said to him, \"You lack one thing; go, sell what you have, and give to the poor, and you will have treasure in heaven; and come, follow me.\""},{"ref":"Mark 10:25","text":"It is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle than for a rich man to enter the kingdom of God.\""},{"ref":"Mark 10:27","text":"Jesus looked at them and said, \"With men it is impossible, but not with God; for all things are possible with God.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Exodus 20:12–16","note":"The commandments Jesus lists to the young man."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"The Rich Young Man Went Away Sorrowful","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"A young man in fine clothes walks away down a hillside path, head lowered, while Jesus and the disciples stand behind him on the road.","significance":"Illustrates Mark 10:22.","symbolism":"He walks toward the viewer and away from Jesus, turning his back on the invitation \"come, follow me.\"","impact":"Captures the sadness of a calling refused.","img":"img/richruler0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Rich_Young_Man_Went_Away_Sorrowful_(Le_jeune_homme_riche_s'en_alla_triste)_-_James_Tissot_-_overall.jpg"}],"tags":["teaching","call"],"orderNote":"Only Matthew calls him young and only Luke calls him a ruler; Mark alone says that Jesus looked at him and loved him."},
  {"id":"laborers","harmony":"§124b","lane":"jesus","place":"perea","date":"Spring AD 33","title":"The laborers in the vineyard","people":["Jesus","The disciples"],"gospels":{"Mt":"20:1–16"},"summary":"A householder hires laborers for his vineyard at dawn for a denarius, and goes out again at nine, noon, three and five o'clock to hire more. At evening he pays everyone the same, starting with the last. Those who worked all day grumble. The master answers that he has done them no wrong: may he not be generous with what is his? So the last will be first, and the first last.","scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 20:15–16","text":"Am I not allowed to do what I choose with what belongs to me? Or do you begrudge my generosity?' So the last will be first, and the first last.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Deuteronomy 24:15","note":"Pay the hired laborer his wages on the same day, before the sun goes down."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"The Parable of the Laborers in the Vineyard","artist":"Rembrandt","year":"1637","medium":"Oil on panel","location":"State Hermitage Museum, St Petersburg","description":"In a dim room lit by a tall window, the householder sits at a table as workers crowd in; some take their pay, while one at the front argues.","significance":"A small, dark panel from Rembrandt's Amsterdam years.","symbolism":"The light from the window falls on the paying of wages: every worker receives the same denarius, a picture of grace.","impact":"One of the few paintings of this parable by a major master.","img":"img/laborers0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rembrandt_-_Parable_of_the_Laborers_in_the_Vineyard.jpg"},{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"The First Shall Be Last","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Jesus stands among palm trees and speaks to disciples seated on a low wall around him.","significance":"Illustrates the saying that frames the parable (Matthew 19:30; 20:16).","symbolism":"Jesus stands while his hearers sit: the teacher who will take the last place of a servant.","impact":"Shows the parable as part of Jesus' teaching to his own disciples.","img":"img/laborers1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_First_Shall_Be_Last_(Le_premier_sera_le_dernier)_-_James_Tissot_-_overall.jpg"}],"tags":["parable","teaching"]},
  {"id":"thirdprediction","harmony":"§125","lane":"jesus","place":"perea","date":"Spring AD 33","title":"Going up to Jerusalem: the third passion prediction","people":["Jesus","The Twelve","James","John","Their mother"],"gospels":{"Mt":"20:17–28","Mk":"10:32–45","Lk":"18:31–34"},"summary":"On the road going up to Jerusalem, Jesus walks ahead and the disciples are amazed and afraid. He takes the Twelve aside and tells them a third time that the Son of man will be condemned, mocked, scourged and killed, and will rise after three days. James and John, with their mother, ask for the seats at his right and left in his glory. Jesus tells the indignant Twelve that whoever would be great must be their servant, for the Son of man came to give his life as a ransom for many.","scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 10:33–34","text":"\"Behold, we are going up to Jerusalem; and the Son of man will be delivered to the chief priests and the scribes, and they will condemn him to death, and deliver him to the Gentiles; and they will mock him, and spit upon him, and scourge him, and kill him; and after three days he will rise.\""},{"ref":"Mark 10:45","text":"For the Son of man also came not to be served but to serve, and to give his life as a ransom for many.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Isaiah 53:11–12","note":"The Servant who bears the sin of many."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"Christ and the Wife and Sons of Zebedee","artist":"Paolo Veronese","year":"c. 1565","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Musée de Grenoble","description":"The mother of James and John kneels before Christ, presenting her two sons, while the other apostles look on beside a classical portico.","significance":"Veronese often set Gospel scenes among the grand architecture and rich fabrics of Venice.","symbolism":"The kneeling mother asks for glory; Christ will answer by speaking of the cup he must drink.","impact":"A rare large painting of this request, which Matthew alone puts in the mother's mouth.","img":"img/thirdprediction0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paul_Véronèse_-_Christ_rencontrant_la_femme_de_Zébédée.jpg"}],"tags":["prophecy","passion","teaching"],"orderNote":"In Mark, James and John make the request themselves; in Matthew their mother asks for them. Robertson places this in Perea, before the crossing of the Jordan to Jericho."},
  {"id":"bartimaeus","harmony":"§126","lane":"jesus","place":"jericho","from":"perea","date":"Spring AD 33","title":"Blind Bartimaeus","people":["Jesus","Bartimaeus son of Timaeus","A second blind man","The disciples","A great crowd"],"gospels":{"Mt":"20:29–34","Mk":"10:46–52","Lk":"18:35–43"},"summary":"At Jericho a blind beggar, Bartimaeus, hears that Jesus of Nazareth is passing and cries out, \"Jesus, Son of David, have mercy on me!\" The crowd tells him to be quiet, but he cries all the more. Jesus calls him; he throws off his cloak and comes. \"Go your way; your faith has made you well\", and at once he sees and follows Jesus on the road.","journey":"About 20 miles, down the Perean side of the valley and across the Jordan ford to Jericho.","route":[[35.65,32.05],[35.63,32.0],[35.6,31.92],[35.556,31.84],[35.5,31.86],[35.45,31.865]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 10:47","text":"And when he heard that it was Jesus of Nazareth, he began to cry out and say, \"Jesus, Son of David, have mercy on me!\""},{"ref":"Mark 10:51–52","text":"And Jesus said to him, \"What do you want me to do for you?\" And the blind man said to him, \"Master, let me receive my sight.\" And Jesus said to him, \"Go your way; your faith has made you well.\" And immediately he received his sight and followed him on the way."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Jeremiah 31:8","note":"The blind and the lame among those God gathers home."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"Christ Healing the Blind of Jericho","artist":"Nicolas Poussin","year":"1650","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Musée du Louvre, Paris","description":"Christ reaches out to touch the eyes of a kneeling blind man, while a second blind man gropes forward and the apostles and townspeople watch; a walled town rises behind.","significance":"Follows Matthew's account of two blind men. It became a model for French academic painters.","symbolism":"The calm, ordered composition centers on Christ's single touch; the reaching hands of the blind show their faith.","impact":"In 1667 it was the subject of a famous lecture at the French Royal Academy, used to teach how a picture should tell a story.","img":"img/bartimaeus0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Poussin_-_Les_Aveugles_de_Jéricho,_1650,_INV_7281_;_MR_2320.jpg"},{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"The Two Blind Men at Jericho","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Two blind men stand on a rocky bank by the road, arms raised, calling out, while a crowd streams past below the town walls.","significance":"Illustrates Matthew 20:29–34.","symbolism":"Their raised arms and loud cries show the persistence the crowd tries to silence.","impact":"Puts the reader at the roadside where Bartimaeus sat.","img":"img/bartimaeus1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Two_Blind_Men_at_Jericho_(Les_deux_aveugles_à_Jericho)_-_James_Tissot.jpg"}],"tags":["miracle","healing"],"orderNote":"Matthew has two blind men; Mark and Luke describe one, probably the better known. Mark and Matthew say Jesus was leaving Jericho, Luke that he was drawing near. Robertson suggests he had left the old town and was approaching Herod's new Jericho nearby."},
  {"id":"zacchaeus","harmony":"§127a","lane":"jesus","place":"jericho","date":"Spring AD 33, about a week before Passover","title":"Zacchaeus","people":["Jesus","Zacchaeus","The crowd"],"gospels":{"Lk":"19:1–10"},"summary":"Zacchaeus, a chief tax collector and rich, wants to see who Jesus is, but he is small, so he climbs a sycamore tree. Jesus stops, calls him by name and says he must stay at his house today. The crowd grumbles that he is the guest of a sinner. Zacchaeus promises half his goods to the poor and fourfold repayment to anyone he has cheated. \"Today salvation has come to this house.\"","stay":"He stayed as a guest in Zacchaeus' house in Jericho.","scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 19:5","text":"And when Jesus came to the place, he looked up and said to him, \"Zacchaeus, make haste and come down; for I must stay at your house today.\""},{"ref":"Luke 19:9–10","text":"And Jesus said to him, \"Today salvation has come to this house, since he also is a son of Abraham. For the Son of man came to seek and to save the lost.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Exodus 22:1","note":"The Law's fourfold restitution for a stolen sheep."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"Zacchaeus in the Sycamore Awaiting the Passage of Jesus","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Zacchaeus, in a yellow robe, straddles a great branch of a sycamore tree; boys climb in the branches, and Jesus and his disciples approach along the road below.","significance":"Illustrates Luke 19:1–6.","symbolism":"The rich tax collector, climbing like a child, gives up his dignity to see Jesus.","impact":"One of the best known images of the story, often reproduced in children's Bibles.","img":"img/zacchaeus0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Zacchaeus_in_the_Sycamore_Awaiting_the_Passage_of_Jesus_(Zachée_sur_le_sycomore_attendant_le_passage_de_Jésus)_-_James_Tissot.jpg"}],"tags":["call","teaching"],"orderNote":"Jericho's \"Zacchaeus tree\", an old sycamore fig in the town, is a local tradition."},
  {"id":"pounds","harmony":"§127b","lane":"jesus","place":"jericho","date":"Spring AD 33, about a week before Passover","title":"The parable of the pounds","people":["Jesus","The disciples","The crowd"],"gospels":{"Lk":"19:11–28"},"summary":"Because he is near Jerusalem and the people think the kingdom will appear at once, Jesus tells a parable. A nobleman goes to a far country to receive a kingdom, leaving ten servants a pound each to trade with, while his citizens send word that they do not want him to reign. On his return he rewards the faithful with cities to rule and takes the pound from the servant who hid it. Then Jesus goes on ahead, going up to Jerusalem.","scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 19:17","text":"And he said to him, 'Well done, good servant! Because you have been faithful in a very little, you shall have authority over ten cities.'"},{"ref":"Luke 19:26","text":"'I tell you, that to every one who has will more be given; but from him who has not, even what he has will be taken away."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"The Entrusted Pounds (relief)","artist":"Paulamaria Walter","year":"1963","medium":"Stone relief (photograph)","location":"Straßdorf, Schwäbisch Gmünd, Germany","description":"A crowned nobleman strides away with outstretched arm while his servants stand in a block holding golden coins; one figure apart clutches a single coin.","significance":"A modern public relief on the parable, one of the Wege zur Kunst (Paths to Art) works in Schwäbisch Gmünd.","symbolism":"The crown recalls that the nobleman goes away to receive a kingdom, the point Luke's version adds to the story.","impact":"Shows how the parable still speaks in modern public art.","img":"img/pounds0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Pedemann, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Die_anvertrauten_Pfunde.jpg"}],"tags":["parable","teaching","prophecy"],"orderNote":"Robertson distinguishes this from the parable of the talents told a few days later (Matthew 25:14–30, §139); here the point is to check the crowd's hope of an immediate kingdom. Many readers hear an echo of Archelaus, who went to Rome in 4 BC to be confirmed as king while a Jewish embassy opposed him (Josephus, Antiquities 17)."},
  {"id":"arrivebethany","harmony":"§128a","lane":"jesus","place":"bethany","from":"jericho","date":"Friday before Passion Week, AD 33","title":"Jesus arrives at Bethany","people":["Jesus","The disciples","Lazarus","Martha","Mary","The crowds"],"gospels":{"Jn":"11:55–12:1, 12:9–11"},"summary":"Passover is near, and pilgrims crowd into Jerusalem early to purify themselves. They look for Jesus in the Temple and wonder if he will dare come, since the chief priests have ordered anyone who knows where he is to report it. Six days before the Passover, Jesus comes up from Jericho to Bethany, where he had raised Lazarus. Crowds come out to see both Jesus and Lazarus, and the chief priests plan to kill Lazarus too.","stay":"Bethany, with his friends Martha, Mary and Lazarus; he went back there each night of the week.","journey":"About 15 miles, climbing some 3,400 ft from the Jordan Valley on the steep Roman road up through the Judean wilderness.","route":[[35.45,31.865],[35.4,31.835],[35.36,31.815],[35.33,31.8],[35.3,31.785],[35.262,31.771]],"scripture":[{"ref":"John 11:56","text":"They were looking for Jesus and saying to one another as they stood in the temple, \"What do you think? That he will not come to the feast?\""},{"ref":"John 12:1","text":"Six days before the Passover, Jesus came to Bethany, where Lazarus was, whom Jesus had raised from the dead."},{"ref":"John 12:9–11","text":"When the great crowd of the Jews learned that he was there, they came, not only on account of Jesus but also to see Lazarus, whom he had raised from the dead. So the chief priests planned to put Lazarus also to death, because on account of him many of the Jews were going away and believing in Jesus."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1889","medium":"Pen and ink on paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","img":"img/arrivebethany0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Valley_of_Jehosaphat_Coming_from_Bethany_(Vall%C3%A9e_Josaphat_en_venant_de_B%C3%A9thanie)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"Valley of Jehoshaphat, Coming from Bethany","description":"A bare, sweeping hillside falls into the Kidron (Jehoshaphat) Valley, with the walls of Jerusalem along the ridge at the upper left, as seen by a traveler on the road from Bethany.","significance":"One of Tissot's pen-and-ink studies of the landscape of Palestine, drawn on his travels and used for the Life of Christ.","symbolism":"This is the view Jesus and the pilgrims had each day of Passion Week, walking from Bethany over the Mount of Olives to the city.","impact":"Tissot's on-the-spot drawings are a record of the land's terraces, roads and villages before modern building."}],"tags":["passion"],"orderNote":"John sets the supper and anointing at Bethany here (12:2–8), right after the arrival. Robertson thinks John mentions it early only because it is his last word on Bethany, and follows Mark, who places it on the Tuesday evening (§141). Robertson dates the week to AD 30, or AD 29 if John 5:1 was not a Passover."},
  {"id":"palmsunday","harmony":"§128b","lane":"jesus","place":"temple","from":"bethany","date":"Sunday, Passion Week, AD 33","title":"The triumphal entry into Jerusalem","people":["Jesus","Two disciples","The crowds","The Pharisees","The children in the Temple"],"gospels":{"Mt":"21:1–11, 14–17","Mk":"11:1–11","Lk":"19:29–44","Jn":"12:12–19"},"summary":"Near Bethphage, Jesus sends two disciples to bring a colt that has never been ridden. He rides it down the Mount of Olives while the crowds spread cloaks and branches on the road and shout \"Hosanna to the Son of David!\" When the Pharisees tell him to silence his disciples, he says the very stones would cry out. Seeing the city, he weeps over it and foretells its destruction. In the Temple he heals the blind and lame and accepts the children's praise, then goes back to Bethany for the night.","stay":"He looked around the Temple and, as it was late, went out to Bethany with the Twelve (Mark 11:11).","journey":"About 2 miles from Bethany over the Mount of Olives by Bethphage, down across the Kidron Valley and up into the Temple.","route":[[35.262,31.771],[35.255,31.78],[35.247,31.779],[35.24,31.778],[35.235,31.778]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 21:5","text":"\"Tell the daughter of Zion, Behold, your king is coming to you, humble, and mounted on an ass, and on a colt, the foal of an ass.\""},{"ref":"Matthew 21:9","text":"And the crowds that went before him and that followed him shouted, \"Hosanna to the Son of David! Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord! Hosanna in the highest!\""},{"ref":"Luke 19:41–42","text":"And when he drew near and saw the city he wept over it, saying, \"Would that even today you knew the things that make for peace! But now they are hid from your eyes."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Zechariah 9:9","note":"\"Lo, your king comes to you... humble and riding on an ass, on a colt the foal of an ass.\""},{"ref":"Psalm 118:25–26","note":"The pilgrim psalm of Passover: \"Save us\" (Hosanna) and \"Blessed be he who enters in the name of the Lord!\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/palmsunday0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giotto_di_Bondone_-_No._26_Scenes_from_the_Life_of_Christ_-_10._Entry_into_Jerusalem_-_WGA09206.jpg","title":"Entry into Jerusalem","artist":"Giotto","year":"c. 1305","medium":"Fresco","location":"Scrovegni (Arena) Chapel, Padua","description":"Christ rides a donkey toward the city gate, blessing, with the apostles behind him. Boys climb trees to cut branches, and townspeople spread their cloaks on the ground before him.","significance":"Part of Giotto's fresco cycle of the life of Christ in the chapel built by Enrico Scrovegni, a turning point toward natural, weighty figures in Western painting.","symbolism":"Christ moves steadily toward the gate on the right, the direction of the Passion scenes that follow on the chapel wall.","impact":"The Scrovegni Chapel is a UNESCO World Heritage Site (2021) and one of the founding monuments of Renaissance painting."},{"kind":"Descriptive","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","img":"img/palmsunday1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Procession_on_the_Mount_of_Olives_(Le_cort%C3%A8ge_sur_le_mont_des_Oliviers)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"The Procession on the Mount of Olives","description":"Seen from below, a crowd winds down a stony hillside path. Men carry palm branches and lay cloaks on the road as Jesus comes down on the colt.","significance":"One of about 350 watercolors in Tissot's Life of Christ, painted after his journeys to Palestine in 1886–87 and 1889.","symbolism":"The steep, rocky path and olive trees show the real descent from Bethphage toward the Kidron and the city.","impact":"Shown in Paris in 1894 and bought by the Brooklyn Museum in 1900; the series shaped how a generation pictured the Gospel world."}],"tags":["prophecy","passion"],"orderNote":"Mark says Jesus only looked around the Temple on Sunday and cleansed it on Monday. Matthew and Luke tell the cleansing straight after the entry; Robertson follows Mark's more exact day-by-day order. Matthew alone mentions both an ass and her colt."},
  {"id":"figcursed","harmony":"§129a","lane":"jesus","place":"bethphage","from":"bethany","date":"Monday, Passion Week, AD 33","title":"The barren fig tree cursed","people":["Jesus","The disciples"],"gospels":{"Mt":"21:18–19","Mk":"11:12–14"},"summary":"On Monday morning, walking in from Bethany, Jesus is hungry. He sees a fig tree in full leaf, but finds nothing on it but leaves, \"for it was not the season for figs.\" He says that no one will ever eat fruit from it again, and the disciples hear him. It is an acted parable: a show of leaves without fruit, like the Temple religion he will confront the same morning.","stay":"He spent the night at Bethany and walked back into the city in the morning.","journey":"About a mile from Bethany up toward Bethphage on the road to Jerusalem.","route":[[35.262,31.771],[35.258,31.776],[35.255,31.78]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 11:13–14","text":"And seeing in the distance a fig tree in leaf, he went to see if he could find anything on it. When he came to it, he found nothing but leaves, for it was not the season for figs. And he said to it, \"May no one ever eat fruit from you again.\" And his disciples heard it."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Jeremiah 8:13","note":"\"There are no grapes on the vine, nor figs on the fig tree\": the prophets' picture of a fruitless people."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","img":"img/figcursed0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Accursed_Fig_Tree_(Le_figuier_maudit)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"The Accursed Fig Tree","description":"Jesus turns away from a large fig tree in full leaf beside a stone wall, one hand raised to his head. The disciples follow along the dusty road behind him.","significance":"One of about 350 watercolors in Tissot's Life of Christ, painted after his journeys to Palestine in 1886–87 and 1889.","symbolism":"The tree is thick with leaves but has no fruit: an image of outward show without the fruit God looks for.","impact":"Shown in Paris in 1894 and bought by the Brooklyn Museum in 1900; the series shaped how a generation pictured the Gospel world."}],"tags":["miracle","nature-miracle","prophecy"],"orderNote":"Matthew tells the cursing and the withering together, as if in one morning (21:18–22). Mark spreads them over two days, with the Temple cleansing in between, and Robertson follows Mark. The exact place on the road is not known."},
  {"id":"secondcleansing","harmony":"§129b","lane":"jesus","place":"temple","from":"bethphage","date":"Monday, Passion Week, AD 33","title":"Jesus cleanses the Temple","people":["Jesus","Money-changers","Sellers of doves","The chief priests and scribes","The crowds"],"gospels":{"Mt":"21:12–13","Mk":"11:15–18","Lk":"19:45–48"},"summary":"In the Temple courts Jesus drives out those who buy and sell, overturns the tables of the money-changers and the seats of the dove sellers, and will not let anyone carry goods through the Temple. Quoting Isaiah and Jeremiah, he says God's house is to be a house of prayer for all the nations, but they have made it a den of robbers. The chief priests and scribes look for a way to destroy him, but they fear him, because the people hang on his words.","journey":"About a mile on from Bethphage down the Mount of Olives and into the Temple.","route":[[35.255,31.78],[35.247,31.779],[35.24,31.778],[35.235,31.778]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 11:15–16","text":"And they came to Jerusalem. And he entered the temple and began to drive out those who sold and those who bought in the temple, and he overturned the tables of the money-changers and the seats of those who sold pigeons; and he would not allow any one to carry anything through the temple."},{"ref":"Mark 11:17","text":"And he taught, and said to them, \"Is it not written, 'My house shall be called a house of prayer for all the nations'? But you have made it a den of robbers.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Isaiah 56:7","note":"\"My house shall be called a house of prayer for all peoples.\""},{"ref":"Jeremiah 7:11","note":"\"Has this house, which is called by my name, become a den of robbers in your eyes?\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/secondcleansing0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giotto_di_Bondone_-_No._27_Scenes_from_the_Life_of_Christ_-_11._Expulsion_of_the_Money-changers_from_the_Temple_-_WGA09209.jpg","title":"Expulsion of the Money-changers from the Temple","artist":"Giotto","year":"c. 1305","medium":"Fresco","location":"Scrovegni (Arena) Chapel, Padua","description":"Before a Gothic temple facade, Christ raises a whip of cords at the traders. Tables are overturned, animals run, and two children shelter among the apostles, one clutching a dove.","significance":"It follows the Entry into Jerusalem in Giotto's Passion sequence, as in Mark's order of the week.","symbolism":"The frightened children seek safety with the disciples, while the priests gather at the right to plot: the cleansing sets the Passion in motion.","impact":"One of the most reproduced images of the scene in Western art."},{"kind":"Descriptive","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","img":"img/secondcleansing1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Jesus_Forbids_the_Carrying_of_Loads_in_the_Forecourt_of_the_Temple_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"Jesus Forbids the Carrying of Loads in the Forecourt of the Temple","description":"On the steps of a Temple gateway, Jesus in white turns back people carrying jars and bundles on their heads, who were using the courts as a shortcut.","significance":"One of about 350 watercolors in Tissot's Life of Christ, painted after his journeys to Palestine in 1886–87 and 1889.","symbolism":"It illustrates a detail found only in Mark (11:16): Jesus would not let anyone carry goods through the Temple, treating even its outer court as holy ground.","impact":"Shown in Paris in 1894 and bought by the Brooklyn Museum in 1900; the series shaped how a generation pictured the Gospel world."}],"tags":["conflict","prophecy"],"orderNote":"John tells of a cleansing at the first Passover of the ministry (John 2:13–22). Robertson, like many, holds that Jesus cleansed the Temple twice, at the start and the end; many other scholars think there was one cleansing, which John placed early for theological reasons."},
  {"id":"greeks","harmony":"§130","lane":"jesus","place":"temple","date":"Monday, Passion Week, AD 33","title":"Some Greeks ask to see Jesus","people":["Jesus","Some Greeks","Philip","Andrew","The crowd","The voice from heaven"],"gospels":{"Jn":"12:20–50"},"summary":"Some Greeks who have come up to worship at the feast ask Philip, \"Sir, we wish to see Jesus.\" Philip tells Andrew, and they tell Jesus. He answers that his hour has come: like a grain of wheat, he must die to bear much fruit. Troubled in soul, he prays \"Father, glorify your name,\" and a voice answers from heaven. He says that when he is lifted up from the earth he will draw all to himself. John closes Jesus' public ministry with the people's unbelief and his last appeal to believe in the light.","scripture":[{"ref":"John 12:20–21","text":"Now among those who went up to worship at the feast were some Greeks. So these came to Philip, who was from Beth-saida in Galilee, and said to him, \"Sir, we wish to see Jesus.\""},{"ref":"John 12:24","text":"Truly, truly, I say to you, unless a grain of wheat falls into the earth and dies, it remains alone; but if it dies, it bears much fruit."},{"ref":"John 12:32","text":"and I, when I am lifted up from the earth, will draw all men to myself.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Isaiah 53:1","note":"\"Lord, who has believed our report?\", quoted by John (12:38) of the people's unbelief."},{"ref":"Isaiah 6:9–10","note":"The blinded eyes and hardened heart, also quoted in John 12:40."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","img":"img/greeks0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Gentiles_Ask_to_See_Jesus_(Les_gentils_demandent_%C3%A0_voir_J%C3%A9sus)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"The Gentiles Ask to See Jesus","description":"In a colonnade of the Temple overlooking the city, Jesus in white stands at the right while a disciple points him out to a group of strangers in foreign dress.","significance":"One of about 350 watercolors in Tissot's Life of Christ, painted after his journeys to Palestine in 1886–87 and 1889.","symbolism":"Tissot sets the scene on the edge of the Temple, where Gentiles could come no further than the outer court.","impact":"Shown in Paris in 1894 and bought by the Brooklyn Museum in 1900; the series shaped how a generation pictured the Gospel world."}],"tags":["teaching","prophecy","passion"],"orderNote":"Only John tells this, without naming the day. Robertson places it on the Monday in the Temple. The Greeks were probably Gentile God-fearers, who could pray only in the outer Court of the Gentiles."},
  {"id":"figwithered","harmony":"§131","lane":"jesus","place":"bethphage","from":"bethany","date":"Tuesday, Passion Week, AD 33","title":"The fig tree found withered","people":["Jesus","Peter","The disciples"],"gospels":{"Mt":"21:19–22","Mk":"11:19–25","Lk":"21:37–38"},"summary":"Each evening Jesus leaves the city, and each morning the people come early to hear him in the Temple. On Tuesday morning, passing the same way, the disciples see the fig tree withered to its roots. Peter remembers and points it out. Jesus answers with a lesson on faith and prayer: whoever believes without doubting could tell a mountain to be cast into the sea. He adds that when they pray they must forgive.","stay":"Luke says he spent the nights \"on the mount called Olivet\" (21:37), which includes Bethany on its eastern slope.","journey":"About a mile from Bethany back up the road toward Bethphage.","route":[[35.262,31.771],[35.258,31.776],[35.255,31.78]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 11:20–21","text":"As they passed by in the morning, they saw the fig tree withered away to its roots. And Peter remembered and said to him, \"Master, look! The fig tree which you cursed has withered.\""},{"ref":"Mark 11:22–23","text":"And Jesus answered them, \"Have faith in God. Truly, I say to you, whoever says to this mountain, 'Be taken up and cast into the sea,' and does not doubt in his heart, but believes that what he says will come to pass, it will be done for him."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","img":"img/figwithered0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Jesus_Goes_in_the_Evening_to_Bethany_(J%C3%A9sus_va_le_soir_%C3%A0_B%C3%A9thanie)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"Jesus Goes in the Evening to Bethany","description":"Jesus walks down a stony path on the Mount of Olives with a disciple and some women behind him, the walls of Jerusalem rising across the valley.","significance":"One of about 350 watercolors in Tissot's Life of Christ, painted after his journeys to Palestine in 1886–87 and 1889.","symbolism":"It pictures Mark 11:19, \"when evening came they went out of the city\": the daily walk between Jerusalem and Bethany on which the disciples saw the withered tree.","impact":"Shown in Paris in 1894 and bought by the Brooklyn Museum in 1900; the series shaped how a generation pictured the Gospel world."}],"tags":["miracle","nature-miracle","teaching","prayer"],"orderNote":"Matthew says the tree withered \"at once\" and tells it on the same morning as the curse. Mark has the disciples notice it the next morning. Robertson takes Mark's two-day account as the exact one."},
  {"id":"byauthority","harmony":"§132a","lane":"jesus","place":"temple","from":"bethphage","date":"Tuesday, Passion Week, AD 33","title":"\"By what authority?\"","people":["Jesus","The chief priests","The scribes","The elders"],"gospels":{"Mt":"21:23–27","Mk":"11:27–33","Lk":"20:1–8"},"summary":"As Jesus walks in the Temple, a delegation of the Sanhedrin, chief priests, scribes and elders, demands to know by what authority he acts and who gave it to him. He answers with a question: was the baptism of John from heaven or from men? They dare not say \"from heaven,\" nor \"from men,\" since the people hold John to be a prophet. When they answer \"We do not know,\" Jesus refuses to tell them. It opens a long day of debate in the Temple courts.","journey":"About a mile on from Bethphage into the Temple.","route":[[35.255,31.78],[35.247,31.779],[35.24,31.778],[35.235,31.778]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 11:28","text":"and they said to him, \"By what authority are you doing these things, or who gave you this authority to do them?\""},{"ref":"Mark 11:30","text":"Was the baptism of John from heaven or from men? Answer me.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","img":"img/byauthority0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Chief_Priests_Ask_Jesus_by_What_Right_Does_He_Act_in_This_Way_(Les_princes_des_pr%C3%AAtres_demandent_%C3%A0_J%C3%A9sus_de_quel_droit_il_agit_ainsi)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"The Chief Priests Ask Jesus by What Right Does He Act in This Way","description":"Jesus, in white, raises a finger as he answers a group of elders and priests leaning on a low wall, while a crowd presses in behind them.","significance":"One of about 350 watercolors in Tissot's Life of Christ, painted after his journeys to Palestine in 1886–87 and 1889.","symbolism":"The barrier between Jesus and the rulers shows the line drawn on this day of controversy.","impact":"Shown in Paris in 1894 and bought by the Brooklyn Museum in 1900; the series shaped how a generation pictured the Gospel world."}],"tags":["conflict","teaching"],"orderNote":"Robertson calls Tuesday \"a day of controversy\": the leaders formally test Jesus' standing as a teacher, as they had the right to do, and he answers with three parables (the two sons, the wicked tenants, the wedding feast)."},
  {"id":"twosons","harmony":"§132b","lane":"jesus","place":"temple","date":"Tuesday, Passion Week, AD 33","title":"The parable of the two sons","people":["Jesus","The chief priests and elders"],"gospels":{"Mt":"21:28–32"},"summary":"A man asks his two sons to work in his vineyard. The first says no, but later repents and goes. The second says \"I go, sir,\" but does not go. Jesus asks which did his father's will, and the leaders answer, the first. He tells them that tax collectors and harlots, who believed John, are going into the kingdom of God before them.","scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 21:28–30","text":"\"What do you think? A man had two sons; and he went to the first and said, 'Son, go and work in the vineyard today.' And he answered, 'I will not'; but afterward he repented and went. And he went to the second and said the same; and he answered, 'I go, sir,' but did not go."},{"ref":"Matthew 21:31","text":"Which of the two did the will of his father?\" They said, \"The first.\" Jesus said to them, \"Truly, I say to you, the tax collectors and the harlots go into the kingdom of God before you."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1889","medium":"Pen and ink on paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","img":"img/twosons0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Vineyards_with_Their_Watch_Towers_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"Vineyards with Their Watch Towers","description":"Terraced vineyards of bare vines, enclosed by dry-stone walls, climb a hillside toward two round stone watchtowers.","significance":"One of Tissot's pen-and-ink studies of the landscape of Palestine, drawn on his travels and used for the Life of Christ.","symbolism":"The walls and towers are the ones Jesus describes in his vineyard parables (Matthew 21:33): the setting of the two sons sent to work in their father's vineyard.","impact":"Tissot's on-the-spot drawings are a record of the land's terraces, roads and villages before modern building."}],"tags":["parable","conflict"]},
  {"id":"tenants","harmony":"§132c","lane":"jesus","place":"temple","date":"Tuesday, Passion Week, AD 33","title":"The parable of the wicked tenants","people":["Jesus","The chief priests, scribes and Pharisees","The people"],"gospels":{"Mt":"21:33–46","Mk":"12:1–12","Lk":"20:9–19"},"summary":"A man plants a vineyard, lets it out to tenants and goes away. When he sends servants for his share of the fruit, the tenants beat and kill them. Last of all he sends his beloved son, and they kill him too, hoping to seize the inheritance. Jesus quotes the psalm of the rejected stone that becomes the cornerstone. The leaders see that he has told the parable against them and want to arrest him, but they fear the crowd.","scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 12:6","text":"He had still one other, a beloved son; finally he sent him to them, saying, 'They will respect my son.'"},{"ref":"Matthew 21:42","text":"Jesus said to them, \"Have you never read in the scriptures: 'The very stone which the builders rejected has become the head of the corner; this was the Lord's doing, and it is marvelous in our eyes'?"}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Isaiah 5:1–7","note":"The Song of the Vineyard: \"the vineyard of the Lord of hosts is the house of Israel.\""},{"ref":"Psalm 118:22–23","note":"\"The stone which the builders rejected has become the head of the corner.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","img":"img/tenants0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Son_of_the_Vineyard_(Le_fils_de_la_vigne)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"The Son of the Vineyard","description":"The owner's son lies dead on a heap of stones outside a walled vineyard, while the tenants run off across the terraces below the watchtowers.","significance":"One of about 350 watercolors in Tissot's Life of Christ, painted after his journeys to Palestine in 1886–87 and 1889.","symbolism":"Tissot paints the parable's climax, \"they took him and killed him, and cast him out of the vineyard\" (Mark 12:8), as a foreshadowing of Christ's own death outside the city.","impact":"Shown in Paris in 1894 and bought by the Brooklyn Museum in 1900; the series shaped how a generation pictured the Gospel world."}],"tags":["parable","conflict","prophecy"]},
  {"id":"weddingfeast","harmony":"§132d","lane":"jesus","place":"temple","date":"Tuesday, Passion Week, AD 33","title":"The parable of the wedding feast","people":["Jesus","The chief priests and Pharisees"],"gospels":{"Mt":"22:1–14"},"summary":"A king gives a marriage feast for his son, but the invited guests refuse to come; some mock and even kill his servants. The king sends his servants into the streets to bring in all they find, good and bad, until the hall is full. One guest is found without a wedding garment and is cast out. \"Many are called, but few are chosen.\"","scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 22:2","text":"\"The kingdom of heaven may be compared to a king who gave a marriage feast for his son,"},{"ref":"Matthew 22:9–10","text":"Go therefore to the thoroughfares, and invite to the marriage feast as many as you find.' And those servants went out into the streets and gathered all whom they found, both bad and good; so the wedding hall was filled with guests."},{"ref":"Matthew 22:14","text":"For many are called, but few are chosen.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Isaiah 25:6","note":"The Lord's feast for all peoples on his holy mountain."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/weddingfeast0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Didier Descouens, public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:(Venice)_The_Parable_of_the_Wedding_Banquet_by_Bernardo_Strozzi_-_Gallerie_Accademia.jpg","title":"The Parable of the Wedding Feast (detail)","artist":"Bernardo Strozzi","year":"c. 1636","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Gallerie dell'Accademia, Venice","description":"In this detail, the king, in a gold mantle and a heavy chain, flings out his arm in command, while an old servant at his side looks up at him.","significance":"Strozzi, a Genoese Capuchin friar turned painter, worked in Venice from about 1633, and his loose, rich brushwork helped renew Venetian painting.","symbolism":"The king's outstretched hand sends his servants out to the thoroughfares to gather guests, good and bad.","impact":"A fine example of Strozzi's late Venetian style, influenced by Veronese."}],"tags":["parable","teaching"],"orderNote":"Luke's parable of the great banquet (Luke 14:16–24) is similar, but it is set at a Pharisee's dinner earlier in the journey; Robertson treats the two as different parables."},
  {"id":"tribute","harmony":"§133","lane":"jesus","place":"temple","date":"Tuesday, Passion Week, AD 33","title":"Tribute to Caesar","people":["Jesus","Disciples of the Pharisees","The Herodians"],"gospels":{"Mt":"22:15–22","Mk":"12:13–17","Lk":"20:20–26"},"summary":"The Pharisees send some of their disciples with the Herodians to trap Jesus: is it lawful to pay taxes to Caesar or not? A yes would anger the people, a no would mark him as a rebel against Rome. Jesus asks for a coin and asks whose likeness and inscription it bears. \"Caesar's,\" they say. \"Render to Caesar the things that are Caesar's, and to God the things that are God's.\" They are amazed and fall silent.","scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 22:20–21","text":"And Jesus said to them, \"Whose likeness and inscription is this?\" They said, \"Caesar's.\" Then he said to them, \"Render therefore to Caesar the things that are Caesar's, and to God the things that are God's.\""},{"ref":"Mark 12:17","text":"Jesus said to them, \"Render to Caesar the things that are Caesar's, and to God the things that are God's.\" And they were amazed at him."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/tribute0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Titian_-_The_Tribute_Money_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg","title":"The Tribute Money","artist":"Titian","year":"c. 1560–68","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"The National Gallery, London","description":"A grey-bearded man in a rough shoulder cloth holds up a small coin to Christ, who looks back calmly and points upward with one finger.","significance":"A late work signed TITIANVS F., painted when Titian was in his seventies. He had painted the same subject about fifty years earlier (Gemäldegalerie, Dresden).","symbolism":"Christ's raised finger points beyond the coin to God, to whom the things that are God's belong.","impact":"Titian's two versions made the half-length 'Tribute Money' a favorite subject for later Venetian and Baroque painters."},{"kind":"Descriptive","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","img":"img/tribute1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Tribute_Money_(Le_denier_de_C%C3%A9sar)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"The Tribute Money","description":"On the steps of a Temple portico, Jesus in white looks down at a coin held out to him by a man in a turban, surrounded by curious elders and scholars.","significance":"One of about 350 watercolors in Tissot's Life of Christ, painted after his journeys to Palestine in 1886–87 and 1889.","symbolism":"The crowd of questioners pressing around Jesus shows the trap being sprung in public, before many witnesses.","impact":"Shown in Paris in 1894 and bought by the Brooklyn Museum in 1900; the series shaped how a generation pictured the Gospel world."}],"tags":["conflict","teaching"],"history":"The coin was a denarius, the Roman silver coin of a day's wage. Under Tiberius it carried his portrait and the inscription TI CAESAR DIVI AVG F AVGVSTVS, \"Tiberius Caesar, son of the divine Augustus, Augustus.\""},
  {"id":"sadducees","harmony":"§134","lane":"jesus","place":"temple","date":"Tuesday, Passion Week, AD 33","title":"The Sadducees and the resurrection","people":["Jesus","The Sadducees","Some scribes"],"gospels":{"Mt":"22:23–33","Mk":"12:18–27","Lk":"20:27–40"},"summary":"The Sadducees, who deny the resurrection, bring a puzzle: a woman married seven brothers in turn under the law of levirate marriage. Whose wife will she be in the resurrection? Jesus says they know neither the Scriptures nor the power of God. In the resurrection people do not marry but are like angels. And God called himself the God of Abraham, Isaac and Jacob: he is not God of the dead, but of the living.","scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 12:26–27","text":"And as for the dead being raised, have you not read in the book of Moses, in the passage about the bush, how God said to him, 'I am the God of Abraham, and the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob'? He is not God of the dead, but of the living; you are quite wrong.\""},{"ref":"Luke 20:38","text":"Now he is not God of the dead, but of the living; for all live to him.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Exodus 3:6","note":"God at the burning bush: \"I am the God of your father, the God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob.\""},{"ref":"Deuteronomy 25:5–6","note":"The law that a brother should marry his dead brother's widow, on which the puzzle rests."}],"art":[],"tags":["conflict","teaching"],"orderNote":"Robertson thinks this was probably a stock riddle the Sadducees had often used against the Pharisees."},
  {"id":"greatcommand","harmony":"§135","lane":"jesus","place":"temple","date":"Tuesday, Passion Week, AD 33","title":"The great commandment","people":["Jesus","A scribe (lawyer) of the Pharisees"],"gospels":{"Mt":"22:34–40","Mk":"12:28–34"},"summary":"Hearing that Jesus has silenced the Sadducees, a scribe asks him which commandment is the first of all. Jesus answers with the Shema: love the Lord your God with all your heart, soul, mind and strength. The second is like it: love your neighbor as yourself. The scribe agrees that this is more than all burnt offerings and sacrifices. Jesus tells him, \"You are not far from the kingdom of God,\" and after that no one dares ask him any question.","scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 12:29–31","text":"Jesus answered, \"The first is, 'Hear, O Israel: The Lord our God, the Lord is one; and you shall love the Lord your God with all your heart, and with all your soul, and with all your mind, and with all your strength.' The second is this, 'You shall love your neighbor as yourself.' There is no other commandment greater than these.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Deuteronomy 6:4–5","note":"The Shema, prayed daily: \"Hear, O Israel: The Lord our God is one Lord.\""},{"ref":"Leviticus 19:18","note":"\"You shall love your neighbor as yourself.\""}],"art":[],"tags":["teaching"],"orderNote":"Matthew presents the question as a test; Mark shows the scribe as sincere and praised by Jesus. Luke has a similar exchange with a lawyer much earlier, before the parable of the Good Samaritan (Luke 10:25–28)."},
  {"id":"davidsson","harmony":"§136","lane":"jesus","place":"temple","date":"Tuesday, Passion Week, AD 33","title":"Whose son is the Christ?","people":["Jesus","The Pharisees","The crowd"],"gospels":{"Mt":"22:41–46","Mk":"12:35–37","Lk":"20:41–44"},"summary":"Now Jesus asks the question. The scribes say the Christ is the son of David. Yet David, inspired by the Holy Spirit, calls him \"my Lord\" in Psalm 110. How then can he be David's son? No one can answer him a word, and the great crowd hears him gladly. The riddle points to a Messiah who is more than a royal descendant.","scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 12:36–37","text":"David himself, inspired by the Holy Spirit, declared, 'The Lord said to my Lord, Sit at my right hand, till I put your enemies under your feet.' David himself calls him Lord; so how is he his son?\" And the great throng heard him gladly."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Psalm 110:1","note":"\"The Lord says to my lord: 'Sit at my right hand.'\" The Old Testament verse most often quoted in the New."}],"art":[],"tags":["teaching","conflict"]},
  {"id":"sevenwoes","harmony":"§137","lane":"jesus","place":"temple","date":"Tuesday, Passion Week, AD 33","title":"Woes on the scribes and Pharisees","people":["Jesus","The crowds","The disciples","The scribes and Pharisees"],"gospels":{"Mt":"23:1–39","Mk":"12:38–40","Lk":"20:45–47"},"summary":"In his last public discourse, Jesus warns the crowds and his disciples against the scribes and Pharisees: do what they teach, but not what they do. They love the places of honor and long robes, and devour widows' houses. Then come seven woes against their hypocrisy: they tithe mint and dill but neglect justice, mercy and faith, and are like whitewashed tombs. He ends with a lament over Jerusalem, which kills the prophets, and whose children he would have gathered as a hen gathers her brood.","scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 23:11–12","text":"He who is greatest among you shall be your servant; whoever exalts himself will be humbled, and whoever humbles himself will be exalted."},{"ref":"Matthew 23:27","text":"\"Woe to you, scribes and Pharisees, hypocrites! for you are like whitewashed tombs, which outwardly appear beautiful, but within they are full of dead men's bones and all uncleanness."},{"ref":"Matthew 23:37","text":"\"O Jerusalem, Jerusalem, killing the prophets and stoning those who are sent to you! How often would I have gathered your children together as a hen gathers her brood under her wings, and you would not!"}],"echoes":[{"ref":"2 Chronicles 24:20–22","note":"The stoning of Zechariah son of Jehoiada in the Temple court, the last murder of a prophet in the Hebrew Bible's order (Matthew 23:35)."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","img":"img/sevenwoes0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Woe_unto_You,_Scribes_and_Pharisees_(Malheur_%C3%A0_vous,_scribes_et_pharisiens)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"Woe unto You, Scribes and Pharisees","description":"In a colonnade of the Temple, Jesus strides forward with his arm raised in denunciation, while scribes and Pharisees sit hunched on benches, hiding their faces.","significance":"One of about 350 watercolors in Tissot's Life of Christ, painted after his journeys to Palestine in 1886–87 and 1889.","symbolism":"The seated teachers on their benches recall \"Moses' seat\" (Matthew 23:2), the place of authority they have used for themselves.","impact":"Shown in Paris in 1894 and bought by the Brooklyn Museum in 1900; the series shaped how a generation pictured the Gospel world."},{"kind":"Descriptive","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","img":"img/sevenwoes1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Jerusalem_Jerusalem_(J%C3%A9rusalem_J%C3%A9rusalem)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"Jerusalem, Jerusalem","description":"Seen from behind, Jesus stands in a Temple court with arms spread wide over a great crowd seated on the pavement, the city rising beyond the walls.","significance":"One of about 350 watercolors in Tissot's Life of Christ, painted after his journeys to Palestine in 1886–87 and 1889.","symbolism":"His open arms echo the lament's image of a hen gathering her brood under her wings (Matthew 23:37).","impact":"Shown in Paris in 1894 and bought by the Brooklyn Museum in 1900; the series shaped how a generation pictured the Gospel world."}],"tags":["teaching","conflict","prophecy"],"orderNote":"Luke gives similar woes at a Pharisee's table in Galilee or Perea (Luke 11:37–54), and the lament over Jerusalem earlier on the journey (Luke 13:34–35). Robertson holds that Jesus spoke such words more than once; others think Matthew gathered them here."},
  {"id":"widowsmite","harmony":"§138","lane":"jesus","place":"temple","date":"Tuesday, Passion Week, AD 33","title":"The widow's mite","people":["Jesus","A poor widow","Rich donors","The disciples"],"gospels":{"Mk":"12:41–44","Lk":"21:1–4"},"summary":"Jesus sits opposite the treasury and watches people put in their gifts. Many rich people put in large sums. A poor widow puts in two copper coins, worth about a penny. Jesus calls his disciples and tells them she has given more than all the rest, for they gave from their abundance, but she out of her poverty gave everything she had. Robertson notes it is Jesus' last scene in the Temple.","scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 12:43–44","text":"And he called his disciples to him, and said to them, \"Truly, I say to you, this poor widow has put in more than all those who are contributing to the treasury. For they all contributed out of their abundance; but she out of her poverty has put in everything she had, her whole living.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","img":"img/widowsmite0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Widow's_Mite_(Le_denier_de_la_veuve)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"The Widow's Mite","description":"A poor woman in a plain grey mantle walks away from the Temple treasury carrying a child, while Jesus, seated with his disciples by the doorway, points her out to them.","significance":"One of about 350 watercolors in Tissot's Life of Christ, painted after his journeys to Palestine in 1886–87 and 1889.","symbolism":"A richly dressed man strides out of the same door behind her: the contrast is the whole lesson.","impact":"Shown in Paris in 1894 and bought by the Brooklyn Museum in 1900; the series shaped how a generation pictured the Gospel world."}],"tags":["teaching"],"history":"The \"treasury\" was probably the thirteen trumpet-shaped offering chests in the Court of the Women, described in the Mishnah (Shekalim 6.5). The widow's coins were lepta, the smallest bronze coins in circulation."},
  {"id":"olivet","harmony":"§139a","lane":"jesus","place":"olives","from":"temple","date":"Tuesday afternoon, Passion Week, AD 33","title":"The Olivet discourse","people":["Jesus","Peter","James","John","Andrew"],"gospels":{"Mt":"24:1–51","Mk":"13:1–37","Lk":"21:5–36"},"summary":"As they leave the Temple, a disciple admires its huge stones. Jesus says not one stone will be left on another. Sitting on the Mount of Olives opposite the Temple, Peter, James, John and Andrew ask him privately when this will be. He warns of false messiahs, wars, persecution and the desolating sacrilege, and tells them to flee Judea when they see Jerusalem surrounded. Then the Son of man will come on the clouds with power and glory. No one knows the day or the hour, so they must watch.","journey":"Under a mile: out of the Temple, across the Kidron Valley and up the slope of the Mount of Olives.","route":[[35.235,31.778],[35.239,31.779],[35.243,31.778],[35.245,31.778]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 13:1–2","text":"And as he came out of the temple, one of his disciples said to him, \"Look, Teacher, what wonderful stones and what wonderful buildings!\" And Jesus said to him, \"Do you see these great buildings? There will not be left here one stone upon another, that will not be thrown down.\""},{"ref":"Mark 13:26","text":"And then they will see the Son of man coming in clouds with great power and glory."},{"ref":"Mark 13:31–33","text":"Heaven and earth will pass away, but my words will not pass away. \"But of that day or that hour no one knows, not even the angels in heaven, nor the Son, but only the Father. Take heed, watch and pray; for you do not know when the time will come."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Daniel 7:13–14","note":"\"One like a son of man\" coming with the clouds of heaven and given an everlasting kingdom."},{"ref":"Daniel 9:27; 11:31","note":"The \"abomination that makes desolate\" (Mark 13:14)."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","img":"img/olivet0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Prophecy_of_the_Destruction_of_the_Temple_(La_pr%C3%A9dication_de_la_ruine_du_Temple)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"The Prophecy of the Destruction of the Temple","description":"Under a large tree on the Mount of Olives, Jesus sits with his head in his hand, four disciples around him, facing the massive walls of the Temple across the valley.","significance":"One of about 350 watercolors in Tissot's Life of Christ, painted after his journeys to Palestine in 1886–87 and 1889.","symbolism":"The great walls he foretells will be thrown down fill the whole background, solid and seemingly permanent.","impact":"Shown in Paris in 1894 and bought by the Brooklyn Museum in 1900; the series shaped how a generation pictured the Gospel world."},{"kind":"Descriptive","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","img":"img/olivet1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Disciples_Admire_the_Buildings_of_the_Temple_(Les_disciples_admirent_les_constructions_du_Temple)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"The Disciples Admire the Buildings of the Temple","description":"Coming down a broad stairway of the Temple, the disciples gesture with excitement at the buildings around them while Jesus walks among them, eyes lowered.","significance":"One of about 350 watercolors in Tissot's Life of Christ, painted after his journeys to Palestine in 1886–87 and 1889.","symbolism":"Their wonder at \"what wonderful stones\" (Mark 13:1) meets his silence, just before the prophecy.","impact":"Shown in Paris in 1894 and bought by the Brooklyn Museum in 1900; the series shaped how a generation pictured the Gospel world."}],"tags":["prophecy","teaching"],"history":"The Romans under Titus took Jerusalem and burned the Temple in AD 70, about forty years later. Huge Herodian stones thrown down from the Temple Mount still lie on the 1st-century street below the Western Wall.","orderNote":"How much of the discourse refers to the fall of Jerusalem in AD 70 and how much to the end of the age is much debated. Robertson sees the city's destruction as a symbol of the end and warns against pressing details of time. Luke sets this teaching in the Temple (21:5–7); Mark and Matthew on the Mount of Olives."},
  {"id":"tenvirgins","harmony":"§139b","lane":"jesus","place":"olives","date":"Tuesday afternoon, Passion Week, AD 33","title":"The parable of the ten virgins","people":["Jesus","The disciples"],"gospels":{"Mt":"25:1–13"},"summary":"Ten maidens take their lamps to meet the bridegroom. Five wise ones bring extra oil; five foolish ones do not. The bridegroom is delayed and they all fall asleep. At midnight comes the cry that he is here, and the foolish find their lamps going out. While they go to buy oil the door is shut. \"Watch therefore, for you know neither the day nor the hour.\"","scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 25:10","text":"And while they went to buy, the bridegroom came, and those who were ready went in with him to the marriage feast; and the door was shut."},{"ref":"Matthew 25:13","text":"Watch therefore, for you know neither the day nor the hour."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","img":"img/tenvirgins0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Wise_Virgins_(Les_vierges_sages)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"The Wise Virgins","description":"Five young women in white sit asleep against a stone wall hung with garlands, waiting for the bridegroom. Their small oil lamps still burn on the petal-strewn floor.","significance":"One of about 350 watercolors in Tissot's Life of Christ, painted after his journeys to Palestine in 1886–87 and 1889.","symbolism":"Their lamps stay lit even as they sleep: the oil they brought stands for a readiness that does not run out.","impact":"Shown in Paris in 1894 and bought by the Brooklyn Museum in 1900; the series shaped how a generation pictured the Gospel world."},{"kind":"Descriptive","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","img":"img/tenvirgins1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Foolish_Virgins_(Les_vierges_folles)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"The Foolish Virgins","description":"In the dark, the foolish virgins run in a windblown line, holding up empty lamps and oil jars as they hurry to buy oil.","significance":"One of about 350 watercolors in Tissot's Life of Christ, painted after his journeys to Palestine in 1886–87 and 1889.","symbolism":"The dark night and the rush of figures stress how suddenly the bridegroom comes and how late they are.","impact":"Shown in Paris in 1894 and bought by the Brooklyn Museum in 1900; the series shaped how a generation pictured the Gospel world."}],"tags":["parable","prophecy"]},
  {"id":"talents","harmony":"§139c","lane":"jesus","place":"olives","date":"Tuesday afternoon, Passion Week, AD 33","title":"The parable of the talents","people":["Jesus","The disciples"],"gospels":{"Mt":"25:14–30"},"summary":"A man going on a journey entrusts his property to three servants: five talents, two talents and one, each according to his ability. The first two trade and double what they were given. The third buries his talent in the ground. When the master returns, he praises the faithful servants and takes the talent from the one who hid it out of fear. A talent was a very large sum, many years' wages.","scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 25:21","text":"His master said to him, 'Well done, good and faithful servant; you have been faithful over a little, I will set you over much; enter into the joy of your master.'"},{"ref":"Matthew 25:29","text":"For to every one who has will more be given, and he will have abundance; but from him who has not, even what he has will be taken away."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/talents0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Willem_de_Poorter's_The_Parable_of_The_Talents_or_Minas.png","title":"The Parable of the Talents","artist":"Willem de Poorter","year":"17th century","medium":"Oil painting","location":"Location not recorded","description":"In a lofty hall, a servant leans over a table to lay out his coins before his richly robed master, while other servants wait their turn.","significance":"De Poorter was a Haarlem painter in the circle of Rembrandt, known for small, finely lit biblical scenes.","symbolism":"The shaft of light across the floor and the master's open hand stress the moment of reckoning when the master returns.","impact":"Shows how Dutch painters of the 1600s read the parable as a story about honest stewardship and accounting."}],"tags":["parable","teaching"],"orderNote":"Luke's parable of the pounds (Luke 19:11–27), told near Jericho a few days earlier, is similar. Robertson treats them as two different parables."},
  {"id":"lastjudgment","harmony":"§139d","lane":"jesus","place":"olives","date":"Tuesday afternoon, Passion Week, AD 33","title":"The sheep and the goats","people":["Jesus","The disciples"],"gospels":{"Mt":"25:31–46"},"summary":"Jesus closes the discourse with the Last Judgment. The Son of man comes in glory with his angels and sits on his throne, and all the nations are gathered before him. He separates them as a shepherd separates the sheep from the goats. Those who fed the hungry, welcomed the stranger, clothed the naked and visited the sick and imprisoned did it to him, and inherit the kingdom.","scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 25:31–32","text":"\"When the Son of man comes in his glory, and all the angels with him, then he will sit on his glorious throne. Before him will be gathered all the nations, and he will separate them one from another as a shepherd separates the sheep from the goats,"},{"ref":"Matthew 25:40","text":"And the King will answer them, 'Truly, I say to you, as you did it to one of the least of these my brethren, you did it to me.'"}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Ezekiel 34:17","note":"\"I judge between sheep and sheep, rams and he-goats.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/lastjudgment0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fra_Angelico_%E2%80%94_The_Last_Judgment.jpg","title":"The Last Judgment","artist":"Fra Angelico","year":"c. 1431","medium":"Tempera on panel","location":"Museo di San Marco, Florence","description":"Christ sits in judgment in a ring of angels, with saints ranged on either side. Below, a road of open tombs divides the blessed, dancing with angels in a garden at left, from the damned driven by demons into hell at right.","significance":"Painted for the Camaldolese monastery of Santa Maria degli Angeli in Florence, early in Fra Angelico's career.","symbolism":"The division at right and left follows Matthew 25: the sheep on the King's right hand, the goats on his left.","impact":"The ring dance of the blessed with angels is one of the most loved images of heaven in Italian art."}],"tags":["teaching","prophecy"]},
  {"id":"passionplot","harmony":"§140","lane":"jesus","place":"bethany","from":"olives","date":"Tuesday evening, Passion Week, AD 33","title":"The plot to kill Jesus","people":["Jesus","The disciples","Caiaphas","The chief priests and elders"],"gospels":{"Mt":"26:1–5","Mk":"14:1–2","Lk":"22:1–2"},"summary":"When he has finished these sayings, Jesus tells his disciples that after two days the Passover is coming, and the Son of man will be delivered up to be crucified. Meanwhile the chief priests and elders gather in the palace of the high priest Caiaphas. They take counsel to arrest Jesus by stealth and kill him, but not during the feast, for fear of a riot among the people.","stay":"Robertson thinks Jesus spent Tuesday night and the quiet Wednesday with the disciples at Bethany.","journey":"About 1.5 miles back over the Mount of Olives to Bethany.","route":[[35.245,31.778],[35.252,31.776],[35.258,31.773],[35.262,31.771]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 26:2","text":"\"You know that after two days the Passover is coming, and the Son of man will be delivered up to be crucified.\""},{"ref":"Matthew 26:3–4","text":"Then the chief priests and the elders of the people gathered in the palace of the high priest, who was called Caiaphas, and took counsel together in order to arrest Jesus by stealth and kill him."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","img":"img/passionplot0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Conspiracy_of_the_Jews_(Conspiration_des_juifs)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"Conspiracy of the Jews","description":"A huddle of elderly priests and elders in turbans and heavy robes confer in low voices in a dim hall behind an iron grille.","significance":"One of about 350 watercolors in Tissot's Life of Christ, painted after his journeys to Palestine in 1886–87 and 1889.","symbolism":"Tissot shows the council as a closed circle in shadow, plotting \"by stealth\" (Matthew 26:4).","impact":"Shown in Paris in 1894 and bought by the Brooklyn Museum in 1900; the series shaped how a generation pictured the Gospel world."}],"tags":["passion","prophecy"],"orderNote":"Robertson places Jesus' prediction at Bethany on Tuesday evening, which by Jewish reckoning was already Wednesday, \"two days\" before the Passover. The council itself met in Jerusalem at Caiaphas' house. The Gospels record nothing of Wednesday; Robertson takes it as a day of rest in Bethany."},
  {"id":"bethanyanoint","harmony":"§141","lane":"jesus","place":"bethany","date":"Tuesday evening, Passion Week, AD 33","title":"Mary anoints Jesus at Bethany","people":["Jesus","Mary of Bethany","Martha","Lazarus","Simon the leper","Judas Iscariot","The disciples"],"gospels":{"Mt":"26:6–13","Mk":"14:3–9","Jn":"12:2–8"},"summary":"At a supper in the house of Simon the leper, Martha serves and Lazarus is at table. Mary breaks an alabaster flask of costly nard and pours it over Jesus' head and feet, wiping his feet with her hair, and the house fills with the fragrance. Some, Judas loudest of all, complain that it could have been sold for three hundred denarii and given to the poor. Jesus defends her: she has anointed his body beforehand for burial. Wherever the gospel is preached, what she has done will be told in memory of her.","scripture":[{"ref":"John 12:3","text":"Mary took a pound of costly ointment of pure nard and anointed the feet of Jesus and wiped his feet with her hair; and the house was filled with the fragrance of the ointment."},{"ref":"Mark 14:8–9","text":"She has done what she could; she has anointed my body beforehand for burying. And truly, I say to you, wherever the gospel is preached in the whole world, what she has done will be told in memory of her.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","img":"img/bethanyanoint0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Ointment_of_the_Magdalene_(Le_parfum_de_Madeleine)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"The Ointment of the Magdalene","description":"Jesus reclines on a cushioned couch in a garden courtyard while a woman, her long hair loose, bends over his feet. The other guests lean forward from the table, some startled, some disapproving.","significance":"One of about 350 watercolors in Tissot's Life of Christ, painted after his journeys to Palestine in 1886–87 and 1889.","symbolism":"Tissot follows the Western tradition that identified Mary of Bethany with Mary Magdalene, hence the title. The guests' reactions echo the complaint about waste in John 12:4–5.","impact":"Shown in Paris in 1894 and bought by the Brooklyn Museum in 1900; the series shaped how a generation pictured the Gospel world."}],"tags":["passion"],"orderNote":"John places the supper six days before the Passover; Mark and Matthew two days before. Robertson follows Mark. Mark and Matthew say \"a woman\"; John names Mary. Robertson insists it is a different event from the anointing by a sinful woman in Galilee (Luke 7:36–50), though Western tradition long identified Mary of Bethany with that woman and with Mary Magdalene."},
  {"id":"judasbargain","harmony":"§142","lane":"apostles","place":"caiaphas","date":"Tuesday night, Passion Week, AD 33","title":"Judas agrees to betray Jesus","people":["Judas Iscariot","The chief priests","The captains of the Temple"],"gospels":{"Mt":"26:14–16","Mk":"14:10–11","Lk":"22:3–6"},"summary":"Judas Iscariot, one of the Twelve, goes to the chief priests and asks what they will give him to hand Jesus over. Luke says Satan entered into him. The priests are glad and agree to pay him thirty pieces of silver. From then on he looks for an opportunity to betray Jesus to them away from the crowds.","scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 26:14–16","text":"Then one of the twelve, who was called Judas Iscariot, went to the chief priests and said, \"What will you give me if I deliver him to you?\" And they paid him thirty pieces of silver. And from that moment he sought an opportunity to betray him."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Zechariah 11:12–13","note":"The shepherd's wages, \"thirty shekels of silver,\" thrown to the potter in the house of the Lord."},{"ref":"Exodus 21:32","note":"Thirty shekels of silver: the price paid for a slave."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/judasbargain0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giotto_di_Bondone_-_No._28_Scenes_from_the_Life_of_Christ_-_12._Judas%27_Betrayal_-_WGA09213.jpg","title":"The Pact of Judas","artist":"Giotto di Bondone","year":"c. 1305","medium":"Fresco","location":"Scrovegni Chapel, Padua","description":"Judas, in a yellow cloak, takes the money bag from the priests beside a small open loggia. A black devil stands behind him with a hand on his shoulder.","significance":"Part of Giotto's cycle in the Scrovegni Chapel, a turning point in Western painting toward weight, space and human feeling.","symbolism":"Judas's yellow robe became the traditional color of betrayal; the devil at his back shows Luke's words that Satan entered into him.","impact":"The Scrovegni frescoes are a UNESCO World Heritage Site and among the most studied paintings of the Middle Ages."},{"kind":"Descriptive","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","img":"img/judasbargain1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Judas_Goes_to_Find_the_Jews_(Judas_va_trouver_les_Juifs)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"Judas Goes to Find the Jews","description":"Judas, wrapped in a dark cloak, walks away from a carved balustrade where a crowd of elders leans over, listening to a pleading man.","significance":"One of about 350 watercolors in Tissot's Life of Christ, painted after his journeys to Palestine in 1886–87 and 1889.","symbolism":"Tissot isolates Judas in the foreground, his face closed and downcast, already apart from the Twelve.","impact":"Shown in Paris in 1894 and bought by the Brooklyn Museum in 1900; the series shaped how a generation pictured the Gospel world."}],"tags":["passion"],"orderNote":"Robertson links the bargain to the anointing: Judas, \"stung by the rebuke\" at the feast, goes to the rulers that same night. Matthew and Mark tell it straight after the anointing; Luke after the council's plot."},
  {"id":"passoverprep","harmony":"§143","lane":"jesus","place":"upperroom","from":"bethany","date":"Thursday afternoon, Passion Week, AD 33","title":"Preparing the Passover","people":["Jesus","Peter","John","A man carrying a jar of water","The householder"],"gospels":{"Mt":"26:17–19","Mk":"14:12–16","Lk":"22:7–13"},"summary":"On the day when the Passover lamb is sacrificed, Jesus sends Peter and John into the city. A man carrying a jar of water, an unusual sight, since women usually carried water, will lead them to a house. Its owner will show them a large upper room, furnished and ready. They find it as he told them and prepare the Passover: the lamb sacrificed in the Temple that afternoon, the unleavened bread, the bitter herbs and the wine.","stay":"The upper room of a friend's house in Jerusalem; Robertson suggests the home of John Mark's parents.","journey":"About 2 miles from Bethany over the Mount of Olives and across the Kidron into the city.","route":[[35.262,31.771],[35.252,31.774],[35.243,31.776],[35.238,31.774],[35.234,31.772],[35.229,31.7716]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 22:7–8","text":"Then came the day of Unleavened Bread, on which the passover lamb had to be sacrificed. So Jesus sent Peter and John, saying, \"Go and prepare the passover for us, that we may eat it.\""},{"ref":"Mark 14:15","text":"And he will show you a large upper room furnished and ready; there prepare for us.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Exodus 12:1–14","note":"The first Passover: the lamb without blemish, its blood on the doorposts, eaten with unleavened bread and bitter herbs."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","img":"img/passoverprep0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Man_Bearing_a_Pitcher_(L'homme_%C3%A0_la_cruche)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"The Man Bearing a Pitcher","description":"Two disciples, Peter and John, pause beneath the high walls of Jerusalem and watch a man carrying a large water jar on his shoulder up a stepped street.","significance":"One of about 350 watercolors in Tissot's Life of Christ, painted after his journeys to Palestine in 1886–87 and 1889.","symbolism":"The water carrier is the sign Jesus gave them (Mark 14:13); a man carrying water would have stood out, since it was usually women's work.","impact":"Shown in Paris in 1894 and bought by the Brooklyn Museum in 1900; the series shaped how a generation pictured the Gospel world."}],"tags":["passion"],"orderNote":"The Synoptics present the Last Supper as the Passover meal, and Robertson agrees. John seems to place the slaying of the Passover lambs on Friday, as Jesus was dying (John 18:28; 19:14). Scholars explain this in several ways, among them a different calendar used by some Jews, or a Passover meal held a day early."},
  {"id":"lastsupper","harmony":"§144","lane":"jesus","place":"upperroom","date":"Thursday evening, Passion Week, AD 33","title":"The Last Supper begins","people":["Jesus","The Twelve"],"gospels":{"Mt":"26:20","Mk":"14:17","Lk":"22:14–16, 24–30"},"summary":"When the hour comes, Jesus reclines at table with the Twelve. He tells them he has earnestly desired to eat this Passover with them before he suffers. A dispute breaks out over which of them is the greatest. Jesus tells them that among the Gentiles kings lord it over others, but among them the greatest must be as the youngest, and the leader as one who serves, \"I am among you as one who serves.\" He promises them a kingdom and thrones to judge the twelve tribes of Israel.","stay":"The upper room in Jerusalem, from sunset on Thursday, the start of the Jewish Friday.","scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 22:15–16","text":"And he said to them, \"I have earnestly desired to eat this passover with you before I suffer; for I tell you I shall not eat it until it is fulfilled in the kingdom of God.\""},{"ref":"Luke 22:26–27","text":"But not so with you; rather let the greatest among you become as the youngest, and the leader as one who serves. For which is the greater, one who sits at table, or one who serves? Is it not the one who sits at table? But I am among you as one who serves."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","img":"img/lastsupper0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Last_Supper_(La_C%C3%A8ne_l%C3%A9gale)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"The Last Supper (The Legal Passover)","description":"Jesus and the apostles stand around a table draped in white, staffs in hand and cloaks over their heads, in a room hung with garlands.","significance":"One of about 350 watercolors in Tissot's Life of Christ, painted after his journeys to Palestine in 1886–87 and 1889.","symbolism":"Tissot shows the Passover eaten as Exodus 12:11 commands, standing \"with your loins girded... and your staff in your hand.\" His French title calls it the legal Passover meal.","impact":"Shown in Paris in 1894 and bought by the Brooklyn Museum in 1900; the series shaped how a generation pictured the Gospel world."},{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/lastsupper1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Last_Supper_(by_Giotto_di_Bondone)_-_Capella_degli_Scrovegni_(Padova).jpg","title":"The Last Supper","artist":"Giotto","year":"c. 1305","medium":"Fresco","location":"Scrovegni (Arena) Chapel, Padua","description":"Christ and the Twelve sit at a long table under an open loggia. John leans against Jesus' breast, and Judas, at his side, reaches into the same dish.","significance":"Part of Giotto's Passion cycle; the apostles seen from behind invite the viewer to sit at the table with them.","symbolism":"The halos of the apostles have darkened with age; Judas's is painted dark on purpose, marking the betrayer.","impact":"An early landmark in the long Italian tradition of Last Supper paintings that culminates in Leonardo."}],"tags":["passion","teaching"],"orderNote":"Luke tells the dispute after the supper (22:24–30). Robertson places it at the start of the meal, perhaps a quarrel over places at table, which would also explain the foot washing that follows."},
  {"id":"footwashing","harmony":"§145","lane":"jesus","place":"upperroom","date":"Thursday evening, Passion Week, AD 33","title":"Jesus washes the disciples' feet","people":["Jesus","Simon Peter","The Twelve"],"gospels":{"Jn":"13:1–20"},"summary":"Knowing that his hour has come, and having loved his own, Jesus loves them to the end. During supper he rises, lays aside his garments, ties a towel around himself and washes the disciples' feet, a slave's task. Peter protests, \"You shall never wash my feet,\" until Jesus tells him that unless he is washed he has no part in him. Jesus then tells them that he has given them an example: as he has done, so they must do to one another.","scripture":[{"ref":"John 13:5","text":"Then he poured water into a basin, and began to wash the disciples' feet, and to wipe them with the towel with which he was girded."},{"ref":"John 13:8","text":"Peter said to him, \"You shall never wash my feet.\" Jesus answered him, \"If I do not wash you, you have no part in me.\""},{"ref":"John 13:14–15","text":"If I then, your Lord and Teacher, have washed your feet, you also ought to wash one another's feet. For I have given you an example, that you also should do as I have done to you."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Psalm 41:9","note":"\"He who ate of my bread has lifted his heel against me,\" quoted by Jesus here of his betrayer (John 13:18)."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/footwashing0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jesus_washing_Peter's_feet.jpg","title":"Jesus Washing Peter's Feet","artist":"Ford Madox Brown","year":"1852–56","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Tate Britain, London","description":"Christ kneels on the floor, a towel tied at his waist, washing Peter's feet in a copper basin. Peter sits with hands clasped and head bowed, while the other apostles crowd around the table to watch.","significance":"Brown, close to the Pre-Raphaelites, first painted Christ bare-chested; critics objected, and he repainted him clothed.","symbolism":"The Lord on his knees below the seated disciple turns the order of master and servant upside down (John 13:14–15).","impact":"Its frank, physical realism made it one of the most discussed religious paintings of Victorian England."},{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/footwashing1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Washing_of_the_Feet_-_Capella_dei_Scrovegni_-_Padua_2016.jpg","title":"Washing of the Feet","artist":"Giotto","year":"c. 1305","medium":"Fresco","location":"Scrovegni (Arena) Chapel, Padua","description":"Christ kneels before Peter, who raises a hand in protest, while the other apostles wait their turn; one at the left unties his sandal, another holds the water jug.","significance":"Part of Giotto's Passion cycle, set in the same open room as his Last Supper next to it.","symbolism":"Christ's halo is lower than the apostles' heads: the Master has taken the servant's place.","impact":"A model for the many images of the Mandatum that later decorated church and monastery walls."}],"tags":["passion","teaching"],"orderNote":"Only John tells the foot washing, and he does not describe the institution of the Eucharist. The Church re-enacts the washing in the Mass of the Lord's Supper on Holy Thursday."},
  {"id":"betrayer","harmony":"§146","lane":"jesus","place":"upperroom","date":"Thursday evening, Passion Week, AD 33","title":"\"One of you will betray me\"","people":["Jesus","Judas Iscariot","Peter","The beloved disciple","The Twelve"],"gospels":{"Mt":"26:21–25","Mk":"14:18–21","Lk":"22:21–23","Jn":"13:21–30"},"summary":"Troubled in spirit, Jesus says, \"One of you will betray me.\" The disciples are deeply grieved and ask one after another, \"Is it I?\" Peter signals to the beloved disciple, reclining next to Jesus, to ask whom he means. Jesus says it is the one to whom he gives the morsel after dipping it, and gives it to Judas. Satan enters Judas, and Jesus tells him, \"What you are going to do, do quickly.\" Judas goes out at once, \"and it was night.\"","scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 14:18–19","text":"And as they were at table eating, Jesus said, \"Truly, I say to you, one of you will betray me, one who is eating with me.\" They began to be sorrowful, and to say to him one after another, \"Is it I?\""},{"ref":"John 13:26","text":"Jesus answered, \"It is he to whom I shall give this morsel when I have dipped it.\" So when he had dipped the morsel, he gave it to Judas, the son of Simon Iscariot."},{"ref":"John 13:30","text":"So, after receiving the morsel, he immediately went out; and it was night."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/betrayer0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leonardo_da_Vinci_(1452-1519)_-_The_Last_Supper_(1495-1498).jpg","title":"The Last Supper","artist":"Leonardo da Vinci","year":"1495–98","medium":"Tempera and oil on plaster","location":"Refectory of Santa Maria delle Grazie, Milan","description":"Christ sits calmly at the center of a long table as the apostles recoil in groups of three at his words, \"One of you will betray me.\" Judas, clutching a money bag, leans back into shadow.","significance":"Painted for the Dominican friars' dining hall at the request of Duke Ludovico Sforza. Leonardo's experimental technique began to flake within his lifetime.","symbolism":"Christ's head is framed by the bright central window and is the vanishing point of the whole room, so every line leads to him.","impact":"Perhaps the most reproduced religious painting in the world. With the church, it has been a UNESCO World Heritage Site since 1980."},{"kind":"Descriptive","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","img":"img/betrayer1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Judas_Leaves_the_Cenacle_(Judas_quitte_le_C%C3%A9nacle)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"Judas Leaves the Cenacle","description":"Judas, wrapped in a heavy brown cloak, slips out of a dark doorway into the cold light of the night street, looking back over his shoulder.","significance":"One of about 350 watercolors in Tissot's Life of Christ, painted after his journeys to Palestine in 1886–87 and 1889.","symbolism":"It pictures John 13:30, \"he immediately went out; and it was night\": light behind him in the room, darkness ahead.","impact":"Shown in Paris in 1894 and bought by the Brooklyn Museum in 1900; the series shaped how a generation pictured the Gospel world."}],"tags":["passion","prophecy"],"orderNote":"Luke places the warning about the betrayer after the bread and cup (22:21–23), which suggests Judas was present at the institution. Robertson follows Mark and John and places Judas's departure first."},
  {"id":"peterwarned","harmony":"§147","lane":"jesus","place":"upperroom","date":"Thursday evening, Passion Week, AD 33","title":"The new commandment and the warning to Peter","people":["Jesus","Simon Peter","The disciples"],"gospels":{"Mt":"26:31–35","Mk":"14:27–31","Lk":"22:31–38","Jn":"13:31–38"},"summary":"After Judas leaves, Jesus says the Son of man is now glorified. He gives them a new commandment: love one another as he has loved them. He warns that they will all fall away this night, as Zechariah foretold. Peter protests that even if all fall away, he will not, and that he will die with him. Jesus tells him that before the cock crows twice, he will deny him three times. Yet he has prayed that Peter's faith may not fail, and once he has turned again, he must strengthen his brethren.","scripture":[{"ref":"John 13:34–35","text":"A new commandment I give to you, that you love one another; even as I have loved you, that you also love one another. By this all men will know that you are my disciples, if you have love for one another.\""},{"ref":"Luke 22:31–32","text":"\"Simon, Simon, behold, Satan demanded to have you, that he might sift you like wheat, but I have prayed for you that your faith may not fail; and when you have turned again, strengthen your brethren.\""},{"ref":"Mark 14:30","text":"And Jesus said to him, \"Truly, I say to you, this very night, before the cock crows twice, you will deny me three times.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Zechariah 13:7","note":"\"Strike the shepherd, that the sheep may be scattered\" (Mark 14:27)."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","img":"img/peterwarned0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Protestations_of_Saint_Peter_(Protestations_de_Saint_Pierre)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"The Protestations of Saint Peter","description":"On a rocky path in the dark outside the city walls, Peter turns to Jesus with his hand raised, protesting his loyalty, as the other disciples walk on in silence.","significance":"One of about 350 watercolors in Tissot's Life of Christ, painted after his journeys to Palestine in 1886–87 and 1889.","symbolism":"Tissot follows Mark and Matthew, who set the warning on the way to the Mount of Olives; a stair climbs the dark valley behind them.","impact":"Shown in Paris in 1894 and bought by the Brooklyn Museum in 1900; the series shaped how a generation pictured the Gospel world."}],"tags":["prophecy","teaching","passion"],"orderNote":"Luke and John place the warning to Peter in the upper room; Mark and Matthew on the way to the Mount of Olives after the hymn. Robertson harmonizes them in one section; some think Jesus warned Peter twice. Only Mark has the cock crowing twice."},
  {"id":"eucharist","harmony":"§148","lane":"jesus","place":"upperroom","date":"Thursday evening, Passion Week, AD 33","title":"The institution of the Eucharist","people":["Jesus","The apostles"],"gospels":{"Mt":"26:26–29","Mk":"14:22–25","Lk":"22:17–20","1Cor":"11:23–26"},"summary":"As they are eating, Jesus takes bread, blesses and breaks it, and gives it to the disciples, saying, \"Take, eat; this is my body.\" Then he takes the cup, gives thanks, and gives it to them: \"This is my blood of the covenant, which is poured out for many for the forgiveness of sins.\" He commands them, \"Do this in remembrance of me.\" The Church has always received these words as the institution of the Holy Eucharist: the gift of his Body and Blood, given the night before he offered himself on the Cross, and the beginning of the ministerial priesthood of the apostles. St Paul hands on the same words as a tradition he received from the Lord.","stay":"The upper room in Jerusalem.","scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 26:26–28","text":"Now as they were eating, Jesus took bread, and blessed, and broke it, and gave it to the disciples and said, \"Take, eat; this is my body.\" And he took a cup, and when he had given thanks he gave it to them, saying, \"Drink of it, all of you; for this is my blood of the covenant, which is poured out for many for the forgiveness of sins."},{"ref":"Luke 22:19","text":"And he took bread, and when he had given thanks he broke it and gave it to them, saying, \"This is my body which is given for you. Do this in remembrance of me.\""},{"ref":"1 Corinthians 11:26","text":"For as often as you eat this bread and drink the cup, you proclaim the Lord's death until he comes."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Exodus 24:8","note":"Moses seals the covenant at Sinai: \"Behold the blood of the covenant which the Lord has made with you.\""},{"ref":"Jeremiah 31:31–34","note":"The promised new covenant, written on the heart."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/eucharist0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dieric_Bouts_-_Altaarstuk_van_het_Heilig_SacramentFXD_(central_panel).jpg","title":"Altarpiece of the Holy Sacrament: The Last Supper","artist":"Dieric Bouts","year":"1464–68","medium":"Oil on panel","location":"St Peter's Church, Leuven, Belgium","description":"In a calm Flemish hall, Christ sits at the center of a square table and raises his hand in blessing over a single Host above the chalice. The apostles sit in stillness around him, and four men in contemporary dress look on.","significance":"Commissioned by the Confraternity of the Holy Sacrament in Leuven, whose contract specified the subjects. It is among the first Netherlandish paintings to show Christ consecrating the Host rather than naming the betrayer.","symbolism":"Christ holds the Host where a priest holds it at Mass, so the Last Supper is shown as the first Eucharist. The onlookers are thought to be members of the confraternity, or Bouts himself.","impact":"A landmark of early Netherlandish painting and one of the earliest northern works to use a single vanishing point for the room."},{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/eucharist1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fra_Angelico_-_Institution_of_the_Eucharist_(Cell_35)_-_WGA00549.jpg","title":"Institution of the Eucharist (Communion of the Apostles)","artist":"Fra Angelico","year":"c. 1441–42","medium":"Fresco","location":"Cell 35, Convent of San Marco, Florence","description":"Christ moves along the table giving Communion to the apostles with his own hand. Some sit, some kneel, and the Virgin Mary kneels in prayer at the left.","significance":"Painted for a friar's cell in the Dominican convent of San Marco, as an aid to prayer.","symbolism":"The scene looks like a Mass: Christ holds a paten and chalice and gives the Host as a priest does, and the kneeling apostles mirror the friars receiving Communion.","impact":"A classic image of the Eucharist as Christ's own gift, often reproduced in First Communion books."}],"tags":["passion","teaching"],"orderNote":"Mark and Matthew give one account and Paul (1 Corinthians, about AD 56, the earliest written) and Luke another. Luke mentions a cup before the bread (22:17) and the cup of the covenant after supper (22:20). Robertson follows Mark's order and places the institution after Judas has gone out; Luke's order suggests Judas was still present."},
  {"id":"farewell","harmony":"§149","lane":"jesus","place":"upperroom","date":"Thursday night, Passion Week, AD 33","title":"The farewell discourse","people":["Jesus","Thomas","Philip","Judas (not Iscariot)","The disciples"],"gospels":{"Jn":"14:1–31"},"summary":"Jesus comforts his troubled disciples. In his Father's house are many rooms, and he goes to prepare a place for them. When Thomas asks the way, he answers, \"I am the way, and the truth, and the life.\" Philip asks to see the Father, and Jesus says that whoever has seen him has seen the Father. He promises another Counselor, the Holy Spirit, who will teach them all things, and he leaves them his peace.","scripture":[{"ref":"John 14:2–3","text":"In my Father's house are many rooms; if it were not so, would I have told you that I go to prepare a place for you? And when I go and prepare a place for you, I will come again and will take you to myself, that where I am you may be also."},{"ref":"John 14:6","text":"Jesus said to him, \"I am the way, and the truth, and the life; no one comes to the Father, but by me."},{"ref":"John 14:27","text":"Peace I leave with you; my peace I give to you; not as the world gives do I give to you. Let not your hearts be troubled, neither let them be afraid."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","img":"img/farewell0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Last_Sermon_of_Our_Lord_(Dernier_Sermon_de_Notre-Seigneur)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"The Last Sermon of Our Lord","description":"In the upper room, hung with red curtains, Jesus stands in the center holding a long white cloth, while the apostles stand in two rows facing him, hands joined in prayer.","significance":"One of about 350 watercolors in Tissot's Life of Christ, painted after his journeys to Palestine in 1886–87 and 1889.","symbolism":"Tissot's title names the farewell discourse of John 14–17, Jesus' last teaching before the Passion.","impact":"Shown in Paris in 1894 and bought by the Brooklyn Museum in 1900; the series shaped how a generation pictured the Gospel world."}],"tags":["teaching","prophecy"],"orderNote":"Robertson reads John 13–17 as one whole: dialogue in the upper room, then a monologue, then the prayer. Jesus ends chapter 14 with \"Rise, let us go hence,\" so the following chapters may have been spoken on the way."},
  {"id":"truevine","harmony":"§150","lane":"jesus","place":"upperroom","date":"Thursday night, Passion Week, AD 33","title":"The true vine","people":["Jesus","The disciples"],"gospels":{"Jn":"15:1–16:33"},"summary":"\"I am the vine, you are the branches.\" Jesus tells his disciples to abide in him and in his love, for apart from him they can do nothing. No one has greater love than to lay down his life for his friends, and he calls them friends, not servants. He warns that the world will hate them as it hated him. He promises that the Spirit of truth will come, and that their sorrow will turn to joy. \"Be of good cheer, I have overcome the world.\"","scripture":[{"ref":"John 15:5","text":"I am the vine, you are the branches. He who abides in me, and I in him, he it is that bears much fruit, for apart from me you can do nothing."},{"ref":"John 15:13","text":"Greater love has no man than this, that a man lay down his life for his friends."},{"ref":"John 16:33","text":"I have said this to you, that in me you may have peace. In the world you have tribulation; but be of good cheer, I have overcome the world.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Psalm 80:8–14","note":"Israel as the vine God brought out of Egypt and planted."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/truevine0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Christ_the_True_Vine_icon_(Athens,_16th_century).jpg","title":"Christ the True Vine","artist":"Cretan school (possibly Leos Moskos)","year":"16th century","medium":"Tempera and gold on panel","location":"Athens","description":"Christ sits at the center of a vine growing from one trunk, holding an open Gospel book. The twelve apostles sit on its branches among grape clusters, each with a book or scroll.","significance":"A classic Byzantine and post-Byzantine icon type, made in Crete for Orthodox and Catholic patrons alike.","symbolism":"The open book carries the words of John 15: \"I am the vine, you are the branches.\" The apostles grow from Christ and bear fruit only in him.","impact":"One of the most widely reproduced icons of the subject."}],"tags":["teaching","prophecy"],"orderNote":"Robertson suggests this was spoken \"possibly on the street,\" after they left the upper room at the end of John 14 and sang the hymn. Others think Jesus spoke it before leaving the room; John 18:1 says he went out only after the prayer of chapter 17."},
  {"id":"priestlyprayer","harmony":"§151","lane":"jesus","place":"upperroom","date":"Thursday night, Passion Week, AD 33","title":"The high priestly prayer","people":["Jesus","The disciples"],"gospels":{"Jn":"17:1–26"},"summary":"Lifting up his eyes to heaven, Jesus prays: \"Father, the hour has come; glorify your Son.\" He prays for his disciples, whom the Father gave him out of the world, that they may be kept from the evil one and consecrated in the truth. He prays also for all who will believe through their word, that they may all be one, as he and the Father are one, so that the world may believe. Robertson calls it \"the real Lord's Prayer.\"","scripture":[{"ref":"John 17:3","text":"And this is eternal life, that they know you the only true God, and Jesus Christ whom you have sent."},{"ref":"John 17:20–21","text":"\"I do not pray for these only, but also for those who believe in me through their word, that they may all be one; even as you, Father, are in me, and I in you, that they also may be in us, so that the world may believe that you have sent me."}],"art":[],"tags":["prayer","teaching"],"orderNote":"Robertson suggests Jesus prayed this \"possibly near Gethsemane.\" John 18:1 says that after these words he went out across the Kidron, so many place it in the upper room or in the city."},
  {"id":"agony","harmony":"§152","lane":"jesus","place":"gethsemane","from":"upperroom","date":"Thursday night, Passion Week, AD 33","title":"The agony in Gethsemane","people":["Jesus","Peter","James","John","The disciples","An angel"],"gospels":{"Mt":"26:30, 36–46","Mk":"14:26, 32–42","Lk":"22:39–46","Jn":"18:1"},"summary":"After singing a hymn, they go out across the Kidron to a garden called Gethsemane. Jesus takes Peter, James and John with him, saying his soul is very sorrowful, even to death. He falls on the ground and prays that the cup may pass from him, \"yet not what I will, but what you will.\" Luke tells of an angel strengthening him and of sweat like great drops of blood. Three times he returns and finds the disciples asleep. At last he rouses them: the hour has come, and the betrayer is at hand.","journey":"About a mile from the upper room down through the city and across the Kidron Valley to the foot of the Mount of Olives.","route":[[35.229,31.7716],[35.233,31.771],[35.236,31.772],[35.238,31.775],[35.2397,31.7794]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 14:36","text":"And he said, \"Abba, Father, all things are possible to you; remove this cup from me; yet not what I will, but what you will.\""},{"ref":"Luke 22:43–44","text":"And there appeared to him an angel from heaven, strengthening him. And being in an agony he prayed more earnestly; and his sweat became like great drops of blood falling down upon the ground."},{"ref":"Matthew 26:41","text":"Watch and pray that you may not enter into temptation; the spirit indeed is willing, but the flesh is weak.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Isaiah 51:17","note":"The cup of the Lord's wrath, drunk to the dregs."},{"ref":"2 Samuel 15:23, 30","note":"David, betrayed by his counselor, crosses the Kidron and goes up the Mount of Olives weeping."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/agony0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bellini,Giovanni_-_Agony_in_the_Garden_-_National_Gallery.jpg","title":"The Agony in the Garden","artist":"Giovanni Bellini","year":"c. 1458–60","medium":"Egg tempera on panel","location":"The National Gallery, London","description":"Christ kneels in prayer on a rocky outcrop at dawn while Peter, James and John sleep below. A small angel holds out a chalice in the sky, and Judas leads the soldiers along the road across the valley.","significance":"An early work by Bellini, closely modeled on Mantegna's version of the same subject; the two now hang in the same London gallery.","symbolism":"The chalice answers Christ's prayer, \"remove this cup from me\" (Mark 14:36); the dawn light already announces the day of the Passion.","impact":"Celebrated as one of the first Italian paintings to make light and landscape carry the emotion of the scene."},{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/agony1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Andrea_Mantegna_036.jpg","title":"The Agony in the Garden","artist":"Andrea Mantegna","year":"c. 1455–56","medium":"Egg tempera on panel","location":"The National Gallery, London","description":"Christ kneels on a rock ledge before a group of child angels holding the instruments of the Passion. The three apostles sleep in the foreground, and Judas and the soldiers come out of a walled Jerusalem behind.","significance":"Painted by Bellini's brother-in-law; Bellini knew it and made his own version.","symbolism":"A vulture on a dead tree waits as an omen of death, while the angels show the cross and column of the coming Passion.","impact":"With Bellini's picture, a famous paired case study in how two Renaissance masters treated one subject."}],"tags":["prayer","passion"],"orderNote":"Luke 22:43–44 (the angel and the bloody sweat) is missing from some early manuscripts but is in many others and in the Church's text. Robertson places the agony late in the night, at the start of the Jewish Friday."},
  {"id":"arrest","harmony":"§153","lane":"jesus","place":"gethsemane","date":"Thursday night, Passion Week, AD 33","title":"The betrayal and arrest","people":["Jesus","Judas Iscariot","Peter","Malchus","Soldiers and Temple officers","The disciples","A young man"],"gospels":{"Mt":"26:47–56","Mk":"14:43–52","Lk":"22:47–53","Jn":"18:2–12"},"summary":"While Jesus is still speaking, Judas arrives with a crowd armed with swords and clubs, sent by the chief priests, together with a band of soldiers. He greets Jesus with a kiss, the agreed sign. When Jesus says \"I am he,\" they draw back and fall to the ground. Peter cuts off the right ear of Malchus, the high priest's slave, but Jesus heals him and goes willingly. All the disciples forsake him and flee; Mark alone adds a young man who escapes, leaving his linen cloth behind.","scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 22:48","text":"but Jesus said to him, \"Judas, would you betray the Son of man with a kiss?\""},{"ref":"John 18:11","text":"Jesus said to Peter, \"Put your sword into its sheath; shall I not drink the cup which the Father has given me?\""},{"ref":"Mark 14:50","text":"And they all forsook him, and fled."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Zechariah 13:7","note":"\"Strike the shepherd, that the sheep may be scattered,\" which Jesus quoted that same night (Mark 14:27)."},{"ref":"2 Samuel 20:9–10","note":"Joab kisses Amasa in greeting and kills him: the treacherous kiss."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/arrest0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giotto_di_Bondone_-_No._31_Scenes_from_the_Life_of_Christ_-_15._The_Arrest_of_Christ_(Kiss_of_Judas)_-_WGA09216.jpg","title":"The Arrest of Christ (Kiss of Judas)","artist":"Giotto","year":"c. 1304–06","medium":"Fresco","location":"Scrovegni (Arena) Chapel, Padua","description":"Judas wraps Christ in his yellow cloak to kiss him. The two faces meet in profile at the center, surrounded by soldiers, torches, clubs and spears. At left Peter cuts off the ear of the high priest's servant.","significance":"Part of Giotto's cycle of the lives of Mary and Christ, painted for Enrico Scrovegni's private chapel. It is one of the most famous images of the Passion.","symbolism":"Christ's calm, steady gaze meets Judas' coarse, ape-like profile: the moment good and evil look each other in the face. Judas' cloak swallows Christ, as betrayal tries to swallow him.","impact":"The Scrovegni frescoes mark the birth of a new, human and dramatic style in Western painting; this scene is often reproduced as its high point."},{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/arrest1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Taking_of_Christ-Caravaggio_(c.1602).jpg","title":"The Taking of Christ","artist":"Caravaggio","year":"1602","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"National Gallery of Ireland, Dublin (on loan from the Jesuit Community)","description":"In a tight, dark frieze of figures, Judas presses his kiss on Christ as armored soldiers seize him. Christ's hands are folded in quiet surrender. At left a disciple flees screaming; at right a man holds up a lantern.","significance":"Painted in Rome for the Marchese Ciriaco Mattei. It was lost for two centuries and rediscovered in 1990 hanging in the Jesuit house in Dublin.","symbolism":"The man with the lantern at far right is generally taken to be Caravaggio himself: like the viewer, he looks on and holds a little light to the scene. The fleeing disciple at left is often linked to the young man of Mark 14:51–52.","impact":"Its rediscovery was a sensation in the art world, and it is now one of the best-known Caravaggios outside Italy."}],"tags":["passion","healing"],"orderNote":"Only Luke records the healing of the ear, and only John names Peter and Malchus. Many readers since early times have guessed that the young man of Mark 14:51–52 was Mark himself, but the Gospel does not say."},
  {"id":"annas","harmony":"§154","lane":"jesus","place":"caiaphas","from":"gethsemane","date":"Thursday night, Passion Week, AD 33","title":"Jesus before Annas","people":["Jesus","Annas","An officer","Peter","The other disciple"],"gospels":{"Jn":"18:12–14, 19–23"},"summary":"The arresting party binds Jesus and leads him first to Annas, father-in-law of the reigning high priest Caiaphas. Annas had been high priest himself (AD 6–15) and five of his sons held the office after him, so he still wielded great power. He questions Jesus about his disciples and his teaching. Jesus answers that he has taught openly, and when an officer strikes him he calmly asks why.","journey":"About 1 mile: back across the Kidron Valley and up into the Upper City. Tradition follows the ancient stepped street that climbs to St Peter in Gallicantu.","route":[[35.2397,31.7794],[35.2385,31.7765],[35.2372,31.7725],[35.235,31.7698],[35.233,31.77],[35.2315,31.7708]],"scripture":[{"ref":"John 18:13","text":"First they led him to Annas; for he was the father-in-law of Caiaphas, who was high priest that year."},{"ref":"John 18:20","text":"Jesus answered him, \"I have spoken openly to the world; I have always taught in synagogues and in the temple, where all Jews come together; I have said nothing secretly."},{"ref":"John 18:23","text":"Jesus answered him, \"If I have spoken wrongly, bear witness to the wrong; but if I have spoken rightly, why do you strike me?\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/annas0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Tribunal_of_Annas_(Le_tribunal_d'Anne)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"The Tribunal of Annas","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Guards drag Jesus by his robe through a pillared hall, away from the raised seat where Annas and the elders sit. A crowd of servants raise their fists behind him.","significance":"Part of Tissot's 350-picture Life of Christ, painted after long study trips to Palestine in the 1880s.","symbolism":"The light falls on the white-robed Christ in the middle of a rough, shadowed crowd; the high priests sit apart, high and remote.","impact":"Shows how the night hearing may have looked: a large priestly house, armed retainers and a hurried, violent handling of the prisoner."}],"tags":["passion","conflict"],"orderNote":"Only John records this hearing. Robertson counts it as the first of three stages of the Jewish trial: Annas, then an informal night session before Caiaphas and the Sanhedrin, then the formal sentence at dawn. John calls Annas \"the high priest\" here (18:19), as the Jews still did."},
  {"id":"sanhedrin","harmony":"§155","lane":"jesus","place":"caiaphas","date":"Before dawn Friday, Passion Week, AD 33","title":"The trial before Caiaphas and the Sanhedrin","people":["Jesus","Caiaphas","The chief priests, elders and scribes","False witnesses","The guards"],"gospels":{"Mt":"26:57, 59–68","Mk":"14:53, 55–65","Lk":"22:54, 63–65","Jn":"18:24"},"summary":"Annas sends Jesus bound to Caiaphas, where the council has gathered by night. Many false witnesses come forward, but their stories do not agree. Jesus stays silent until the high priest puts him under oath: is he the Christ, the Son of the Blessed? Jesus answers \"I am\" and speaks of the Son of man seated at the right hand of Power. Caiaphas tears his robe, the council judges him deserving of death, and they spit on him, blindfold him and strike him.","scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 14:61–62","text":"But he was silent and made no answer. Again the high priest asked him, \"Are you the Christ, the Son of the Blessed?\" And Jesus said, \"I am; and you will see the Son of man sitting at the right hand of Power, and coming with the clouds of heaven.\""},{"ref":"Mark 14:65","text":"And some began to spit on him, and to cover his face, and to strike him, saying to him, \"Prophesy!\" And the guards received him with blows."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Daniel 7:13–14","note":"\"One like a son of man\" coming with the clouds of heaven to receive an everlasting kingdom."},{"ref":"Isaiah 50:6","note":"The servant gives his back to the smiters and does not hide his face from shame and spitting."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/sanhedrin0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giotto_di_Bondone_-_No._32_Scenes_from_the_Life_of_Christ_-_16._Christ_before_Caiaphas_-_WGA09217.jpg","title":"Christ before Caiaphas","artist":"Giotto","year":"c. 1304–06","medium":"Fresco","location":"Scrovegni (Arena) Chapel, Padua","description":"At night, in a plain room lit by a single torch, Caiaphas tears open his robe on his seat while Annas sits beside him. Jesus stands before them held by guards, and a man raises his hand to strike him.","significance":"The scene follows the Arrest in Giotto's Passion cycle in the Scrovegni Chapel.","symbolism":"The torch on the wall marks the hour of darkness. Caiaphas' torn robe and the raised hand contrast with Christ's quiet, upright stance.","impact":"Part of the fresco cycle that shaped the way Western art tells the Passion as human drama."},{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/sanhedrin1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Torn_Cloak--Jesus_Condemned_to_Death_by_the_Jews_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"The Torn Cloak: Jesus Condemned to Death","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"In a lamplit hall packed to the walls, the high priest stands on a raised seat at right and rips the neck of his robe with both hands. Council members in the dark foreground raise their arms, and across the room Jesus stands bound among the guards.","significance":"Tissot imagines the night session as a crowded, theatrical assembly, following Matthew 26:65 and Mark 14:63.","symbolism":"Tearing one's garment was a sign of grief at hearing blasphemy; here it becomes the gesture that seals the sentence. Jesus, small and still in white, is the only calm figure in the room.","impact":"Brings out the drama and haste that Robertson saw in the night trial."}],"tags":["passion","conflict"],"orderNote":"Robertson treats this as an informal night session, followed by a formal vote after dawn (§157); Luke describes only the morning meeting. A night capital trial broke the later rules of the Mishnah, which Robertson takes as a sign of the council's haste, not of an unhistorical story. An ossuary inscribed \"Joseph son of Caiaphas\", found in Jerusalem in 1990, probably belonged to this high priest."},
  {"id":"denial","harmony":"§156","lane":"apostles","place":"caiaphas","date":"Before dawn Friday, Passion Week, AD 33","title":"Peter denies Jesus three times","people":["Peter","The maid who kept the door","Bystanders","A kinsman of Malchus","The other disciple","Jesus"],"gospels":{"Mt":"26:58, 69–75","Mk":"14:54, 66–72","Lk":"22:54–62","Jn":"18:15–18, 25–27"},"summary":"Peter follows at a distance and, through another disciple known to the high priest, gets into the courtyard, where he warms himself at a charcoal fire. A maid, then others, say he was with Jesus; his Galilean accent gives him away. Three times he denies it, the last time with an oath. The cock crows, the Lord turns and looks at Peter, and Peter goes out and weeps bitterly.","scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 26:74","text":"Then he began to invoke a curse on himself and to swear, \"I do not know the man.\" And immediately the cock crowed."},{"ref":"Luke 22:61–62","text":"And the Lord turned and looked at Peter. And Peter remembered the word of the Lord, how he had said to him, \"Before the cock crows today, you will deny me three times.\" And he went out and wept bitterly."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/denial0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Denial_of_Saint_Peter-Caravaggio_(1610).jpg","title":"The Denial of Saint Peter","artist":"Caravaggio","year":"1610","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York","description":"Three half-length figures by a fire at night. A soldier in a helmet points at Peter; the servant girl beside him points too. The aged Peter, lit from the side, turns his hands toward his own chest as he denies knowing Jesus.","significance":"One of Caravaggio's last paintings, made in Naples in the final months of his life.","symbolism":"Three pointing hands carry the drama: two accuse, while Peter's point back at himself. Sparks from the fire glow behind his head as the night closes in.","impact":"Its broad, rapid brushwork shows Caravaggio's late style. The Met acquired it in 1997, and it is one of the most moving images of Peter's fall."},{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/denial1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rembrandt_Harmensz._van_Rijn_012.jpg","title":"The Denial of Saint Peter","artist":"Rembrandt","year":"1660","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Rijksmuseum, Amsterdam","description":"A maid holds up a candle, shielding it with her hand, to see Peter's face; he spreads one hand in denial. Two soldiers in armor look on from the left, and in the shadows at upper right Jesus can be made out among his captors, turning to look back.","significance":"A late work, painted in the hard years after Rembrandt's bankruptcy.","symbolism":"The candle, shielded by the maid's hand, lights Peter's face at the moment of his fall, while Christ waits in the shadows.","impact":"One of the masterpieces of Rembrandt's late style, among the Rijksmuseum's best-known paintings."}],"tags":["passion"],"orderNote":"All four Gospels record three denials, but the speakers and places differ. Robertson suggests the denials ran through the hours of all three hearings (Luke notes an hour between the second and third), so each Gospel gathers them into one scene. The \"other disciple\" is usually taken to be John."},
  {"id":"dawntrial","harmony":"§157","lane":"jesus","place":"caiaphas","date":"Friday at dawn, Passion Week, AD 33","title":"The Sanhedrin condemns Jesus at dawn","people":["Jesus","The chief priests, elders and scribes","The whole council"],"gospels":{"Mt":"27:1","Mk":"15:1","Lk":"22:66–71"},"summary":"As soon as it is day, the whole council meets again to confirm the night's verdict. Asked once more if he is the Christ, Jesus answers that from now on the Son of man will be seated at the right hand of God, and does not deny he is the Son of God. They need no further witnesses. Because only the Roman governor can order an execution, they bind Jesus and lead him away to Pilate.","scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 15:1","text":"And as soon as it was morning the chief priests, with the elders and scribes, and the whole council held a consultation; and they bound Jesus and led him away and delivered him to Pilate."},{"ref":"Luke 22:70–71","text":"And they all said, \"Are you the Son of God, then?\" And he said to them, \"You say that I am.\" And they said, \"What further testimony do we need? We have heard it ourselves from his own lips.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Psalm 110:1","note":"\"Sit at my right hand, till I make your enemies your footstool.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/dawntrial0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Jesus_Led_from_Caiaphas_to_Pilate_(Jésus_conduit_de_Caïphe_à_Pilate)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"Jesus Led from Caiaphas to Pilate","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"In the early morning a jeering crowd fills a stepped street of the Upper City. Boys wave palm branches in mockery around Jesus, while a soldier leads the way and women watch from a balcony.","significance":"Tissot imagined each step of the Passion as a separate scene, following the route he believed Jesus was taken through Jerusalem.","symbolism":"The palm branches seem to recall the Palm Sunday welcome only days before, now turned into scorn.","impact":"Helps the viewer picture the short, crowded walk from the high priest's house to the governor, with the whole city awake for the feast."}],"tags":["passion","conflict"],"orderNote":"Robertson sees this morning session as an attempt to make the night's condemnation legal. Luke says they led him \"to their council\", which may mean the council chamber near the Temple rather than Caiaphas' house; the exact place is unknown."},
  {"id":"judasdeath","harmony":"§158","lane":"apostles","place":"akeldama","date":"Friday morning, Passion Week, AD 33","title":"The remorse and death of Judas","people":["Judas Iscariot","The chief priests and elders"],"gospels":{"Mt":"27:3–10","Acts":"1:18–19"},"summary":"When Judas sees that Jesus is condemned, he is seized with remorse and brings the thirty pieces of silver back to the chief priests: \"I have sinned in betraying innocent blood.\" They will not take it back. He throws the silver down in the Temple and goes and hangs himself. The priests will not put blood money in the treasury, so they buy a potter's field to bury strangers in, and it is called the Field of Blood.","scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 27:3–4","text":"When Judas, his betrayer, saw that he was condemned, he repented and brought back the thirty pieces of silver to the chief priests and the elders, saying, \"I have sinned in betraying innocent blood.\" They said, \"What is that to us? See to it yourself.\""},{"ref":"Matthew 27:5","text":"And throwing down the pieces of silver in the temple, he departed; and he went and hanged himself."},{"ref":"Acts 1:19","text":"And it became known to all the inhabitants of Jerusalem, so that the field was called in their language Akeldama, that is, Field of Blood."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Zechariah 11:12–13","note":"Thirty shekels of silver, the shepherd's wages, cast \"to the potter\" in the house of the Lord. Matthew credits the prophecy to Jeremiah, who also bought a field (Jeremiah 32) and went down to the potter's house (Jeremiah 18–19)."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/judasdeath0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Judas_Returning_the_Thirty_Silver_Pieces_-_Rembrandt.jpg","title":"Judas Returning the Thirty Pieces of Silver","artist":"Rembrandt","year":"1629","medium":"Oil on panel","location":"Private collection (Mulgrave Castle, England)","description":"Judas kneels, wringing his hands in despair, before the chief priests and elders. The silver coins lie scattered on the floor; the priests turn away, one raising his hand to refuse them.","significance":"An early masterpiece painted in Leiden when Rembrandt was about 23. Constantijn Huygens praised the figure of Judas above anything from antiquity.","symbolism":"The light falls on the priests and the great book of the Law, while Judas kneels in the half-dark: the law cannot take back the betrayal.","impact":"It launched Rembrandt's reputation as a painter of human emotion."},{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/judasdeath1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Judas_Returns_the_Money_(Judas_rend_l'argent)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"Judas Returns the Money","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"In a colonnade of the Temple, Judas whirls round in a swirling red-brown cloak, hurling the silver coins across the pavement. Priests and elders shrink back into the shadows behind the pillars.","significance":"One of Tissot's few scenes devoted to Judas alone, part of his Life of Christ series.","symbolism":"The coins scattered in the air and on the floor make the \"blood money\" visible; Judas' wild motion contrasts with the still, watching priests who refuse it.","impact":"Captures the despair of Matthew 27:5 more than any calm altarpiece could."}],"tags":["passion"],"orderNote":"Matthew places this here, though Judas may have died a little later. Matthew says Judas hanged himself and the priests bought the field; Peter in Acts says Judas acquired the field, fell headlong and burst open. Since early times, many have read these as two sides of one event: the priests bought the field with his money, and his body fell there."},
  {"id":"pilatefirst","harmony":"§159","lane":"rome","place":"praetorium","from":"caiaphas","date":"Friday, early morning, Passion Week, AD 33","title":"Jesus before Pilate","people":["Jesus","Pontius Pilate","The chief priests and elders"],"gospels":{"Mt":"27:2, 11–14","Mk":"15:1–5","Lk":"23:1–5","Jn":"18:28–38"},"summary":"Early in the morning the council hands Jesus over to Pontius Pilate, but will not enter the praetorium, so as not to be defiled before the Passover. They accuse him of perverting the nation, forbidding tribute to Caesar and calling himself a king. Inside, Pilate asks, \"Are you the King of the Jews?\" Jesus says his kingship is not of this world and that he came to bear witness to the truth. Pilate answers, \"What is truth?\" and goes out to declare, \"I find no crime in him.\"","journey":"Less than half a mile across the Upper City, from the high priest's house to Herod's palace on the western hill (or about a mile to the Antonia Fortress, if the praetorium was there).","route":[[35.2315,31.7708],[35.2305,31.7725],[35.2295,31.7742],[35.2285,31.7762]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 23:2","text":"And they began to accuse him, saying, \"We found this man perverting our nation, and forbidding us to give tribute to Caesar, and saying that he himself is Christ a king.\""},{"ref":"John 18:36","text":"Jesus answered, \"My kingship is not of this world; if my kingship were of this world, my servants would fight, that I might not be handed over to the Jews; but my kingship is not from the world.\""},{"ref":"John 18:37–38","text":"Pilate said to him, \"So you are a king?\" Jesus answered, \"You say that I am a king. For this I was born, and for this I have come into the world, to bear witness to the truth. Every one who is of the truth hears my voice.\" Pilate said to him, \"What is truth?\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/pilatefirst0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:What-is-truth02.jpg","title":"What Is Truth? (Christ and Pilate)","artist":"Nikolai Ge","year":"1890","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Tretyakov Gallery, Moscow","description":"Pilate, a heavy Roman in a white toga, stands in bright sunlight and gestures as he speaks. Jesus, gaunt and dishevelled, stands silent in the shadow, looking at him.","significance":"Ge's realistic picture was removed from exhibition in Russia as offensive soon after it was shown.","symbolism":"Light and shadow divide worldly power from the silent Truth standing before it.","impact":"A key work of Russian religious realism, still debated for its stark image of Christ."},{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/pilatefirst1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Jesus_Before_Pilate_First_Interview_(Jésus_devant_Pilate._Premier_entretien)._-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"Jesus Before Pilate, First Interview","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Inside a richly furnished hall with latticed windows, Pilate in a white toga with red borders leans forward from a cushioned bench to question Jesus, who stands before him barefoot with his hands bound behind his back.","significance":"Tissot follows John 18:33–38, where Pilate questions Jesus privately inside the praetorium while the accusers wait outside.","symbolism":"The empty space of floor between the two men makes the conversation about kingship and truth the whole subject of the picture.","impact":"A rare depiction of the private dialogue, which most artists pass over for the public trial scenes."}],"tags":["passion"],"orderNote":"Robertson divides the Roman trial into three stages: before Pilate, before Herod Antipas, and before Pilate again. John gives the private dialogue inside the praetorium that the other Gospels compress into one question. Where the praetorium stood (Herod's palace or the Antonia) is still debated; see the place note."},
  {"id":"herodantipas","harmony":"§160","lane":"herod","place":"herodpalace","from":"praetorium","date":"Friday, early morning, Passion Week, AD 33","title":"Jesus before Herod Antipas","people":["Jesus","Herod Antipas","Herod's soldiers","The chief priests and scribes"],"gospels":{"Lk":"23:6–12"},"summary":"Hearing that Jesus is a Galilean, Pilate sends him to Herod Antipas, tetrarch of Galilee, who is in Jerusalem for the feast. Herod, who had beheaded John the Baptist, is glad: he has long wanted to see Jesus and hopes to see a sign. Jesus gives him no answer at all. Herod and his soldiers mock him, dress him in gorgeous apparel and send him back. That day Herod and Pilate, once enemies, become friends.","journey":"A few hundred yards within the Upper City, to the old Hasmonean palace.","route":[[35.2285,31.7762],[35.2298,31.7764],[35.231,31.7765]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 23:8–9","text":"When Herod saw Jesus, he was very glad, for he had long desired to see him, because he had heard about him, and he was hoping to see some sign done by him. So he questioned him at some length; but he made no answer."},{"ref":"Luke 23:12","text":"And Herod and Pilate became friends with each other that very day, for before this they had been at enmity with each other."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Psalm 2:1–2","note":"\"The kings of the earth set themselves... against the Lord and his anointed.\" The early Church applied it to Herod and Pilate (Acts 4:25–27)."},{"ref":"Isaiah 53:7","note":"\"Like a sheep that before its shearers is dumb, so he opened not his mouth.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/herodantipas0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Jesus_Before_Herod_(Jésus_devant_Hérode)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"Jesus Before Herod","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Herod, in a richly patterned robe and crown, looks down from a balcony with his courtiers. In the courtyard below, a crowd throws a white cloak over Jesus and jostles him, while Roman soldiers look on and a boy picks up something to throw.","significance":"Tissot gives a whole picture to a scene told only by Luke, setting it in a palace courtyard he imagined from his studies of Jerusalem.","symbolism":"The white garment is Tissot's reading of the \"gorgeous apparel\" of Luke 23:11, a mock royal robe; Herod stays above, remote and idle, as Jesus stays silent below.","impact":"One of the few well-known images of this brief hearing, which most painters leave out of the Passion."}],"tags":["passion"],"orderNote":"Only Luke records this hearing. Luke had sources close to Herod's court: Joanna, wife of Herod's steward Chuza (Luke 8:3), and Manaen, a member of Herod's court (Acts 13:1)."},
  {"id":"barabbas","harmony":"§161a","lane":"rome","place":"praetorium","from":"herodpalace","date":"Friday, about 6 a.m., Passion Week, AD 33","title":"Barabbas, and Pilate washes his hands","people":["Jesus","Pontius Pilate","Barabbas","Pilate's wife","The chief priests","The crowd"],"gospels":{"Mt":"27:15–26","Mk":"15:6–15","Lk":"23:13–25","Jn":"18:39–40"},"summary":"Pilate tells the priests that neither he nor Herod has found Jesus guilty. He offers, by the feast's custom, to release a prisoner: Jesus or Barabbas, a rebel who had committed murder in an uprising. His wife sends word: \"Have nothing to do with that righteous man.\" Stirred up by the chief priests, the crowd asks for Barabbas and cries, \"Crucify him!\" Three times Pilate protests Jesus' innocence; then, fearing a riot, he washes his hands before the crowd, releases Barabbas and hands Jesus over to be crucified.","journey":"A few hundred yards back to the praetorium.","route":[[35.231,31.7765],[35.2298,31.7764],[35.2285,31.7762]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 27:17","text":"So when they had gathered, Pilate said to them, \"Whom do you want me to release for you, Barabbas or Jesus who is called Christ?\""},{"ref":"Matthew 27:19","text":"Besides, while he was sitting on the judgment seat, his wife sent word to him, \"Have nothing to do with that righteous man, for I have suffered much over him today in a dream.\""},{"ref":"Matthew 27:24","text":"So when Pilate saw that he was gaining nothing, but rather that a riot was beginning, he took water and washed his hands before the crowd, saying, \"I am innocent of this righteous man's blood; see to it yourselves.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Deuteronomy 21:6–7","note":"The elders wash their hands to declare themselves innocent of a man's blood."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/barabbas0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Munkacsy_-_Christ_in_front_of_Pilate.jpg","title":"Christ before Pilate","artist":"Mihály Munkácsy","year":"1881","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Déri Museum, Debrecen, Hungary","description":"In Pilate's crowded hall, Jesus in white stands calm while priests accuse him and the crowd shouts. Pilate, in white on his raised seat, ponders, and a man in the crowd raises his arms crying for crucifixion.","significance":"The first of Munkácsy's huge Christ trilogy; it toured Europe and America in the 1880s and drew enormous crowds.","symbolism":"Jesus' still, upright figure stands out against the agitated crowd; Pilate sits caught between them.","impact":"One of the most famous religious paintings of the 19th century."},{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/barabbas1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Pilate_Washes_His_Hands_(Pilate_se_lave_les_mains)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"Pilate Washes His Hands","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Seen from behind, Pilate in a white toga stands on a raised platform, holding out his hands as servants pour water from a vessel. Roman soldiers and standard-bearers stand by, and beyond the arch a vast crowd fills the square and the rooftops.","significance":"Tissot places the viewer among Pilate's guard, looking out with him at the crowd he is trying to satisfy.","symbolism":"We never see Pilate's face: the gesture of washing, meant to declare his innocence, becomes the only thing he offers. The Roman standards show the power he could have used.","impact":"Shows the public, theatrical side of Matthew 27:24, and the size of the feast-day crowd in the city."}],"tags":["passion"],"orderNote":"No source outside the Gospels mentions the Passover release. Some manuscripts of Matthew 27:16–17 call him \"Jesus Barabbas\"; Barabbas means \"son of the father\". Robertson stresses that Pilate cannot escape responsibility for his cowardly surrender, and that the guilt belongs to the plotters, not to a whole people. The Catholic Church teaches the same (Nostra Aetate 4)."},
  {"id":"eccehomo","harmony":"§161b","lane":"rome","place":"praetorium","date":"Friday, about 6 a.m., Passion Week, AD 33","title":"The scourging and \"Behold the man\"","people":["Jesus","Pontius Pilate","Roman soldiers","The chief priests and officers"],"gospels":{"Mt":"27:26","Mk":"15:15","Jn":"19:1–16"},"summary":"Pilate has Jesus scourged, a brutal Roman flogging with weighted whips. The soldiers crown him with thorns and dress him in purple. Pilate brings him out and says, \"Here is the man!\", perhaps hoping the sight will be enough, but the priests cry, \"Crucify him!\" When they say he made himself the Son of God, Pilate is more afraid. Jesus tells him he has no power except what is given from above. At last, seated on the judgment seat at the Pavement (Gabbatha), Pilate hands him over to be crucified.","scripture":[{"ref":"John 19:1","text":"Then Pilate took Jesus and scourged him."},{"ref":"John 19:5","text":"So Jesus came out, wearing the crown of thorns and the purple robe. Pilate said to them, \"Here is the man!\""},{"ref":"John 19:11","text":"Jesus answered him, \"You would have no power over me unless it had been given you from above; therefore he who delivered me to you has the greater sin.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Isaiah 53:5","note":"\"He was wounded for our transgressions... and with his stripes we are healed.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/eccehomo0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ecce_homo_by_Antonio_Ciseri_(1).jpg","title":"Ecce Homo","artist":"Antonio Ciseri","year":"1860–80","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Galleria d'arte moderna, Palazzo Pitti, Florence","description":"On a colonnaded balcony, Pilate, seen from behind in a pale toga, gestures toward Jesus, who stands with his back bare, hands bound and crowned with thorns, a red robe slipping from his shoulders. Below, a great crowd fills the square; at right Pilate's wife turns away, troubled.","significance":"Ciseri, a Swiss-Italian academic painter, worked on this picture for many years; it is his best-known work.","symbolism":"By turning Pilate and Jesus away from us, Ciseri puts the viewer among the officials on the balcony, facing the crowd that must answer, \"Here is the man!\"","impact":"Widely reproduced in Bibles and catechisms, it shaped how many people picture John 19:5."},{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/eccehomo1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Behold_the_Man_(Ecce_Homo)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"Behold the Man (Ecce Homo)","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"High on a balcony of the governor's palace, hung with a Roman banner, Pilate in white leans out and gestures toward Jesus, who stands beside him in a red robe and crown of thorns. A dense crowd fills the street far below, arms raised.","significance":"Tissot, who studied Jerusalem in the 1880s, sets the scene on a tall palace facing a narrow city street.","symbolism":"The steep height between balcony and street sets the lonely figure of Christ against the shouting mass below.","impact":"Conveys the scale of the crowd and the public nature of John 19:5."}],"tags":["passion"],"orderNote":"John places the scourging and crowning before the sentence; Matthew and Mark mention the scourging with the sentence and the crowning after it (§162). John's \"about the sixth hour\" (19:14) seems to clash with Mark's \"third hour\" for the crucifixion; Robertson follows the view that John counts from midnight, Roman style, so the sentence fell about 6 a.m. The words \"Ecce homo\" are the Latin Vulgate of John 19:5."},
  {"id":"mocking","harmony":"§162","lane":"rome","place":"praetorium","date":"Friday, between 6 and 9 a.m., AD 33","title":"The soldiers mock Jesus","people":["Jesus","The soldiers of the governor's battalion"],"gospels":{"Mt":"27:27–30","Mk":"15:16–19"},"summary":"The governor's soldiers take Jesus inside the palace and call together the whole battalion. They strip him, put a scarlet or purple cloak on him, press a crown of plaited thorns on his head and put a reed in his hand. Kneeling, they salute him, \"Hail, King of the Jews!\" Then they spit on him and strike his head with the reed.","scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 27:29","text":"and plaiting a crown of thorns they put it on his head, and put a reed in his right hand. And kneeling before him they mocked him, saying, \"Hail, King of the Jews!\""},{"ref":"Mark 15:19","text":"And they struck his head with a reed, and spat upon him, and they knelt down in homage to him."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Isaiah 53:3","note":"\"He was despised and rejected by men; a man of sorrows, and acquainted with grief.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/mocking0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Titian_-_Crowning_with_Thorns_-_WGA22806.jpg","title":"The Crowning with Thorns","artist":"Titian","year":"c. 1542–43","medium":"Oil on panel","location":"Musée du Louvre, Paris","description":"Soldiers drive the crown of thorns onto Christ's head with long staves, crowding round him on the steps before a stone arch. A bust of the emperor Tiberius looks down from above.","significance":"Painted for the church of Santa Maria delle Grazie in Milan; Napoleon's armies carried it to Paris.","symbolism":"The crossing rods form a cage of violence around Christ; the bust of Caesar names the power in whose name he is mocked as a king.","impact":"A landmark of Titian's dramatic middle period, showing the influence of Michelangelo and ancient sculpture."},{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/mocking1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Crowning_of_Thorns_(Le_couronnement_d'épines)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"The Crowning of Thorns","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"In a stone courtyard, Jesus sits on a block in a red cloak, his hands chained, crowned with thorns and holding a reed. Soldiers and servants crowd round, kneeling in mock homage, jeering, and pressing the crown down with sticks; one holds a basket of thorny branches.","significance":"Tissot follows Matthew 27:27–30 and Mark 15:16–19 detail by detail: the cloak, the crown, the reed and the kneeling mockery.","symbolism":"The mock court around a seated \"king\" turns every royal sign upside down; for Christian readers the mockery speaks the truth.","impact":"A vivid, crowded picture of the cruelty of the soldiers' game."}],"tags":["passion"],"orderNote":"Robertson notes that the Sanhedrin's men had mocked Jesus too (§155); Herod's soldiers did the same (§160). Pilate's soldiers were probably auxiliaries recruited from non-Jewish towns of the region, such as Sebaste and Caesarea."},
  {"id":"waycross","harmony":"§163","lane":"jesus","place":"golgotha","from":"praetorium","date":"Friday, before 9 a.m., AD 33","title":"The Way of the Cross","people":["Jesus","Simon of Cyrene","The daughters of Jerusalem","Two criminals","Roman soldiers"],"gospels":{"Mt":"27:31–34","Mk":"15:20–23","Lk":"23:26–33","Jn":"19:16–17"},"summary":"Jesus goes out bearing his own cross. The soldiers compel a passer-by, Simon of Cyrene, coming in from the country, to carry it behind him; Mark names his sons, Alexander and Rufus, as if his readers knew them. A great crowd follows, with women wailing, and Jesus tells them, \"Daughters of Jerusalem, do not weep for me.\" Two criminals are led out with him. At Golgotha they offer him wine mixed with myrrh, but he will not drink it.","journey":"A short walk, perhaps a quarter of a mile, from Herod's palace out through a gate in the city wall to Golgotha. The traditional Via Dolorosa, walked since the Middle Ages, runs about half a mile from the Antonia.","route":[[35.2285,31.7762],[35.2289,31.777],[35.2293,31.7778],[35.2296,31.7784]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 23:26","text":"And as they led him away, they seized one Simon of Cyrene, who was coming in from the country, and laid on him the cross, to carry it behind Jesus."},{"ref":"Luke 23:28","text":"But Jesus turning to them said, \"Daughters of Jerusalem, do not weep for me, but weep for yourselves and for your children."},{"ref":"John 19:17","text":"So they took Jesus, and he went out, bearing his own cross, to the place called the place of a skull, which is called in Hebrew Golgotha."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Genesis 22:6","note":"Isaac carries the wood for the sacrifice up the mountain."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/waycross0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giotto_di_Bondone_-_No._34_Scenes_from_the_Life_of_Christ_-_18._Road_to_Calvary_-_WGA09220.jpg","title":"The Road to Calvary","artist":"Giotto","year":"c. 1304–06","medium":"Fresco","location":"Scrovegni (Arena) Chapel, Padua","description":"Jesus carries his cross out through the city gate, led by soldiers. Behind, at the gate, his mother tries to follow and is pushed back.","significance":"One of the Passion scenes in Giotto's Scrovegni cycle.","symbolism":"The procession moves to the right, out of the city, toward the Crucifixion painted next on the wall; Mary held back at the gate is the sorrow of the mother that devotion remembers in the Stations of the Cross.","impact":"Giotto's spare, solemn storytelling influenced Passion imagery for centuries."},{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/waycross1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Simon_the_Cyrenian_Compelled_to_Carry_the_Cross_with_Jesus_(Simon_de_Cyrène_contraint_de_porter_la_Croix_avec_Jésus)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"Simon the Cyrenian Compelled to Carry the Cross with Jesus","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Jesus, crowned with thorns, has fallen face down on the paving stones under the cross. Soldiers heave the beams off him, and in the press of the crowd a passer-by is being seized to carry it. Women kneel in grief at the right; behind, a placard with the charge is carried above the crowd.","significance":"Tissot combines the Gospel detail of Simon with the traditional fall of Jesus on the road, one of the Stations of the Cross.","symbolism":"Jesus lies flat beneath the full cross, a picture of the weight laid on him; the grieving women recall the daughters of Jerusalem.","impact":"Shows the narrow city street, crowds and soldiers that make Simon's sudden conscription easy to imagine."}],"tags":["passion"],"orderNote":"The fourteen Stations of the Cross follow the traditional Via Dolorosa. Several stations come from tradition, not the Gospels: Jesus' three falls, his meeting with his mother, and Veronica wiping his face (a story first found in medieval writings). Robertson himself, writing in 1922, favored Gordon's Calvary as the site; most scholars today hold to the Holy Sepulchre."},
  {"id":"crucifixion","harmony":"§164a","lane":"jesus","place":"golgotha","date":"Friday, about 9 a.m., AD 33","title":"The Crucifixion","people":["Jesus","Two robbers","Roman soldiers","The chief priests and scribes","Passers-by"],"gospels":{"Mt":"27:35–44","Mk":"15:24–32","Lk":"23:33–38","Jn":"19:18–24"},"summary":"At the third hour they crucify Jesus between two robbers. He prays, \"Father, forgive them; for they know not what they do.\" Pilate's title over the cross, in Hebrew, Latin and Greek, reads \"Jesus of Nazareth, the King of the Jews\", and he refuses to change it. The soldiers divide his garments and cast lots for his seamless tunic. Passers-by, the chief priests and the soldiers mock him: \"He saved others; he cannot save himself.\"","scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 23:33–34","text":"And when they came to the place which is called The Skull, there they crucified him, and the criminals, one on the right and one on the left. And Jesus said, \"Father, forgive them; for they know not what they do.\""},{"ref":"John 19:19","text":"Pilate also wrote a title and put it on the cross; it read, \"Jesus of Nazareth, the King of the Jews.\""},{"ref":"Mark 15:25","text":"And it was the third hour, when they crucified him."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Psalm 22:16–18","note":"\"They have pierced my hands and feet... they divide my garments among them, and for my raiment they cast lots.\""},{"ref":"Isaiah 53:12","note":"He \"was numbered with the transgressors... and made intercession for the transgressors.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/crucifixion0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peter_Paul_Rubens_-_Raising_of_the_Cross_-_WGA20437.jpg","title":"The Raising of the Cross (oil sketch)","artist":"Peter Paul Rubens","year":"c. 1610","medium":"Oil on panel","location":"Musée du Louvre, Paris","description":"Three panels in Rubens' rapid sketch. In the center, straining, muscular men heave the cross with Christ nailed to it up from the ground on a steep diagonal; at left John, Mary and the weeping women look on; at right Roman officers on horseback oversee the execution.","significance":"Rubens' preparatory sketch for his great triptych painted for the church of St Walburga in Antwerp (now in Antwerp Cathedral), soon after he returned from Italy.","symbolism":"The diagonal of the cross cuts across the picture as the whole of human effort is spent lifting it; the three panels join grief, labor and power around the one dying man.","impact":"The triptych made Rubens' name in Flanders and announced the full Baroque style in northern Europe."},{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/crucifixion1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_What_Our_Lord_Saw_from_the_Cross_(Ce_que_voyait_Notre-Seigneur_sur_la_Croix)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"What Our Lord Saw from the Cross","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"The scene is painted from the viewpoint of Jesus on the cross, looking down. Below are his mother, John and the other women, with Mary Magdalene prostrate at the foot of the cross. Around them stand soldiers, mounted priests and elders, and crowds on the hillside, with a rock-cut tomb in the garden behind.","significance":"Tissot's most original composition, and the best-known work of his Life of Christ.","symbolism":"We never see Jesus; instead we stand in his place and look at the faces of those who love him and those who mock him. The open tomb in the background quietly anticipates the burial and the Resurrection.","impact":"Widely reproduced; its bold point of view seems to anticipate the camera angles of film."}],"tags":["passion","prayer"],"orderNote":"Robertson dates the crucifixion to April AD 30 (or 29). This map follows the date many scholars now prefer, Friday, 3 April AD 33; both years have a Passover that fits a Friday crucifixion. The Synoptics present the Last Supper as the Passover meal, while John places the crucifixion on the day of Preparation, when the lambs were slain; Robertson, like many harmonists, takes the Supper as the Passover meal. He follows Mark's order for the sayings on the cross: \"Father, forgive them,\" the promise to the robber, and the words to Mary and John."},
  {"id":"goodthief","harmony":"§164b","lane":"jesus","place":"golgotha","date":"Friday, 9 a.m. to noon, AD 33","title":"The good thief","people":["Jesus","The penitent robber","The other robber"],"gospels":{"Mt":"27:44","Mk":"15:32","Lk":"23:39–43"},"summary":"At first both robbers revile Jesus. Then one of them rebukes the other: they are suffering justly, \"but this man has done nothing wrong.\" He asks Jesus to remember him when he comes in his kingly power. Jesus promises him Paradise that very day. Tradition calls him the good thief and names him Dismas.","scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 23:40–41","text":"But the other rebuked him, saying, \"Do you not fear God, since you are under the same sentence of condemnation? And we indeed justly; for we are receiving the due reward of our deeds; but this man has done nothing wrong.\""},{"ref":"Luke 23:42–43","text":"And he said, \"Jesus, remember me when you come in your kingly power.\" And he said to him, \"Truly, I say to you, today you will be with me in Paradise.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/goodthief0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Pardon_of_the_Good_Thief_(Le_pardon_du_bon_Larron)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"The Pardon of the Good Thief","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Under a darkening sky, Jesus hangs on the cross at right; close beside him, the penitent thief turns his head toward him. The other robber hangs farther off at left above a shouting crowd, while soldiers, women and workmen with ladders fill the hill below.","significance":"Tissot sets the thief's cross close to Jesus, so the whispered exchange of Luke 23:42–43 can be imagined in the middle of the noise.","symbolism":"The two robbers are placed apart: one near Christ and turned toward him, one far off among the mockers, the two responses to the cross.","impact":"A vivid picture of a scene loved in Christian devotion as the promise of mercy even at the last hour."},{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/goodthief1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Titian_-_Christ_and_the_Good_Thief_-_WGA22832.jpg","title":"Christ and the Good Thief","artist":"Titian","year":"c. 1566","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Pinacoteca Nazionale, Bologna","description":"Christ hangs on the cross at right, his haloed head bowed toward the penitent thief, who looks up and raises his hand toward him from his own cross under a darkening sky.","significance":"A late work by Titian, unusual for showing only Christ and the good thief.","symbolism":"The two crosses lean toward each other: a picture of the conversation of Luke 23:42–43 and of mercy offered at the last hour.","impact":"Shows Titian's free, loose late brushwork and his deep religious feeling in old age."}],"tags":["passion"],"orderNote":"Matthew and Mark say the robbers reviled him; only Luke tells of one repenting. Many harmonists take it that both mocked at first and one later changed his heart. The name Dismas comes from the apocryphal Gospel of Nicodemus, not from Scripture."},
  {"id":"marycross","harmony":"§164c","lane":"jesus","place":"golgotha","date":"Friday, 9 a.m. to noon, AD 33","title":"Mary and the beloved disciple at the cross","people":["Jesus","Mary, his mother","The beloved disciple (John)","Mary the wife of Clopas","Mary Magdalene"],"gospels":{"Jn":"19:25–27"},"summary":"Near the cross stand Jesus' mother, her sister, Mary the wife of Clopas, and Mary Magdalene. Seeing his mother and the disciple whom he loved, Jesus entrusts them to each other: \"Woman, behold, your son!\" and \"Behold, your mother!\" From that hour the disciple takes her into his own home. The Church has long seen in this gift Mary given as mother to all disciples.","scripture":[{"ref":"John 19:25","text":"But standing by the cross of Jesus were his mother, and his mother's sister, Mary the wife of Clopas, and Mary Magdalene."},{"ref":"John 19:26–27","text":"When Jesus saw his mother, and the disciple whom he loved standing near, he said to his mother, \"Woman, behold, your son!\" Then he said to the disciple, \"Behold, your mother!\" And from that hour the disciple took her to his own home."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/marycross0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rogier_van_der_Weyden,_Netherlandish_(active_Tournai_and_Brussels)_-_The_Crucifixion,_with_the_Virgin_and_Saint_John_the_Evangelist_Mourning_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg","title":"The Crucifixion, with the Virgin and Saint John the Evangelist Mourning","artist":"Rogier van der Weyden","year":"c. 1460","medium":"Oil on panel","location":"Philadelphia Museum of Art","description":"Two panels against a bare stone wall hung with red cloth. On the left the fainting Mary is held up by John; on the right Christ hangs on the cross.","significance":"Painted late in Rogier's career, perhaps for a Carthusian monastery; it was probably once the wings or panels of a larger work.","symbolism":"The bare wall and dark sky leave nothing but the grief of Mary and John and the body of Christ; mother and disciple are joined to each other as Jesus commanded.","impact":"Regarded as one of the most moving and austere works of early Netherlandish painting."},{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/marycross1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Woman_Behold_Thy_Son_(Stabat_Mater)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"Woman, Behold Your Son (Stabat Mater)","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"At the foot of the cross, John in white looks up at Jesus, while Mary, in blue-grey, stands with clasped hands. Mary Magdalene, in a many-colored robe, kneels and clings to the wood of the cross. Soldiers and elders stand behind.","significance":"Tissot's French title, Stabat Mater, recalls the medieval hymn of Mary standing at the cross.","symbolism":"The tall upright of the cross divides and joins the figures: Jesus is cut off at the top of the frame, and the scene is about those he entrusts to each other below.","impact":"A tender devotional image of John 19:25–27, in which the Church sees Mary given as mother to the beloved disciple."}],"tags":["passion"],"orderNote":"Only John records this. The disciple is traditionally John, son of Zebedee. Whether \"his mother's sister\" is the same person as \"Mary the wife of Clopas\" (three women) or a fourth woman, perhaps Salome, is debated. The medieval hymn Stabat Mater meditates on this scene."},
  {"id":"death","harmony":"§165","lane":"jesus","place":"golgotha","date":"Friday, noon to 3 p.m., AD 33","title":"The darkness and the death of Jesus","people":["Jesus","Bystanders","A soldier with vinegar"],"gospels":{"Mt":"27:45–50","Mk":"15:33–37","Lk":"23:44–46","Jn":"19:28–30"},"summary":"From the sixth hour to the ninth, darkness covers the whole land. At the ninth hour Jesus cries out the opening of Psalm 22, \"My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?\", and some think he is calling Elijah. He says \"I thirst\", and they lift a sponge of vinegar to his mouth on a reed. Then he says \"It is finished\" and, crying with a loud voice, \"Father, into your hands I commit my spirit!\" He bows his head and gives up his spirit.","scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 15:33–34","text":"And when the sixth hour had come, there was darkness over the whole land until the ninth hour. And at the ninth hour Jesus cried with a loud voice, \"Elo-i, Elo-i, lama sabach-thani?\" which means, \"My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?\""},{"ref":"John 19:30","text":"When Jesus had received the vinegar, he said, \"It is finished\"; and he bowed his head and gave up his spirit."},{"ref":"Luke 23:46","text":"Then Jesus, crying with a loud voice, said, \"Father, into your hands I commit my spirit!\" And having said this he breathed his last."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Psalm 22:1","note":"\"My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?\" The psalm moves from desolation to praise."},{"ref":"Psalm 31:5","note":"\"Into your hand I commit my spirit,\" a Jewish evening prayer."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/death0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Matthias_Grünewald_-_The_Crucifixion_-_WGA10723.jpg","title":"The Crucifixion (Isenheim Altarpiece)","artist":"Matthias Grünewald","year":"c. 1512–16","medium":"Oil on panel","location":"Musée Unterlinden, Colmar","description":"Against a black sky, the dead Christ hangs from a bending cross, his body torn and covered with sores, his hands clawed in agony. At left John supports the fainting Mary and Mary Magdalene kneels in prayer; at right John the Baptist points to Christ, with a lamb at his feet.","significance":"The central panel of the altarpiece painted for the Antonite monastery at Isenheim, whose monks cared for victims of ergotism (\"Saint Anthony's fire\") and other skin diseases.","symbolism":"Christ's ravaged body showed the sick that he shared their suffering. John the Baptist's words, \"He must increase, but I must decrease\" (John 3:30), are written beside him; the lamb with its cross recalls \"Behold, the Lamb of God.\"","impact":"Often called the most powerful image of the Crucifixion ever painted, it has moved artists and writers from the 19th century onward."},{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/death1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_It_Is_Finished_(Consummatum_Est)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"It Is Finished (Consummatum Est)","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Christ hangs dead on the cross under a dark sky. Above him shines a triangle bearing the Hebrew name of God, and figures of the Old Testament, holding up scrolls, gather in the heavens around the cross. At its foot the women grieve.","significance":"One of the few visionary images in Tissot's otherwise realistic Life of Christ.","symbolism":"The prophets and patriarchs holding their scrolls show the Scriptures fulfilled in his death (John 19:28–30); the triangle with the divine name speaks of the Trinity and of God present at the cross.","impact":"A rare attempt to paint the meaning, not only the appearance, of Jesus' last word in John's Gospel."}],"tags":["passion","prayer"],"orderNote":"Robertson orders the last four sayings: the cry of desolation (Mark, Matthew), \"I thirst\" (John), \"It is finished\" (John), and \"Father, into your hands\" (Luke). The darkness cannot have been a solar eclipse, which is impossible at Passover's full moon."},
  {"id":"veiltorn","harmony":"§166","lane":"jesus","place":"golgotha","date":"Friday, about 3 p.m., AD 33","title":"The veil torn and the centurion's confession","people":["The centurion","Mary Magdalene","Mary the mother of James and Joses","Salome","The women from Galilee","The crowds"],"gospels":{"Mt":"27:51–56","Mk":"15:38–41","Lk":"23:45, 47–49"},"summary":"At the moment of his death the curtain of the Temple is torn in two from top to bottom. Matthew adds an earthquake, split rocks and opened tombs, and saints raised who appear in the holy city after his resurrection. The centurion who stood facing him says, \"Truly this man was the Son of God!\" The crowds go home beating their breasts, while the women who had followed him from Galilee watch from a distance.","scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 27:51","text":"And behold, the curtain of the temple was torn in two, from top to bottom; and the earth shook, and the rocks were split;"},{"ref":"Mark 15:39","text":"And when the centurion, who stood facing him, saw that he thus breathed his last, he said, \"Truly this man was the Son of God!\""},{"ref":"Luke 23:49","text":"And all his acquaintances and the women who had followed him from Galilee stood at a distance and saw these things."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Exodus 26:31–33","note":"The veil that separated the Holy Place from the Most Holy, which only the high priest entered once a year."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/veiltorn0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Confession_of_the_Centurion_(La_Confession_du_Centurion)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"The Confession of the Centurion","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"On the hilltop outside the walls of Jerusalem, the centurion in his red cloak sinks to his hands and knees, head bowed. Around him other soldiers kneel and bow to the ground; one shades his eyes and stares upward.","significance":"Tissot gives a whole picture to the moment the Roman officer in charge of the execution confesses Jesus (Mark 15:39).","symbolism":"The executioners themselves end up on their knees: the first Gentile confession of faith at the cross. The city wall behind them marks the place \"outside the gate\".","impact":"Brings out the reversal that Mark makes the climax of his Gospel, which begins and ends with Jesus as \"the Son of God\"."},{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/veiltorn1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Earthquake_(Le_tremblement_de_terre_)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"The Earthquake","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Under a dark sky, with the city walls and hills behind, the onlookers at Golgotha throw themselves face down on the cracked rock around the three crosses. Only the feet of the crucified are seen at the top of the picture.","significance":"Illustrates Matthew 27:51–54: the earth shaking and the rocks split at Jesus' death.","symbolism":"By cutting off the crosses at the top, Tissot makes us look down with the terrified crowd; the split rock beneath them shows creation itself responding to the death of its Lord.","impact":"A dramatic image of a detail only Matthew records."}],"tags":["passion","miracle"],"orderNote":"Luke mentions the torn curtain just before Jesus' death, the others just after. In Luke the centurion says \"Certainly this man was innocent!\" Only Matthew tells of the raised saints."},
  {"id":"piercing","harmony":"§167a","lane":"jesus","place":"golgotha","date":"Friday afternoon, AD 33","title":"His side is pierced","people":["Jesus","Roman soldiers","The two robbers","The beloved disciple"],"gospels":{"Jn":"19:31–37"},"summary":"So that the bodies will not stay on the crosses over the great Sabbath, the Jewish leaders ask Pilate to have the legs broken and the bodies removed. The soldiers break the legs of the two robbers, but find Jesus already dead. One of them pierces his side with a spear, and at once blood and water flow out. John insists that an eyewitness saw it, so that we may believe. Tradition names the soldier Longinus.","scripture":[{"ref":"John 19:33–34","text":"but when they came to Jesus and saw that he was already dead, they did not break his legs. But one of the soldiers pierced his side with a spear, and at once there came out blood and water."},{"ref":"John 19:36–37","text":"For these things took place that the scripture might be fulfilled, \"Not a bone of him shall be broken.\" And again another scripture says, \"They shall look on him whom they have pierced.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Exodus 12:46","note":"No bone of the Passover lamb may be broken."},{"ref":"Zechariah 12:10","note":"\"When they look on him whom they have pierced, they shall mourn for him.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/piercing1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Thieves_Legs_Are_Broken_(On_rompt_les_jambes_aux_larrons)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"The Thieves' Legs Are Broken","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Soldiers, one on a ladder and one on the ground, swing heavy clubs at the legs of the two robbers bound to their crosses. Jesus hangs dead on the cross at left, his head bowed, while women watch from below.","significance":"Tissot follows John 19:31–33 closely, including the detail that the robbers were tied to their crosses.","symbolism":"The violence done to the robbers sets off the stillness of Jesus, already dead, whose bones are not broken, as John says the Scripture foretold.","impact":"Shows how the bodies were hurried down before the Sabbath, and why John stresses that Jesus' legs were spared."}],"tags":["passion"],"orderNote":"Robertson includes this in §167 as the proof of Jesus' death before burial. The Fathers saw in the blood and water the sacraments of Baptism and the Eucharist flowing from Christ's side, and the Church born from it. The name Longinus comes from later tradition."},
  {"id":"burial","harmony":"§167b","lane":"jesus","place":"tomb","from":"golgotha","date":"Friday, before sunset, AD 33","title":"The burial of Jesus","people":["Joseph of Arimathea","Nicodemus","Pontius Pilate","The centurion"],"gospels":{"Mt":"27:57–60","Mk":"15:42–46","Lk":"23:50–54","Jn":"19:38–42"},"summary":"Joseph of Arimathea, a respected member of the council who had not agreed to its decision, takes courage and asks Pilate for the body. Pilate, surprised Jesus is already dead, confirms it with the centurion and grants it. Nicodemus brings about a hundred pounds of myrrh and aloes. They take the body down, wrap it in linen with the spices, and lay it in Joseph's own new rock-hewn tomb in a nearby garden, where no one had yet been laid. A great stone is rolled against the door as the Sabbath begins.","journey":"A few dozen yards from the cross to a garden tomb close at hand (John 19:41–42).","route":[[35.2296,31.7784],[35.2298,31.7786]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 15:43","text":"Joseph of Arimathea, a respected member of the council, who was also himself looking for the kingdom of God, took courage and went to Pilate, and asked for the body of Jesus."},{"ref":"John 19:39–40","text":"Nicodemus also, who had at first come to him by night, came bringing a mixture of myrrh and aloes, about a hundred pounds' weight. They took the body of Jesus, and bound it in linen cloths with the spices, as is the burial custom of the Jews."},{"ref":"Matthew 27:59–60","text":"And Joseph took the body, and wrapped it in a clean linen shroud, and laid it in his own new tomb, which he had hewn in the rock; and he rolled a great stone to the door of the tomb, and departed."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Isaiah 53:9","note":"\"They made his grave with the wicked and with a rich man in his death.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","img":"img/burial0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Entombment_of_Christ-Caravaggio_(c.1602-3).jpg","title":"The Entombment of Christ","artist":"Caravaggio","year":"1603–04","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Vatican Pinacoteca, Vatican City","description":"Nicodemus and John lower Christ's body toward the tomb's stone slab. Behind them the Virgin Mary, Mary Magdalene and Mary of Clopas grieve, one raising her arms to heaven.","significance":"Painted for the Vittrice chapel in the Chiesa Nuova in Rome; it was taken to Paris by Napoleon and returned to the Vatican.","symbolism":"The corner of the stone slab juts toward the viewer, as if the body were being laid on the altar: Christ's burial and the Eucharist are joined.","impact":"Rubens and many later painters copied it; it is often counted among the greatest altarpieces of the 17th century."},{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/burial1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Descent_from_the_Cross_(La_descent_de_croix)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"The Descent from the Cross","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Two men on ladders lower Jesus' body from the cross with long linen cloths, while others reach up to receive it. The women crowd below in grief under a stormy sky, with the walls of Jerusalem behind.","significance":"Tissot follows John 19:38–40, with Joseph of Arimathea and Nicodemus taking down the body.","symbolism":"The white linen running from the crossbeam to the body anticipates the burial shroud.","impact":"Shows how the body may have been taken down in haste before the Sabbath."}],"tags":["passion"],"orderNote":"Devotion (the Stations of the Cross and the Pietà) pictures Mary receiving her son's body when it is taken down; the Gospels do not say so. Arimathea is usually identified with Ramathaim, northwest of Jerusalem."},
  {"id":"womenwatch","harmony":"§168a","lane":"women","place":"tomb","date":"Friday evening to Saturday, AD 33","title":"The women watch the tomb","people":["Mary Magdalene","Mary the mother of Joses","The women from Galilee"],"gospels":{"Mt":"27:61","Mk":"15:47","Lk":"23:55–56"},"summary":"Mary Magdalene and the other Mary sit opposite the tomb and see where and how the body is laid. The women who had come with him from Galilee go home and prepare spices and ointments. Then, as the Sabbath begins at sundown, they rest according to the commandment, waiting to return when it is over.","stay":"The women rested in Jerusalem through the Sabbath, Friday evening to Saturday evening.","scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 15:47","text":"Mary Magdalene and Mary the mother of Joses saw where he was laid."},{"ref":"Luke 23:55–56","text":"The women who had come with him from Galilee followed, and saw the tomb, and how his body was laid; then they returned, and prepared spices and ointments. On the sabbath they rested according to the commandment."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Exodus 20:8–10","note":"The Sabbath commandment the women keep."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/womenwatch0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Two_Marys_Watch_the_Tomb_(Les_deux_Maries_observent_le_tombeau)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"The Two Marys Watch the Tomb","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Two women wrapped in mantles sit side by side on a rock ledge in an old quarry, keeping watch. The three crosses stand empty on the hill behind them against a grey sky.","significance":"Illustrates Matthew 27:61: \"Mary Magdalene and the other Mary were there, sitting opposite the sepulchre.\"","symbolism":"The empty crosses and the quiet, worn stone make a picture of waiting and grief; the women's faithful watch prepares for their visit at dawn on Sunday.","impact":"Tissot imagined the tomb in a disused quarry near Golgotha, as archaeology has since shown the area to have been."}],"tags":["passion"],"orderNote":"Robertson notes that the Sabbath began at 6 p.m. on Friday, so the women's rest covered our Friday night and Saturday. Luke has them prepare spices before the Sabbath, Mark buying them after it (16:1); they may have done both."},
  {"id":"guard","harmony":"§168b","lane":"rome","place":"tomb","date":"Saturday, Passion Week, AD 33","title":"The guard at the tomb","people":["The chief priests and Pharisees","Pontius Pilate","The guard of soldiers"],"gospels":{"Mt":"27:62–66"},"summary":"The next day, the Sabbath, the chief priests and Pharisees go to Pilate. They remember that Jesus said he would rise after three days and fear that his disciples will steal the body. Pilate tells them they have a guard and should make the tomb as secure as they can. They seal the stone and set a watch.","scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 27:63–64","text":"\"Sir, we remember how that imposter said, while he was still alive, 'After three days I will rise again.' Therefore order the sepulchre to be made secure until the third day, lest his disciples go and steal him away, and tell the people, 'He has risen from the dead,' and the last fraud will be worse than the first.\""},{"ref":"Matthew 27:65–66","text":"Pilate said to them, \"You have a guard of soldiers; go, make it as secure as you can.\" So they went and made the sepulchre secure by sealing the stone and setting a guard."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","img":"img/guard0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Watch_Over_the_Tomb_(La_garde_du_Tombeau)_-_James_Tissot.jpg","title":"The Watch Over the Tomb","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Soldiers keep guard at the entrance of a rock-cut tomb by lantern light. Some doze wrapped in cloaks, one sits slumped in the doorway, another lies on top of the tomb, and a sentry on the hill shades his eyes as dawn breaks.","significance":"Illustrates Matthew 27:66, the guard set at the sealed tomb.","symbolism":"The weary, sleeping guards and the first light on the horizon hint at what is coming: no seal or soldier will hold the tomb.","impact":"A quiet, almost ordinary scene that makes the guard of Matthew's account easy to picture."}],"tags":["passion"],"orderNote":"Only Matthew records the guard, and he later tells how the guards were bribed to say the disciples stole the body (28:11–15). Pilate's words \"You have a guard\" can mean either the Temple police or a Roman detachment; Robertson takes it as a guard of Roman soldiers."},
  {"id":"spices","harmony":"§169","lane":"women","place":"tomb","date":"Saturday evening, AD 33","title":"The women watch the tomb and buy spices","people":["Mary Magdalene","Mary the mother of James","Salome"],"gospels":{"Mt":"28:1","Mk":"16:1"},"summary":"The women who had followed Jesus from Galilee rested on the Sabbath, as the Law required. Late on the Sabbath they went to look at the tomb. When the Sabbath ended at sundown, Mary Magdalene, Mary the mother of James and Salome bought spices so they could anoint his body at first light. They expected to find a dead man.","scripture":[{"ref":"Mark 16:1","text":"And when the sabbath was past, Mary Magdalene, and Mary the mother of James, and Salome, bought spices, so that they might go and anoint him."},{"ref":"Matthew 28:1","text":"Now after the sabbath, toward the dawn of the first day of the week, Mary Magdalene and the other Mary went to see the sepulchre."}],"art":[],"tags":[],"orderNote":"Matthew 28:1 can be read \"late on the sabbath\" (Robertson, following the King James sense) or \"after the sabbath, toward the dawn\" (RSV). Robertson takes it as a Saturday-evening visit to see the tomb, before the Sunday-morning visit of §171; many others read it as the same Sunday visit."},
  {"id":"earthquake","harmony":"§170","lane":"jesus","place":"tomb","date":"Sunday before dawn, AD 33","title":"The earthquake and the angel at the tomb","people":["An angel of the Lord","The guards"],"gospels":{"Mt":"28:2–4"},"summary":"Before sunrise on the first day of the week there is a great earthquake. An angel of the Lord comes down, rolls back the stone and sits on it. The guards set at the tomb shake with fear and become like dead men. No Gospel describes the moment of the Resurrection itself: the stone is rolled away not to let Jesus out, but to let the witnesses see that the tomb is empty.","scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 28:2","text":"And behold, there was a great earthquake; for an angel of the Lord descended from heaven and came and rolled back the stone, and sat upon it."},{"ref":"Matthew 28:3–4","text":"His appearance was like lightning, and his raiment white as snow. And for fear of him the guards trembled and became like dead men."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"The Resurrection","artist":"Piero della Francesca","year":"c. 1460s","medium":"Fresco","location":"Museo Civico, Sansepolcro","description":"Christ stands with one foot on the edge of the tomb, holding a banner and looking straight out at us, while four soldiers sleep below.","significance":"Painted for the town hall of Sansepolcro, whose name means \"Holy Sepulchre.\"","symbolism":"The landscape is bare on the left and in leaf on the right: winter turns to spring. One sleeping soldier is traditionally said to be Piero's self-portrait.","impact":"Aldous Huxley called it \"the best picture in the world\" (1925); the story goes that a British officer spared the town from shelling in 1944 because of it.","img":"img/earthquake0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Resurrection.JPG"},{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"The Angel Seated on the Stone of the Tomb","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"A pale, almost transparent angel in white fills the doorway of the tomb, beside the empty burial shelf where the grave cloths lie.","significance":"Part of Tissot's Life of Christ, 350 watercolors made after his return to the Church and three study trips to Palestine (1886–1896).","symbolism":"Tissot paints the angel in white on gray, light dissolving into light, to suggest \"raiment white as snow.\"","impact":"Exhibited in Paris, London and New York in the 1890s, the series drew huge crowds; the Brooklyn Museum bought all 350 in 1900.","img":"img/earthquake1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Angel_Seated_on_the_Stone_of_the_Tomb_(L'ange_assis_sur_la_pierre_du_tombeau)_-_James_Tissot.jpg"}],"tags":["miracle"],"orderNote":"Robertson counts the days in the Jewish way: part of Friday, all of Saturday and the early hours of Sunday make \"the third day.\" Only Matthew tells of the guard, the earthquake and the angel on the stone."},
  {"id":"emptytomb","harmony":"§171","lane":"women","place":"tomb","date":"Sunday at dawn, AD 33","title":"The women find the tomb empty","people":["Mary Magdalene","Mary the mother of James","Salome","Joanna","Other women","Angels"],"gospels":{"Mt":"28:5–8","Mk":"16:2–8","Lk":"24:1–8","Jn":"20:1"},"summary":"At early dawn the women come with their spices, wondering who will roll away the large stone. They find it already rolled back and the body gone. An angel (Luke and John speak of two) tells them, \"He has risen, he is not here,\" and sends them to tell the disciples, and Peter, that Jesus is going before them to Galilee. The first witnesses of the empty tomb are women.","scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 24:5","text":"and as they were frightened and bowed their faces to the ground, the men said to them, \"Why do you seek the living among the dead? He is not here, but has risen."},{"ref":"Mark 16:6","text":"And he said to them, \"Do not be amazed; you seek Jesus of Nazareth, who was crucified. He has risen, he is not here; see the place where they laid him."},{"ref":"Mark 16:7","text":"But go, tell his disciples and Peter that he is going before you to Galilee; there you will see him, as he told you.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Hosea 6:2","note":"\"On the third day he will raise us up, that we may live before him.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"Resurrection of Christ and Women at the Tomb","artist":"Fra Angelico","year":"c. 1440–42","medium":"Fresco","location":"Convent of San Marco, Florence (Cell 8)","description":"An angel seated on the open tomb points upward to the risen Christ in a mandorla of light, unseen by the women who peer into the empty tomb. St Dominic kneels at the left.","significance":"Painted for a friar's cell, as an aid to prayer; St Dominic kneels at the side as a model of contemplation.","symbolism":"The women look into the empty tomb and do not yet see Christ above them: the viewer sees what they will soon believe.","impact":"The San Marco cell frescoes are among the most loved devotional images of the Italian Renaissance.","img":"img/emptytomb0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fra_Angelico_-_Resurrection_of_Christ_and_Women_at_the_Tomb_(Cell_8)_-_WGA00542.jpg"},{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"Mary Magdalene and the Holy Women at the Tomb","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"The women shrink back at the tomb door: inside stands a shining angel beside the folded grave cloths, while the guards lie sprawled on the ground like dead men.","significance":"Part of Tissot's Life of Christ, 350 watercolors made after his return to the Church and three study trips to Palestine (1886–1896).","symbolism":"The red lanterns of the night watch still hang by the door, but the light now comes from inside the tomb.","impact":"Exhibited in Paris, London and New York in the 1890s, the series drew huge crowds; the Brooklyn Museum bought all 350 in 1900.","img":"img/emptytomb1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Mary_Magdalene_and_the_Holy_Women_at_the_Tomb_(Madeleine_et_les_saintes_femmes_au_tombeau)_-_James_Tissot.jpg"}],"tags":[],"orderNote":"The four accounts differ in detail: one angel (Matthew, Mark) or two (Luke, John), \"while it was still dark\" (John) or \"when the sun had risen\" (Mark). Many harmonists take John 20:1 to mean that Mary Magdalene came first, in the dark, saw the stone moved and ran back to Peter (§172) while the others went on."},
  {"id":"peterjohn","harmony":"§172","lane":"apostles","place":"tomb","date":"Sunday morning, AD 33","title":"Peter and John at the empty tomb","people":["Mary Magdalene","Simon Peter","The beloved disciple (John)","Joanna","The eleven"],"gospels":{"Lk":"24:9–12","Jn":"20:2–10"},"summary":"Mary Magdalene runs to Simon Peter and the beloved disciple: \"They have taken the Lord out of the tomb.\" The women's report seems to the apostles an idle tale. Peter and the other disciple run to the tomb. The younger disciple arrives first and looks in; Peter goes inside and sees the linen cloths lying, and the head cloth rolled up by itself. The beloved disciple then goes in, and \"he saw and believed.\"","scripture":[{"ref":"John 20:4","text":"They both ran, but the other disciple outran Peter and reached the tomb first;"},{"ref":"John 20:8–9","text":"Then the other disciple, who reached the tomb first, also went in, and he saw and believed; for as yet they did not know the scripture, that he must rise from the dead."},{"ref":"Luke 24:11","text":"but these words seemed to them an idle tale, and they did not believe them."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Psalm 16:10","note":"\"You do not give me up to Sheol, or let your godly one see the Pit\": the scripture St Peter later cites for the Resurrection (Acts 2:27)."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"The Disciples Peter and John Running to the Sepulchre on the Morning of the Resurrection","artist":"Eugène Burnand","year":"1898","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Musée d'Orsay, Paris","description":"Peter and the young John run side by side at dawn, their faces full of hope and fear.","significance":"Shown at the Paris Salon of 1898 and bought by the French state.","symbolism":"Everything is in the faces and the wind-blown hair: we see not the tomb but the race toward it.","impact":"Widely reproduced as one of the best-known Easter images of the modern era.","img":"img/peterjohn0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BurnandJeanPierre.jpg"},{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"Saint Peter and Saint John Run to the Sepulchre","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"The young John, in white, reaches the low tomb door first; the older Peter runs behind him, outside the city wall.","significance":"Part of Tissot's Life of Christ, 350 watercolors made after his return to the Church and three study trips to Palestine (1886–1896).","symbolism":"Tissot follows John's detail that the other disciple outran Peter; the city wall behind shows the tomb lay outside Jerusalem.","impact":"Exhibited in Paris, London and New York in the 1890s, the series drew huge crowds; the Brooklyn Museum bought all 350 in 1900.","img":"img/peterjohn1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Saint_Peter_and_Saint_John_Run_to_the_Sepulchre_(Saint_Pierre_et_Saint_Jean_courent_au_sépulcre)_-_James_Tissot.jpg"}],"tags":[],"orderNote":"Some ancient manuscripts omit Luke 24:12. John's \"other disciple, whom Jesus loved\" is traditionally identified as John son of Zebedee."},
  {"id":"magdalene","harmony":"§173","lane":"jesus","place":"tomb","date":"Sunday morning, AD 33","title":"Jesus appears to Mary Magdalene","people":["Jesus","Mary Magdalene","Two angels"],"gospels":{"Mk":"16:9–11","Jn":"20:11–18"},"summary":"Mary stays at the tomb, weeping. Two angels ask why she weeps; then she turns and sees Jesus but takes him for the gardener. When he calls her by name she knows him: \"Rabboni!\" He tells her not to hold on to him, and sends her to his brethren. She becomes the first witness of the risen Lord, and the Church honors her as \"apostle to the apostles.\"","scripture":[{"ref":"John 20:15","text":"Jesus said to her, \"Woman, why are you weeping? Whom do you seek?\" Supposing him to be the gardener, she said to him, \"Sir, if you have carried him away, tell me where you have laid him, and I will take him away.\""},{"ref":"John 20:16","text":"Jesus said to her, \"Mary.\" She turned and said to him in Hebrew, \"Rabboni!\" (which means Teacher)."},{"ref":"John 20:17","text":"Jesus said to her, \"Do not hold me, for I have not yet ascended to the Father; but go to my brethren and say to them, I am ascending to my Father and your Father, to my God and your God.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Song of Songs 3:1–4","note":"\"I sought him whom my soul loves... I held him, and would not let him go\": read in the liturgy of her feast."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"Resurrection (Noli me tangere)","artist":"Giotto di Bondone","year":"c. 1305","medium":"Fresco","location":"Scrovegni (Arena) Chapel, Padua","description":"Two angels sit on the open tomb above the sleeping soldiers. Mary Magdalene kneels and reaches out; the risen Christ, holding a victory banner, steps away from her hands.","significance":"One of the closing scenes of Giotto's cycle of the life of Christ in the Scrovegni Chapel.","symbolism":"Christ's body turns away while his face and hand turn back to her: \"Do not hold me.\" The banner with the cross is the sign of victory over death.","impact":"The Scrovegni cycle is often called the starting point of Western Renaissance painting.","img":"img/magdalene0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giotto_di_Bondone_-_No._37_Scenes_from_the_Life_of_Christ_-_21._Resurrection_(Noli_me_tangere)_-_WGA09224.jpg"},{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"Noli me tangere","artist":"Titian","year":"c. 1514","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"The National Gallery, London","description":"Mary Magdalene kneels in a Venetian landscape and reaches toward Christ, who draws back his robe, a gardener's hoe in his hand.","significance":"An early masterpiece of Titian, painted when he was in his twenties.","symbolism":"The hoe recalls that Mary took him for the gardener; the tree and farm buildings link Easter to the renewal of the earth.","impact":"One of the best-loved Venetian paintings in London, bequeathed by the poet Samuel Rogers in 1856.","img":"img/magdalene1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Noli-me-tangere-titien.jpg"}],"tags":["appearance"],"orderNote":"Mark 16:9 says he appeared \"first\" to Mary Magdalene, and Robertson counts this the first of five appearances on Easter Day. Mark 16:9–20, the longer ending of Mark, is missing from many early manuscripts, including the two oldest complete Greek Bibles (Sinaiticus and Vaticanus); the Church receives it as inspired, canonical Scripture."},
  {"id":"otherwomen","harmony":"§174","lane":"jesus","place":"tomb","date":"Sunday morning, AD 33","title":"Jesus meets the other women","people":["Jesus","Mary Magdalene","The other Mary","Other women"],"gospels":{"Mt":"28:9–10"},"summary":"As the women hurry from the tomb with fear and great joy, Jesus meets them with a greeting. They take hold of his feet and worship him. He tells them not to be afraid, and to tell his brethren to go to Galilee, where they will see him. Even after the Passion, he still calls the disciples who fled \"my brethren.\"","scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 28:9","text":"And behold, Jesus met them and said, \"Hail!\" And they came up and took hold of his feet and worshiped him."},{"ref":"Matthew 28:10","text":"Then Jesus said to them, \"Do not be afraid; go and tell my brethren to go to Galilee, and there they will see me.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"Jesus Appears to the Holy Women","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"The risen Jesus, in shining white among olive trees, raises his hand in greeting as the women come toward him along the path.","significance":"Part of Tissot's Life of Christ, 350 watercolors made after his return to the Church and three study trips to Palestine (1886–1896).","symbolism":"The women have not yet seen him: Tissot paints the moment just before they fall at his feet and worship him.","impact":"Exhibited in Paris, London and New York in the 1890s, the series drew huge crowds; the Brooklyn Museum bought all 350 in 1900.","img":"img/otherwomen0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Jesus_Appears_to_the_Holy_Women_(Apparition_de_Jésus_aux_saintes_femmes)_-_James_Tissot.jpg"}],"tags":["appearance"],"orderNote":"Matthew names Mary Magdalene and \"the other Mary.\" Robertson treats this as a separate appearance to the other women, after the one to Mary Magdalene alone in John; others take Matthew's account as a shorter telling of the same meeting."},
  {"id":"guardreport","harmony":"§175","lane":"rome","place":"caiaphas","date":"Sunday, AD 33","title":"The guard's report and the bribe","people":["Some of the guard","The chief priests","The elders"],"gospels":{"Mt":"28:11–15"},"summary":"While the women are on their way, some of the guard go into the city and tell the chief priests everything that has happened. The priests and elders take counsel and pay the soldiers to say that the disciples stole the body while they slept. They promise to protect them if the governor hears of it. Matthew notes that this story was still told among the Jews in his own day.","scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 28:12–13","text":"And when they had assembled with the elders and taken counsel, they gave a sum of money to the soldiers and said, \"Tell people, 'His disciples came by night and stole him away while we were asleep.'"},{"ref":"Matthew 28:15","text":"So they took the money and did as they were directed; and this story has been spread among the Jews to this day."}],"art":[],"tags":["conflict"],"orderNote":"Matthew does not say where the priests met; the map uses the high priest's house. Robertson calls the guard \"Roman watchers\"; Pilate's \"You have a guard\" (Matthew 27:65) can also be read as \"take your own Temple guard,\" though the fear of the governor in 28:14 suggests Roman soldiers."},
  {"id":"emmaus","harmony":"§176","lane":"jesus","place":"emmaus","from":"upperroom","date":"Sunday afternoon, AD 33","title":"The road to Emmaus","people":["Jesus","Cleopas","Another disciple"],"gospels":{"Mk":"16:12–13","Lk":"24:13–32"},"summary":"That afternoon two disciples, one named Cleopas, walk from Jerusalem to the village of Emmaus, talking sadly about all that has happened. Jesus joins them, but they do not recognize him. Beginning with Moses and all the prophets, he explains how the Christ had to suffer and enter his glory. At table he takes bread, blesses and breaks it, and their eyes are opened; then he vanishes. Luke's words echo the Last Supper and the Eucharist.","stay":"The two pressed him to stay with them for the night, \"for it is toward evening.\"","journey":"Luke says about 7 miles (60 stadia) from Jerusalem; if Emmaus is Nicopolis, as some manuscripts (160 stadia) and early tradition suggest, about 19 miles west along the road toward the coastal plain.","route":[[35.229,31.7716],[35.2,31.79],[35.16,31.8],[35.11,31.81],[35.05,31.83],[34.99,31.84]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 24:27","text":"And beginning with Moses and all the prophets, he interpreted to them in all the scriptures the things concerning himself."},{"ref":"Luke 24:30–31","text":"When he was at table with them, he took the bread and blessed, and broke it, and gave it to them. And their eyes were opened and they recognized him; and he vanished out of their sight."},{"ref":"Luke 24:32","text":"They said to each other, \"Did not our hearts burn within us while he talked to us on the road, while he opened to us the scriptures?\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Isaiah 53","note":"The suffering servant, \"wounded for our transgressions\": the heart of \"Moses and all the prophets\" read as pointing to the Christ."},{"ref":"Genesis 3:7","note":"\"Then the eyes of both were opened\": the Fathers saw the eyes opened at Emmaus as undoing the first eating."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"Supper at Emmaus","artist":"Caravaggio","year":"1601","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"The National Gallery, London","description":"A beardless young Christ blesses the bread. One disciple grips the arms of his chair, the other flings his arms wide, while the innkeeper looks on, unaware.","significance":"Painted in Rome for the nobleman Ciriaco Mattei, at the height of Caravaggio's early fame.","symbolism":"The basket of fruit teeters at the table's edge, as if into our space; Christ's unfamiliar face recalls that their eyes were kept from recognizing him.","impact":"One of the most famous moments of recognition in Western art, and a model for Baroque realism.","img":"img/emmaus0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caravaggio_-_Supper_at_Emmaus_-_National_Gallery_London.jpeg"},{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"The Supper at Emmaus","artist":"Rembrandt van Rijn","year":"1648","medium":"Oil on panel","location":"Musée du Louvre, Paris","description":"In a bare, high stone room, Christ breaks the bread with a soft light around his head, while the two disciples begin to understand.","significance":"A late Rembrandt, quiet where Caravaggio is dramatic.","symbolism":"The great niche behind Christ works like an apse, so that the simple supper becomes an altar.","impact":"Admired as one of the most inward religious paintings of the Dutch Golden Age.","img":"img/emmaus1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rembrandt_The_Supper_at_Emmaus.jpg"}],"tags":["appearance"],"orderNote":"The site of Emmaus is disputed: Nicopolis, Abu Ghosh, el-Qubeibeh and Motza all have advocates (see the place note). Mark 16:12–13 tells the same appearance briefly; Mark 16:9–20, the longer ending of Mark, is missing from many early manuscripts, including the two oldest complete Greek Bibles (Sinaiticus and Vaticanus); the Church receives it as inspired, canonical Scripture."},
  {"id":"simon","harmony":"§177","lane":"jesus","place":"upperroom","from":"emmaus","date":"Sunday evening, AD 33","title":"\"He has appeared to Simon!\"","people":["Cleopas","Another disciple","The eleven","Simon Peter"],"gospels":{"Lk":"24:33–35","1Cor":"15:5"},"summary":"The two from Emmaus get up that same hour and go back to Jerusalem. Before they can speak, the eleven and those with them greet them with the news: \"The Lord has risen indeed, and has appeared to Simon!\" Then the two tell how he was known to them in the breaking of the bread. The appearance to Peter alone is never described; St Paul lists it first among the witnesses he hands on.","journey":"The two disciples walked back to Jerusalem the same evening, about 7 miles by Luke's figure (about 19 if Emmaus is Nicopolis).","route":[[34.99,31.84],[35.05,31.83],[35.11,31.81],[35.16,31.8],[35.2,31.79],[35.229,31.7716]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 24:33–34","text":"And they rose that same hour and returned to Jerusalem; and they found the eleven gathered together and those who were with them, who said, \"The Lord has risen indeed, and has appeared to Simon!\""},{"ref":"Luke 24:35","text":"Then they told what had happened on the road, and how he was known to them in the breaking of the bread."},{"ref":"1 Corinthians 15:5","text":"and that he appeared to Cephas, then to the twelve."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"Apparition of Our Lord to Saint Peter","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Among olive trees, Peter, in a dark cloak, stops short as he sees Jesus in white beside a tree. Tissot imagines the appearance to Peter alone, which the Gospels mention but never describe.","significance":"Part of Tissot's Life of Christ, 350 watercolors made after his return to the Church and three study trips to Palestine (1886–1896).","symbolism":"Peter, who had denied him three times, is the first of the apostles to see the risen Lord.","impact":"Exhibited in Paris, London and New York in the 1890s, the series drew huge crowds; the Brooklyn Museum bought all 350 in 1900.","img":"img/simon0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Apparition_of_Our_Lord_to_Saint_Peter_(Apparition_de_Notre-Seigneur_à_St-Pierre)_-_James_Tissot.jpg"}],"tags":["appearance"],"orderNote":"Neither Luke nor Paul says where or exactly when Jesus appeared to Simon Peter, only that it was on Easter Day, before the evening gathering. The map places the event where the news was told, the room where the eleven were gathered, traditionally the Upper Room."},
  {"id":"firstevening","harmony":"§178","lane":"jesus","place":"upperroom","date":"Sunday evening, AD 33","title":"Jesus appears to the disciples (Thomas absent)","people":["Jesus","Ten apostles","Others with them","Thomas (absent)"],"gospels":{"Mk":"16:14","Lk":"24:36–43","Jn":"20:19–25"},"summary":"That evening, with the doors shut for fear, Jesus stands among the disciples and says, \"Peace be with you.\" They think they see a spirit, so he shows them his hands, feet and side, and eats a piece of broiled fish before them. He sends them as the Father sent him, breathes on them and gives them the Holy Spirit and the power to forgive sins. Thomas is not there, and refuses to believe unless he can touch the wounds.","scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 24:39","text":"See my hands and my feet, that it is I myself; handle me, and see; for a spirit has not flesh and bones as you see that I have.\""},{"ref":"John 20:21–22","text":"Jesus said to them again, \"Peace be with you. As the Father has sent me, even so I send you.\" And when he had said this, he breathed on them, and said to them, \"Receive the Holy Spirit."},{"ref":"John 20:23","text":"If you forgive the sins of any, they are forgiven; if you retain the sins of any, they are retained.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Genesis 2:7","note":"God breathed into man the breath of life; the risen Christ breathes on his disciples, a new creation."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"The Appearance of Christ at the Cenacle","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Jesus, in glowing white, stands among the disciples in a dim pillared room; they bow low or shrink back in fear.","significance":"Part of Tissot's Life of Christ, 350 watercolors made after his return to the Church and three study trips to Palestine (1886–1896).","symbolism":"Tissot sets the scene in the Cenacle, following the tradition that the disciples gathered where they had eaten the Last Supper.","impact":"Exhibited in Paris, London and New York in the 1890s, the series drew huge crowds; the Brooklyn Museum bought all 350 in 1900.","img":"img/firstevening0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Appearance_of_Christ_at_the_Cenacle_(Apparition_du_Christ_au_cénacle)_-_James_Tissot.jpg"},{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"Christ Appears to the Apostles at Table","artist":"Duccio di Buoninsegna","year":"1308–11","medium":"Tempera on panel","location":"Museo dell'Opera del Duomo, Siena","description":"The risen Christ enters from the left and greets the apostles seated around a table under arches; dishes of fish lie on the cloth.","significance":"From the back of the Maestà, Siena Cathedral's high altarpiece.","symbolism":"Mark says he appeared \"as they sat at table\"; Luke adds that he ate fish before them.","impact":"The Maestà was carried in procession to the cathedral in 1311, and is a cornerstone of Sienese painting.","img":"img/firstevening1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Duccio_di_Buoninsegna_017.jpg"}],"tags":["appearance"],"orderNote":"Robertson counts this the first of three commissions to the apostles (with §181 and §183). The Church sees in John 20:22–23 the institution of the sacrament of Penance (Council of Trent). Mark 16:14 tells it briefly; Mark 16:9–20, the longer ending of Mark, is missing from many early manuscripts, including the two oldest complete Greek Bibles (Sinaiticus and Vaticanus); the Church receives it as inspired, canonical Scripture."},
  {"id":"thomas","harmony":"§179","lane":"jesus","place":"upperroom","date":"The next Sunday, AD 33","title":"Doubting Thomas: \"My Lord and my God!\"","people":["Jesus","Thomas (Didymus)","The eleven"],"gospels":{"Jn":"20:26–31","1Cor":"15:5"},"summary":"Eight days later the disciples are again indoors, and this time Thomas is with them. Jesus comes and stands among them, and invites Thomas to put his finger in the wounds. Thomas makes the fullest confession of faith in the Gospels: \"My Lord and my God!\" Jesus blesses all who believe without seeing. John adds that he wrote so that readers may believe and have life in his name.","stay":"The disciples were still in Jerusalem a week later; Robertson thinks Jesus stayed at or near the city this first week, then went to Galilee.","scripture":[{"ref":"John 20:27","text":"Then he said to Thomas, \"Put your finger here, and see my hands; and put out your hand, and place it in my side; do not be faithless, but believing.\""},{"ref":"John 20:28–29","text":"Thomas answered him, \"My Lord and my God!\" Jesus said to him, \"Have you believed because you have seen me? Blessed are those who have not seen and yet believe.\""},{"ref":"John 20:31","text":"but these are written that you may believe that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God, and that believing you may have life in his name."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"The Incredulity of Saint Thomas","artist":"Caravaggio","year":"c. 1601–02","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Sanssouci Picture Gallery, Potsdam","description":"Christ guides Thomas's hand so that his finger enters the wound in his side, as two other apostles lean in to look.","significance":"One of Caravaggio's most copied works in his own century.","symbolism":"The four heads form a tight cross around the wound; Thomas's furrowed brow shows doubt giving way to faith.","impact":"Its blunt physical realism made it one of the defining images of this Gospel scene.","img":"img/thomas0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caravaggio_incredulity.jpg"},{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"The Disbelief of Saint Thomas","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Thomas kneels and bows his head to the risen Jesus' hand, while the other disciples crowd around in the shadows.","significance":"Part of Tissot's Life of Christ, 350 watercolors made after his return to the Church and three study trips to Palestine (1886–1896).","symbolism":"The wounds remain in the glorified body: the risen Christ is the same one who was crucified.","impact":"Exhibited in Paris, London and New York in the 1890s, the series drew huge crowds; the Brooklyn Museum bought all 350 in 1900.","img":"img/thomas1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Disbelief_of_Saint_Thomas_(Incredulité_de_Saint_Thomas)_-_James_Tissot.jpg"}],"tags":["appearance"],"orderNote":"Robertson links this appearance to Paul's \"then to the twelve\" (1 Corinthians 15:5). John 20:30–31 reads like the original close of the Gospel, so many scholars see chapter 21 as an epilogue."},
  {"id":"secondcatch","harmony":"§180a","lane":"jesus","place":"tabgha","from":"upperroom","date":"Spring AD 33 (within the forty days)","title":"Breakfast by the sea and the great catch","people":["Jesus","Simon Peter","Thomas","Nathanael","James and John","Two other disciples"],"gospels":{"Jn":"21:1–14"},"summary":"Back in Galilee, seven disciples go fishing with Peter on the Sea of Tiberias and catch nothing all night. At daybreak a stranger on the shore tells them to cast the net on the right side, and it fills with 153 large fish. The beloved disciple says, \"It is the Lord!\" and Peter throws himself into the sea. Jesus has a charcoal fire ready, and gives them bread and fish. It is his third appearance to the disciples in John.","journey":"The disciples went back to Galilee as Jesus had told them, about 100 miles, 4 to 5 days on foot, probably down the Jordan Valley.","route":[[35.229,31.7716],[35.3,31.8],[35.45,31.865],[35.53,32.15],[35.5,32.45],[35.56,32.66],[35.535,32.79],[35.549,32.873]],"scripture":[{"ref":"John 21:6","text":"He said to them, \"Cast the net on the right side of the boat, and you will find some.\" So they cast it, and now they were not able to haul it in, for the quantity of fish."},{"ref":"John 21:7","text":"That disciple whom Jesus loved said to Peter, \"It is the Lord!\" When Simon Peter heard that it was the Lord, he put on his clothes, for he was stripped for work, and sprang into the sea."},{"ref":"John 21:12","text":"Jesus said to them, \"Come and have breakfast.\" Now none of the disciples dared ask him, \"Who are you?\" They knew it was the Lord."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Ezekiel 47:10","note":"Fishermen stand beside a river flowing from the Temple, with fish \"of very many kinds.\" St Jerome linked the 153 fish to every kind of fish then known."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"The Miraculous Draught of Fishes","artist":"Konrad Witz","year":"1444","medium":"Oil on panel","location":"Musée d'Art et d'Histoire, Geneva","description":"Christ, in a red cloak, stands at the water's edge while the disciples haul in the net and Peter swims toward him.","significance":"Part of an altarpiece for St Peter's Cathedral in Geneva.","symbolism":"Witz sets the scene on Lake Geneva, with the Mont Salève behind, bringing the Gospel into his viewers' own world.","impact":"Often called the first recognizable landscape in European painting.","img":"img/secondcatch0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Konrad_Witz._Der_Wunderbare_Fischzug_(1444).jpg"},{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"Meal of Our Lord and the Apostles","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"At dawn on the shore of the lake, Jesus and the disciples sit around a charcoal fire, with loaves on the ground and the boat drawn up behind.","significance":"Part of Tissot's Life of Christ, 350 watercolors made after his return to the Church and three study trips to Palestine (1886–1896).","symbolism":"The thin column of smoke from the fire recalls John's detail of \"a charcoal fire there, with fish lying on it, and bread.\"","impact":"Exhibited in Paris, London and New York in the 1890s, the series drew huge crowds; the Brooklyn Museum bought all 350 in 1900.","img":"img/secondcatch1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Meal_of_Our_Lord_and_the_Apostles_(Repas_de_Notre-Seigneur_et_des_apôtres)_-_James_Tissot.jpg"}],"tags":["appearance","nature-miracle"],"orderNote":"Robertson notes the exact date is unknown, only that it falls between Easter Day and the Ascension. It closely echoes the first miraculous catch when Peter was called (Luke 5:1–11). The traditional site is Tabgha, but John names only the Sea of Tiberias."},
  {"id":"feedmysheep","harmony":"§180b","lane":"jesus","place":"tabgha","date":"Spring AD 33 (within the forty days)","title":"\"Feed my sheep\": Peter restored","people":["Jesus","Simon Peter","The beloved disciple (John)"],"gospels":{"Jn":"21:15–25"},"summary":"After breakfast Jesus asks Peter three times, \"Simon, son of John, do you love me?\" Three times Peter, who had denied him three times beside a charcoal fire, says yes, and three times Jesus gives him charge of his flock. He foretells that Peter will one day stretch out his hands and die for him, and says again, \"Follow me.\" The Catholic Church reads this as the confirmation of Peter's pastoral primacy.","scripture":[{"ref":"John 21:15","text":"When they had finished breakfast, Jesus said to Simon Peter, \"Simon, son of John, do you love me more than these?\" He said to him, \"Yes, Lord; you know that I love you.\" He said to him, \"Feed my lambs.\""},{"ref":"John 21:17","text":"He said to him the third time, \"Simon, son of John, do you love me?\" Peter was grieved because he said to him the third time, \"Do you love me?\" And he said to him, \"Lord, you know everything; you know that I love you.\" Jesus said to him, \"Feed my sheep."},{"ref":"John 21:18–19","text":"Truly, truly, I say to you, when you were young, you girded yourself and walked where you would; but when you are old, you will stretch out your hands, and another will gird you and carry you where you do not wish to go.\" (This he said to show by what death he was to glorify God.) And after this he said to him, \"Follow me.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Ezekiel 34:11–16","note":"God himself will seek out his sheep and feed them; now Christ entrusts that work to Peter."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"Christ's Charge to Peter","artist":"Raphael","year":"1515–16","medium":"Bodycolour on paper mounted on canvas (tapestry cartoon)","location":"Victoria and Albert Museum, London (Royal Collection)","description":"On a lakeside meadow Christ points to a flock of sheep while Peter kneels before him with the keys; the other apostles crowd behind.","significance":"One of the full-size designs Raphael made for tapestries commissioned by Pope Leo X for the Sistine Chapel.","symbolism":"The keys (Matthew 16:19) and the sheep (John 21) are joined in one image of Peter's charge as shepherd of the Church.","impact":"The cartoons were bought by Charles I in 1623 and are among the treasures of the Royal Collection.","img":"img/feedmysheep0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:V&A_-_Raphael,_Christ's_Charge_to_Peter_(1515).jpg"},{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"Feed My Lambs","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"On the stony lake shore Jesus lays his hand on Peter's shoulder and speaks with him; other disciples follow behind.","significance":"Part of Tissot's Life of Christ, 350 watercolors made after his return to the Church and three study trips to Palestine (1886–1896).","symbolism":"The threefold question answers Peter's threefold denial.","impact":"Exhibited in Paris, London and New York in the 1890s, the series drew huge crowds; the Brooklyn Museum bought all 350 in 1900.","img":"img/feedmysheep1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Feed_My_Lambs_(Pais_mes_brebis)_-_James_Tissot.jpg"}],"tags":["call"],"orderNote":"The saying about the beloved disciple (21:23) shows a rumor in the early Church that he would not die. Tradition holds that Peter was crucified in Rome under Nero, about AD 64–67."},
  {"id":"commission","harmony":"§181","lane":"jesus","place":"galileemount","from":"tabgha","date":"Spring AD 33 (within the forty days)","title":"The Great Commission on the mountain in Galilee","people":["Jesus","The eleven","More than five hundred brethren"],"gospels":{"Mt":"28:16–20","Mk":"16:15–18","1Cor":"15:6"},"summary":"The eleven go to the mountain in Galilee where Jesus told them to meet him. When they see him they worship, though some doubt. He declares that all authority in heaven and on earth has been given to him, and sends them to make disciples of all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit. He promises to be with them always, to the close of the age.","journey":"From the lake shore up into the Galilean hills, perhaps about 5 to 10 miles; the mountain is not named.","route":[[35.549,32.873],[35.52,32.845],[35.49,32.83],[35.45,32.82]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Matthew 28:18–19","text":"And Jesus came and said to them, \"All authority in heaven and on earth has been given to me. Go therefore and make disciples of all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit,"},{"ref":"Matthew 28:20","text":"teaching them to observe all that I have commanded you; and lo, I am with you always, to the close of the age.\""},{"ref":"1 Corinthians 15:6","text":"Then he appeared to more than five hundred brethren at one time, most of whom are still alive, though some have fallen asleep."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Daniel 7:14","note":"The one like a son of man receives dominion over \"all peoples, nations, and languages.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"Christ Appears to the Disciples on the Mountain in Galilee","artist":"Duccio di Buoninsegna","year":"1308–11","medium":"Tempera on panel","location":"Museo dell'Opera del Duomo, Siena","description":"The eleven stand together on a bare, rocky slope as Christ, in dark blue and gold, speaks to them with open hands.","significance":"From the back of the Maestà, Siena Cathedral's great high altarpiece, which ends with the appearances after the Resurrection.","symbolism":"The gold ground lifts the mountain out of time: this is the commission to all nations.","impact":"The Maestà was carried in procession to the cathedral in 1311, and is a cornerstone of Sienese painting.","img":"img/commission0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Duccio_di_Buoninsegna_016.jpg"}],"tags":["appearance","teaching"],"orderNote":"Robertson thinks the appearance to \"more than five hundred brethren\" (1 Corinthians 15:6) was most probably this appointed meeting, and reckons Mark's commission the same as Matthew's; others place Mark 16:15–18 in Jerusalem. Mark 16:9–20, the longer ending of Mark, is missing from many early manuscripts, including the two oldest complete Greek Bibles (Sinaiticus and Vaticanus); the Church receives it as inspired, canonical Scripture."},
  {"id":"james","harmony":"§182","lane":"jesus","place":"jerusalem","date":"Within the forty days, AD 33","title":"Jesus appears to James","people":["Jesus","James, the brother of the Lord"],"gospels":{"1Cor":"15:7"},"summary":"St Paul records, in a single line, that the risen Lord \"appeared to James, then to all the apostles.\" This James, called the brother of the Lord, had not believed in Jesus during the ministry (John 7:5). Soon after he is with the apostles and the women in the Upper Room (Acts 1:14), and later he leads the church in Jerusalem. Paul met him there about three years after his own conversion (Galatians 1:19).","scripture":[{"ref":"1 Corinthians 15:7","text":"Then he appeared to James, then to all the apostles."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"Saint James the Less","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"A bearded man in white kneels on a rock with his arms flung wide in prayer, part of Tissot's series of the apostles.","significance":"Part of Tissot's Life of Christ, 350 watercolors made after his return to the Church and three study trips to Palestine (1886–1896).","symbolism":"Hegesippus, quoted by Eusebius, says James the Just prayed so much that his knees grew hard like a camel's. Western tradition often identifies James the Less with James the brother of the Lord; the identification is debated.","impact":"Exhibited in Paris, London and New York in the 1890s, the series drew huge crowds; the Brooklyn Museum bought all 350 in 1900.","img":"img/james0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Saint_James_the_Less_(Saint_Jacques_le_Mineur)_-_James_Tissot.jpg"}],"tags":["appearance"],"orderNote":"No place or time is given. A tradition from the Gospel of the Hebrews, quoted by St Jerome, places the appearance in Jerusalem; the map follows it, as a tradition only. Catholic tradition holds that the \"brethren\" of the Lord were his kinsmen, not sons of Mary, and often identifies this James with James son of Alphaeus, \"the Less.\""},
  {"id":"witnesses","harmony":"§183","lane":"jesus","place":"upperroom","from":"galileemount","date":"End of the forty days, AD 33","title":"\"You shall be my witnesses\"","people":["Jesus","The eleven","Those with them"],"gospels":{"Lk":"24:44–49","Acts":"1:3–8"},"summary":"Back in Jerusalem near the end of the forty days, Jesus opens the disciples' minds to understand the scriptures: the Christ must suffer and rise on the third day, and repentance and forgiveness of sins must be preached to all nations, beginning from Jerusalem. He tells them to stay in the city until they are clothed with power from on high. When they ask about the kingdom of Israel, he promises the Holy Spirit and sends them to be his witnesses to the end of the earth.","stay":"Acts says he appeared to them during forty days, speaking of the kingdom of God and eating with them.","journey":"The disciples returned from Galilee to Jerusalem before the end of the forty days, about 90 to 100 miles, probably by the Jordan Valley.","route":[[35.45,32.82],[35.4,32.62],[35.5,32.45],[35.53,32.15],[35.45,31.865],[35.3,31.8],[35.229,31.7716]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 24:46–47","text":"and said to them, \"Thus it is written, that the Christ should suffer and on the third day rise from the dead, and that repentance and forgiveness of sins should be preached in his name to all nations, beginning from Jerusalem."},{"ref":"Luke 24:49","text":"And behold, I send the promise of my Father upon you; but stay in the city, until you are clothed with power from on high.\""},{"ref":"Acts 1:8","text":"But you shall receive power when the Holy Spirit has come upon you; and you shall be my witnesses in Jerusalem and in all Judea and Samaria and to the end of the earth.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Joel 2:28","note":"\"I will pour out my spirit on all flesh\": the promise of the Father, fulfilled at Pentecost (Acts 2:16–17)."},{"ref":"Isaiah 49:6","note":"\"A light to the nations, that my salvation may reach to the end of the earth.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"Jesus Discourses with His Disciples","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Jesus, in white, walks along a hillside road with a group of disciples, talking with them as they go.","significance":"Part of Tissot's Life of Christ, 350 watercolors made after his return to the Church and three study trips to Palestine (1886–1896).","symbolism":"The walking teacher and his listeners suit Luke's summary of the forty days: he opened their minds to understand the scriptures and spoke of the kingdom of God.","impact":"Exhibited in Paris, London and New York in the 1890s, the series drew huge crowds; the Brooklyn Museum bought all 350 in 1900.","img":"img/witnesses0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_Jesus_Discourses_with_His_Disciples_(Jésus_s'entretient_avec_ses_disciples)_-_James_Tissot.jpg"}],"tags":["appearance","teaching"],"orderNote":"Read alone, Luke 24 seems to put this teaching on Easter evening; Acts 1:3 shows Luke knew of forty days of appearances. Robertson, like many harmonists, places Luke 24:44–49 at the end of the forty days as the third commission."},
  {"id":"ascension","harmony":"§184","lane":"jesus","place":"olives","from":"upperroom","date":"Forty days after Easter, AD 33","title":"The Ascension","people":["Jesus","The eleven","Two men in white"],"gospels":{"Mk":"16:19–20","Lk":"24:50–53","Acts":"1:9–12"},"summary":"Jesus leads the disciples out toward Bethany on the Mount of Olives. He lifts up his hands and blesses them, and as they watch he is lifted up, and a cloud takes him out of their sight. Two men in white promise that he will come again in the same way. The disciples return to Jerusalem with great joy, praising God in the Temple and waiting for the Spirit.","journey":"From the city across the Kidron Valley and up the Mount of Olives, about a mile, \"a sabbath day's journey\" (Acts 1:12).","route":[[35.229,31.7716],[35.236,31.775],[35.24,31.779],[35.245,31.778]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Luke 24:50–51","text":"Then he led them out as far as Bethany, and lifting up his hands he blessed them. While he blessed them, he parted from them and was carried up into heaven."},{"ref":"Acts 1:9","text":"And when he had said this, as they were looking on, he was lifted up, and a cloud took him out of their sight."},{"ref":"Acts 1:11","text":"and said, \"Men of Galilee, why do you stand looking into heaven? This Jesus, who was taken up from you into heaven, will come in the same way as you saw him go into heaven.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"2 Kings 2:11","note":"Elijah is taken up into heaven before Elisha's eyes, who then receives a double share of his spirit."},{"ref":"Psalm 110:1","note":"\"Sit at my right hand\": Mark says the Lord \"sat down at the right hand of God.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"Ascension","artist":"Giotto di Bondone","year":"c. 1305","medium":"Fresco","location":"Scrovegni (Arena) Chapel, Padua","description":"Christ rises on a cloud between two hosts of angels and saints, his hands reaching past the top of the frame. Below, Mary and the apostles kneel, and two angels in white point upward.","significance":"The scene follows the Noli me tangere in Giotto's life of Christ in the Scrovegni Chapel.","symbolism":"Christ's hands already touch the edge of the picture, as if entering heaven beyond it; the two angels in white are the \"two men\" of Acts 1:10.","impact":"The Scrovegni cycle is often called the starting point of Western Renaissance painting.","img":"img/ascension0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giotto_di_Bondone_-_No._38_Scenes_from_the_Life_of_Christ_-_22._Ascension_-_WGA09226.jpg"},{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"The Ascension","artist":"James Tissot","year":"1886–1894","medium":"Opaque watercolor over graphite on gray wove paper","location":"Brooklyn Museum, New York","description":"Christ rises above the Mount of Olives, his arms spread in blessing, as the disciples look up from below.","significance":"Part of Tissot's Life of Christ, 350 watercolors made after his return to the Church and three study trips to Palestine (1886–1896).","symbolism":"The raised hands follow Luke: \"lifting up his hands he blessed them... and was carried up into heaven.\"","impact":"Exhibited in Paris, London and New York in the 1890s, the series drew huge crowds; the Brooklyn Museum bought all 350 in 1900.","img":"img/ascension1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brooklyn_Museum_-_The_Ascension_(L'Ascension)_-_James_Tissot.jpg"}],"tags":["appearance","miracle"],"orderNote":"Luke 24:50 says he led them \"as far as Bethany,\" Acts 1:12 says Olivet; Bethany lies on the far slope of the same hill, and the traditional site is near the summit. The Church keeps the feast forty days after Easter. Mark 16:19–20 also tells it; Mark 16:9–20, the longer ending of Mark, is missing from many early manuscripts, including the two oldest complete Greek Bibles (Sinaiticus and Vaticanus); the Church receives it as inspired, canonical Scripture."},
  {"id":"matthias","harmony":"Acts 1:12–26","lane":"apostles","place":"upperroom","from":"olives","date":"May AD 33, between the Ascension and Pentecost","title":"Matthias Chosen","people":["Peter","The eleven","Mary the mother of Jesus","The women","Jesus' brethren","Joseph Barsabbas","Matthias"],"gospels":{"Acts":"1:12–26"},"summary":"The apostles walk back from the Mount of Olives to the upper room where they are staying. About a hundred and twenty disciples, with Mary the mother of Jesus and the women, devote themselves to prayer. Peter says that Judas' place must be filled by a man who has been with them from John's baptism to the Ascension, a witness to the Resurrection. Two are put forward, Joseph Barsabbas and Matthias; they pray and cast lots, and the lot falls on Matthias, who is counted with the eleven.","stay":"The eleven were staying in the upper room (Acts 1:13) and waited there in prayer for the promised Spirit.","journey":"Down from the Mount of Olives, across the Kidron Valley and up into the city to the upper room, about a mile, \"a sabbath day's journey\" (Acts 1:12).","route":[[35.245,31.778],[35.24,31.779],[35.236,31.775],[35.229,31.7716]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 1:14","text":"All these with one accord devoted themselves to prayer, together with the women and Mary the mother of Jesus, and with his brethren."},{"ref":"Acts 1:26","text":"And they cast lots for them, and the lot fell on Matthias; and he was enrolled with the eleven apostles."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Psalm 109:8","note":"\"May another seize his goods\" (\"His office let another take\"): Peter cites it for filling Judas' place."},{"ref":"Psalm 69:25","note":"\"May their camp be a desolation\": Peter applies it to Judas' field, Akeldama."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"The Return of the Apostles from Mount Olivet and the Election of Matthias","artist":"Philips Galle after Maarten van Heemskerck","year":"1575","medium":"Engraving","location":"Royal Library of Belgium (KBR), Brussels","description":"In the upper room Peter stands and points as the apostles, Mary and the women kneel in prayer. Through the window at left, Christ rises into the clouds above the Mount of Olives, the disciples gather on the hill, and two men in white stand below; in the street, two apostles walk back to the city.","significance":"One plate of Heemskerck's Acta Apostolorum, a series of engravings on the Acts of the Apostles, designed by the Haarlem painter and engraved by Philips Galle.","symbolism":"The Latin line below reads \"The Lord ascends into heaven and Matthias is made an apostle\" (Acts 1); the print joins the Ascension and the choosing of Matthias in one view, as Luke does.","impact":"Galle's Antwerp prints of Heemskerck's designs spread his biblical scenes across Europe as models for painters, glass makers and book illustrators.","img":"img/matthias0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Return_of_The_Apostles_from_Mount_Olivet_and_The_Election_of_Matthias_as_Apostle_1575_print_by_Maarten_van_Heemskerck,_S.I_55821,_Prints_Department,_Royal_Library_of_Belgium.jpg"}],"tags":["prayer","call"],"orderNote":"Acts 1:18–19 sets Judas' death in a parenthesis inside Peter's speech; Matthew 27:3–10 tells it differently, and the two accounts are usually harmonized (see the Gospel event judasdeath). Acts places the choosing \"in those days\" before Pentecost but gives no day."},
  {"id":"pentecost","harmony":"Acts 2:1–13","lane":"apostles","place":"upperroom","date":"Pentecost, May AD 33","title":"The Descent of the Holy Spirit","people":["The apostles","Mary the mother of Jesus","The disciples","Pilgrims from every nation"],"gospels":{"Acts":"2:1–13"},"summary":"Fifty days after the Passover, on the Jewish feast of Weeks, the disciples are all together in one place. A sound like a mighty wind fills the house, tongues as of fire rest on each of them, and they are filled with the Holy Spirit and speak in other tongues. Jews from every nation, in Jerusalem for the feast, hear them speak of God's mighty works in their own languages. Some are amazed; others mock and say they are drunk. The Church keeps this day as its birthday.","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 2:2–4","text":"And suddenly a sound came from heaven like the rush of a mighty wind, and it filled all the house where they were sitting. And there appeared to them tongues as of fire, distributed and resting on each one of them. And they were all filled with the Holy Spirit and began to speak in other tongues, as the Spirit gave them utterance."},{"ref":"Acts 2:12–13","text":"And all were amazed and perplexed, saying to one another, \"What does this mean?\" But others mocking said, \"They are filled with new wine.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Genesis 11:7–9","note":"At Babel God confused the languages and scattered the nations; at Pentecost the nations hear one message, each in its own tongue."},{"ref":"Exodus 19:18","note":"The Lord came down on Sinai in fire; later Jewish tradition linked the feast of Weeks with the giving of the Law."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"Pentecost","artist":"Giotto di Bondone","year":"c. 1305","medium":"Fresco","location":"Scrovegni (Arena) Chapel, Padua","description":"The apostles sit on benches inside an open Gothic loggia, some facing us, some turned away. Red rays stream down onto their heads from above.","significance":"The last scene of Giotto's life of Christ in the Scrovegni Chapel, following the Ascension.","symbolism":"The rays stand for the \"tongues as of fire\"; the open hall lets us see the gathered apostles \"all together in one place.\"","impact":"The Scrovegni cycle is often called the starting point of Western Renaissance painting.","img":"img/pentecost0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giotto_di_Bondone_-_No._39_Scenes_from_the_Life_of_Christ_-_23._Pentecost_-_WGA09227.jpg"},{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"Pentecost","artist":"El Greco","year":"c. 1600","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Museo del Prado, Madrid","description":"Mary sits at the center with hands joined, among the apostles and another woman. Above, the dove of the Holy Spirit shines, and a small flame burns over each head as the apostles look up or fall back in wonder.","significance":"A late work of El Greco's Toledo years, painted with the tall, flame-like figures typical of his mature style.","symbolism":"Mary at the center follows Acts 1:14, where she prays with the apostles; the dove and flames join the Spirit of the Baptism with the fire of Pentecost.","impact":"El Greco's late visionary style, long neglected, was rediscovered around 1900 and admired by modern painters.","img":"img/pentecost1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pentecostés_(El_Greco,_c._1600)_Prado.jpg"}],"tags":["miracle"],"orderNote":"Acts says only that they were \"all together in one place\" and that the sound filled \"all the house\" (2:1–2). Tradition places it in the upper room of 1:13 on Mount Zion; some scholars think of the Temple courts, where a crowd of thousands could gather. The map marks the Cenacle."},
  {"id":"petersermon","harmony":"Acts 2:14–41","lane":"apostles","place":"upperroom","date":"Pentecost, May AD 33","title":"Peter's Sermon at Pentecost","people":["Peter","The eleven","The crowd"],"gospels":{"Acts":"2:14–41"},"summary":"Peter stands up with the eleven and tells the crowd that these men are not drunk: this is what the prophet Joel foretold, the Spirit poured out on all flesh. He proclaims that Jesus of Nazareth, whom they crucified, God has raised up, and David's psalms foretold it. Jesus, now exalted at God's right hand, has poured out the Spirit. Cut to the heart, the people ask what to do; Peter tells them to repent and be baptized, and about three thousand are baptized that day.","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 2:36","text":"Let all the house of Israel therefore know assuredly that God has made him both Lord and Christ, this Jesus whom you crucified.\""},{"ref":"Acts 2:38","text":"And Peter said to them, \"Repent, and be baptized every one of you in the name of Jesus Christ for the forgiveness of your sins; and you shall receive the gift of the Holy Spirit."},{"ref":"Acts 2:41","text":"So those who received his word were baptized, and there were added that day about three thousand souls."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Joel 2:28–32","note":"\"I will pour out my spirit on all flesh\": the text of Peter's sermon."},{"ref":"Psalm 16:10","note":"\"You do not give me up to Sheol\": Peter reads it of the Resurrection."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"St Peter Preaching","artist":"Masolino da Panicale","year":"c. 1425","medium":"Fresco","location":"Brancacci Chapel, Santa Maria del Carmine, Florence","description":"Peter stands at left with his hand raised, preaching to a close crowd of listeners: a veiled woman, an old man lost in thought, and men in the dress of 15th-century Florence, with two white-robed friars at right and green hills behind.","significance":"Part of the Brancacci Chapel's cycle of the life of St Peter, painted by Masolino and his young partner Masaccio and finished decades later by Filippino Lippi.","symbolism":"The friars in white are Carmelites, the order that served the church; Masolino places his own city among those who hear the apostle.","impact":"The Brancacci Chapel became a school for Florentine artists; Michelangelo studied and copied its frescoes.","img":"img/petersermon0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cappella_brancacci,_Predica_di_San_Pietro_(restaurato),_Masolino.jpg"},{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"St Peter Preaching in Jerusalem","artist":"Philips Galle after Maarten van Heemskerck","year":"1575","medium":"Engraving","location":"Royal Library of Belgium (KBR), Brussels","description":"Peter, haloed, stands at the top of a stair and preaches with an outstretched arm to a crowd of men in turbans and robes. At upper left, through a window, the Spirit's rays fall on Mary and the apostles; a round, Roman-looking temple rises at right.","significance":"One plate of Heemskerck's Acta Apostolorum, a series of engravings on the Acts of the Apostles, designed by the Haarlem painter and engraved by Philips Galle.","symbolism":"The title reads \"Peter converted three thousand men\" (Acts 2:41); the Pentecost scene above shows the source of his boldness.","impact":"Galle's Antwerp prints of Heemskerck's designs spread his biblical scenes across Europe as models for painters, glass makers and book illustrators.","img":"img/petersermon1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St_Peter_Preaching_In_Jerusalem_1575_print_by_Maarten_van_Heemskerck,_S.I_55791,_Prints_Department,_Royal_Library_of_Belgium.jpg"}],"tags":["sermon","prophecy"],"orderNote":"Where the crowd heard Peter is not stated; it may have been in the streets near the house or in the Temple courts, where the many baptisms could have used the ritual pools south of the Temple Mount."},
  {"id":"commonlife","harmony":"Acts 2:42–47","lane":"apostles","place":"jerusalemchurch","date":"AD 33","title":"The Life of the First Church","people":["The apostles","The believers"],"gospels":{"Acts":"2:42–47"},"summary":"The new believers devote themselves to the apostles' teaching and fellowship, to the breaking of bread and the prayers. They hold all things in common, selling their goods and sharing with any in need. Day by day they attend the Temple together and break bread in their homes with glad and generous hearts. The Lord adds to their number every day.","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 2:42","text":"And they devoted themselves to the apostles' teaching and fellowship, to the breaking of bread and the prayers."},{"ref":"Acts 2:44–45","text":"And all who believed were together and had all things in common; and they sold their possessions and goods and distributed them to all, as any had need."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"Fractio Panis (The Breaking of Bread)","artist":"Unknown early Christian painter","year":"c. 3rd century","medium":"Fresco","location":"Cappella Greca, Catacomb of Priscilla, Rome","description":"Seven figures recline at a curved table in faded red and white, one of them stretching out his hands over the bread. Dishes lie on the table before them.","significance":"One of the oldest Christian pictures of a meal, painted in the burial chapel of the Roman catacomb of Priscilla. Its name, given by its discoverer Joseph Wilpert, is Luke's phrase \"the breaking of bread.\"","symbolism":"The gesture of breaking bread echoes the Last Supper and Emmaus; whether the scene shows a Eucharist, a funeral meal or an agape is debated.","impact":"Often reproduced as an image of the early Church's shared meal and of the Eucharist.","img":"img/commonlife0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Catacombe_di_Priscilla,_Rome_-_Fresco_of_a_Christian_Agape_feast_-_Fractio_panis_-_Greek_chapel_-_2nd_-_4th_century.jpg"},{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"The Apostles Baptizing in Jerusalem","artist":"Philips Galle after Maarten van Heemskerck","year":"1575","medium":"Engraving","location":"Royal Library of Belgium (KBR), Brussels","description":"In a crowded courtyard, an apostle pours water over a kneeling convert while others undress and wait their turn; another apostle preaches from the steps behind. At upper left the dove of the Spirit hovers, and goods are laid on a table above.","significance":"One plate of Heemskerck's Acta Apostolorum, a series of engravings on the Acts of the Apostles, designed by the Haarlem painter and engraved by Philips Galle.","symbolism":"The Latin reads \"Where very many are baptized, all their goods brought together in common\" (Acts 2): baptism and the sharing of goods shown as one life.","impact":"Galle's Antwerp prints of Heemskerck's designs spread his biblical scenes across Europe as models for painters, glass makers and book illustrators.","img":"img/commonlife1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Apostles_Baptizing_in_Jerusalem_1575_print_by_Maarten_van_Heemskerck,_S.I_55824,_Prints_Department,_Royal_Library_of_Belgium.jpg"}],"tags":["prayer","teaching"]},
  {"id":"lameman","harmony":"Acts 3:1–10","lane":"apostles","place":"temple","date":"c. AD 33","title":"The Lame Man at the Beautiful Gate","people":["Peter","John","A man lame from birth"],"gospels":{"Acts":"3:1–10"},"summary":"Peter and John go up to the Temple at the hour of prayer, three in the afternoon. A man lame from birth, carried every day to the gate called Beautiful, asks them for alms. Peter says he has no silver or gold, but in the name of Jesus Christ of Nazareth he tells him to walk, and takes him by the hand. The man leaps up and goes into the Temple with them, walking and leaping and praising God.","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 3:6","text":"But Peter said, \"I have no silver and gold, but I give you what I have; in the name of Jesus Christ of Nazareth, walk.\""},{"ref":"Acts 3:8","text":"And leaping up he stood and walked and entered the temple with them, walking and leaping and praising God."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Isaiah 35:6","note":"\"Then shall the lame man leap like a hart\": a sign of the age of salvation."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"The Healing of the Lame Man","artist":"Raphael","year":"1515–1516","medium":"Bodycolour on paper, mounted on canvas","location":"Royal Collection, on loan to the Victoria and Albert Museum, London","description":"Between great twisted columns, Peter bends to take the hand of the crippled man, as John looks on. Another cripple leans on a stick at right, and mothers with children and onlookers crowd the Temple gate.","significance":"One of seven surviving full-size cartoons Raphael made for tapestries ordered by Pope Leo X for the Sistine Chapel, woven in Brussels in the workshop of Pieter van Aelst.","symbolism":"The twisted columns copy the ones in Old St Peter's that were believed to come from Solomon's Temple; they set the scene at the Temple's gate and tie it to the Pope's own basilica.","impact":"Bought by the future Charles I in 1623, the cartoons have hung in the V&A since 1865 and were among the most studied and copied works of art in Britain.","img":"img/lameman0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:V&A_-_Raphael,_The_Healing_of_the_Lame_Man_(1515).jpg"},{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"Saints Peter and John Healing the Lame Man","artist":"Nicolas Poussin","year":"1655","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York","description":"On the Temple steps, Peter reaches out his hand to the lame man sitting on the stair, while John points to heaven. A beggar woman with a baby and townspeople fill the steps below.","significance":"A late work by Poussin, the French classical painter who spent most of his life in Rome.","symbolism":"John's raised finger shows where the power comes from, \"in the name of Jesus Christ\"; the calm, stage-like steps are typical of Poussin's classical order.","impact":"Poussin's ordered, sculptural style became the model for the French Academy.","img":"img/lameman1.jpg","credit":"Public domain (CC0), The Metropolitan Museum of Art, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saints_Peter_and_John_Healing_the_Lame_Man_MET_DP340180.jpg"}],"tags":["miracle","healing"],"orderNote":"The Beautiful Gate is named nowhere else. It is often identified with the Nicanor Gate, the great bronze gate between the Court of the Gentiles (or of the Women) and the inner courts that Josephus describes; others think of the Shushan Gate on the east."},
  {"id":"solomonsportico","harmony":"Acts 3:11–26","lane":"apostles","place":"temple","date":"c. AD 33","title":"Peter Preaches in Solomon's Portico","people":["Peter","John","The healed man","The people"],"gospels":{"Acts":"3:11–26"},"summary":"As the healed man clings to Peter and John, the people run together to them in the colonnade called Solomon's Portico. Peter tells them the man was not healed by the apostles' own power but by faith in the name of Jesus, the Author of life, whom they denied before Pilate and whom God raised from the dead. They acted in ignorance, he says, and now they must repent so that their sins may be blotted out. Jesus is the prophet like Moses whom Moses foretold.","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 3:15","text":"and killed the Author of life, whom God raised from the dead. To this we are witnesses."},{"ref":"Acts 3:19","text":"Repent therefore, and turn again, that your sins may be blotted out, that times of refreshing may come from the presence of the Lord,"}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Deuteronomy 18:15","note":"\"The Lord your God will raise up for you a prophet like me\": Peter applies it to Jesus."},{"ref":"Genesis 22:18","note":"\"By your descendants shall all the nations of the earth bless themselves\": the promise to Abraham."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"St Peter Healing the Lame at the Beautiful Gate","artist":"Philips Galle after Maarten van Heemskerck","year":"1575","medium":"Engraving","location":"Royal Library of Belgium (KBR), Brussels","description":"On the Temple steps Peter takes the lame man by the hand as John stands by; at left men wheel in another cripple on a barrow, and a third lies on the pavement. Deep in the colonnaded courts behind, the apostles go on into the Temple, where the crowd gathers.","significance":"One plate of Heemskerck's Acta Apostolorum, a series of engravings on the Acts of the Apostles, designed by the Haarlem painter and engraved by Philips Galle.","symbolism":"The line in the picture reads \"The man born lame is healed by the apostle Peter\"; the long colonnades recall the porticoes of the Temple court where Peter then preached.","impact":"Galle's Antwerp prints of Heemskerck's designs spread his biblical scenes across Europe as models for painters, glass makers and book illustrators.","img":"img/solomonsportico0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St_Peter_Healing_The_Lame_at_The_Beautiful_Gate_1575_print_by_Maarten_van_Heemskerck,_S.I_55793,_Prints_Department,_Royal_Library_of_Belgium.jpg"}],"tags":["sermon"],"orderNote":"Solomon's Portico was the colonnade along the east side of Herod's Temple court; Jesus taught there at the feast of Dedication (John 10:23)."},
  {"id":"petercouncil","harmony":"Acts 4:1–22","lane":"apostles","place":"temple","date":"c. AD 33","title":"Peter and John before the Sanhedrin","people":["Peter","John","Annas","Caiaphas","The captain of the Temple","The Sadducees","The healed man"],"gospels":{"Acts":"4:1–22"},"summary":"The priests, the captain of the Temple and the Sadducees arrest Peter and John for preaching the resurrection in Jesus, and hold them overnight; still the believers grow to about five thousand men. Next day, before Annas, Caiaphas and the rulers, Peter, filled with the Holy Spirit, says the man was healed by the name of Jesus, whom they crucified and God raised. The council marvels at the boldness of these uneducated men and recognizes that they had been with Jesus. They order them never to speak in that name, but Peter and John answer that they cannot stop speaking of what they have seen and heard.","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 4:12","text":"And there is salvation in no one else, for there is no other name under heaven given among men by which we must be saved.\""},{"ref":"Acts 4:19–20","text":"But Peter and John answered them, \"Whether it is right in the sight of God to listen to you rather than to God, you must judge; for we cannot but speak of what we have seen and heard.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Psalm 118:22","note":"\"The stone which the builders rejected has become the head of the corner\": Peter turns it on the council, as Jesus had (Mark 12:10)."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"Ossuary of Joseph son of Caiaphas","artist":"Unknown Jerusalem stone carver","year":"1st century AD","medium":"Carved limestone","location":"Israel Museum, Jerusalem","description":"A rectangular limestone box with a curved lid, its front carved with two large circles of rosettes framed by palm-like branches. A short inscription is scratched on the plain end.","significance":"Found in 1990 in a burial cave in south Jerusalem. One of its two inscriptions reads \"Yehosef bar Qayafa,\" Joseph son of Caiaphas; Josephus gives the high priest's full name as Joseph Caiaphas. Many scholars think it held the bones of the high priest who sat in judgment on Jesus and on Peter and John.","symbolism":"The fine carving fits a rich, high-priestly family; the bones of several people were found inside, one of them a man of about sixty.","impact":"Among the best-known finds linked to a person named in the Gospels and Acts.","img":"img/petercouncil0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Gary Todd, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Limestone_Ossuary_of_\"Joseph_Son_of_Caiaphas\"_Jerusalem,_1st_Century_AD_(41409261800).jpg"}],"tags":["conflict"],"orderNote":"Acts gives no place for the hearing. The Mishnah says the Sanhedrin sat in the Chamber of Hewn Stone in the Temple, so the map marks the Temple; some think of the high priest's house. Luke calls Annas \"the high priest\"; his son-in-law Caiaphas held the office in AD 18–36, as in Luke 3:2 and John 18:13."},
  {"id":"boldness","harmony":"Acts 4:23–37","lane":"apostles","place":"jerusalemchurch","date":"c. AD 33","title":"The Prayer for Boldness; One Heart and Soul","people":["Peter","John","The believers","Joseph Barnabas"],"gospels":{"Acts":"4:23–37"},"summary":"Released, Peter and John report the council's threats, and the church prays together, reading Psalm 2 of Herod and Pilate gathered against God's holy servant Jesus. They ask not for safety but for boldness to speak; the place where they are gathered is shaken, and all are filled with the Holy Spirit. The believers are of one heart and soul: owners of land or houses sell them and lay the money at the apostles' feet, so that no one is in need. Among them is Joseph, a Levite from Cyprus, whom the apostles call Barnabas, \"son of encouragement\"; he sells a field and brings the money. Barnabas will later stand by Saul and go with him on the first missionary journey.","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 4:31","text":"And when they had prayed, the place in which they were gathered together was shaken; and they were all filled with the Holy Spirit and spoke the word of God with boldness."},{"ref":"Acts 4:32","text":"Now the company of those who believed were of one heart and soul, and no one said that any of the things which he possessed was his own, but they had everything in common."},{"ref":"Acts 4:36–37","text":"Thus Joseph who was surnamed by the apostles Barnabas (which means, Son of encouragement), a Levite, a native of Cyprus, sold a field which belonged to him, and brought the money and laid it at the apostles' feet."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Psalm 2:1–2","note":"\"Why do the nations conspire... against the Lord and his anointed\": the church's prayer reads it as fulfilled in the Passion."},{"ref":"Deuteronomy 15:4","note":"\"There will be no poor among you\": Luke's \"there was not a needy person among them\" (4:34) echoes the promise."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"The Distribution of Alms and the Death of Ananias","artist":"Masaccio","year":"c. 1426–1427","medium":"Fresco","location":"Brancacci Chapel, Santa Maria del Carmine, Florence","description":"Peter, with John beside him, places a coin in the hand of a mother holding a baby, while poor people wait. On the ground at his feet lies the dead Ananias, and a Tuscan village and hills stretch behind.","significance":"One of Masaccio's scenes in the Brancacci Chapel's life of St Peter, painted shortly before his death at 26.","symbolism":"Masaccio joins two scenes, the sharing of goods \"as any had need\" (4:35) and the death of Ananias who held back (5:5), making one lesson of the church's common life.","impact":"Masaccio's solid figures, real light and Florentine street settings made the chapel a school for Renaissance painters.","img":"img/boldness0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Masaccio_-_The_Distribution_of_Alms_and_the_Death_of_Ananias_-_WGA14184.jpg"}],"tags":["prayer","miracle"]},
  {"id":"ananiassapphira","harmony":"Acts 5:1–11","lane":"apostles","place":"jerusalemchurch","date":"c. AD 33–34","title":"Ananias and Sapphira","people":["Peter","Ananias","Sapphira","The young men"],"gospels":{"Acts":"5:1–11"},"summary":"Ananias and his wife Sapphira sell a piece of property, keep back part of the price and lay the rest at the apostles' feet as if it were all. Peter tells Ananias that the land was his own to keep, but he has lied not to men but to the Holy Spirit; Ananias falls down and dies. Three hours later Sapphira tells the same lie and dies at Peter's feet. Great fear comes upon the whole church; this is the first time Acts uses the word \"church.\"","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 5:3","text":"But Peter said, \"Ananias, why has Satan filled your heart to lie to the Holy Spirit and to keep back part of the proceeds of the land?"},{"ref":"Acts 5:5","text":"When Ananias heard these words, he fell down and died. And great fear came upon all who heard of it."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Joshua 7:1","note":"Achan kept back what was devoted to God at Jericho and died for it; the Greek Old Testament uses the same verb, \"kept back,\" as Acts 5:2."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"The Death of Ananias","artist":"Raphael","year":"1515–1516","medium":"Bodycolour on paper, mounted on canvas","location":"Royal Collection, on loan to the Victoria and Albert Museum, London","description":"Peter stands on a raised platform among the apostles and points down as Ananias collapses in a convulsion before him; the bystanders recoil in horror. At left people bring their gifts, and at right Sapphira counts money, unaware of what has happened.","significance":"One of the seven surviving cartoons Raphael made for the Sistine Chapel tapestries of Pope Leo X, woven in Brussels.","symbolism":"The apostles raised above the crowd show their authority; the falling body of Ananias is one of Raphael's most dramatic figures.","impact":"The cartoons hung at Hampton Court for two centuries and shaped the way British artists composed history paintings.","img":"img/ananiassapphira0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:V&A_-_Raphael,_The_Death_of_Ananias_(1515).jpg"},{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"The Death of Sapphira","artist":"Philips Galle after Maarten van Heemskerck","year":"1575","medium":"Engraving","location":"Royal Library of Belgium (KBR), Brussels","description":"Peter and the apostles stand on the Temple steps as Sapphira sinks dying at their feet. In front, Ananias lies dead beside his scattered money, and young men carry a body away in the background.","significance":"One plate of Heemskerck's Acta Apostolorum, a series of engravings on the Acts of the Apostles, designed by the Haarlem painter and engraved by Philips Galle.","symbolism":"The Latin verses say that unhappy Ananias died for his false deed and his wife was struck with the same punishment, for God can neither be deceived nor deceive.","impact":"Galle's Antwerp prints of Heemskerck's designs spread his biblical scenes across Europe as models for painters, glass makers and book illustrators.","img":"img/ananiassapphira1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Death_of_Sapphira_1575_print_by_Maarten_van_Heemskerck,_S.I_55759,_Prints_Department,_Royal_Library_of_Belgium.jpg"}],"tags":["miracle"]},
  {"id":"petershadow","harmony":"Acts 5:12–16","lane":"apostles","place":"temple","date":"c. AD 33–34","title":"Signs and Wonders: Peter's Shadow","people":["Peter","The apostles","The sick","The people"],"gospels":{"Acts":"5:12–16"},"summary":"Many signs and wonders are done through the apostles, who meet together in Solomon's Portico. The people hold them in high honor, and more and more men and women believe. They carry the sick into the streets on beds and pallets, hoping that at least Peter's shadow may fall on some of them as he passes. Crowds come from the towns around Jerusalem with the sick and those troubled by unclean spirits, and all are healed.","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 5:15–16","text":"so that they even carried out the sick into the streets, and laid them on beds and pallets, that as Peter came by at least his shadow might fall on some of them. The people also gathered from the towns around Jerusalem, bringing the sick and those afflicted with unclean spirits, and they were all healed."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"St Peter Healing the Sick with His Shadow","artist":"Masaccio","year":"c. 1426–1427","medium":"Fresco","location":"Brancacci Chapel, Santa Maria del Carmine, Florence","description":"Peter walks down a narrow Florentine street with John and another apostle, looking straight ahead. Crippled men kneel and sit by the walls, and one rises as Peter's shadow falls on him.","significance":"Masaccio sets Acts 5:15 in a street of 15th-century Florence, in the Brancacci Chapel's life of St Peter.","symbolism":"Peter does not touch or even look at the sick: the healing passes through his shadow, a sign that the power is God's, not his.","impact":"Restored in 1983–1990, the frescoes regained their original color and are counted among the founding works of Renaissance painting.","img":"img/petershadow0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cappella_brancacci,_San_Pietro_risana_gli_infermi_con_la_sua_ombra_(restaurato),_Masaccio.jpg"}],"tags":["miracle","healing","exorcism"]},
  {"id":"prisonopened","harmony":"Acts 5:17–26","lane":"apostles","place":"temple","date":"c. AD 33–34","title":"The Apostles Freed by an Angel","people":["The apostles","The high priest","The Sadducees","An angel of the Lord","The captain of the Temple"],"gospels":{"Acts":"5:17–26"},"summary":"Filled with jealousy, the high priest and the Sadducees arrest the apostles and put them in the public prison. At night an angel of the Lord opens the doors, leads them out and tells them to go and speak in the Temple \"all the words of this Life.\" At daybreak they are teaching in the Temple. When the council sends for them, the officers find the prison locked and the guards at the doors, but no one inside, and the captain brings the apostles back without violence, for fear of the people.","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 5:19–20","text":"But at night an angel of the Lord opened the prison doors and brought them out and said, \"Go and stand in the temple and speak to the people all the words of this Life.\""},{"ref":"Acts 5:23","text":"\"We found the prison securely locked and the sentries standing at the doors, but when we opened it we found no one inside.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"The Angel Delivering the Apostles out of Prison","artist":"Philips Galle after Maarten van Heemskerck","year":"1575","medium":"Engraving","location":"Royal Library of Belgium (KBR), Brussels","description":"A shining angel leads Peter, holding his keys, and the other apostles out through an arched gate, while armed guards sleep on the stairs and around the doorway.","significance":"One plate of Heemskerck's Acta Apostolorum, a series of engravings on the Acts of the Apostles, designed by the Haarlem painter and engraved by Philips Galle.","symbolism":"The sleeping guards match the report that the prison was \"securely locked and the sentries standing at the doors\" (5:23); Peter's keys mark him as leader of the Twelve.","impact":"Galle's Antwerp prints of Heemskerck's designs spread his biblical scenes across Europe as models for painters, glass makers and book illustrators.","img":"img/prisonopened0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Angel_Delivering_The_Apostles_out_of_Prison_1575_print_by_Maarten_van_Heemskerck,_S.I_55828,_Prints_Department,_Royal_Library_of_Belgium.jpg"}],"tags":["miracle","conflict"],"orderNote":"The \"common prison\" is not located; the map marks the Temple, where the apostles were found teaching at dawn."},
  {"id":"gamaliel","harmony":"Acts 5:27–42","lane":"apostles","place":"temple","date":"c. AD 33–34","title":"\"We Must Obey God\": Gamaliel's Counsel","people":["Peter","The apostles","The high priest","Gamaliel","The council"],"gospels":{"Acts":"5:27–42"},"summary":"Before the council the high priest reminds the apostles they were forbidden to teach in Jesus' name. Peter answers that they must obey God rather than men, and bears witness to Jesus, raised and exalted as Leader and Savior. The council wants to kill them, but Gamaliel, an honored Pharisee and teacher of the law, warns them: if this work is of men it will fail, but if it is of God they cannot overthrow it. The apostles are beaten and released, and go away rejoicing that they were counted worthy to suffer for the name.","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 5:29","text":"But Peter and the apostles answered, \"We must obey God rather than men."},{"ref":"Acts 5:38–39","text":"So in the present case I tell you, keep away from these men and let them alone; for if this plan or this undertaking is of men, it will fail; but if it is of God, you will not be able to overthrow them. You might even be found opposing God!\""},{"ref":"Acts 5:41","text":"Then they left the presence of the council, rejoicing that they were counted worthy to suffer dishonor for the name."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"The Apostles Flogged before the Council","artist":"Sadeler after Maerten de Vos","year":"Late 16th century","medium":"Engraving","location":"Rijksmuseum, Amsterdam","description":"In front of the council hall, two stripped and bound apostles, one young and one old with a halo, are driven forward by men swinging whips and rods. At right, councilors in turbans argue and point; in the background others gather before a palace.","significance":"An Antwerp engraving by one of the Sadeler family of printmakers, after a design by Maerten de Vos, the leading Antwerp painter of the time.","symbolism":"The Latin reads \"And calling in the apostles, they beat them and charged them not to speak in the name of Jesus\" (Acts 5:40); the apostles' upturned faces show the joy of 5:41.","impact":"Flemish Bible prints like this were copied by painters from Europe to Latin America.","img":"img/gamaliel0.jpg","credit":"Public domain (CC0), Rijksmuseum, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Geseling_van_de_apostelen,_RP-P-OB-5870.jpg"}],"tags":["conflict"],"orderNote":"Gamaliel the Elder, grandson of Hillel by later Jewish tradition, was Saul's teacher (Acts 22:3). His mention of Theudas (5:36) is debated, since Josephus dates a Theudas about AD 44–46; Luke may mean an earlier rebel of that name."},
  {"id":"seven","harmony":"Acts 6:1–7","lane":"apostles","place":"jerusalemchurch","date":"c. AD 34","title":"The Seven Chosen","people":["The twelve","Stephen","Philip","Prochorus","Nicanor","Timon","Parmenas","Nicolaus"],"gospels":{"Acts":"6:1–7"},"summary":"As the disciples grow, the Greek-speaking Jewish believers complain that their widows are being overlooked in the daily distribution of food. The Twelve say they must not give up preaching to serve tables, and ask the church to choose seven men of good repute, full of the Spirit and of wisdom. The church chooses Stephen, Philip and five others, all with Greek names; the apostles pray and lay their hands on them. The word of God spreads, and many priests become obedient to the faith.","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 6:3","text":"Therefore, brethren, pick out from among you seven men of good repute, full of the Spirit and of wisdom, whom we may appoint to this duty."},{"ref":"Acts 6:6","text":"These they set before the apostles, and they prayed and laid their hands upon them."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Numbers 27:18–23","note":"Moses lays his hands on Joshua before the people to commission him."},{"ref":"Exodus 18:17–23","note":"Jethro advises Moses to share the burden by appointing able men."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"St Peter Consecrates Stephen as Deacon; St Stephen Distributing Alms","artist":"Fra Angelico","year":"1447–1449","medium":"Fresco","location":"Niccoline Chapel, Apostolic Palace, Vatican City","description":"At left, Peter, with the apostles behind him, hands the kneeling Stephen a chalice and paten, the sign of his ordination. At right, Stephen in a deacon's dalmatic gives alms to a widow, a child and other poor people.","significance":"The first scene of Stephen's life in the private chapel of Pope Nicholas V, where Fra Angelico painted the stories of the two great deacon-martyrs, Stephen and Lawrence.","symbolism":"The chalice shows the deacon's service at the altar; the alms show the \"serving tables\" of Acts 6:2. The pillar dividing the two halves joins the two duties.","impact":"Fra Angelico's last great fresco cycle, among the finest works of early Renaissance Rome.","img":"img/seven0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Angelico,_niccolina_06.jpg"}],"tags":["call","prayer"],"orderNote":"Acts does not call the Seven \"deacons,\" though it uses the verb \"to serve\" (diakonein, 6:2); since Irenaeus (2nd century) the Church has seen in them the first deacons."},
  {"id":"stephenarrest","harmony":"Acts 6:8–15","lane":"apostles","place":"temple","date":"c. AD 34","title":"Stephen Seized","people":["Stephen","Men of the synagogue of the Freedmen","Cyrenians, Alexandrians, men of Cilicia and Asia","False witnesses","The council"],"gospels":{"Acts":"6:8–15"},"summary":"Stephen, full of grace and power, does great wonders and signs among the people. Men from the synagogue of the Freedmen and from Cyrene, Alexandria, Cilicia and Asia argue with him but cannot withstand his wisdom and the Spirit with which he speaks. They stir up the people, seize him and bring him before the council, with false witnesses who say he speaks against the Temple and the Law. As the council gazes at him, his face is like the face of an angel.","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 6:8","text":"And Stephen, full of grace and power, did great wonders and signs among the people."},{"ref":"Acts 6:15","text":"And gazing at him, all who sat in the council saw that his face was like the face of an angel."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Exodus 34:29–30","note":"Moses' face shone when he came down from Sinai; Stephen, accused of speaking against Moses, shines like him."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"The Disputation of St Stephen","artist":"Vittore Carpaccio","year":"1514","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Pinacoteca di Brera, Milan","description":"Under an open arcade, Stephen stands on a low platform and argues with a seated audience of scholars in turbans and robes, with Venetian citizens in red and black at right. Beyond lies an imagined Jerusalem with an obelisk, an equestrian statue and towers.","significance":"Part of Carpaccio's cycle of the life of St Stephen for the Scuola di Santo Stefano, a Venetian confraternity (1511–1520).","symbolism":"The learned men with books are the members of the synagogues who \"disputed with Stephen\" but \"could not withstand the wisdom and the Spirit with which he spoke\" (6:9–10). The date MDXIIII and Carpaccio's signature are on the pillars.","impact":"Carpaccio's crowded, exotic cityscapes are a vivid record of how Venice imagined the East.","img":"img/stephenarrest0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vittore_Carpaccio_Disputation_of_St_Stephen.jpg"}],"tags":["conflict","miracle"],"orderNote":"The charges echo those at Jesus' trial (Mark 14:57–58). Saul of Tarsus in Cilicia may have been among the men of the Cilician synagogue, but Acts does not say so."},
  {"id":"stephenspeech","harmony":"Acts 7:1–53","lane":"apostles","place":"temple","date":"c. AD 34","title":"Stephen's Speech","people":["Stephen","The high priest","The council"],"gospels":{"Acts":"7:1–53"},"summary":"Asked by the high priest whether the charges are true, Stephen retells Israel's story: God's call of Abraham, Joseph sold by his brothers, Moses rejected by his own people, the tent in the wilderness and the house Solomon built. God met his people outside the land and does not dwell in houses made with hands. Again and again, he says, the fathers resisted the Holy Spirit and killed the prophets, and now the council has betrayed and murdered the Righteous One. It is the longest speech in Acts.","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 7:48","text":"Yet the Most High does not dwell in houses made with hands"},{"ref":"Acts 7:51","text":"\"You stiff-necked people, uncircumcised in heart and ears, you always resist the Holy Spirit. As your fathers did, so do you."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Isaiah 66:1","note":"\"Heaven is my throne and the earth is my footstool; what is the house which you would build for me?\": quoted by Stephen (7:49–50)."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"St Stephen Preaching; St Stephen Addressing the Council","artist":"Fra Angelico","year":"1447–1449","medium":"Fresco","location":"Niccoline Chapel, Apostolic Palace, Vatican City","description":"At left, Stephen stands on a low step and preaches to seated women and a group of men in a city square. At right, inside a hall, he stands before the seated high priest and the elders, raising a finger as he makes his defense.","significance":"The second lunette of Stephen's story in the chapel Fra Angelico painted for Pope Nicholas V.","symbolism":"The wall between the two scenes sets Stephen's open-air preaching against the closed council chamber; the high priest's raised hand questions, Stephen's raised finger points to God.","impact":"Fra Angelico's last great fresco cycle, among the finest works of early Renaissance Rome.","img":"img/stephenspeech0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Angelico,_niccolina_05.jpg"},{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"St Stephen before the High Priest and the Council","artist":"Philips Galle after Maarten van Heemskerck","year":"1575","medium":"Engraving","location":"Royal Library of Belgium (KBR), Brussels","description":"The high priest, robed and mitred, sits enthroned with the council under a canopy as soldiers bring Stephen before him; Stephen stands calmly with open hands. Through the colonnade at right, his stoning and the opened heavens are already shown in the distance.","significance":"One plate of Heemskerck's Acta Apostolorum, a series of engravings on the Acts of the Apostles, designed by the Haarlem painter and engraved by Philips Galle.","symbolism":"The Latin verses hail Stephen as the first to win the crown and palm of martyrdom, who saw the heavens opened.","impact":"Galle's Antwerp prints of Heemskerck's designs spread his biblical scenes across Europe as models for painters, glass makers and book illustrators.","img":"img/stephenspeech1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St_Stephen_Before_The_High_Priest_and_The_Council_1575_print_by_Maarten_van_Heemskerck,_S.I_55762,_Prints_Department,_Royal_Library_of_Belgium.jpg"}],"tags":["sermon","conflict"]},
  {"id":"stephendeath","harmony":"Acts 7:54–8:2","lane":"apostles","place":"stephensite","from":"temple","date":"c. AD 34","title":"The Martyrdom of Stephen","people":["Stephen","The council","Saul","Devout men"],"gospels":{"Acts":"7:54–8:2"},"summary":"Enraged, the council grinds its teeth at Stephen. Full of the Holy Spirit, he gazes into heaven and sees the glory of God and Jesus standing at God's right hand. They stop their ears, rush on him, drive him out of the city and stone him, and the witnesses lay their garments at the feet of a young man named Saul. Stephen prays, \"Lord Jesus, receive my spirit,\" and kneeling asks forgiveness for his killers before he falls asleep. Saul consents to his death, and devout men bury Stephen with great lamentation; he is honored as the first martyr.","journey":"Driven out of the city from the council; by the oldest tradition north, through the gate on the Damascus road, under half a mile.","route":[[35.235,31.778],[35.2325,31.7805],[35.2305,31.783],[35.2296,31.7853]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 7:56","text":"and he said, \"Behold, I see the heavens opened, and the Son of man standing at the right hand of God.\""},{"ref":"Acts 7:58","text":"Then they cast him out of the city and stoned him; and the witnesses laid down their garments at the feet of a young man named Saul."},{"ref":"Acts 7:59–60","text":"And as they were stoning Stephen, he prayed, \"Lord Jesus, receive my spirit.\" And he knelt down and cried with a loud voice, \"Lord, do not hold this sin against them.\" And when he had said this, he fell asleep."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Daniel 7:13","note":"Daniel sees \"one like a son of man\" come before the Ancient of Days; Stephen sees the Son of man standing at God's right hand."},{"ref":"2 Chronicles 24:20–22","note":"Zechariah, stoned in the Temple court, dies saying \"May the Lord see and avenge\"; Stephen dies forgiving, as Jesus did (Luke 23:34)."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"The Stoning of St Stephen","artist":"Rembrandt","year":"1625","medium":"Oil on panel","location":"Musée des Beaux-Arts, Lyon","description":"Stephen, in a red deacon's dalmatic, kneels with raised hands as men heave stones over him. A rider in red watches from the left, and on a rise behind, a young man sits with the cloaks of the witnesses on his lap, with elders around him.","significance":"Rembrandt's earliest known dated painting, made in Leiden when he was 19.","symbolism":"The young man holding the garments is Saul (Acts 7:58), the future apostle. The face just above Stephen's head is often taken as Rembrandt's own, his first self-portrait.","impact":"It shows the young Rembrandt already using strong light and dark to pick out the drama of a scene.","img":"img/stephendeath0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rembrandt-Lapidation-Saint-Étienne-MBA-Lyon.jpg"},{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"The Stoning of St Stephen","artist":"Adam Elsheimer","year":"c. 1603–1604","medium":"Oil on copper","location":"Scottish National Gallery, Edinburgh","description":"Stephen kneels in a rich dalmatic, looking up as a half-naked man lifts a great stone over him and others gather more. Above, an angel swoops down with a palm, followed by little angels, and heaven opens in light at the top left.","significance":"A small, jewel-like picture on copper by the German painter Elsheimer, painted in Rome.","symbolism":"The palm and the opening heaven answer Stephen's vision: \"I see the heavens opened\" (Acts 7:56).","impact":"Elsheimer's small, glowing paintings on copper influenced Rubens and the young Rembrandt's teachers.","img":"img/stephendeath1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adam_Elsheimer_-_Steinigung_des_heiligen_Stephanus_(Edinburgh).jpg"}],"tags":["passion","prayer","appearance"],"orderNote":"Under Roman rule the council could not lawfully carry out a death sentence (John 18:31), so the stoning may have been mob violence, or happened in a lapse of Roman control; some date it to AD 36, when Pilate was recalled. Dates for Stephen range from AD 31 to 36, depending on the date of the Crucifixion."},
  {"id":"scattering","harmony":"Acts 8:1–4","lane":"apostles","place":"jerusalemchurch","date":"c. AD 34","title":"The Persecution and the Scattering","people":["Saul of Tarsus","The church in Jerusalem","The apostles"],"gospels":{"Acts":"8:1–4","Gal":"1:13"},"summary":"On the day of Stephen's death a great persecution breaks out against the church in Jerusalem. Saul goes from house to house, dragging men and women off to prison. The believers scatter through Judea and Samaria; only the apostles stay. The scattering spreads the Gospel: those who flee preach wherever they go, as Jesus had said, \"in all Judea and Samaria\" (Acts 1:8).","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 8:1","text":"And on that day a great persecution arose against the church in Jerusalem; and they were all scattered throughout the region of Judea and Samaria, except the apostles."},{"ref":"Acts 8:3–4","text":"But Saul laid waste the church, and entering house after house, he dragged off men and women and committed them to prison. Now those who were scattered went about preaching the word."},{"ref":"Galatians 1:13","text":"For you have heard of my former life in Judaism, how I persecuted the church of God violently and tried to destroy it;"}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"Saul Persecutes the Church in Jerusalem","artist":"Pieter Tanjé (engraver)","year":"18th century","medium":"Engraving (book illustration)","location":"Rijksmuseum, Amsterdam","description":"Two scenes on one page. Above (Acts 8): soldiers drag men and women from their houses and lead them off bound, while a young Saul on horseback points the way. Below (Acts 9): Saul is struck down on the road to Damascus by a shaft of light.","significance":"From an eighteenth-century Dutch picture Bible, with captions in Dutch and French: \"The arrest of various men and women\" and \"The Conversion of Saul.\"","symbolism":"Saul rides high in the first scene and lies on the ground in the second: the persecutor's fall told in one page.","impact":"Such illustrated Bibles were widely used for teaching the Bible story in Dutch homes.","img":"img/scattering0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saulus_vervolgt_de_gemeente_in_Jeruzalem_Bekering_van_Paulus_Gevangenneming_van_Verscheide_Mannen_en_Vrouwen_(..)_Bekeering_van_Saulus_(..)_(titel_op_object),_RP-P-1933-113.jpg"}],"tags":["conflict"],"orderNote":"The year is uncertain: Stephen's death and the persecution are usually dated AD 33–35, depending on the date of the Crucifixion (AD 30 or 33). Saul's own word for what he did is \"violently\" (Galatians 1:13; also 1 Corinthians 15:9, Philippians 3:6)."},
  {"id":"philipsamaria","harmony":"Acts 8:5–8","lane":"apostles","place":"sebaste","from":"jerusalemchurch","date":"c. AD 34","title":"Philip Preaches in Samaria","people":["Philip the deacon","The Samaritans"],"gospels":{"Acts":"8:5–8"},"summary":"Philip, one of the Seven chosen with Stephen, flees north to a city of Samaria and proclaims the Christ. The crowds listen, seeing the signs he does: unclean spirits come out, and the paralyzed and lame are healed. The Samaritans, long at odds with the Jews, receive the Gospel, and there is much joy in the city. It is the first step of the Gospel beyond Judea.","journey":"About 40 miles north from Jerusalem along the ridge road past Bethel and Shechem, two or three days on foot.","route":[[35.231,31.776],[35.21,31.86],[35.22,31.93],[35.25,32.07],[35.28,32.21],[35.192,32.276]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 8:5–6","text":"Philip went down to a city of Samaria, and proclaimed to them the Christ. And the multitudes with one accord gave heed to what was said by Philip, when they heard him and saw the signs which he did."},{"ref":"Acts 8:8","text":"So there was much joy in that city."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"2 Kings 17:24–41","note":"The origin of the Samaritans, settled in the land after the fall of the northern kingdom; their rift with the Jews runs through the Gospels (John 4:9)."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"Ruins at Sebastia (Samaria)","artist":"Photo by Daniel Ventura","year":"Photo 1990","medium":"Photograph","location":"Sebastia, West Bank","description":"Massive ancient walls and a tower of squared stones rise on the hill of Samaria-Sebaste, above a road winding past the ruins.","significance":"Samaria was the capital of Israel's northern kingdom (1 Kings 16:24). Herod the Great rebuilt it as Sebaste with a temple of Augustus, a stadium and a colonnaded street.","symbolism":"Stones of a capital once at odds with Jerusalem, where the Gospel was now gladly received.","impact":"Excavated by Harvard (1908–10) and a joint expedition (1931–35), the site is among the great archaeological sites of the region.","img":"img/philipsamaria0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Daniel Ventura, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ruins_by_Sebastia_02.jpg"}],"tags":["miracle","healing","exorcism","sermon"],"orderNote":"This Philip is the deacon of Acts 6:5, later called \"the evangelist\" (Acts 21:8), not the apostle Philip. Acts does not name the city; see the place note."},
  {"id":"simonmagus","harmony":"Acts 8:9–25","lane":"apostles","place":"sebaste","from":"jerusalemchurch","date":"c. AD 34","title":"Peter and John in Samaria; Simon the Magician","people":["Peter","John","Philip","Simon the magician","The Samaritans"],"gospels":{"Acts":"8:9–25"},"summary":"A magician named Simon, whom the Samaritans called \"the power of God which is called Great,\" believes Philip's preaching and is baptized. The apostles in Jerusalem send Peter and John, who pray and lay hands on the new believers, and they receive the Holy Spirit. Simon offers money for the same power. Peter rebukes him sharply, and Simon asks for prayer. From his name comes the word simony, the buying and selling of holy things.","journey":"Peter and John come about 40 miles north from Jerusalem; on the way back they preach in many Samaritan villages.","route":[[35.231,31.776],[35.21,31.86],[35.22,31.93],[35.25,32.07],[35.28,32.21],[35.192,32.276]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 8:14–15","text":"Now when the apostles at Jerusalem heard that Samaria had received the word of God, they sent to them Peter and John, who came down and prayed for them that they might receive the Holy Spirit;"},{"ref":"Acts 8:18–20","text":"Now when Simon saw that the Spirit was given through the laying on of the apostles' hands, he offered them money, saying, \"Give me also this power, that any one on whom I lay my hands may receive the Holy Spirit.\" But Peter said to him, \"Your silver perish with you, because you thought you could obtain the gift of God with money!"}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Deuteronomy 29:18","note":"Peter's \"gall of bitterness\" (Acts 8:23) recalls Moses' warning against \"a root bearing poisonous and bitter fruit\" in Israel."},{"ref":"2 Kings 5:15–27","note":"Elisha refuses Naaman's money for God's healing, and Gehazi is punished for taking it."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"Simon Magus Offering Saint Peter Money for the Power of Conferring the Holy Spirit","artist":"Liberale da Verona","year":"c. 1470–1475","medium":"Tempera on panel","location":"Fitzwilliam Museum, Cambridge","description":"Before an elegant Renaissance palace, a young man in a red cloak and blue tunic, Simon, steps forward holding out a purse to Peter, who draws back with a gesture of refusal, another apostle behind him. At right a woman in red, veiled in white, comes down the palace stairs.","significance":"A small panel by the Veronese painter and illuminator Liberale da Verona, probably part of a series or predella of St Peter's life.","symbolism":"The purse held out at arm's length and the apostle's raised hand say it all: \"Your silver perish with you\" (Acts 8:20). The fine courtly dress makes Simon a man of the world.","impact":"Simon's sin gave its name to simony, the buying of spiritual office, a burning issue in the Church of Liberale's day.","img":"img/simonmagus0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Liberale_da_Verona_(c.1445-c.1526)_-_Simon_Magus_Offering_St_Peter_Money_for_the_Power_of_Conferring_the_Holy_Spirit_-_PD.21-1961_-_Fitzwilliam_Museum.jpg"}],"tags":["conflict"],"orderNote":"John is the same apostle who once wanted to call down fire on a Samaritan village (Luke 9:54). Later writers (Justin Martyr, Irenaeus) make Simon the father of Gnostic heresy, and legend tells of his contest with Peter in Rome; Acts says nothing of his end."},
  {"id":"eunuch","harmony":"Acts 8:26–40","lane":"apostles","place":"gazaroad","from":"sebaste","date":"c. AD 34","title":"Philip and the Ethiopian Eunuch","people":["Philip","An Ethiopian eunuch, treasurer of the Candace","An angel of the Lord"],"gospels":{"Acts":"8:26–40"},"summary":"An angel sends Philip south to the desert road from Jerusalem to Gaza. There an Ethiopian official, treasurer of the Candace (the title of the queens of Meroë, in today's Sudan), is riding home from worship in Jerusalem, reading Isaiah aloud in his chariot. Philip runs alongside and asks if he understands. Starting from Isaiah's suffering servant, Philip tells him the good news of Jesus, and at the first water they come to, the eunuch asks for baptism. Then the Spirit carries Philip off to Azotus, and he preaches along the coast to Caesarea.","stay":"Philip settles at Caesarea, where Paul finds him with his four daughters some twenty years later (Acts 21:8–9).","journey":"From Samaria back south past Jerusalem and down toward the coast, about 75 miles, four or five days on foot. Afterwards Philip goes north up the coast from Azotus to Caesarea, about 60 miles.","route":[[35.192,32.276],[35.28,32.21],[35.25,32.07],[35.22,31.93],[35.21,31.8],[35.06,31.7],[34.95,31.62],[34.8,31.6]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 8:30–31","text":"So Philip ran to him, and heard him reading Isaiah the prophet, and asked, \"Do you understand what you are reading?\" And he said, \"How can I, unless some one guides me?\" And he invited Philip to come up and sit with him."},{"ref":"Acts 8:35–36","text":"Then Philip opened his mouth, and beginning with this scripture he told him the good news of Jesus. And as they went along the road they came to some water, and the eunuch said, \"See, here is water! What is to prevent my being baptized?\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Isaiah 53:7–8","note":"The passage he is reading: the servant led like a lamb to the slaughter."},{"ref":"Isaiah 56:3–5","note":"\"Let not the eunuch say, 'Behold, I am a dry tree'\": God promises eunuchs who keep his covenant a name better than sons and daughters."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"The Baptism of the Eunuch","artist":"Rembrandt van Rijn","year":"1626","medium":"Oil on panel","location":"Museum Catharijneconvent, Utrecht","description":"The eunuch kneels in a fur-trimmed robe with his hands on his breast as Philip, bald and bearded, raises his hand over him. A servant holds open the great book of Isaiah, attendants watch from the chariot and horses, and a dog drinks from the stream.","significance":"An early work, painted in Leiden when Rembrandt was about twenty, after the example of his teacher Pieter Lastman. Long known only from copies and prints, it was rediscovered in the 1970s.","symbolism":"The open book is the scroll of Isaiah he was reading on the road; the parasol and fine robes mark the court official, and the kneeling posture his faith.","impact":"One of a small number of early Rembrandt history paintings; the subject was a favorite of Dutch painters, who saw in it the Gospel reaching the ends of the earth.","img":"img/eunuch0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rembrandt,_The_Baptism_of_the_Eunuch,_1626,_Museum_Catharijneconvent,_Utrecht.jpg"}],"tags":["call"],"orderNote":"Verse 37 (\"I believe that Jesus Christ is the Son of God\") is missing from the oldest manuscripts and is printed as a footnote in the RSV; the Church has long used it as a baptismal confession. Ethiopian Christians honor the eunuch as the first to bring the faith to Africa."},
  {"id":"damascusroad","harmony":"Acts 9:1–9","lane":"paul","place":"kawkab","from":"jerusalemchurch","date":"c. AD 34–35","title":"The Conversion of Saul on the Road to Damascus","people":["Saul of Tarsus","The risen Jesus","Saul's companions"],"gospels":{"Acts":"9:1–9","Gal":"1:15–16","1Cor":"15:8"},"summary":"Still breathing threats against the disciples, Saul gets letters from the high priest to arrest any followers of \"the Way\" in the synagogues of Damascus. Near the city a light from heaven flashes around him. He falls to the ground and hears a voice: \"Saul, Saul, why do you persecute me?\" It is Jesus, who calls the persecution of his followers a persecution of himself. Saul rises blind, and his companions lead him by the hand into Damascus, where for three days he neither eats nor drinks.","journey":"About 140 miles north from Jerusalem through Samaria, up the Jordan Valley past the Sea of Galilee, over the Jordan near the Bridge of Jacob's Daughters and across the Golan plateau, a journey of about a week.","route":[[35.231,31.776],[35.22,31.93],[35.25,32.07],[35.28,32.21],[35.3,32.46],[35.5,32.5],[35.55,32.7],[35.57,32.88],[35.63,33.01],[35.82,33.13],[36.18,33.42]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 9:3–5","text":"Now as he journeyed he approached Damascus, and suddenly a light from heaven flashed about him. And he fell to the ground and heard a voice saying to him, \"Saul, Saul, why do you persecute me?\" And he said, \"Who are you, Lord?\" And he said, \"I am Jesus, whom you are persecuting;"},{"ref":"Acts 9:8","text":"Saul arose from the ground; and when his eyes were opened, he could see nothing; so they led him by the hand and brought him into Damascus."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"1 Samuel 3:10","note":"\"Samuel! Samuel!\": God calls a servant by name, twice, as with Abraham and Moses."},{"ref":"Ezekiel 1:28","note":"Ezekiel falls on his face before the brightness of the glory of the Lord and hears a voice speaking."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"The Conversion on the Way to Damascus","artist":"Caravaggio","year":"1600–1601","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Cerasi Chapel, Santa Maria del Popolo, Rome","description":"Saul lies on his back in the road, thrown from his horse, eyes closed and arms raised to the light. The great piebald horse lifts a hoof carefully over him while an old groom holds its bridle. There is no vision of Christ, only light.","significance":"Painted for the chapel of Tiberio Cerasi, facing Caravaggio's Crucifixion of St Peter. It is his second version: the first, crowded with a descending Christ and angels, was not used and is now in the Odescalchi collection in Rome.","symbolism":"Most of the canvas is horse and darkness; the man who came to arrest others is overturned and helpless, and the light falls on his open arms. His sword and helmet lie useless on the ground.","impact":"One of the most famous images of the conversion, and of Caravaggio's art: a miracle shown as a single inward moment in an ordinary stable-dark world.","img":"img/damascusroad0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Conversion_on_the_Way_to_Damascus-Caravaggio_(c.1600-1).jpg"},{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"The Conversion of Saul","artist":"Michelangelo Buonarroti","year":"1542–1545","medium":"Fresco","location":"Pauline Chapel (Cappella Paolina), Apostolic Palace, Vatican City","description":"Christ plunges head-first out of heaven with his arm stretched down, sending a shaft of light onto Saul. Saul, an old bearded man, has fallen and covers his eyes, while his horse bolts and his companions scatter in terror.","significance":"One of Michelangelo's last two frescoes, painted for Pope Paul III, his namesake's chapel, opposite the Crucifixion of St Peter.","symbolism":"The shaft of light links Christ's hand and Saul's face. Michelangelo makes Saul an old man, unlike Acts' \"young man\" (7:58); many have seen in the face the painter himself, then nearly seventy.","impact":"Long hidden in the pope's private chapel, the frescoes were cleaned in 2004–2009 and are now seen as the dark, spiritual close of his painting career.","img":"img/damascusroad1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Conversion_of_Saint_Paul_(Michelangelo_Buonarroti).jpg"}],"tags":["appearance","call","miracle"],"orderNote":"Acts tells the story three times (9:1–19, 22:6–16, 26:12–18), with small differences: here the companions hear the voice but see no one, in 22:9 they see the light but do not hear the voice. Paul counts it as the last appearance of the risen Christ (1 Corinthians 15:8). Dates range from AD 31 to 36; the Church keeps the feast of the Conversion of St Paul on 25 January. Acts mentions no horse: that is the painters' tradition."},
  {"id":"saulbaptized","harmony":"Acts 9:10–19a","lane":"paul","place":"damascus","from":"kawkab","date":"c. AD 34–35","title":"Ananias Baptizes Saul","people":["Saul","Ananias of Damascus","Judas of Straight Street"],"gospels":{"Acts":"9:10–19"},"summary":"In a vision the Lord sends Ananias, a disciple in Damascus, to the house of Judas on the street called Straight. Ananias protests that Saul has come to arrest believers. The Lord answers that Saul is his chosen instrument to carry his name to the Gentiles, to kings and to Israel. Ananias lays his hands on him and calls him \"Brother Saul\"; something like scales falls from his eyes, and he is baptized.","stay":"In the house of Judas on Straight Street, then \"several days with the disciples at Damascus.\"","journey":"Led by the hand into the city, about 10 miles.","route":[[36.18,33.42],[36.24,33.47],[36.306,33.511]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 9:15","text":"But the Lord said to him, \"Go, for he is a chosen instrument of mine to carry my name before the Gentiles and kings and the sons of Israel;"},{"ref":"Acts 9:17–18","text":"So Ananias departed and entered the house. And laying his hands on him he said, \"Brother Saul, the Lord Jesus who appeared to you on the road by which you came, has sent me that you may regain your sight and be filled with the Holy Spirit.\" And immediately something like scales fell from his eyes and he regained his sight. Then he rose and was baptized,"}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Tobit 11:13","note":"When Tobias treats his father's eyes, the white films scale off and old Tobit sees again."},{"ref":"Isaiah 49:6","note":"\"I will give you as a light to the nations\": the servant's mission that Paul later claims for himself (Acts 13:47)."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"Ananias Restoring the Sight of Saint Paul","artist":"Pietro da Cortona","year":"c. 1631","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Santa Maria della Concezione dei Cappuccini, Rome","description":"Saul, in Roman armor with his helmet on the floor, kneels with hands open as the white-bearded Ananias lays a hand on his head. A youth brings a basin and ewer for the baptism, and the dove of the Spirit shines above.","significance":"An altarpiece by the leading painter of Roman Baroque, for the Capuchin church on the Via Veneto, whose side chapels hold works by Guido Reni and others.","symbolism":"The fallen helmet and the bowed soldier show the persecutor disarmed; the basin and the dove join the two parts of Ananias' errand, \"that you may regain your sight and be filled with the Holy Spirit.\"","impact":"A widely copied image of the scene; Cortona's warm, theatrical style shaped Roman church painting for generations.","img":"img/saulbaptized0.jpg","credit":"Photo: IlSistemone, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anania_guarisce_San_Paolo_-_Pietro_da_Cortona_(chiesa_di_S.M._della_Concezione,_Roma).jpg"},{"kind":"Artifact","title":"Damascus: The Street Called \"Straight\"","artist":"Félix Bonfils","year":"c. 1870s","medium":"Albumen photograph","location":"Library of Congress, Washington","description":"A narrow, cobbled lane runs between mud-brick and stone houses with overhanging wooden upper storeys; the caption reads \"DAMASCUS. The Street called 'Straight.'\"","significance":"Bonfils, a French photographer based in Beirut, made some of the first photographs of the Holy Land and Syria, sold widely to pilgrims and travelers.","symbolism":"An ordinary street of an ordinary city: the Lord sends Ananias to a real address, \"the street called Straight... the house of Judas\" (Acts 9:11).","impact":"The Bonfils studio's images, now largely in the Library of Congress, are a key record of the region before modern change.","img":"img/saulbaptized1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Damascus._The_street_called_\"Straight\"_-_Bonfils._LCCN92513509.jpg"}],"tags":["healing","miracle","call"],"orderNote":"The street called Straight survives as the main east–west street of Old Damascus. The underground chapel shown as the House of Ananias is a tradition, not certain."},
  {"id":"damascuspreach","harmony":"Acts 9:19b–22","lane":"paul","place":"damascus","date":"c. AD 35","title":"Saul Preaches in the Synagogues of Damascus","people":["Saul","The Jews of Damascus","The disciples"],"gospels":{"Acts":"9:19–22"},"summary":"At once Saul goes into the same synagogues he came to purge and proclaims that Jesus is the Son of God. His hearers are amazed: this is the man who made havoc of the believers in Jerusalem. Saul grows stronger and confounds the Jews of Damascus, proving from the Scriptures that Jesus is the Christ.","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 9:20–21","text":"And in the synagogues immediately he proclaimed Jesus, saying, \"He is the Son of God.\" And all who heard him were amazed, and said, \"Is not this the man who made havoc in Jerusalem of those who called on this name? And he has come here for this purpose, to bring them bound before the chief priests.\""},{"ref":"Acts 9:22","text":"But Saul increased all the more in strength, and confounded the Jews who lived in Damascus by proving that Jesus was the Christ."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"The Roman Arch on the Street Called Straight","artist":"Photo by Varun Shiv Kapur","year":"Photo 2011","medium":"Photograph","location":"Straight Street (Via Recta), Old City of Damascus","description":"A Roman arch of white limestone, its columns half-buried by the rise of the street level, stands beside the cobbled Straight Street, among old Damascene houses with wooden balconies.","significance":"The arch belonged to the colonnaded Roman main street, the Via Recta (\"Straight Street\") of Acts 9:11, which ran east–west across the city, where the Jewish quarter and its synagogues lay.","symbolism":"The street itself carries the story: the road down which the blind Saul was led, and in whose synagogues he soon preached.","impact":"The Roman arch is one of the best-known landmarks of the Old City of Damascus, a UNESCO World Heritage Site.","img":"img/damascuspreach0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Varun Shiv Kapur, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Straight_Street_and_Roman_Arch_(5348408394).jpg"}],"tags":["sermon","teaching"],"orderNote":"Acts runs straight on to the plot against him \"when many days had passed\" (9:23). Paul's own account puts a stay in Arabia between his first days in Damascus and his return there (Galatians 1:17); most place it here, within Luke's \"many days.\""},
  {"id":"arabia","harmony":"Gal 1:17","lane":"paul","place":"arabia","from":"damascus","date":"c. AD 35–37","title":"Saul in Arabia","people":["Saul"],"gospels":{"Gal":"1:15–17","Acts":"9:23"},"summary":"Paul's letter to the Galatians adds what Acts leaves out. After his call he did not go up to Jerusalem to consult the apostles, but went away into Arabia, the Nabataean kingdom of King Aretas, and then came back to Damascus. He gives no reason. Many think of prayer and study after his overturning; others of his first preaching to Gentiles, which would explain why the Nabataean king's governor later wanted him arrested.","stay":"Part of the three years between his conversion and his first visit to Jerusalem (Galatians 1:18).","journey":"Where he went is not known. To Petra by the King's Highway, through Bostra and Philadelphia (Amman), is about 260 miles, some two to three weeks on foot; the nearer towns of the Hauran are two or three days from Damascus.","route":[[36.306,33.511],[36.3,33.1],[36.48,32.52],[36.1,32.3],[35.93,31.95],[35.79,31.72],[35.7,31.18],[35.62,30.85],[35.8,30.6]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Galatians 1:15–17","text":"But when he who had set me apart before I was born, and had called me through his grace, was pleased to reveal his Son to me, in order that I might preach him among the Gentiles, I did not confer with flesh and blood, nor did I go up to Jerusalem to those who were apostles before me, but I went away into Arabia; and again I returned to Damascus."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"1 Kings 19:8","note":"Elijah, after his zeal for the Lord, goes forty days to Horeb, the mountain of God; some think Paul too went toward Sinai, which he places \"in Arabia\" (Galatians 4:25)."},{"ref":"Jeremiah 1:5","note":"\"Before I formed you in the womb I knew you\": Paul's \"set me apart before I was born\" echoes the call of Jeremiah."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"Al-Khazneh (\"The Treasury\"), Petra","artist":"Nabataean builders; photo by Vyacheslav Argenberg","year":"1st century BC–1st century AD (photo 2007)","medium":"Facade carved in the sandstone cliff","location":"Petra, Jordan","description":"The famous two-storey facade of columns, pediment and round pavilion, cut straight into the rose-red rock at the end of the narrow Siq gorge.","significance":"Petra was the capital of the Nabataean kingdom that Paul calls \"Arabia.\" Many scholars date the Khazneh, probably a royal tomb, to the reign of Aretas IV (9 BC–AD 40), the king whose governor later tried to seize Paul at Damascus (2 Corinthians 11:32).","symbolism":"Greek and Roman forms carved by an Arab kingdom: the mixed world of the caravan roads Paul would have walked.","impact":"A UNESCO World Heritage Site since 1985 and one of the New Seven Wonders of the World.","img":"img/arabia0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Vyacheslav Argenberg, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al-Khazneh_(The_Treasury),_Petra,_Jordan.jpg"}],"tags":["prayer","call"],"orderNote":"Acts does not mention Arabia. Where it fits is debated: most place it within the \"many days\" of Acts 9:23, before the escape over the wall; a few put it after verse 19. \"After three years\" (Galatians 1:18) may count from the conversion, in the ancient inclusive way, so the stay may have been short or long."},
  {"id":"basket","harmony":"Acts 9:23–25","lane":"paul","place":"damascus","from":"arabia","date":"c. AD 37","title":"Saul Escapes Over the Wall of Damascus","people":["Saul","His disciples","The governor of King Aretas"],"gospels":{"Acts":"9:23–25","2Cor":"11:32–33","Gal":"1:17"},"summary":"Back in Damascus, Saul's preaching stirs a plot to kill him. The gates are watched day and night. By night his disciples lower him over the city wall in a basket, and he escapes. Paul remembers this humbling exit years later, when he lists the things he can boast of: his weakness.","journey":"Back north to Damascus from Arabia (see the previous leg).","route":[[35.8,30.6],[35.62,30.85],[35.7,31.18],[35.79,31.72],[35.93,31.95],[36.1,32.3],[36.48,32.52],[36.3,33.1],[36.306,33.511]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 9:24–25","text":"but their plot became known to Saul. They were watching the gates day and night, to kill him; but his disciples took him by night and let him down over the wall, lowering him in a basket."},{"ref":"2 Corinthians 11:32–33","text":"At Damascus, the governor under King Aretas guarded the city of Damascus in order to seize me, but I was let down in a basket through a window in the wall, and escaped his hands."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Joshua 2:15","note":"Rahab lets the spies down by a rope through her window in the wall of Jericho."},{"ref":"1 Samuel 19:12","note":"Michal lets David down through a window, and he flees from Saul, his namesake king."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"Bab Kisan and the Chapel of St Paul, Damascus","artist":"Photo by Bassel Khabbaz","year":"Photo 2016","medium":"Photograph","location":"Bab Kisan, southeastern wall of the Old City, Damascus","description":"A squat stone tower with two Chi-Rho medallions on its face, built into the old Bab Kisan, a gate in the southeastern wall of Damascus, with an iron gate and steps leading to the chapel inside.","significance":"Christians have long shown this stretch of wall as the place where Paul was let down in a basket. The gate was rebuilt in the Middle Ages and turned into the Chapel of St Paul in the twentieth century.","symbolism":"The Chi-Rho, the first two Greek letters of \"Christ,\" marks the gate the persecutor-turned-apostle escaped through.","impact":"One of the main Christian pilgrim stops in Damascus, with Straight Street and the House of Ananias.","img":"img/basket0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Bassel Khabbaz, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bab_Kisan_-_Chapel_of_St._Paul.jpg"},{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"St Paul's Wall, Damascus","artist":"Sketch signed \"A H\", lithographed by Vincent Brooks","year":"1861","medium":"Tinted lithograph (book illustration)","location":"British Library (scanned book, Mechanical Curator collection)","description":"Round towers and patched stone curtain walls of Damascus stand above a dusty track, with trees and bushes at their foot, as a British traveler drew them in 1861.","significance":"Nineteenth-century travelers were shown this part of the wall as \"St Paul's Wall,\" where he was lowered in a basket.","symbolism":"The high blank wall with its few windows makes plain how Paul escaped: \"through a window in the wall\" (2 Corinthians 11:33).","impact":"Such travel views shaped how Victorian readers pictured the places of the Bible.","img":"img/basket1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HARVEY(1861)_p131_DAMASCUS,_CITY_WALLS_(OR)_ST._PAUL'S_WALL.jpg"}],"tags":["conflict"],"orderNote":"Acts blames the Jews of Damascus; Paul names the ethnarch of King Aretas IV of Nabataea, who died about AD 40. Both may have watched for him, the Jews in the city and the king's men at the gates. Aretas's role helps date the escape to before AD 40."},
  {"id":"saulinjerusalem","harmony":"Acts 9:26–29","lane":"paul","place":"jerusalemchurch","from":"damascus","date":"c. AD 37","title":"Saul's First Visit to Jerusalem","people":["Saul","Barnabas","Peter (Cephas)","James the Lord's brother","The Hellenists"],"gospels":{"Acts":"9:26–29","Gal":"1:18–20"},"summary":"Saul comes to Jerusalem and tries to join the disciples, but they are all afraid of him. Barnabas takes him to the apostles and tells how he saw the Lord and preached boldly in Damascus. Paul says he came to see Peter and stayed with him fifteen days, seeing only James the Lord's brother of the other leaders. He preaches boldly and argues with the Greek-speaking Jews, the same circle that had turned on Stephen, and they plot to kill him.","stay":"Fifteen days with Peter (Galatians 1:18).","journey":"About 150 miles back south from Damascus over the Golan, down the Jordan Valley and up through Samaria, about a week on foot.","route":[[36.306,33.511],[36.18,33.42],[35.82,33.13],[35.63,33.01],[35.57,32.88],[35.55,32.7],[35.5,32.5],[35.3,32.46],[35.28,32.21],[35.25,32.07],[35.22,31.93],[35.231,31.776]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 9:26–27","text":"And when he had come to Jerusalem he attempted to join the disciples; and they were all afraid of him, for they did not believe that he was a disciple. But Barnabas took him, and brought him to the apostles, and declared to them how on the road he had seen the Lord, who spoke to him, and how at Damascus he had preached boldly in the name of Jesus."},{"ref":"Galatians 1:18–19","text":"Then after three years I went up to Jerusalem to visit Cephas, and remained with him fifteen days. But I saw none of the other apostles except James the Lord's brother."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"Barnabas Brings Paul to the Apostles","artist":"Cornelis Bos (monogram CB)","year":"1546","medium":"Engraving","location":"Rijksmuseum, Amsterdam","description":"In a columned hall Barnabas, his arm around Saul, presents him to the wary apostles; Peter, with a staff, points as if questioning. Through the open door, in the distance, a tiny figure is let down in a basket from the wall of Damascus.","significance":"Plate 7 of a ten-print series on the life of Paul, made in Antwerp in 1546.","symbolism":"The background shows what Barnabas is telling: how Saul had preached and escaped at Damascus. Barnabas's embrace is his whole role here: \"Barnabas took him.\"","impact":"Small Netherlandish print series like this spread Bible scenes, including rarely painted ones, across sixteenth-century Europe.","img":"img/saulinjerusalem0.jpg","credit":"Public domain (CC0), Rijksmuseum, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Barnabas_brengt_Paulus_naar_de_apostelen_Het_leven_van_Paulus_(serietitel),_RP-P-1877-A-330.jpg"}],"tags":["call"],"orderNote":"Luke's \"the apostles\" and Paul's \"none of the other apostles except James\" are usually reconciled as Barnabas bringing him to the two leaders present. In Acts 22:17–21 Paul adds that while praying in the Temple on this visit he saw the Lord, who told him to leave Jerusalem and go far away to the Gentiles."},
  {"id":"tarsus","harmony":"Acts 9:30–31","lane":"paul","place":"tarsus","from":"jerusalemchurch","date":"c. AD 37–43","title":"Saul Sent Home to Tarsus","people":["Saul","The brethren of Jerusalem"],"gospels":{"Acts":"9:30–31","Gal":"1:21–24"},"summary":"When the believers learn of the plot, they take Saul down to Caesarea and send him off to Tarsus, his home city. There he stays for several years, \"in the regions of Syria and Cilicia,\" in what are often called his hidden years. The churches of Judea know him only by report: the persecutor now preaches the faith he tried to destroy. Meanwhile the church in Judea, Galilee and Samaria has peace and grows.","stay":"Some six or seven years in Tarsus and the regions of Syria and Cilicia, until Barnabas comes to look for him (Acts 11:25).","journey":"Down to the coast at Caesarea, about 65 miles, then by ship about 350 miles north along the coast of Phoenicia and Syria and across the mouth of the Gulf of Issus to Tarsus, a week or more by sea.","route":[[35.231,31.776],[35.04,31.9],[34.895,31.951],[34.93,32.1],[34.89,32.5],[34.9,32.85],[35.05,33.1],[35.15,33.3],[35.45,34.0],[35.75,34.5],[35.65,35.55],[35.7,35.9],[35.45,36.4],[35.0,36.78],[34.895,36.918]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 9:30–31","text":"And when the brethren knew it, they brought him down to Caesarea, and sent him off to Tarsus. So the church throughout all Judea and Galilee and Samaria had peace and was built up; and walking in the fear of the Lord and in the comfort of the Holy Spirit it was multiplied."},{"ref":"Galatians 1:21–23","text":"Then I went into the regions of Syria and Cilicia. And I was still not known by sight to the churches of Christ in Judea; they only heard it said, \"He who once persecuted us is now preaching the faith he once tried to destroy.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"Cleopatra's Gate, Tarsus","artist":"Photo by Nedim Ardoğa","year":"Photo 2011","medium":"Photograph","location":"Tarsus, Mersin Province, Turkey","description":"A massive arched gateway of cut stone, overgrown with grass on top, stands alone in a traffic circle among palm trees.","significance":"Called Cleopatra's Gate after the tradition that Cleopatra landed at Tarsus to meet Mark Antony in 41 BC. It is one of the few standing monuments of ancient Tarsus, though its present form may be late Roman or later.","symbolism":"A gate of the \"no mean city\" (Acts 21:39) that Paul called home, and from which he would set out again.","impact":"A landmark of modern Tarsus, alongside St Paul's Well and the excavated Roman street.","img":"img/tarsus0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Nedim Ardoğa, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cleopatra's_Gate,_Tarsus,_Mersin_Province.jpg"}],"tags":["call"],"orderNote":"Acts does not say whether he sailed or went by land; \"sent him off\" from the port of Caesarea suggests a ship. Galatians 1:21 names Syria before Cilicia, which some read as an overland journey. Some of the sufferings listed in 2 Corinthians 11:23–27 may belong to these years, of which nothing else is told."},
  {"id":"aeneas","harmony":"Acts 9:32–35","lane":"apostles","place":"lydda","from":"jerusalemchurch","date":"c. AD 38–40","title":"Peter Heals Aeneas at Lydda","people":["Peter","Aeneas","The saints at Lydda"],"gospels":{"Acts":"9:32–35"},"summary":"In the time of peace, Peter travels around the churches and comes down to the believers at Lydda. There he finds Aeneas, paralyzed and bedridden for eight years. \"Aeneas, Jesus Christ heals you,\" Peter says, and he gets up at once. All of Lydda and the plain of Sharon see him and turn to the Lord.","journey":"About 25 miles northwest from Jerusalem, down through the Beth-horon pass to the coastal plain, a day or two on foot.","route":[[35.231,31.776],[35.17,31.87],[35.1,31.88],[35.0,31.92],[34.895,31.951]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 9:33–34","text":"There he found a man named Aeneas, who had been bedridden for eight years and was paralyzed. And Peter said to him, \"Aeneas, Jesus Christ heals you; rise and make your bed.\" And immediately he rose."},{"ref":"Acts 9:35","text":"And all the residents of Lydda and Sharon saw him, and they turned to the Lord."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"The Healing of Aeneas by Saint Peter","artist":"Philips Galle, after Maarten van Heemskerck","year":"16th century","medium":"Engraving","location":"National Gallery of Art, Washington","description":"In a Dutch household, with a well, a hearth and pots on the shelf, Peter speaks to the bedridden Aeneas, who swings his legs out of the canopied bed. The Latin verses below retell Acts 9:33–34: \"Rise, in Christ's name.\"","significance":"From a series of prints of the Acts of the Apostles designed by Heemskerck and engraved by Galle in Haarlem and Antwerp; this impression bears the address of the publisher Claes Jansz. Visscher.","symbolism":"Peter holds the keys; the busy, ordinary room sets the miracle in daily life, as Acts does.","impact":"Such print series carried the Acts scenes into Protestant and Catholic homes alike across northern Europe.","img":"img/aeneas0.jpg","credit":"Public domain (CC0), National Gallery of Art, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Philip_Galle_after_Maerten_van_Heemskerck,_The_Healing_of_Eneas_by_Saint_Peter,_NGA_54317.jpg"}],"tags":["miracle","healing"],"orderNote":"Peter's words echo Jesus' healing of the paralytic at Capernaum (Mark 2:11), but Peter makes plain who heals: \"Jesus Christ heals you.\" The date is not given; Acts places it in the peace that followed Saul's conversion."},
  {"id":"tabitha","harmony":"Acts 9:36–43","lane":"apostles","place":"joppa","from":"lydda","date":"c. AD 38–40","title":"Peter Raises Tabitha at Joppa","people":["Peter","Tabitha (Dorcas)","The widows of Joppa","Simon the tanner"],"gospels":{"Acts":"9:36–43"},"summary":"At Joppa a disciple named Tabitha, in Greek Dorcas (\"Gazelle\"), known for her good works and charity, falls sick and dies. The believers send for Peter at Lydda. The widows show him the coats and garments she made for them. Peter sends them all out, kneels and prays, and says, \"Tabitha, rise.\" She opens her eyes and sits up, and many in Joppa believe.","stay":"\"Many days\" in Joppa with Simon, a tanner, whose house was by the sea.","journey":"About 11 miles northwest across the plain from Lydda to the coast, half a day's walk.","route":[[34.895,31.951],[34.82,32.0],[34.752,32.054]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 9:39","text":"So Peter rose and went with them. And when he had come, they took him to the upper room. All the widows stood beside him weeping, and showing coats and garments which Dorcas made while she was with them."},{"ref":"Acts 9:40","text":"But Peter put them all outside and knelt down and prayed; then turning to the body he said, \"Tabitha, rise.\" And she opened her eyes, and when she saw Peter she sat up."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"2 Kings 4:32–37","note":"Elisha shuts the door, prays, and the Shunammite's son is raised in the upper room."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"Healing of the Cripple and Raising of Tabitha","artist":"Masolino da Panicale","year":"c. 1424–1425","medium":"Fresco","location":"Brancacci Chapel, Santa Maria del Carmine, Florence","description":"Two of Peter's miracles share one Florentine square. At left Peter and John heal the lame beggar at the Temple gate (Acts 3); at right Peter, in a loggia, raises Tabitha, who sits up on her bier among astonished onlookers. Two young dandies stroll between the scenes.","significance":"Part of the Brancacci Chapel cycle of St Peter's life, begun by Masolino and Masaccio and finished by Filippino Lippi; this fresco is usually given to Masolino, perhaps with Masaccio's help on the buildings.","symbolism":"The perspective street of fifteenth-century Florence makes the apostle's miracles happen in the painter's own city, among its citizens.","impact":"The chapel was a school for later Florentine painters, Michelangelo among them, who came to study its figures and space.","img":"img/tabitha0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cappella_brancacci,_Guarigione_dello_storpio_e_resurrezione_di_Tabita_(restaurato),_Masolino.jpg"}],"tags":["miracle","raising"],"orderNote":"Peter does as he saw Jesus do with Jairus' daughter: he puts the mourners out, and his words \"Tabitha, rise\" (Tabitha koum) are one letter from Jesus' \"Talitha cumi\" (Mark 5:40–41). Staying with a tanner, whose trade made him ritually unclean in strict eyes, already hints at what comes next."},
  {"id":"petervision","harmony":"Acts 10:1–23","lane":"apostles","place":"joppa","date":"c. AD 38–40","title":"Cornelius's Vision and Peter's Vision at Joppa","people":["Peter","Cornelius the centurion","An angel","Cornelius's messengers"],"gospels":{"Acts":"10:1–23"},"summary":"At Caesarea a God-fearing Roman centurion, Cornelius, is told by an angel to send for Simon Peter at Joppa. The next day at noon, praying on the roof of the tanner's house, Peter falls into a trance. He sees a great sheet let down from heaven full of animals clean and unclean, and hears, \"Rise, Peter; kill and eat.\" When he refuses, the voice says, \"What God has cleansed, you must not call common.\" While he is puzzling over it, Cornelius's men arrive, and the Spirit tells him to go with them.","stay":"On the housetop of Simon the tanner's house by the sea.","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 10:11–13","text":"and saw the heaven opened, and something descending, like a great sheet, let down by four corners upon the earth. In it were all kinds of animals and reptiles and birds of the air. And there came a voice to him, \"Rise, Peter; kill and eat.\""},{"ref":"Acts 10:14–15","text":"But Peter said, \"No, Lord; for I have never eaten anything that is common or unclean.\" And the voice came to him again a second time, \"What God has cleansed, you must not call common.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Leviticus 11","note":"The law of clean and unclean animals that Peter had kept all his life."},{"ref":"Jonah 1:3","note":"From this same port Jonah fled rather than preach to Gentile Nineveh; Peter, \"Simon son of Jonah\" (Matthew 16:17), is sent to the Gentiles."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"Saint Peter's Vision of the Sheet with Animals","artist":"Domenico Fetti","year":"c. 1619","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Kunsthistorisches Museum, Vienna","description":"Peter, old and heavy-limbed, has fallen asleep on the flat roof with his head on his hand and his book open beside him. Above him, in a corner of the sky, a figure lowers the great sheet full of animals: a calf, a sheep, a rabbit, birds.","significance":"Fetti, court painter to the Gonzaga in Mantua, painted the vision as a dream, keeping the heavenly sheet small and the sleeping apostle large and human.","symbolism":"The housetop's balustrade and the distant landscape set the scene \"on the housetop\" at Joppa; the clean and unclean beasts tumble together in one cloth.","impact":"A rare painting of a rarely painted vision, by an artist whose free, warm brushwork was prized by collectors in his own day.","img":"img/petervision0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Domenico_Fetti_-_Peter's_vision_of_a_sheet_with_animals_-_Kunsthistorisches_Museum_Wien.jpg"},{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"The Vision of Cornelius the Centurion","artist":"Gerbrand van den Eeckhout","year":"1664","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Walters Art Museum, Baltimore","description":"Cornelius, in armor, kneels in a dim room with his hands crossed on his breast as a winged angel in white appears on a cloud and points onward, telling him to send to Joppa. His staff of command lies on the floor.","significance":"By one of Rembrandt's closest pupils and friends, painted in his teacher's warm, dark manner.","symbolism":"The baton laid down shows the officer humbled before God; the angel's outstretched hand points the way to Peter, as the angel in Acts sends him.","impact":"Cornelius's vision was a favorite subject in the Dutch Republic, where it spoke of God's welcome to every nation.","img":"img/petervision1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gerbrand_van_den_Eeckhout_-_Vision_of_Cornelius_the_Centurion_-_Walters_372492.jpg"}],"tags":["prayer","call"],"orderNote":"The vision is about food, but Peter reads it about people: \"God has shown me that I should not call any man common or unclean\" (Acts 10:28). Acts tells it twice more (11:5–10, and in summary at 15:7–9)."},
  {"id":"cornelius","harmony":"Acts 10:23b–48","lane":"apostles","place":"caesarea","from":"joppa","date":"c. AD 38–40","title":"Peter at the House of Cornelius","people":["Peter","Cornelius","Cornelius's kinsmen and friends","Six brethren from Joppa"],"gospels":{"Acts":"10:23–48"},"summary":"Peter goes up the coast to Caesarea with six brethren from Joppa. Cornelius has gathered his household and friends; he falls at Peter's feet, and Peter raises him: \"I too am a man.\" Peter, a Jew in a Gentile house, declares that God shows no partiality and tells the story of Jesus. While he is still speaking the Holy Spirit falls on all who hear, and they speak in tongues as at Pentecost. Peter has them baptized, the first Gentiles received into the Church.","stay":"Cornelius asks him to remain \"for some days.\"","journey":"About 35 miles north along the coast road through the plain of Sharon, a two-day walk (they arrive \"on the following day\").","route":[[34.752,32.054],[34.8,32.15],[34.84,32.28],[34.87,32.4],[34.89,32.5]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 10:34–35","text":"And Peter opened his mouth and said: \"Truly I perceive that God shows no partiality, but in every nation any one who fears him and does what is right is acceptable to him."},{"ref":"Acts 10:44–45","text":"While Peter was still saying this, the Holy Spirit fell on all who heard the word. And the believers from among the circumcised who came with Peter were amazed, because the gift of the Holy Spirit had been poured out even on the Gentiles."},{"ref":"Acts 10:47","text":"\"Can any one forbid water for baptizing these people who have received the Holy Spirit just as we have?\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Deuteronomy 10:17","note":"The Lord \"is not partial and takes no bribe\": Peter's \"God shows no partiality.\""},{"ref":"Isaiah 56:6–7","note":"Foreigners who join themselves to the Lord: \"my house shall be called a house of prayer for all peoples.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"Saint Peter Baptizing the Centurion Cornelius","artist":"Alberto Carlieri","year":"Early 18th century","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Walters Art Museum, Baltimore","description":"Among grand ruined arches and columns, Peter pours water over the head of the kneeling centurion, whose armor lies at his feet. His household, women and children, press forward to be baptized next.","significance":"Carlieri, a Roman painter of architectural fantasies, set the first baptism of Gentiles among classical ruins, a reminder that the Roman world itself was entering the Church.","symbolism":"The discarded armor marks the soldier's new allegiance; the waiting family recalls that \"all his household\" feared God (Acts 10:2).","impact":"A typical Roman Baroque treatment of a scene that the Church has read as the opening of the Gospel to the nations.","img":"img/cornelius0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alberto_Carlieri_-_St_Peter_Baptizing_the_Centurion,_Cornelius_-_Walters_37503.jpg"}],"tags":["sermon","miracle","call"],"orderNote":"Peter's sermon here is a short outline of the Gospel, from John's baptism through Galilee to the cross and the Resurrection; many think Mark's Gospel follows the same shape. The \"Italian Cohort\" is known from inscriptions in Syria later in the century."},
  {"id":"peterreport","harmony":"Acts 11:1–18","lane":"apostles","place":"jerusalemchurch","from":"caesarea","date":"c. AD 38–40","title":"Peter Explains Himself in Jerusalem","people":["Peter","The apostles and brethren","The circumcision party"],"gospels":{"Acts":"11:1–18"},"summary":"News reaches Judea that Gentiles have received the word of God. When Peter comes up to Jerusalem, believers who insist on circumcision criticize him for eating with uncircumcised men. Peter tells them everything in order: the vision, the messengers, and the Spirit falling on Cornelius's household as on the apostles at the beginning. \"Who was I that I could withstand God?\" They fall silent and then praise God.","journey":"About 65 miles from Caesarea to Jerusalem, by Antipatris and the climb up from Lydda, three days on foot.","route":[[34.89,32.5],[34.92,32.3],[34.93,32.1],[34.895,31.951],[35.04,31.9],[35.17,31.87],[35.231,31.776]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 11:2–3","text":"So when Peter went up to Jerusalem, the circumcision party criticized him, saying, \"Why did you go to uncircumcised men and eat with them?\""},{"ref":"Acts 11:17–18","text":"If then God gave the same gift to them as he gave to us when we believed in the Lord Jesus Christ, who was I that I could withstand God?\" When they heard this they were silenced. And they glorified God, saying, \"Then to the Gentiles also God has granted repentance unto life.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"Peter's Vision of the Unclean Animals","artist":"After Jan van der Straet (Stradanus); published by Claes Jansz. Visscher","year":"1643","medium":"Engraving","location":"Rijksmuseum, Amsterdam","description":"A busy cutaway of Simon the tanner's house at Joppa: tanners scrape hides in the vaulted workshop below; Peter prays on the roof; at right he dozes by a table while the cook brings food, and through the arch the great sheet of animals descends from heaven as Cornelius's messengers approach the town.","significance":"Plate 21 of a Dutch picture Bible series of the Acts; the Latin verses tell the whole story through to the baptisms at Caesarea, the story Peter retells in Jerusalem.","symbolism":"The hanging hides and the tanner's trade show what Peter's hosts thought unclean; the vision comes to him in the middle of it.","impact":"Such dense, story-telling prints let readers follow every detail of Peter's account in Acts 11:5–12.","img":"img/peterreport0.jpg","credit":"Public domain (CC0), Rijksmuseum, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Petrus'_visioen_van_de_onreine_dieren_Acta_Apostolorum_(serietitel)_Handelingen_van_de_apostelen_(serietitel)_Theatrum_Biblicum_Hoc_Est_Historiae_Sacrae_Veteris_et_Novi_Testamenti_Tabulis_Aeneis_Expressae_(serietitel),_RP-P-1976-30-411.jpg"}],"tags":["teaching","conflict"],"orderNote":"The question is settled here for Cornelius, but it returns in full at the Council of Jerusalem (Acts 15), where Peter again points to this day."},
  {"id":"antiochchurch","harmony":"Acts 11:19–24","lane":"apostles","place":"antioch","from":"jerusalemchurch","date":"c. AD 40–43","title":"The Church at Antioch; Barnabas Sent","people":["Believers from Cyprus and Cyrene","Barnabas","The Greeks of Antioch"],"gospels":{"Acts":"11:19–24"},"summary":"Some of those scattered after Stephen's death travel as far as Phoenicia, Cyprus and Antioch, at first speaking only to Jews. But men from Cyprus and Cyrene, coming to Antioch, preach the Lord Jesus to Greeks as well, and a great number believe. The Jerusalem church sends Barnabas, himself a Cypriot. He sees the grace of God, rejoices, and encourages them all, and many more are added.","journey":"Barnabas's journey from Jerusalem, about 350 miles north, probably by the coast road through Caesarea, Tyre, Sidon and Laodicea, about three weeks on foot.","route":[[35.231,31.776],[35.04,31.9],[34.93,32.1],[34.89,32.5],[35.07,32.92],[35.19,33.27],[35.37,33.56],[35.5,33.89],[35.84,34.43],[35.88,34.89],[35.78,35.52],[35.95,35.85],[36.161,36.202]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 11:20–21","text":"But there were some of them, men of Cyprus and Cyrene, who on coming to Antioch spoke to the Greeks also, preaching the Lord Jesus. And the hand of the Lord was with them, and a great number that believed turned to the Lord."},{"ref":"Acts 11:23–24","text":"When he came and saw the grace of God, he was glad; and he exhorted them all to remain faithful to the Lord with steadfast purpose; for he was a good man, full of the Holy Spirit and of faith. And a large company was added to the Lord."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"The Antioch \"Chalice\"","artist":"Unknown Byzantine silversmith; photo by Marie-Lan Nguyen","year":"c. 500–550","medium":"Silver, silver-gilt","location":"The Cloisters, Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York","description":"A silver cup set in an openwork gilded holder of grapevines, with birds and animals among the leaves and seated figures, often identified as Christ and apostles.","significance":"Said to have been found near Antioch in 1910, it was once hailed as the Holy Grail. The Metropolitan Museum now dates it to about 500–550 and thinks it was probably a standing lamp.","symbolism":"The vine recalls Christ's \"I am the vine, you are the branches\" (John 15:5); its figures may show Christ among his apostles.","impact":"One of the most famous early Christian silver objects, a witness to the wealth and faith of Antioch, one of the great sees of the early Church.","img":"img/antiochchurch0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Marie-Lan Nguyen, CC BY 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antioch_chalice_Met_50.4.jpg"}],"tags":["sermon"],"orderNote":"Some manuscripts read \"Hellenists\" (Greek-speaking Jews) for \"Greeks\" in 11:20, but the contrast with \"none except Jews\" in verse 19 shows Gentiles are meant. Acts does not say whether this came before or after Cornelius; it picks up the thread from 8:4. Antioch later counts St Peter as its first bishop, and the Antioch tradition gives Luke as a native of the city."},
  {"id":"barnabassaul","harmony":"Acts 11:25–26","lane":"paul","place":"antioch","from":"tarsus","date":"c. AD 43–44","title":"Barnabas Brings Saul to Antioch; \"Christians\"","people":["Barnabas","Saul","The church at Antioch"],"gospels":{"Acts":"11:25–26"},"summary":"With the work at Antioch growing, Barnabas goes to Tarsus to look for Saul and brings him back. For a whole year the two meet with the church and teach a large company of people. In Antioch the disciples are first called \"Christians,\" Christ's people. The city becomes the base from which Paul will set out on all his journeys.","stay":"\"For a whole year\" at Antioch.","journey":"Barnabas goes to Tarsus and back: about 140 miles each way, east across the Cilician plain past Adana, round the head of the Gulf of Issus and through the Syrian Gates (the Belen Pass) over the Amanus range, about a week on foot each way.","route":[[34.895,36.918],[35.32,37.0],[35.62,36.96],[35.95,36.95],[36.2,36.84],[36.17,36.59],[36.22,36.49],[36.161,36.202]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 11:25–26","text":"So Barnabas went to Tarsus to look for Saul; and when he had found him, he brought him to Antioch. For a whole year they met with the church, and taught a large company of people; and in Antioch the disciples were for the first time called Christians."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"The Orontes at Antioch (Antakya)","artist":"Photo by Carole Raddato","year":"Photo 2017","medium":"Photograph","location":"Antakya, Hatay, Turkey","description":"The Orontes River, low in summer, runs between embanked shores through the middle of modern Antakya, under a footbridge, with tree-lined parks along its bank.","significance":"Ancient Antioch spread along the left bank of the Orontes at the foot of Mount Silpius, with an island quarter in the river. Little of the Roman city is visible today; most of it lies deep under the modern town, which was badly damaged by the earthquakes of February 2023.","symbolism":"The river joined the inland capital to the sea at Seleucia, the road by which Barnabas and Saul would later sail out on their first journey (Acts 13:4).","impact":"Antioch remained one of the great centers of the Church, one of the ancient patriarchates, whose Greek Orthodox and other patriarchs still bear its name.","img":"img/barnabassaul0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Carole Raddato, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antioch_on_the_Orontes,_Antakya,_Turkey_(35863345530).jpg"}],"tags":["teaching","call"],"orderNote":"\"Christians\" (Christianoi) is a Latin-style party name, like \"Herodians\" (Mark 3:6), probably first given by outsiders; it appears only three times in the New Testament (also Acts 26:28 and 1 Peter 4:16). The word for \"look for\" suggests Barnabas had to search for him."},
  {"id":"faminerelief","harmony":"Acts 11:27–30","lane":"paul","place":"jerusalemchurch","from":"antioch","date":"c. AD 44–46","title":"Agabus Foretells a Famine; Relief for Judea","people":["Agabus","Prophets from Jerusalem","Barnabas","Saul","The elders in Judea"],"gospels":{"Acts":"11:27–30","Gal":"2:1–10"},"summary":"Prophets come down from Jerusalem to Antioch, and one of them, Agabus, foretells by the Spirit a great famine over all the world. It comes in the reign of the emperor Claudius. The disciples at Antioch, each as he is able, send relief to the believers in Judea. They send it to the elders by the hands of Barnabas and Saul: the Gentile church's first gift to the mother church.","journey":"About 350 miles south from Antioch to Jerusalem by the coast road, about three weeks on foot.","route":[[36.161,36.202],[35.95,35.85],[35.78,35.52],[35.88,34.89],[35.84,34.43],[35.5,33.89],[35.37,33.56],[35.19,33.27],[35.07,32.92],[34.89,32.5],[34.93,32.1],[35.04,31.9],[35.231,31.776]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 11:28","text":"And one of them named Agabus stood up and foretold by the Spirit that there would be a great famine over all the world; and this took place in the days of Claudius."},{"ref":"Acts 11:29–30","text":"And the disciples determined, every one according to his ability, to send relief to the brethren who lived in Judea; and they did so, sending it to the elders by the hand of Barnabas and Saul."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Genesis 41:54–57","note":"Joseph foretells the famine and stores grain, and all the earth comes to Egypt to buy."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"Bust of the Emperor Claudius","artist":"Roman sculptor; photo by Marie-Lan Nguyen","year":"AD 41–54","medium":"Marble","location":"National Archaeological Museum, Naples","description":"A marble bust of Claudius, crowned with an oak wreath, with a calm, lined face and a toga pinned at the shoulder.","significance":"Claudius (emperor AD 41–54) is the emperor named in Acts 11:28, in whose reign the famine came; he also expelled the Jews from Rome (Acts 18:2). He owed his throne in part to Herod Agrippa I.","symbolism":"The civic crown of oak leaves honored a ruler as savior of citizens; Luke dates God's word by Rome's rulers, as he does at Christ's birth (Luke 2:1).","impact":"Food shortages in his reign are recorded by Roman writers such as Suetonius and Tacitus.","img":"img/faminerelief0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Marie-Lan Nguyen, CC BY 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Claudius_MAN_Napoli_Inv6060.jpg"}],"tags":["prophecy"],"orderNote":"Roman writers record food shortages under Claudius (AD 41–54), and Josephus tells of a severe famine in Judea about AD 45–48, when Queen Helena of Adiabene bought grain in Egypt for Jerusalem. Acts 12, which ends with Herod's death in AD 44, is set \"about that time\"; the visit itself may fall a little later. Whether this famine visit is the visit of Galatians 2:1–10 (\"after fourteen years... with Barnabas\"), or that is the Council of Acts 15, is one of the most debated questions of Paul's chronology."},
  {"id":"jameskilled","harmony":"Acts 12:1–2","lane":"herod","place":"jerusalemchurch","date":"AD 44 (or 43)","title":"Herod Agrippa Kills James","people":["Herod Agrippa I","James son of Zebedee, brother of John"],"gospels":{"Acts":"12:1–2"},"summary":"Herod Agrippa I, grandson of Herod the Great, now king over all the old kingdom, lays violent hands on the church. He has James the brother of John, one of the three closest to Jesus, killed with the sword. James is the first of the Twelve to die a martyr, fulfilling Jesus' word that he would drink his cup. Seeing that it pleases the people, Herod arrests Peter too.","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 12:1–3","text":"About that time Herod the king laid violent hands upon some who belonged to the church. He killed James the brother of John with the sword; and when he saw that it pleased the Jews, he proceeded to arrest Peter also. This was during the days of Unleavened Bread."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"The Martyrdom of Saint James","artist":"Francisco de Zurbarán","year":"c. 1640","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Museo del Prado, Madrid","description":"James kneels with his hands joined on the block and turns his calm face toward us as the executioner, in a red shirt, grips his hair and raises the sword. A turbaned king, Herod, looks on with soldiers and bystanders; above, a cherub brings the martyr's palm and wreath of roses.","significance":"Zurbarán, the great painter of Seville's monasteries, gives the apostle-martyr the plain, still dignity of his saints and monks.","symbolism":"The sword is the instrument named in Acts 12:2 and James's emblem. The palm and crown are the martyr's victory; the hound at right is a common Spanish touch of everyday life.","impact":"James (Santiago) is the patron of Spain, and his martyrdom was a favorite subject of Spanish Golden Age painting.","img":"img/jameskilled0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Museo del Prado, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zurbarán_-_Martirio_de_Santiago,_Hacia_1640.jpg"},{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"Saint James Led to His Execution (study)","artist":"Andrea Mantegna","year":"c. 1453","medium":"Pen and brown ink on paper","location":"British Museum, London","description":"A rapid pen sketch: James, a tall figure in a mantle, is led along by a soldier in a crested helmet amid a crowd; at left, figures bend toward the ground, perhaps the man who knelt for the apostle's blessing on the way.","significance":"A study for Mantegna's fresco of St James led to execution in the Ovetari Chapel of the Eremitani church in Padua, destroyed by bombing in 1944; drawings and old photographs are all that remain of most of the cycle.","symbolism":"The legend behind the scene, told by Clement of Alexandria, is that the man who brought James to trial was converted by his witness and died with him.","impact":"The Ovetari frescoes made the young Mantegna famous; their loss in the Second World War was one of the great artistic casualties of the war.","img":"img/jameskilled1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1453_Mantegna_Der_Heilige_Jakobus_wird_zur_Hinrichtung_geführt_Britisch_Museum_anagoria.jpg"}],"tags":["passion","prophecy"],"orderNote":"Jesus foretold it: \"The cup that I drink you will drink\" (Mark 10:39). Clement of Alexandria, quoted by Eusebius, tells that his accuser was moved by his witness, confessed himself a Christian and was beheaded with him. The tradition that James preached in Spain and lies at Santiago de Compostela is medieval and has no support in Acts."},
  {"id":"peterfreed","harmony":"Acts 12:3–19a","lane":"apostles","place":"jerusalemchurch","date":"Passover, AD 44 (or 43)","title":"An Angel Frees Peter from Prison","people":["Peter","An angel of the Lord","Mary the mother of John Mark","Rhoda","Herod's soldiers"],"gospels":{"Acts":"12:3–19"},"summary":"Herod jails Peter during the Passover feast, under four squads of soldiers, meaning to put him on show afterward, while the church prays earnestly for him. On the last night, as Peter sleeps chained between two soldiers, an angel wakes him; the chains fall off, and they pass the guards and the iron gate, which opens by itself. Peter goes to the house of Mary, the mother of John Mark, where many are praying. The maid Rhoda is so glad to hear his voice that she runs in without opening the gate. Peter tells his story, asks them to tell James, and goes \"to another place.\"","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 12:5","text":"So Peter was kept in prison; but earnest prayer for him was made to God by the church."},{"ref":"Acts 12:7","text":"and behold, an angel of the Lord appeared, and a light shone in the cell; and he struck Peter on the side and woke him, saying, \"Get up quickly.\" And the chains fell off his hands."},{"ref":"Acts 12:14–15","text":"Recognizing Peter's voice, in her joy she did not open the gate but ran in and told that Peter was standing at the gate. They said to her, \"You are mad.\" But she insisted that it was so. They said, \"It is his angel!\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Exodus 12:11","note":"At Passover the angel's command echoes Israel's night of deliverance: belt fastened, sandals on, ready to go."},{"ref":"Psalm 34:7","note":"\"The angel of the Lord encamps around those who fear him, and delivers them.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"The Deliverance of Saint Peter","artist":"Raphael","year":"1514","medium":"Fresco","location":"Stanza di Eliodoro, Apostolic Palace, Vatican City","description":"Three moments over a window. In the center, behind bars, a radiant angel wakes Peter, who sleeps chained between two armored soldiers. At right the angel leads the dazed Peter out past sleeping guards. At left the soldiers wake in the moonlight and torchlight and raise the alarm.","significance":"Painted for Pope Julius II, whose titular church was San Pietro in Vincoli (St Peter in Chains), and completed under Leo X; it also alludes to the papacy delivered from its enemies.","symbolism":"Raphael plays four lights against each other: the angel's glow, the moon, a torch and the dawn, and the heavenly light outshines them all.","impact":"Admired as one of the first great night scenes in Western painting.","img":"img/peterfreed0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raphael_-_Deliverance_of_Saint_Peter.jpg"}],"tags":["miracle","prayer"],"orderNote":"Acts does not say where Peter was held; the Antonia Fortress or Herod's palace prison are guesses. The Syriac Orthodox church of St Mark in the Armenian Quarter claims the site of Mary's house. Where Peter went is unknown; he is back in Jerusalem for the Council (Acts 15:7). The chains of St Peter are honored in Rome at San Pietro in Vincoli, and the Church keeps the feast on 1 August."},
  {"id":"agrippadeath","harmony":"Acts 12:19b–23","lane":"herod","place":"caesarea","from":"jerusalemchurch","date":"AD 44","title":"The Death of Herod Agrippa I at Caesarea","people":["Herod Agrippa I","Blastus the chamberlain","Envoys of Tyre and Sidon"],"gospels":{"Acts":"12:19–23"},"summary":"Herod has the guards put to death and goes down to Caesarea. Envoys from Tyre and Sidon, which depend on his land for food, come to make peace. On an appointed day he puts on his royal robes, sits on his throne and makes a speech, and the people shout, \"The voice of a god, and not of man!\" Because he does not give God the glory, an angel of the Lord strikes him; he is eaten by worms and dies. \"But the word of God grew and multiplied.\"","journey":"About 65 miles from Jerusalem down to Caesarea on the coast, by Lydda and Antipatris.","route":[[35.231,31.776],[35.17,31.87],[35.04,31.9],[34.895,31.951],[34.93,32.1],[34.92,32.3],[34.89,32.5]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 12:21–23","text":"On an appointed day Herod put on his royal robes, took his seat upon the throne, and made an oration to them. And the people shouted, \"The voice of a god, and not of man!\" Immediately an angel of the Lord smote him, because he did not give God the glory; and he was eaten by worms and died."},{"ref":"Acts 12:24","text":"But the word of God grew and multiplied."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Ezekiel 28:2","note":"The prince of Tyre says \"I am a god\" and is told \"you are but a man, and no god.\""},{"ref":"2 Maccabees 9:9","note":"The persecutor Antiochus IV, struck by God, dies with worms swarming in his body."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"Bronze Prutah of Herod Agrippa I","artist":"Mint of Jerusalem; photo by Classical Numismatic Group","year":"AD 41/42","medium":"Bronze coin","location":"Private collection (sold by CNG)","description":"Two sides of a small bronze coin: on one, a royal canopy (parasol) with fringes and the Greek legend BASILEOS AGRIPPA, \"of King Agrippa\"; on the other, three ears of barley and the date, year 6 of his reign.","significance":"Agrippa I struck these small coins in Jerusalem in AD 41/42, after Claudius gave him all of Judea. They carry no portrait, out of respect for Jewish law, unlike the coins he issued for his non-Jewish lands.","symbolism":"The canopy is a sign of kingship; the barley speaks of the land's harvest, the same food supply Tyre and Sidon depended on (Acts 12:20).","impact":"One of the most common coins of ancient Judea, still found often in Jerusalem.","img":"img/agrippadeath0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Classical Numismatic Group, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Agrippa_I_prutah.jpg"},{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"The Death of Herod Agrippa","artist":"Jan Luyken","year":"1703","medium":"Etching","location":"Rijksmuseum, Amsterdam","description":"Herod, in royal robes under a canopy on a high tribunal, addresses a vast crowd who raise their arms and acclaim him. High in the sky at upper left, an angel swoops down to strike him.","significance":"One of hundreds of Bible etchings by Jan Luyken, the Amsterdam poet and printmaker, for a picture Bible published by Pieter Mortier.","symbolism":"The crowd's lifted hands answer the king, not God; the small angel in the sky is the real power in the scene.","impact":"Luyken's prints, clear and dramatic, illustrated Dutch Bibles for generations.","img":"img/agrippadeath1.jpg","credit":"Public domain (CC0), Rijksmuseum, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dood_van_Herodes_Agrippa,_RP-P-OB-45.127.jpg"}],"tags":["conflict"],"orderNote":"Josephus tells the same story (Antiquities 19.343–350): at games in Caesarea for the emperor, Agrippa came into the theater at dawn in a robe woven of silver, flatterers hailed him as a god, he saw an owl as an omen of doom, was seized with violent pains in the belly and died five days later, aged 54. It fixes the date at AD 44."},
  {"id":"barnabasreturn","harmony":"Acts 12:25","lane":"paul","place":"antioch","from":"jerusalemchurch","date":"c. AD 46–47","title":"Barnabas and Saul Return to Antioch with John Mark","people":["Barnabas","Saul","John Mark"],"gospels":{"Acts":"12:25"},"summary":"Their mission to Jerusalem done, Barnabas and Saul return to Antioch. They bring with them John Mark, Barnabas's cousin (Colossians 4:10), the son of the Mary in whose house the church prayed for Peter. Mark will go with them on the first missionary journey, and tradition holds he later wrote the Gospel of Mark from Peter's preaching. The scene is set for the church of Antioch to send them out.","journey":"About 350 miles north again by the coast road, about three weeks on foot.","route":[[35.231,31.776],[35.04,31.9],[34.93,32.1],[34.89,32.5],[35.07,32.92],[35.19,33.27],[35.37,33.56],[35.5,33.89],[35.84,34.43],[35.88,34.89],[35.78,35.52],[35.95,35.85],[36.161,36.202]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 12:25","text":"And Barnabas and Saul returned from Jerusalem when they had fulfilled their mission, bringing with them John whose other name was Mark."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"Saint Mark the Evangelist","artist":"Andrea Mantegna","year":"c. 1448–1451","medium":"Tempera on canvas","location":"Städel Museum, Frankfurt","description":"The evangelist looks out from an arched stone window framed with a garland of fruit, his finger raised to his cheek in thought, a closed red book resting on the sill beside him. A painted label below bears a Latin inscription naming the evangelist and the painter.","significance":"Usually counted among Mantegna's earliest works, painted in Padua around 1450. The illusion of the figure leaning out of the painted window shows the young artist's mastery of perspective.","symbolism":"The book is his Gospel; tradition, from Papias, says Mark wrote it from Peter's preaching. The fruit garland is a classical motif Mantegna loved.","impact":"The painted window and the figure leaning out of it became a favorite device of Venetian and north Italian portraits.","img":"img/barnabasreturn0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Andrea_Mantegna_-_St._Mark_the_Evangelist_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg"}],"tags":["call"],"orderNote":"Some of the best manuscripts read \"returned to Jerusalem,\" which is hard to fit; most translations, like the RSV, read \"from Jerusalem.\" Papias (c. AD 120), quoted by Eusebius, is the earliest witness that Mark wrote down Peter's preaching."},
  {"id":"sentout","harmony":"Acts 13:1–3","lane":"paul","place":"antioch","date":"c. AD 47","title":"Barnabas and Saul are set apart at Antioch","people":["Barnabas","Saul","Symeon called Niger","Lucius of Cyrene","Manaen","The church at Antioch"],"gospels":{"Acts":"13:1–3"},"summary":"The church at Antioch has prophets and teachers from many backgrounds: Barnabas from Cyprus, Symeon called Niger, Lucius of Cyrene, Manaen who grew up with Herod Antipas, and Saul. While they worship and fast, the Holy Spirit tells them to set apart Barnabas and Saul for a work he has called them to. After more fasting and prayer they lay hands on the two men and send them off. It is the start of the first planned mission to the wider world.","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 13:2","text":"While they were worshiping the Lord and fasting, the Holy Spirit said, \"Set apart for me Barnabas and Saul for the work to which I have called them.\""},{"ref":"Acts 13:3","text":"Then after fasting and praying they laid their hands on them and sent them off."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"Paul and Barnabas of Cyprus Chosen as Apostles by the Holy Spirit","artist":"Ambrosius Francken the Elder","year":"c. 1600","medium":"Oil on panel","location":"Royal Museum of Fine Arts (KMSKA), Antwerp","description":"The church at Antioch is gathered at worship around an altar. Light streams down from the Hebrew name of God in the upper corner onto two men in the crowd, who look up as the call comes. A Latin inscription quotes Acts 13:2: \"Set apart for me Saul and Barnabas.\"","significance":"A tall panel by the Antwerp painter Ambrosius Francken, painted in the years of the Catholic renewal after the iconoclasm of 1566, when Antwerp's churches were refurnished.","symbolism":"The rays from the divine Name single out the two apostles: the mission begins with God's call in the middle of the church's prayer and fasting.","impact":"One of very few paintings of this moment, which is otherwise almost never shown in art.","img":"img/sentout1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ambrosius_Francken_(I)_-_Paul_and_Barnabas_of_Cyprus_chosen_as_apostles_by_the_Holy_Spirit.jpg"},{"kind":"Artifact","title":"The cave church of St Peter, Antioch","artist":"Photo: Volkan Hatem","year":"2003","medium":"Photograph","location":"Antakya (Antioch), Turkey","description":"A stone facade, built by the Crusaders and restored in 1863, closes the mouth of a cave on the slope of Mount Staurin above Antakya. Inside is a simple grotto church with an altar and traces of an early mosaic floor.","significance":"By old tradition this cave was a meeting place of the first Christians of Antioch, the church that sent Barnabas and Saul on their mission. It is one of the oldest places of Christian worship that can still be visited.","symbolism":"A hidden cave behind a church front: the humble beginnings of the community where the disciples were first called Christians.","impact":"Pilgrims still visit the grotto, and Mass is celebrated there on the feast of Saints Peter and Paul.","img":"img/sentout0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Volkan Hatem, CC BY 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antioch_Saint_Pierre_Church_Front.JPG"}],"tags":["call","prayer"],"orderNote":"Dates for the first journey vary; most place it c. AD 46–48, between the famine visit (Acts 11:30; 12:25) and the Jerusalem council of c. AD 49."},
  {"id":"salamis","harmony":"Acts 13:4–5","lane":"paul","place":"salamis","from":"antioch","date":"c. AD 47","title":"Sailing to Cyprus: the word at Salamis","people":["Barnabas","Saul","John Mark"],"gospels":{"Acts":"13:4–5"},"summary":"Barnabas and Saul go down to Seleucia, Antioch's harbor, and sail for Cyprus, Barnabas's home island. At Salamis, its chief port, they preach in the synagogues of the Jews; there were enough Jews there for more than one. Barnabas's young cousin John Mark goes with them as their helper. As later in every city, Paul begins with the synagogue.","journey":"About 16 miles down the Orontes valley to the port of Seleucia, then about 130 miles by sea southwest to Salamis on the east coast of Cyprus: one or two days' sail with a fair wind.","route":[[36.161,36.202],[36.1,36.18],[36.03,36.15],[35.97,36.13],[35.93,36.12],[35.75,36.0],[35.2,35.75],[34.6,35.48],[34.2,35.3],[33.9,35.18]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 13:4–5","text":"So, being sent out by the Holy Spirit, they went down to Seleucia; and from there they sailed to Cyprus. When they arrived at Salamis, they proclaimed the word of God in the synagogues of the Jews. And they had John to assist them."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"The gymnasium of Salamis","artist":"Photo: Michal Klajban","year":"2019","medium":"Photograph","location":"Salamis, near Famagusta, Cyprus","description":"Marble columns of the colonnade around the gymnasium's exercise court stand among the ruins of Salamis, the city where Barnabas and Saul first preached on Cyprus.","significance":"Salamis was the largest city and main port of Cyprus, with a large Jewish community. The gymnasium was rebuilt after earthquakes in the 1st and 4th centuries.","symbolism":"A Greek gymnasium and Jewish synagogues side by side: the mixed world the first mission entered.","impact":"By tradition Barnabas returned to Salamis and was martyred there; the Monastery of St Barnabas stands nearby.","img":"img/salamis0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Michal Klajban, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Columns_in_Roman_gymnasium,_Salamis,_Northern_Cyprus.jpg"}],"tags":["sermon"],"orderNote":"Colossians 4:10 calls Mark Barnabas's cousin. A later tradition says Barnabas was martyred at Salamis; the Monastery of St Barnabas near the ruins marks his tomb, found, by tradition, in AD 478."},
  {"id":"elymas","harmony":"Acts 13:6–12","lane":"paul","place":"paphos","from":"salamis","date":"c. AD 47","title":"Paphos: Elymas blinded, the proconsul believes","people":["Paul","Barnabas","Elymas (Bar-Jesus)","Sergius Paulus"],"gospels":{"Acts":"13:6–12"},"summary":"At Paphos, the Roman capital of Cyprus, the proconsul Sergius Paulus asks to hear the word of God. A Jewish magician and false prophet, Bar-Jesus, also called Elymas, tries to turn him away from the faith. Saul, \"who is also called Paul,\" looks at him and declares him blind for a time; at once mist and darkness fall on him. The proconsul believes, astonished at the teaching of the Lord. From here on Luke calls Saul by his Roman name, Paul, and puts him first.","journey":"About 100 miles across the island, most likely by the Roman road along the south coast through Kition, Amathus and Kourion: about a week on foot, with stops to preach.","route":[[33.9,35.18],[33.75,35.05],[33.63,34.92],[33.4,34.8],[33.14,34.71],[32.89,34.67],[32.65,34.7],[32.41,34.76]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 13:11","text":"And now, behold, the hand of the Lord is upon you, and you shall be blind and unable to see the sun for a time.\" Immediately mist and darkness fell upon him and he went about seeking people to lead him by the hand."},{"ref":"Acts 13:12","text":"Then the proconsul believed, when he saw what had occurred, for he was astonished at the teaching of the Lord."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"The Conversion of the Proconsul (The Blinding of Elymas)","artist":"Raphael","year":"1515–16","medium":"Bodycolour (distemper) on paper, mounted on canvas","location":"Victoria and Albert Museum, London (lent by the Royal Collection)","description":"Sergius Paulus sits enthroned under a Roman portico. At the left Paul, in red, stretches out his arm; at the right Elymas lurches forward with hands groping the air, struck blind, while the courtiers stare. A plaque on the throne records the proconsul's conversion \"by the preaching of Saul.\"","significance":"One of the seven surviving full-size cartoons Raphael made for the tapestries of the Acts of the Apostles that Pope Leo X commissioned for the Sistine Chapel.","symbolism":"Paul's stretched-out arm and Elymas's helpless, groping hands mirror each other: the word of God has power, and the false prophet can no longer see his way.","impact":"The Raphael Cartoons, bought by Charles I in 1623, were copied and engraved for centuries and shaped how Europe pictured the apostles.","img":"img/elymas0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:V&A_-_Raphael,_The_Conversion_of_the_Proconsul_(1515).jpg"},{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"Saint Paul and Sergius, Roman Proconsul in Cyprus","artist":"Malnazar","year":"1637–38","medium":"Tempera and gold on paper (illuminated manuscript)","location":"J. Paul Getty Museum, Los Angeles","description":"Against a gold sky, Paul stands holding a book of the Scriptures while the proconsul kneels before him with his hands crossed on his breast, between brightly colored city towers.","significance":"A page from an Armenian Bible illuminated by Malnazar, a leading painter of the Armenian community of New Julfa in Isfahan, Persia.","symbolism":"The kneeling Roman governor shows the gospel reaching the rulers of the Gentile world; Paul's halo and the book mark him as apostle and teacher.","impact":"Armenian Bibles of New Julfa carried the Acts scenes in a style that joins Byzantine, Persian and European models.","img":"img/elymas1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Malnazar_-_Saint_Paul_and_Sergius,_Roman_Proconsul_in_Cyprus_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg"}],"tags":["miracle","conflict"],"orderNote":"Luke correctly calls the governor of Cyprus a proconsul: the island was a senatorial province from 22 BC. A Lucius Sergius Paullus appears as a Roman official in an inscription of Claudius's reign; whether he is the same man is debated."},
  {"id":"johnmark","harmony":"Acts 13:13","lane":"paul","place":"perga","from":"paphos","date":"c. AD 47","title":"Perga: John Mark turns back","people":["Paul","Barnabas","John Mark","Paul's company"],"gospels":{"Acts":"13:13"},"summary":"\"Paul and his company\" sail from Paphos to the mainland of Asia Minor and come to Perga in Pamphylia. There John Mark leaves them and goes home to Jerusalem. Luke gives no reason. Paul took it hard: later he refused to take Mark on the second journey, and he and Barnabas parted over it (Acts 15:37–39). Years after, Paul calls Mark \"very useful in serving me\" (2 Timothy 4:11).","journey":"About 170 miles by sea northwest from Paphos, past the Akamas cape and across the open water to the Pamphylian coast, then a few miles up the Cestrus river (or by road from Attalia) to Perga: two to three days' sail.","route":[[32.41,34.76],[32.25,34.95],[32.2,35.1],[31.8,35.6],[31.3,36.2],[30.95,36.75],[30.9,36.84],[30.87,36.92],[30.854,36.961]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 13:13","text":"Now Paul and his company set sail from Paphos, and came to Perga in Pamphylia. And John left them and returned to Jerusalem;"}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"The colonnaded street of Perga","artist":"Photo: Bernard Gagnon","year":"2014","medium":"Photograph","location":"Perge, near Antalya, Turkey","description":"The marble columns of Perga's main street run north toward the acropolis hill. A water channel once ran down the middle of the paved road.","significance":"Perga was one of the great cities of Pamphylia; most of what stands today was built in the 2nd century AD, a few generations after Paul.","symbolism":"Paul passed this way twice: hurrying through on the way in (Acts 13:14), and preaching here on the way out (Acts 14:25).","impact":"The site, with its theater, stadium and Hellenistic gate, is one of the best-preserved Roman cities of southern Turkey.","img":"img/johnmark0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Bernard Gagnon, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Colonnade,_Perge_01.jpg"}],"tags":[],"orderNote":"Why Mark left is unknown. Guesses include homesickness, fear of the hard road over the Taurus, or unease that Paul, not his cousin Barnabas, now led the mission. Some (Ramsay) link the quick move inland to an illness Paul mentions in Galatians 4:13."},
  {"id":"antiochsermon","harmony":"Acts 13:14–43","lane":"paul","place":"pisidianantioch","from":"perga","date":"c. AD 47","title":"Paul's sermon in the synagogue at Pisidian Antioch","people":["Paul","Barnabas","Rulers of the synagogue","Jews and God-fearers"],"gospels":{"Acts":"13:14–43"},"summary":"At Antioch of Pisidia, a Roman colony on the high plateau, Paul and Barnabas go to the synagogue on the sabbath. After the reading of the Law and the Prophets the rulers invite them to speak. Paul stands up and tells Israel's story from the Exodus to David, then proclaims Jesus, David's descendant, crucified and raised by God. This is Paul's first sermon in Acts. Many Jews and devout converts follow them, and the people ask them to speak again next sabbath.","journey":"About 100 miles north over the Taurus mountains, climbing from the coastal plain to some 3,600 ft by rough mountain roads past Adada and Lake Egirdir: about a week on foot, through country known for brigands and swollen rivers.","route":[[30.854,36.961],[30.87,37.05],[30.9,37.2],[30.95,37.4],[31.0,37.6],[31.05,37.87],[31.12,38.12],[31.189,38.306]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 13:16","text":"So Paul stood up, and motioning with his hand said: \"Men of Israel, and you that fear God, listen."},{"ref":"Acts 13:38–39","text":"Let it be known to you therefore, brethren, that through this man forgiveness of sins is proclaimed to you, and by him every one that believes is freed from everything from which you could not be freed by the law of Moses."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Psalm 2:7","note":"\"You are my son, today I have begotten you\": Paul reads it of the Resurrection (Acts 13:33)."},{"ref":"Psalm 16:10","note":"\"You do not give me up to Sheol, or let your godly one see the Pit\": in its Greek form, \"see corruption,\" Paul applies it to Jesus, who rose (Acts 13:35–37)."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"The Temple of Augustus at Antioch of Pisidia","artist":"Photo: Dosseman","year":"2012","medium":"Photograph","location":"Yalvaç, Isparta Province, Turkey","description":"The podium of the temple of the emperor Augustus stands at the center of a semicircular precinct cut into the rock at the highest point of the city. The modern town of Yalvaç and the Sultan Mountains lie beyond.","significance":"A copy of Augustus's own account of his deeds, the Res Gestae, was set up in Antioch; fragments of it were found here. The cult of the emperor was at the heart of the colony's life when Paul preached in its synagogue.","symbolism":"Paul proclaims a Son of David whom God raised from the dead, in a city whose highest place honored the emperor as a son of a god.","impact":"Excavations here helped scholars like W. M. Ramsay picture the Roman colonies of Galatia that Paul evangelized.","img":"img/antiochsermon0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Dosseman, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antioch_in_Pisidia_Augustus_Temple_2951.jpg"}],"tags":["sermon","teaching"],"orderNote":"2 Corinthians 11:26 (\"danger from rivers, danger from robbers\") is often linked to this crossing of the Taurus, but Paul does not say where those dangers were."},
  {"id":"turngentiles","harmony":"Acts 13:44–52","lane":"paul","place":"pisidianantioch","date":"c. AD 47","title":"\"We turn to the Gentiles\"","people":["Paul","Barnabas","The Jews of Antioch","Gentiles of the city","Devout women of high standing"],"gospels":{"Acts":"13:44–52"},"summary":"The next sabbath almost the whole city gathers to hear. Some of the Jews, jealous at the crowds, contradict Paul. Paul and Barnabas answer boldly: the word had to come to Israel first, but since they reject it, \"we turn to the Gentiles.\" The Gentiles rejoice, and the word spreads through the region. Then leading women and men of the city are stirred up against them; they are driven out, shake the dust from their feet, and go on to Iconium, leaving disciples full of joy and the Holy Spirit.","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 13:46","text":"And Paul and Barnabas spoke out boldly, saying, \"It was necessary that the word of God should be spoken first to you. Since you thrust it from you, and judge yourselves unworthy of eternal life, behold, we turn to the Gentiles."},{"ref":"Acts 13:47","text":"For so the Lord has commanded us, saying, 'I have set you to be a light for the Gentiles, that you may bring salvation to the uttermost parts of the earth.'\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Isaiah 49:6","note":"\"I will give you as a light to the nations, that my salvation may reach to the end of the earth\": the Servant's mission, which Paul takes as his own."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"The main street of Antioch of Pisidia","artist":"Photo: Dosseman","year":"2018","medium":"Photograph","location":"Yalvaç, Isparta Province, Turkey","description":"The paved Decumanus Maximus, the main east–west street of the Roman colony, runs past the corner of the square in front of the theater, with the bare hills of the plateau behind.","significance":"Antioch was a Roman colony settled with veterans by Augustus. Its streets, theater and temple of Augustus give a picture of the city where \"almost the whole city gathered together\" to hear Paul.","symbolism":"A Roman street on the edge of the Gentile world: from here Paul and Barnabas turn to the Gentiles.","impact":"The ruins have been excavated since the 1920s; the site is visited by many Christian pilgrims following Paul's route.","img":"img/turngentiles0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Dosseman, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antioch_in_Pisida_Decumanus_Maximus_047.jpg"}],"tags":["conflict","teaching"]},
  {"id":"iconium","harmony":"Acts 14:1–7","lane":"paul","place":"iconium","from":"pisidianantioch","date":"c. AD 47","title":"Iconium: a city divided","people":["Paul","Barnabas","Jews and Greeks of Iconium"],"gospels":{"Acts":"14:1–7"},"summary":"At Iconium they again speak first in the synagogue, and a great number of Jews and Greeks believe. They stay a long time, speaking boldly, and the Lord confirms the word with signs and wonders. The city splits, some siding with the Jews who oppose them and some with the apostles. When Gentiles and Jews with their rulers plot to stone them, they escape to Lystra and Derbe, cities of Lycaonia.","journey":"About 90 miles southeast across the plateau, partly on the Via Sebaste, the new Roman road built under Augustus: four or five days on foot.","route":[[31.189,38.306],[31.45,38.2],[31.75,38.08],[32.1,37.97],[32.48,37.87]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 14:1","text":"Now at Iconium they entered together into the Jewish synagogue, and so spoke that a great company believed, both of Jews and of Greeks."},{"ref":"Acts 14:3","text":"So they remained for a long time, speaking boldly for the Lord, who bore witness to the word of his grace, granting signs and wonders to be done by their hands."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"Paul and Theocleia, fresco in the Grotto of St Paul","artist":"Unknown painter","year":"6th century","medium":"Fresco (photographed when uncovered)","location":"Grotto of St Paul, Bülbül Dağı, Ephesus","description":"Paul, named in Greek letters (PAULOS), raises his hand as he teaches. Beside him stands Theocleia, the mother of Thecla, in a veil and jewels. The fresco was found under layers of plaster in a cave church above Ephesus.","significance":"It illustrates the 2nd-century Acts of Paul and Thecla, set in Iconium, in which Thecla listens to Paul from her window and gives up marriage to follow him, against her mother's will.","symbolism":"Paul's raised hand is the gesture of a teacher; Theocleia's rich dress stands for the family and world Thecla leaves behind.","impact":"Thecla became one of the most popular saints of the early Church; the legend of Iconium was told from Asia Minor to Egypt and Armenia.","img":"img/iconium0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paul_and_Thecla.jpg"},{"kind":"Artifact","title":"Paul and Thecla, relief at Etchmiadzin Cathedral","artist":"Photo: Rita Willaert","year":"2008","medium":"Photograph of a stone relief","location":"Etchmiadzin Cathedral, Vagharshapat, Armenia","description":"A worn stone relief set into the cathedral wall shows two arched niches: on one side a standing figure, on the other a seated figure teaching. It is identified as Paul and Thecla.","significance":"An early medieval relief on the mother church of Armenia, showing how far the story of Paul's convert from Iconium travelled.","symbolism":"Teacher and listener under two arches: the Iconium legend in which Thecla hears the word and follows it.","impact":"Etchmiadzin, by tradition founded in the 4th century, is the seat of the Armenian Catholicos and a UNESCO World Heritage site.","img":"img/iconium1.jpg","credit":"Photo: Rita Willaert, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Etchmiadzin_Cathedral_relief_Saint_Thecla_and_Paul_the_Apostle.jpg"}],"tags":["sermon","conflict"],"orderNote":"The 2nd-century Acts of Paul and Thecla is set at Iconium: Thecla, a young woman, hears Paul preach and follows him. It is a legend, not history, but it preserves the only physical description of Paul (short, bald, bow-legged, with meeting eyebrows)."},
  {"id":"lystraheal","harmony":"Acts 14:8–10","lane":"paul","place":"lystra","from":"iconium","date":"c. AD 47–48","title":"The lame man at Lystra","people":["Paul","Barnabas","A man lame from birth"],"gospels":{"Acts":"14:8–10"},"summary":"At Lystra sits a man who has never walked, crippled from birth. He listens to Paul preaching. Paul looks at him, sees he has faith to be made well, and calls out, \"Stand upright on your feet.\" The man leaps up and walks. The scene echoes Peter's healing of the lame man at the Temple gate (Acts 3).","journey":"About 20 miles south-southwest from Iconium to the Roman colony of Lystra: a day's walk.","route":[[32.48,37.87],[32.42,37.75],[32.33,37.58]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 14:9–10","text":"He listened to Paul speaking; and Paul, looking intently at him and seeing that he had faith to be made well, said in a loud voice, \"Stand upright on your feet.\" And he sprang up and walked."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Isaiah 35:6","note":"\"then shall the lame man leap like a hart\": the sign of the age of salvation."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"Saint Paul Healing the Cripple at Lystra","artist":"Karel Dujardin","year":"1663","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Rijksmuseum, Amsterdam","description":"Paul, in a red mantle, strides forward and stretches out his hand. In front of him the lame man, on crutches and with a bandaged head, looks up in hope, while mothers, children and other sick people press in around them.","significance":"A late work by Dujardin, a Dutch painter trained in the Italianate style, painted during his years in Amsterdam.","symbolism":"Paul's open hand and the cripple's crutches show the moment just before the command, \"Stand upright on your feet.\"","impact":"One of Dujardin's largest works and a showpiece of Dutch classicism in the Rijksmuseum's collection.","img":"img/lystraheal0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karel_Dujardin_-_Paulus_geneest_de_kreupele_te_Lystra_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg"}],"tags":["miracle","healing"]},
  {"id":"lystragods","harmony":"Acts 14:11–18","lane":"paul","place":"lystra","date":"c. AD 47–48","title":"Taken for Zeus and Hermes","people":["Paul","Barnabas","The priest of Zeus","The people of Lystra"],"gospels":{"Acts":"14:11–18"},"summary":"The crowd cries out in their own Lycaonian tongue, \"The gods have come down to us in the likeness of men!\" They call Barnabas Zeus and Paul, the chief speaker, Hermes. The priest of Zeus brings oxen and garlands to the gates to offer sacrifice. Paul and Barnabas tear their clothes and rush into the crowd: they are men like them, bringing news of the living God who made heaven and earth and gives rain and harvests. Even so, they barely stop the sacrifice.","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 14:11–12","text":"And when the crowds saw what Paul had done, they lifted up their voices, saying in Lycaonian, \"The gods have come down to us in the likeness of men!\" Barnabas they called Zeus, and Paul, because he was the chief speaker, they called Hermes."},{"ref":"Acts 14:15","text":"\"Men, why are you doing this? We also are men, of like nature with you, and bring you good news, that you should turn from these vain things to a living God who made the heaven and the earth and the sea and all that is in them."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Psalm 146:6","note":"God \"who made heaven and earth, the sea, and all that is in them\": the words of Paul's appeal."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"The Sacrifice at Lystra","artist":"Raphael","year":"1515–16","medium":"Bodycolour (distemper) on paper, mounted on canvas","location":"Victoria and Albert Museum, London (lent by the Royal Collection)","description":"A garlanded ox is led to an altar, and a man raises an axe to strike it, while a statue of Hermes watches from a column. At the left Paul tears his garment in protest. Among the crowd stands the healed man, his crutches cast down.","significance":"Another of Raphael's cartoons for the Sistine Chapel tapestries; Raphael based the sacrifice on a Roman relief.","symbolism":"The healed cripple links the miracle to the mistake: the crowd sees gods where Paul points them to the living God.","impact":"Widely engraved, the design fixed the scene in European art for three centuries.","img":"img/lystragods0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:V&A_-_Raphael,_The_Sacrifice_at_Lystra_(1515).jpg"},{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"Paul and Barnabas at Lystra","artist":"Jacob Jordaens","year":"1616","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"State Hermitage Museum, Saint Petersburg","description":"A priest in a golden robe and the townspeople press a garlanded ox forward for sacrifice. Paul, in grey, grasps his torn garment and turns away in horror, while a young man pours water into a basin for the rite.","significance":"An early work of the Flemish painter Jordaens, full of the robust figures and crowded energy he learned from Rubens.","symbolism":"Paul tearing his clothes is the Jewish sign of grief at blasphemy: he refuses the honor due to God alone.","impact":"A strong example of Flemish Baroque history painting in the Hermitage collection, and one of the few great paintings of this scene.","img":"img/lystragods1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jacob_Jordaens_(I)_-_Paulus_en_Barnabas_te_Lystra_(Hand.14-16-18)_-_ГЭ-491_-_Hermitage_Museum.jpg"}],"tags":["teaching"],"orderNote":"Ovid (Metamorphoses 8) tells a local Phrygian legend in which Zeus and Hermes visited the region in human form and only the old couple Baucis and Philemon took them in; it may explain the crowd's eagerness. Inscriptions near Lystra link the two gods in worship."},
  {"id":"lystrastoned","harmony":"Acts 14:19–20","lane":"paul","place":"lystra","date":"c. AD 47–48","title":"Paul stoned and left for dead","people":["Paul","Barnabas","Jews from Antioch and Iconium","The disciples"],"gospels":{"Acts":"14:19–20","2Cor":"11:25","2Tim":"3:10–11"},"summary":"Jews come from Antioch and Iconium and win over the crowd. They stone Paul and drag him outside the city, thinking he is dead. But when the disciples gather round him, he gets up and goes back into the city. The next day he leaves with Barnabas for Derbe. Paul never forgot it: \"once I was stoned\" (2 Corinthians 11:25). Timothy, who may have seen it, was from Lystra (Acts 16:1).","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 14:19–20","text":"But Jews came there from Antioch and Iconium; and having persuaded the people, they stoned Paul and dragged him out of the city, supposing that he was dead. But when the disciples gathered about him, he rose up and entered the city; and on the next day he went on with Barnabas to Derbe."},{"ref":"2 Timothy 3:11","text":"my persecutions, my sufferings, what befell me at Antioch, at Iconium, and at Lystra, what persecutions I endured; yet from them all the Lord rescued me."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"Saint Paul Surrounded by Disciples after His Lapidation at Lystra","artist":"Giovanni Guerra","year":"c. 1590–1618","medium":"Pen and brown ink, brush and wash","location":"Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York","description":"Paul kneels in prayer among the stones that were thrown at him, while the disciples crowd round, amazed that he is alive. In the background the city gate stands open, ready for him to walk back in.","significance":"One of a series of narrative drawings of Acts by Guerra, a designer of fresco cycles in late 16th-century Rome. The Latin inscription cites Acts 14:20.","symbolism":"The stones still lie at his knees, but Paul is risen and praying: \"from them all the Lord rescued me.\"","impact":"The stoning at Lystra is rarely painted; drawings and prints like this kept the scene in illustrated Bibles.","img":"img/lystrastoned0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Paul_Surrounded_by_Disciples_after_His_Lapidation_at_Lystra_(Acts_14-20)_MET_DP810090.jpg"}],"tags":["conflict"],"orderNote":"Some connect Paul's vision of being \"caught up to the third heaven\" fourteen years before 2 Corinthians (2 Cor 12:2–4) with this stoning; it is a guess, and the dates fit only roughly."},
  {"id":"derbe","harmony":"Acts 14:20–21","lane":"paul","place":"derbe","from":"lystra","date":"c. AD 48","title":"Derbe: many disciples","people":["Paul","Barnabas","Gaius of Derbe (later)"],"gospels":{"Acts":"14:20–21"},"summary":"At Derbe, the easternmost town of the journey, Paul and Barnabas preach the gospel and make many disciples. Luke records no opposition here. Gaius of Derbe later travels with Paul (Acts 20:4). From Derbe the road ran on east to the Cilician Gates and Paul's home city of Tarsus, but they turn back instead.","journey":"About 60 miles east-southeast across the Lycaonian plain, the day after the stoning: three days or more on foot for a badly bruised man.","route":[[32.33,37.58],[32.6,37.5],[33.0,37.42],[33.36,37.35]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 14:21","text":"When they had preached the gospel to that city and had made many disciples, they returned to Lystra and to Iconium and to Antioch,"}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"Site of Derbe, Karajah Dagh in the distance","artist":"After E. J. Davis","year":"1879","medium":"Chromolithograph (book illustration)","location":"E. J. Davis, Life in Asiatic Turkey (London, 1879)","description":"Travellers on camels and on foot follow a stream across the wide, dry Lycaonian plain toward a small hill, with the jagged volcanic mass of Kara Dağ rising beyond.","significance":"Davis was one of the British travellers who searched for Derbe in the 19th century. Modern inscriptions place the city at Kerti Hüyük, in the same plain below Kara Dağ.","symbolism":"The empty plain shows how remote Derbe was: the far edge of the Roman province, where the first journey turned back.","impact":"Travellers' views like this shaped how Victorian readers imagined Paul's journeys through Asia Minor.","img":"img/derbe0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DAVIS(1879)_p329_SITE_OF_DERBE,_KARAJH_DAGH_MOUNTAINS_IN_THE_DISTANCE.jpg"},{"kind":"Artifact","title":"Bronze coin of Derbe with the empress Lucilla","artist":"Mint of Derbe","year":"c. AD 164–169","medium":"Bronze coin","location":"Bibliothèque nationale de France, Paris (Waddington collection)","description":"The obverse shows the bust of Lucilla, daughter of Marcus Aurelius and wife of Lucius Verus, with her name in Greek letters around the edge.","significance":"Derbe struck its own coins in the 2nd century AD, proof that the small town on the Lycaonian frontier became a city with civic rights in Roman times.","symbolism":"A Roman empress on a frontier town's coin: the empire's reach into the remote places where the gospel came early.","impact":"Coins and inscriptions like these were the key evidence for finding the lost city.","img":"img/derbe1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monnaie_-_Bronze,_Derbe,_Lycaonie,_Lucilla_-_btv1b8516566x_(1_of_2).jpg"}],"tags":["sermon"],"orderNote":"Derbe's site was long unknown. Inscriptions found in 1956 and 1964 point to Kerti Hüyük, about 60 miles from Lystra; some prefer nearby Devri Şehri. The map marks Kerti Hüyük."},
  {"id":"strengthen","harmony":"Acts 14:21–23","lane":"paul","place":"pisidianantioch","from":"derbe","date":"c. AD 48","title":"Back through the cities: elders appointed","people":["Paul","Barnabas","The new churches of Lystra, Iconium and Antioch","Elders"],"gospels":{"Acts":"14:21–23"},"summary":"Rather than go on east to Tarsus, Paul and Barnabas return through Lystra, Iconium and Antioch. They strengthen the new disciples and tell them plainly that \"through many tribulations we must enter the kingdom of God.\" In every church they appoint elders, and with prayer and fasting commit them to the Lord. The young churches are given leaders before the missionaries leave.","journey":"About 170 miles back the way they came, through Lystra and Iconium to Pisidian Antioch, the very cities that had driven them out: two weeks or more on the road, with stays in each.","route":[[33.36,37.35],[33.0,37.42],[32.6,37.5],[32.33,37.58],[32.42,37.75],[32.48,37.87],[32.1,37.97],[31.75,38.08],[31.45,38.2],[31.189,38.306]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 14:22","text":"strengthening the souls of the disciples, exhorting them to continue in the faith, and saying that through many tribulations we must enter the kingdom of God."},{"ref":"Acts 14:23","text":"And when they had appointed elders for them in every church, with prayer and fasting, they committed them to the Lord in whom they believed."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"Gudelissin: mound marking the site of Derbe","artist":"Photograph in W. M. Ramsay, The Church in the Roman Empire before A.D. 170","year":"1893","medium":"Halftone photograph (book illustration)","location":"Lycaonian plain, Konya Province, Turkey","description":"A low, bare mound strewn with stones and a few ruined walls rises from the scrubby Lycaonian plain, with hills on the horizon.","significance":"Ramsay, the great explorer of Paul's routes in Asia Minor, identified this mound as Derbe, the point from which Paul and Barnabas turned back to revisit their churches. Later inscriptions moved the site some miles away to Kerti Hüyük, in the same plain.","symbolism":"From the edge of the map the missionaries turn back, not forward, to strengthen the young churches they had left behind.","impact":"Ramsay's books argued that Luke was an accurate historian and that Galatians was written to these south Galatian churches; they shaped a century of study of Paul's journeys.","img":"img/strengthen0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_church_in_the_Roman_empire_before_A.D._170_(1893)_(14596357417).jpg"}],"tags":["teaching","prayer"],"orderNote":"Many scholars (the \"South Galatian\" view) hold that Paul's Letter to the Galatians was written to these churches of Antioch, Iconium, Lystra and Derbe, in the Roman province of Galatia; others think it went to ethnic Galatians further north."},
  {"id":"attalia","harmony":"Acts 14:24–25","lane":"paul","place":"attalia","from":"pisidianantioch","date":"c. AD 48","title":"The word in Perga, down to Attalia","people":["Paul","Barnabas"],"gospels":{"Acts":"14:24–25"},"summary":"They go back through Pisidia and come down to the plain of Pamphylia. This time they preach in Perga, which they had passed through quickly on the way in. Then they go down to Attalia, the main harbor of the region, to find a ship home.","journey":"About 100 miles south back over the Taurus through Pisidia to Perga, then about 12 miles to the port of Attalia: a week or more on foot.","route":[[31.189,38.306],[31.12,38.12],[31.05,37.87],[31.0,37.6],[30.95,37.4],[30.9,37.2],[30.87,37.05],[30.854,36.961],[30.79,36.93],[30.7,36.886]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 14:24–25","text":"Then they passed through Pisidia, and came to Pamphylia. And when they had spoken the word in Perga, they went down to Attalia;"}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"Hadrian's Gate, Antalya","artist":"Photo: Bernard Gagnon","year":"2014","medium":"Photograph","location":"Antalya (ancient Attalia), Turkey","description":"A triple-arched marble gate with Corinthian columns, set into the old city wall of Antalya. Its paving still shows the ruts worn by cart wheels.","significance":"The gate was built in honor of the emperor Hadrian's visit in AD 130, about eighty years after Paul and Barnabas came down to Attalia's harbor to sail home.","symbolism":"A city gate opening toward the sea: the end of the road through the Taurus and the start of the voyage back to Antioch.","impact":"Attalia, modern Antalya, is still the main port and city of the region; its old quarter, Kaleiçi, keeps the line of the ancient walls and harbor.","img":"img/attalia0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Bernard Gagnon, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hadrian's_Gate,_Antalya_01.jpg"}],"tags":["sermon"]},
  {"id":"returnantioch","harmony":"Acts 14:26–28","lane":"paul","place":"antioch","from":"attalia","date":"c. AD 48","title":"Home to Antioch: \"a door of faith to the Gentiles\"","people":["Paul","Barnabas","The church at Antioch"],"gospels":{"Acts":"14:26–28"},"summary":"They sail back to Antioch, where they had first been commended to the grace of God for this work. Gathering the church, they report all that God has done with them and \"how he had opened a door of faith to the Gentiles.\" They stay a long time with the disciples there. The first journey has lasted perhaps a year and a half, maybe more.","journey":"About 300 miles by sea east along the coasts of Pamphylia and Cilicia, between Asia Minor and Cyprus, to Seleucia, then 16 miles up the Orontes to Antioch: a week or more under sail. The whole first journey covered some 1,250 miles by land and sea.","route":[[30.7,36.886],[31.3,36.72],[32.0,36.45],[32.8,35.98],[33.6,36.05],[34.5,36.1],[35.4,36.1],[35.85,36.1],[35.93,36.12],[35.97,36.13],[36.03,36.15],[36.1,36.18],[36.161,36.202]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 14:26–27","text":"and from there they sailed to Antioch, where they had been commended to the grace of God for the work which they had fulfilled. And when they arrived, they gathered the church together and declared all that God had done with them, and how he had opened a door of faith to the Gentiles."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"The Titus Tunnel at Seleucia Pieria","artist":"Photo: Htkava","year":"Undated","medium":"Photograph","location":"Çevlik, near Samandağ, Hatay, Turkey","description":"A section of the great channel cut through solid rock above Seleucia, Antioch's port, with water pooling at its mouth.","significance":"Begun under Vespasian and Titus in the later 1st century AD, the tunnel and canal, nearly a mile long, diverted a mountain stream so that floods would not silt up the harbor where Paul and Barnabas landed and set sail.","symbolism":"The harbor of Seleucia was the door through which the first missions went out and came home.","impact":"One of the most impressive Roman engineering works in Turkey, it is now on UNESCO's tentative list.","img":"img/returnantioch0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Htkava, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Titus_Tunnel,_Seleucia_Pieria.jpg"}],"tags":[]},
  {"id":"circumcision","harmony":"Acts 15:1–2","lane":"paul","place":"antioch","date":"c. AD 48–49","title":"Dissension at Antioch over circumcision","people":["Paul","Barnabas","Men from Judea","(Galatians 2: Peter, men from James)"],"gospels":{"Acts":"15:1–2","Gal":"2:11–14"},"summary":"Men come down from Judea and teach the believers at Antioch that Gentiles cannot be saved unless they are circumcised and keep the Law of Moses. Paul and Barnabas oppose them hard. The question goes to the heart of the gospel: are Gentiles saved by grace through faith in Christ, or must they first become Jews? The church sends Paul, Barnabas and others up to Jerusalem to the apostles and elders.","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 15:1","text":"But some men came down from Judea and were teaching the brethren, \"Unless you are circumcised according to the custom of Moses, you cannot be saved.\""},{"ref":"Galatians 2:14","text":"But when I saw that they were not straightforward about the truth of the gospel, I said to Cephas before them all, \"If you, though a Jew, live like a Gentile and not like a Jew, how can you compel the Gentiles to live like Jews?\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"Saints Peter and Paul","artist":"Guido Reni","year":"c. 1605","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Pinacoteca di Brera, Milan","description":"Peter, old and bald, sits with his keys on the ground and his head in his hand, listening. Paul, in red, stands over him with the book of the Scriptures, raising a hand to make his point.","significance":"An early masterpiece of Reni's Roman years. It has long been read as the dispute at Antioch, when Paul \"opposed Peter to his face\" (Galatians 2:11); an 1804 engraving of it is titled with Paul's words, \"How can you compel the Gentiles to live like Jews?\"","symbolism":"Peter's thoughtful pose and Paul's urgent gesture show two apostles in earnest debate, not enmity: the keys and the book are both theirs.","impact":"The painting is one of the best-loved images of the two founders of the Roman church together.","img":"img/circumcision0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guido_Reni_-_Sts_Peter_and_Paul_-_WGA19309.jpg"}],"tags":["conflict"],"orderNote":"Galatians 2:11–14 tells how Paul rebuked Peter at Antioch when, after \"certain men came from James,\" Peter stopped eating with Gentiles. Some put that clash here, just before the council; others place it after the council (Robertson and many older harmonies) or before the first journey."},
  {"id":"jerusalemcouncil","harmony":"Acts 15:3–12","lane":"apostles","place":"jerusalemchurch","from":"antioch","date":"c. AD 49","title":"The Council of Jerusalem: Peter speaks","people":["Peter","James","The apostles and elders","Paul","Barnabas","Believing Pharisees","(Galatians 2: Titus, John)"],"gospels":{"Acts":"15:3–12","Gal":"2:1–10"},"summary":"On the way through Phoenicia and Samaria, Paul and Barnabas bring great joy to the believers with news of the Gentiles' conversion. In Jerusalem some believers from the party of the Pharisees insist that Gentiles must be circumcised and keep the Law. The apostles and elders meet. After long debate Peter rises: God gave the Holy Spirit to the Gentiles at Cornelius's house just as to us, cleansing their hearts by faith, so why put on them a yoke no one could bear? \"We shall be saved through the grace of the Lord Jesus, just as they will.\" Then the assembly listens in silence as Barnabas and Paul tell of the signs God has done among the Gentiles.","journey":"About 330 miles south by the coast road through Phoenicia, by Tripolis, Berytus, Sidon, Tyre and Ptolemais, then inland through Samaria to Jerusalem: two to three weeks on foot, with stops to tell the churches of the Gentiles' conversion.","route":[[36.161,36.202],[36.02,35.85],[35.78,35.52],[35.88,34.89],[35.84,34.44],[35.65,34.12],[35.5,33.89],[35.37,33.56],[35.2,33.27],[35.07,32.92],[35.18,32.6],[35.192,32.276],[35.25,32.0],[35.231,31.776]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 15:8–9","text":"And God who knows the heart bore witness to them, giving them the Holy Spirit just as he did to us; and he made no distinction between us and them, but cleansed their hearts by faith."},{"ref":"Acts 15:11","text":"But we believe that we shall be saved through the grace of the Lord Jesus, just as they will.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"The Council of Jerusalem (Peter window)","artist":"Königliche Glasmalereianstalt, Munich","year":"1873","medium":"Stained glass","location":"Cologne Cathedral (south transept, Peter window)","description":"Peter stands on the steps in the middle of the assembly, holding a book and raising his hand as he speaks. Around him the apostles and elders sit and listen; at the right an elder holding a written sheet ponders, and books and scrolls lie open on the floor.","significance":"The main scene of a window on the life of Peter given to Cologne Cathedral when it was being completed in the 19th century.","symbolism":"Peter speaks from the center of the Church's first council; the open books point to Scripture as the ground of its decision.","impact":"The Council of Jerusalem is rarely shown in art; it is remembered as the model for all later councils of the Church.","img":"img/jerusalemcouncil0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Doppelklecks, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Koelner_Dom_Petrusfenster_Apostelkonzil.jpg"}],"tags":["teaching","conflict"],"orderNote":"Most identify Paul's visit in Galatians 2:1–10 (with Barnabas and Titus, \"after fourteen years\") with this council. Others identify it with the famine visit of Acts 11:30, which would put Galatians before the council. This is Peter's last appearance in Acts."},
  {"id":"jamesverdict","harmony":"Acts 15:13–29","lane":"apostles","place":"jerusalemchurch","date":"c. AD 49","title":"James's judgment and the apostles' letter","people":["James","The apostles and elders","Judas Barsabbas","Silas","Paul","Barnabas"],"gospels":{"Acts":"15:13–29"},"summary":"James, the brother of the Lord and leader of the Jerusalem church, sums up. The prophets foretold that the Gentiles would seek the Lord, so \"we should not trouble those of the Gentiles who turn to God.\" They are asked only to abstain from food offered to idols, from unchastity, from what is strangled and from blood, so that Jews and Gentiles can share a table. The apostles, elders and whole church agree and write a letter, sent with Judas Barsabbas and Silas: \"it has seemed good to the Holy Spirit and to us.\" Gentiles need not become Jews to be Christians.","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 15:19–20","text":"Therefore my judgment is that we should not trouble those of the Gentiles who turn to God, but should write to them to abstain from the pollutions of idols and from unchastity and from what is strangled and from blood."},{"ref":"Acts 15:28","text":"For it has seemed good to the Holy Spirit and to us to lay upon you no greater burden than these necessary things:"}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Amos 9:11–12","note":"\"I will raise up the booth of David that is fallen\": James quotes it in the Greek form, of the Gentiles who are called by the Lord's name (Acts 15:16–17)."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"Saint James the Brother of the Lord","artist":"Unknown icon painter","year":"Modern (Neo-Byzantine)","medium":"Icon","location":"Unknown","description":"James, named in Greek \"the Brother of God\" (Adelphotheos), wears a bishop's omophorion marked with crosses and holds a jewelled Gospel book against a gold ground.","significance":"The Orthodox Church honors James as the first bishop of Jerusalem, who presided at the council; his feast is October 23.","symbolism":"The bishop's vestments show his role as head of the Jerusalem church; the closed book is the word he judged by.","impact":"The ancient Liturgy of St James, still used in Jerusalem on his feast, is named after him.","img":"img/jamesverdict0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_James_the_Just.jpg"}],"tags":["teaching"],"orderNote":"The four rules echo the laws for foreigners living in Israel (Leviticus 17–18). Some manuscripts (the Western text) drop \"what is strangled\" and add the Golden Rule, turning the rules into moral ones."},
  {"id":"letterantioch","harmony":"Acts 15:30–35","lane":"paul","place":"antioch","from":"jerusalemchurch","date":"c. AD 49","title":"The letter read at Antioch","people":["Paul","Barnabas","Judas Barsabbas","Silas","The church at Antioch"],"gospels":{"Acts":"15:30–35"},"summary":"Paul and Barnabas return to Antioch with Judas and Silas, gather the congregation and hand over the letter. The church rejoices at its encouragement. Judas and Silas, both prophets, strengthen the believers with many words, and after a time Judas goes back to Jerusalem. Paul and Barnabas stay on in Antioch, teaching and preaching with many others. Silas will soon be Paul's partner on his second journey.","journey":"About 330 miles back north through Samaria and the coast road of Phoenicia to Antioch: two to three weeks on foot.","route":[[35.231,31.776],[35.25,32.0],[35.192,32.276],[35.18,32.6],[35.07,32.92],[35.2,33.27],[35.37,33.56],[35.5,33.89],[35.65,34.12],[35.84,34.44],[35.88,34.89],[35.78,35.52],[36.02,35.85],[36.161,36.202]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 15:30–31","text":"So when they were sent off, they went down to Antioch; and having gathered the congregation together, they delivered the letter. And when they read it, they rejoiced at the exhortation."},{"ref":"Acts 15:35","text":"But Paul and Barnabas remained in Antioch, teaching and preaching the word of the Lord, with many others also."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"The Apostle Paul","artist":"Rembrandt","year":"c. 1633–36","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Kunsthistorisches Museum, Vienna","description":"An old Paul, with a long white beard, sits at a table with a great open book, his hand resting on its pages and a pen in his other hand. He looks out, lost in thought, with the hilt of a sword glinting in the dark behind him.","significance":"One of several images of Paul by Rembrandt, who was drawn to the apostle as a thinker, teacher and letter writer, as Paul was in Antioch after the council, \"teaching and preaching the word of the Lord.\"","symbolism":"The open book stands for Scripture and the written word that settled the dispute; the sword is the sign of Paul's martyrdom.","impact":"Rembrandt's apostles, quiet and human, changed how Paul was pictured: no longer only a hero of action but a pastor and teacher.","img":"img/letterantioch0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rembrandt_-_Apostle_Paul_-_WGA19120.jpg"}],"tags":["teaching"],"orderNote":"Acts 15:34 (\"But it seemed good to Silas to remain there\") is missing from the best manuscripts and from the RSV; Silas is nonetheless in Antioch when Paul chooses him in 15:40."},
  {"id":"paulbarnabassplit","harmony":"Acts 15:36–41","lane":"paul","place":"antioch","date":"c. AD 49","title":"Paul and Barnabas part ways","people":["Paul","Barnabas","John Mark","Silas"],"gospels":{"Acts":"15:36–41"},"summary":"Some days after the council, Paul proposes to Barnabas that they go back and visit the churches founded on their first journey. Barnabas wants to take his cousin John Mark again; Paul refuses, because Mark had left them in Pamphylia. The disagreement is so sharp that they separate. Barnabas sails with Mark to Cyprus, and Paul chooses Silas, a leader of the Jerusalem church, and sets out by land through Syria and Cilicia, strengthening the churches.","stay":"Paul and Barnabas had stayed on at Antioch for some time after the council (Acts 15:35).","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 15:36","text":"And after some days Paul said to Barnabas, \"Come, let us return and visit the brethren in every city where we proclaimed the word of the Lord, and see how they are.\""},{"ref":"Acts 15:39–40","text":"And there arose a sharp contention, so that they separated from each other; Barnabas took Mark with him and sailed away to Cyprus, but Paul chose Silas and departed, being commended by the brethren to the grace of the Lord."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"Saint Barnabas","artist":"Anonymous Lombard painter","year":"Date not recorded (Baroque style)","medium":"Oil painting","location":"Lombardy, Italy (collection not recorded)","description":"An elderly, bearded Barnabas in a golden mantle, a large wooden cross behind his shoulder, looks down at a written sheet in one hand while a small flame burns in the other.","significance":"Barnabas, the \"son of encouragement\" (Acts 4:36), was Paul's partner on the first journey. After their split he sailed with Mark to Cyprus (Acts 15:39), and Acts never mentions him again. Milan honors him as the legendary first preacher of the faith there, which is why he is often painted in Lombardy.","symbolism":"The cross and the written page are common attributes of an apostle and preacher; the flame suggests the fire of the Spirit.","impact":"Cypriot tradition holds that Barnabas was martyred at Salamis; he is honored as founder of the Church of Cyprus.","img":"img/paulbarnabassplit0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:San_Barnaba.jpg"}],"tags":["conflict"],"orderNote":"Mark was later reconciled with Paul: Colossians 4:10, Philemon 24 and 2 Timothy 4:11 (\"he is very useful in serving me\") name him among Paul's helpers. Acts says nothing more of Barnabas after this chapter, though Paul mentions him in 1 Corinthians 9:6."},
  {"id":"timothyjoins","harmony":"Acts 16:1–5","lane":"paul","place":"lystra","from":"antioch","date":"c. AD 49","title":"Timothy joins Paul at Lystra","people":["Paul","Silas","Timothy"],"gospels":{"Acts":"16:1–5","2Tim":"1:5; 3:10–11"},"summary":"Paul and Silas reach Derbe and Lystra, where Paul had been stoned on his first visit. At Lystra they meet a young disciple named Timothy, son of a Jewish believing mother and a Greek father, well spoken of by the brethren. Paul takes him along and circumcises him, so that the Jews of the region would not take offense. Going from city to city, they hand on the decisions of the Jerusalem council, and the churches grow daily.","journey":"About 350 miles by land, a month or more with stops: up the coast through the Syrian Gates to Tarsus, over the Taurus by the Roman road through the Cilician Gates, then west across the Lycaonian plain to Derbe and on to Lystra.","route":[[36.161,36.202],[36.22,36.5],[36.2,36.85],[35.62,36.96],[35.32,37.0],[34.895,36.918],[34.8,37.27],[34.62,37.83],[34.05,37.51],[33.36,37.35],[32.33,37.58]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 16:1–2","text":"And he came also to Derbe and to Lystra. A disciple was there, named Timothy, the son of a Jewish woman who was a believer; but his father was a Greek. He was well spoken of by the brethren at Lystra and Iconium."},{"ref":"Acts 16:5","text":"So the churches were strengthened in the faith, and they increased in numbers daily."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"Timothy and his grandmother Lois","artist":"Willem Drost","year":"1650s","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Hermitage Museum, Saint Petersburg","description":"An old woman in a dark hood and white wimple sits with her arm around a fair-haired boy, who leans against her and reads from a small book she holds open.","significance":"Drost was one of Rembrandt's pupils, and the painting was long attributed to Rembrandt. It illustrates Paul's words in 2 Timothy 1:5 and 3:15: Timothy had known the sacred writings from childhood.","symbolism":"The grandmother's hand rests on the child as the book rests in his: faith handed down from Lois to Eunice to Timothy.","impact":"The subject was rare before the Dutch Golden Age, when Protestant households prized it as a picture of teaching the Bible at home.","img":"img/timothyjoins0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Timothy-and-Lois.jpg"}],"tags":["call"],"orderNote":"2 Timothy 1:5 names his grandmother Lois and mother Eunice. Timothy may have come to faith on Paul's first visit (Paul calls him \"my true child in the faith,\" 1 Timothy 1:2), and 2 Timothy 3:11 recalls that he knew Paul's sufferings at Antioch, Iconium and Lystra."},
  {"id":"phrygiagalatia","harmony":"Acts 16:6–8","lane":"paul","place":"phrygia","from":"lystra","date":"c. AD 49","title":"Through Phrygia and Galatia: the Spirit closes the way","people":["Paul","Silas","Timothy"],"gospels":{"Acts":"16:6–8","Gal":"4:13–14"},"summary":"From Lystra the three travel on through the region of Phrygia and Galatia. They plan to preach in Asia, the rich province around Ephesus, but the Holy Spirit forbids it. When they come opposite Mysia and try to turn north into Bithynia, \"the Spirit of Jesus\" does not allow them. Blocked twice, they turn west and go down to the port of Troas. Acts does not say how the Spirit made his will known.","journey":"About 200 miles by land, several weeks: east to west through Iconium and Antioch of Pisidia, then north across the Phrygian uplands toward the borders of Mysia and Bithynia.","route":[[32.33,37.58],[32.48,37.87],[31.75,38.1],[31.189,38.306],[31.41,38.36],[31.3,38.8],[31.5,39.2]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 16:6–7","text":"And they went through the region of Phrygia and Galatia, having been forbidden by the Holy Spirit to speak the word in Asia. And when they had come opposite Mysia, they attempted to go into Bithynia, but the Spirit of Jesus did not allow them;"}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"The Phrygian highlands","artist":"Photograph by Andrew Kuznetsov","year":"2014","medium":"Photograph","location":"Phrygian Valley (Frig Vadisi), between Eskişehir and Afyon, Turkey","description":"Evening light over the grassland, pine woods and weathered rock outcrops of the Phrygian uplands of central Anatolia.","significance":"Paul, Silas and Timothy crossed country like this on their way north and west toward Mysia and Bithynia, blocked from preaching in Asia (Acts 16:6–8).","symbolism":"An open, empty country: Acts compresses weeks of walking and two closed doors into three verses.","impact":"The valley is famous for the rock-cut monuments of the old Phrygian kingdom, such as the Midas tomb façade, already ancient in Paul's day.","img":"img/phrygiagalatia0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Andrew Kuznetsov, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phrygian_Valley.jpg"}],"tags":[],"orderNote":"\"Phrygia and Galatia\" is debated. On the South Galatian view (Ramsay) it means the Phrygian part of the Roman province of Galatia, around Iconium and Pisidian Antioch, and the Galatians of Paul's letter are these churches. On the North Galatian view it means the old Celtic lands around Ancyra (Ankara), where Paul fell ill and founded new churches (Galatians 4:13). The map marks a general point in the region."},
  {"id":"macedonianvision","harmony":"Acts 16:9–10","lane":"paul","place":"troas","from":"phrygia","date":"c. AD 49","title":"The vision of the man of Macedonia","people":["Paul","Silas","Timothy","Luke (\"we\")","A man of Macedonia (in a vision)"],"gospels":{"Acts":"16:9–10"},"summary":"At Troas, near the site of ancient Troy, Paul sees a vision in the night: a man of Macedonia stands and begs him, \"Come over to Macedonia and help us.\" The company at once concludes that God is calling them to preach there. Here the narrative suddenly changes from \"they\" to \"we\": the author, by tradition Luke the physician, has joined the group. The gospel is about to cross into Europe.","journey":"About 300 miles by land, two to three weeks: west from the Phrygian uplands past the borders of Mysia, by Dorylaeum and the valleys running down to the Aegean, to the port of Alexandria Troas.","route":[[31.5,39.2],[30.52,39.78],[29.5,39.75],[28.4,39.7],[27.88,39.65],[27.02,39.6],[26.6,39.65],[26.155,39.752]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 16:9–10","text":"And a vision appeared to Paul in the night: a man of Macedonia was standing beseeching him and saying, \"Come over to Macedonia and help us.\" And when he had seen the vision, immediately we sought to go on into Macedonia, concluding that God had called us to preach the gospel to them."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"Ruins of Alexandria Troas","artist":"Photograph by Elelicht","year":"2012","medium":"Photograph","location":"Alexandria Troas, near Dalyan, Çanakkale Province, Turkey","description":"A great Roman brick-and-stone arch, part of the city's baths, rises above scrub oak and tumbled blocks at Alexandria Troas.","significance":"Troas was the port where Paul saw the vision of the man of Macedonia and from which the gospel first crossed to Europe. He came back through it twice more (Acts 20:5–12; 2 Corinthians 2:12).","symbolism":"The ruined city on the edge of Asia looks west across the sea toward the Macedonia of Paul's vision.","impact":"The baths are thought to be a gift of the wealthy Athenian Herodes Atticus in the 2nd century, a few generations after Paul.","img":"img/macedonianvision0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Elelicht, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexandria_Troas_Thermen.JPG"},{"kind":"Artifact","title":"The Roman harbor of Alexandria Troas","artist":"Photograph by Carole Raddato","year":"2023","medium":"Photograph","location":"Alexandria Troas, near Dalyan, Turkey","description":"A curving beach and the low remains of the ancient breakwaters of Troas' harbor, looking out on the Aegean.","significance":"The harbor, built under Augustus with an outer and inner basin, is where Paul and his companions took ship for Macedonia (Acts 16:11).","symbolism":"From this shore Europe lies just over the horizon, across the sea of the vision.","impact":"Paul would sail from here again on later journeys; the harbor has long since silted up.","img":"img/macedonianvision1.jpg","credit":"Photo: Carole Raddato, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Roman_Harbour_of_Alexandria_Troas,_built_in_the_reign_of_Augustus_and_consisting_of_an_outer_basin_protected_by_two_breakwaters_and_an_inner_basin,_Troad,_Turkey.jpg"}],"tags":["call"],"orderNote":"The first of the \"we\" passages (16:10–17; 20:5–21:18; 27:1–28:16). The tradition that the author was Luke goes back to Irenaeus (c. 180); some scholars think the \"we\" comes from a travel diary used by a later author."},
  {"id":"lydia","harmony":"Acts 16:11–15","lane":"paul","place":"philippi","from":"troas","date":"c. AD 49–50","title":"Lydia, the first convert in Europe","people":["Paul","Silas","Timothy","Luke (\"we\")","Lydia","The women at the place of prayer"],"gospels":{"Acts":"16:11–15","Phil":"1:3–5"},"summary":"The company sails to Neapolis and walks inland to Philippi, a Roman colony on the Via Egnatia. There seems to be no synagogue, so on the sabbath they go outside the gate to a riverside place of prayer and speak to the women gathered there. Lydia, a dealer in purple cloth from Thyatira and a worshiper of God, listens: \"the Lord opened her heart.\" She and her household are baptized, and she persuades the missionaries to stay in her house. The church at Philippi later became one of Paul's most loyal and generous.","stay":"Lydia's house became the missionaries' base and the meeting place of the church (Acts 16:15, 40).","journey":"About 125 miles by sea, two days with a fair wind (the return voyage in Acts 20:6 took five): from Troas past Tenedos, anchoring overnight at Samothrace, then to Neapolis (Kavala) and 10 miles inland over the hill on the Via Egnatia to Philippi.","route":[[26.155,39.752],[26.0,39.85],[25.75,40.15],[25.53,40.47],[25.1,40.75],[24.7,40.88],[24.41,40.94],[24.35,40.98],[24.287,41.013]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 16:13","text":"and on the sabbath day we went outside the gate to the riverside, where we supposed there was a place of prayer; and we sat down and spoke to the women who had come together."},{"ref":"Acts 16:14–15","text":"One who heard us was a woman named Lydia, from the city of Thyatira, a seller of purple goods, who was a worshiper of God. The Lord opened her heart to give heed to what was said by Paul. And when she was baptized, with her household, she besought us, saying, \"If you have judged me to be faithful to the Lord, come to my house and stay.\" And she prevailed upon us."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"The Baptism of Lydia","artist":"Marie Ellenrieder","year":"1861","medium":"Oil on paper","location":"Alte Nationalgalerie, Berlin","description":"Outside a walled town, Paul pours water from a shell over the head of Lydia, who kneels in a red mantle by a stream; a small child prays beside her and her household stands by, with Silas behind Paul.","significance":"Ellenrieder, one of the first women admitted to an art academy in Germany, painted in the gentle, devout style of the Nazarenes. She shows Lydia's whole household coming to baptism (Acts 16:15).","symbolism":"Lydia's red robe recalls her trade in costly purple cloth; the little stream is the riverside place of prayer.","impact":"A late work of a painter much admired in her lifetime for her religious pictures; it is one of the few paintings devoted to Lydia.","img":"img/lydia0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1861_Ellenrieder_Die_Taufe_der_Lydia_anagoria.JPG"},{"kind":"Artifact","title":"The riverside at Philippi, traditional site of Lydia's baptism","artist":"Photograph by Explorer1940","year":"2009","medium":"Photograph","location":"Lydia, near Philippi, Greece","description":"A clear stream runs between willows and reeds, with old stone slabs at its edge, just outside ancient Philippi.","significance":"The Zygaktis (ancient Gangites) stream west of the city gate is the traditional \"riverside, where we supposed there was a place of prayer\" (Acts 16:13). The modern village here is named Lydia, and a baptistery church stands by the water.","symbolism":"Running water outside the walls fits both the Jewish place of prayer and the baptism of Lydia's household.","impact":"Pilgrims still come to be baptized in the stream, which is one of the most visited Pauline sites in Greece.","img":"img/lydia1.jpg","credit":"Photo: Explorer1940, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Philippi_--_Site_of_baptism_of_Lydia_1.jpg"}],"tags":[],"orderNote":"The river is usually taken to be the Gangites (Zygaktis), about a mile west of the city gate, where a modern baptistery church marks the traditional site; some prefer the nearer Krenides stream. Philippians 1:5 thanks the church for its \"partnership in the gospel from the first day until now.\""},
  {"id":"slavegirl","harmony":"Acts 16:16–24","lane":"paul","place":"philippi","date":"c. AD 50","title":"The slave girl with a spirit of divination","people":["Paul","Silas","A slave girl","Her owners","The magistrates of Philippi"],"gospels":{"Acts":"16:16–24","1Thess":"2:2"},"summary":"A slave girl with a spirit of divination, whose fortune-telling earns her owners a good income, follows the missionaries for days crying out that they are servants of the Most High God. At last Paul, annoyed, commands the spirit in the name of Jesus Christ to come out of her, and it leaves at once. Her owners, seeing their profits gone, drag Paul and Silas before the magistrates as Jews who disturb the city with un-Roman customs. The crowd joins in; they are stripped, beaten with rods and thrown into the inner prison with their feet in the stocks.","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 16:18","text":"And this she did for many days. But Paul was annoyed, and turned and said to the spirit, \"I charge you in the name of Jesus Christ to come out of her.\" And it came out that very hour."},{"ref":"Acts 16:19","text":"But when her owners saw that their hope of gain was gone, they seized Paul and Silas and dragged them into the market place before the rulers;"},{"ref":"Acts 16:23","text":"And when they had inflicted many blows upon them, they threw them into prison, charging the jailer to keep them safely."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"Paul and Silas drive the spirit out of the slave girl; the jailer falls before them","artist":"Engraving after Bernard Picart","year":"c. 1700","medium":"Engraving","location":"Rijksmuseum, Amsterdam","description":"Two scenes on one plate. Above, in a classical square, Paul stretches out his hand toward the girl, who flings her arms wide as the spirit leaves her. Below, in the prison, the jailer kneels by his dropped sword before Paul and Silas while the freed prisoners crowd round.","significance":"A page from a Dutch picture Bible of about 1700, with captions in Dutch and French, which told Acts scene by scene for readers at home.","symbolism":"The girl's open arms and wild pose show the spirit going out of her; in the lower scene the torch and the fallen sword tell the jailer's story.","impact":"Illustrated Bibles like this spread images of the less painted scenes of Acts across Protestant Europe.","img":"img/slavegirl0.jpg","credit":"Rijksmuseum, Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paulus_geneest_een_bezeten_vrouw_en_de_bekering_van_de_gevangenenbewaarder_van_Filippi,_RP-P-1896-A-19368-1908.jpg"}],"tags":["exorcism","conflict"],"orderNote":"The Greek calls it \"a spirit of python,\" the name linked to the oracle of Apollo at Delphi. Paul recalls this beating in 1 Thessalonians 2:2 (\"shamefully treated at Philippi\") and counts three beatings with rods in 2 Corinthians 11:25."},
  {"id":"philippianjailer","harmony":"Acts 16:25–40","lane":"paul","place":"philippi","date":"c. AD 50","title":"The earthquake and the Philippian jailer","people":["Paul","Silas","The jailer and his household","The prisoners","The magistrates"],"gospels":{"Acts":"16:25–40"},"summary":"At midnight, bruised and in the stocks, Paul and Silas are praying and singing hymns while the other prisoners listen. A great earthquake shakes the prison; the doors fly open and every chain falls loose. The jailer, sure the prisoners have fled, draws his sword to kill himself, but Paul cries out, \"We are all here.\" Trembling, he asks, \"What must I do to be saved?\" That same night he washes their wounds and is baptized with all his household. In the morning Paul reveals that they are Roman citizens, beaten without trial; the alarmed magistrates come to apologize and ask them to leave the city.","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 16:25–26","text":"But about midnight Paul and Silas were praying and singing hymns to God, and the prisoners were listening to them, and suddenly there was a great earthquake, so that the foundations of the prison were shaken; and immediately all the doors were opened and every one's fetters were unfastened."},{"ref":"Acts 16:30–31","text":"and brought them out and said, \"Men, what must I do to be saved?\" And they said, \"Believe in the Lord Jesus, and you will be saved, you and your household.\""},{"ref":"Acts 16:37","text":"But Paul said to them, \"They have beaten us publicly, uncondemned, men who are Roman citizens, and have thrown us into prison; and do they now cast us out secretly? No! let them come themselves and take us out.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"The earthquake in the prison at Philippi","artist":"Johann Sadeler I, after Maerten de Vos","year":"1580","medium":"Engraving","location":"Rijksmuseum, Amsterdam","description":"In a vaulted prison the stocks have burst open and the chains lie broken. Paul, with a halo, and Silas look up startled, while the half-naked jailer, a torch-bearer behind him, raises his sword against himself. Through an arch at the back, later scenes of the story play out.","significance":"One of a series of scenes from the life of Paul designed by the Antwerp painter Maerten de Vos and engraved by Sadeler. The Latin verses below quote Acts 16:25–27.","symbolism":"The broken stocks and loose chains in the foreground make the miracle concrete; the raised sword is the moment before Paul's cry, \"Do not harm yourself.\"","impact":"Flemish prints like this carried Counter-Reformation designs all over Europe and served artists as models for paintings.","img":"img/philippianjailer0.jpg","credit":"Rijksmuseum, Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bekering_van_de_gevangenenbewaarder_van_Filippi,_RP-P-OB-5877.jpg"},{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"Paul freed from his chains by an earthquake","artist":"Anonymous Dutch engraver","year":"Between 1751 and 1810","medium":"Etching and engraving","location":"Rijksmuseum, Amsterdam","description":"By torchlight in the shattered prison, the jailer kneels with his sword pointed down and reaches out to Paul, who answers him with an open hand. Behind them a guard throws up his arms, and broken stones lie on the floor.","significance":"A small book illustration of the jailer's question, \"What must I do to be saved?\"","symbolism":"The sword, held point-down, has become harmless: the jailer who was about to take his own life now asks for salvation.","impact":"A typical 18th-century Dutch Bible illustration, made for reading at home.","img":"img/philippianjailer1.jpg","credit":"Rijksmuseum, Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paulus_door_een_aardbeving_uit_de_ketenen_bevrijd,_RP-P-1904-2940.jpg"}],"tags":["miracle","prayer"],"orderNote":"A crypt on the forum at Philippi has been honored since early Christian times as Paul's prison; it was originally a Roman cistern, so the identification is traditional. Luke stays behind at Philippi: the \"we\" does not resume until Acts 20:5, at Philippi."},
  {"id":"thessalonica","harmony":"Acts 17:1–9","lane":"paul","place":"thessalonica","from":"philippi","date":"c. AD 50","title":"Thessalonica: Jason's house and the uproar","people":["Paul","Silas","Timothy","Jason","Devout Greeks and leading women","The politarchs (city authorities)"],"gospels":{"Acts":"17:1–9","1Thess":"1:5–10; 2:1–16","Phil":"4:16"},"summary":"Paul and Silas follow the Via Egnatia west to Thessalonica, capital of Macedonia. For three sabbaths Paul argues in the synagogue from the scriptures that the Christ had to suffer and rise, and that Jesus is the Christ. Some Jews, many God-fearing Greeks and a number of leading women believe. Jealous opponents raise a mob, storm the house of their host Jason and drag him before the city authorities, crying that these men \"have turned the world upside down\" and proclaim another king, Jesus. Jason is made to post bond, and the believers send Paul and Silas away by night.","stay":"At least three sabbaths, probably longer: Paul worked night and day to support himself (1 Thessalonians 2:9), and the Philippians sent him help more than once (Philippians 4:16).","journey":"About 95 miles by land, four to five days on the Via Egnatia: by Amphipolis (about 30 miles) and Apollonia (about 27), each roughly a day's march apart, then on to Thessalonica (about 35).","route":[[24.287,41.013],[24.05,40.92],[23.84,40.82],[23.62,40.7],[23.45,40.65],[23.2,40.66],[22.94,40.64]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 17:2–3","text":"And Paul went in, as was his custom, and for three weeks he argued with them from the scriptures, explaining and proving that it was necessary for the Christ to suffer and to rise from the dead, and saying, \"This Jesus, whom I proclaim to you, is the Christ.\""},{"ref":"Acts 17:6–7","text":"And when they could not find them, they dragged Jason and some of the brethren before the city authorities, crying, \"These men who have turned the world upside down have come here also, and Jason has received them; and they are all acting against the decrees of Caesar, saying that there is another king, Jesus.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Isaiah 53:5","note":"The suffering Christ of Paul's argument: \"He was wounded for our transgressions, he was bruised for our iniquities.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"Saint Paul preaching to the Thessalonians","artist":"Gustave Doré","year":"1866","medium":"Wood engraving","location":"From La Grande Bible de Tours (1866)","description":"In a sunlit stone hall Paul stands with his arm raised, speaking to rows of bearded listeners seated along the walls; others sit apart in the shadow, listening or turning away.","significance":"One of the 241 plates of Doré's illustrated Bible, among the most widely reproduced Bible pictures of the 19th century.","symbolism":"The strong light falling on Paul and the long rows of hearers convey the three sabbaths of argument in the synagogue (Acts 17:2).","impact":"Doré's Bible was printed in many languages and editions and shaped how generations imagined the scenes of Acts.","img":"img/thessalonica0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DoreStPaulPreachingtotheThessalonians.jpg"},{"kind":"Artifact","title":"The politarch inscription from the Vardar Gate","artist":"Photograph by Richard Wilkison","year":"Before 1874","medium":"Photograph of a Greek inscription (1st–2nd century AD)","location":"British Museum, London (the inscription)","description":"A worn Greek inscription built into the old Vardar (\"Golden\") Gate of Thessaloniki, photographed before the gate was demolished in 1874. It begins by listing the city's politarchs.","significance":"Acts calls the Thessalonian magistrates \"politarchs\" (17:6, 8), a title once unknown to classical writers. This inscription, now in the British Museum, was among the first to confirm Luke's exact local term.","symbolism":"A small word in Acts, set in stone in the city itself.","impact":"Dozens more politarch inscriptions have since been found in Macedonia, making this a classic example of Luke's accuracy on local detail.","img":"img/thessalonica1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Politarch_inscription,_Golden_Gate_Thessaloniki,_before_1874.png"}],"tags":["teaching","conflict"],"orderNote":"Luke's title for the city authorities, politarchs, was once thought an error; it is now attested in more than thirty inscriptions from Macedonia, including one from Thessalonica's Vardar Gate. Paul wrote 1 and 2 Thessalonians to this young church a few months later from Corinth."},
  {"id":"beroea","harmony":"Acts 17:10–15","lane":"paul","place":"beroea","from":"thessalonica","date":"c. AD 50","title":"Beroea: searching the scriptures daily","people":["Paul","Silas","Timothy","The Jews of Beroea","Greek women of high standing"],"gospels":{"Acts":"17:10–15"},"summary":"Sent off by night, Paul and Silas reach Beroea and go straight to the synagogue. The Jews there are \"more noble\" than those in Thessalonica: they receive the word eagerly and examine the scriptures every day to see if it is true. Many believe, with a number of Greek women of high standing and men. When agitators come down from Thessalonica and stir up the crowds, the believers send Paul to the sea, while Silas and Timothy stay behind. Sopater of Beroea later travels with Paul (Acts 20:4).","journey":"About 45 miles by land, two days, leaving the Via Egnatia and heading southwest across the Axios and Haliacmon plain to Beroea, at the foot of Mount Vermion.","route":[[22.94,40.64],[22.75,40.6],[22.58,40.56],[22.4,40.54],[22.2,40.52]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 17:11","text":"Now these Jews were more noble than those in Thessalonica, for they received the word with all eagerness, examining the scriptures daily to see if these things were so."},{"ref":"Acts 17:12","text":"Many of them therefore believed, with not a few Greek women of high standing as well as men."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"The \"Bema of the Apostle Paul\" in Veria","artist":"Photograph by Berthold Werner","year":"2017","medium":"Photograph","location":"Veria (ancient Beroea), Greece","description":"A marble shrine with a mosaic of Saint Paul, holding a book and blessing, set above three old stone steps under a mosaic arch.","significance":"The monument marks the traditional place where Paul preached to the Jews of Beroea, who \"received the word with all eagerness\" (Acts 17:11).","symbolism":"The book in Paul's hand fits Beroea's praise: its people examined the scriptures daily.","impact":"Veria remains a pilgrimage site for Orthodox Christians, and its yearly Pauline festival (Pavleia) in late June draws large crowds.","img":"img/beroea0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Berthold Werner, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Veria_BW_2017-10-06_09-39-17.jpg"}],"tags":["teaching"],"orderNote":"A modern monument in Veria, the \"Bema of the Apostle Paul,\" marks the traditional spot where he preached."},
  {"id":"athens","harmony":"Acts 17:16–21","lane":"paul","place":"athens","from":"beroea","date":"c. AD 50","title":"Athens: a city full of idols","people":["Paul","Jews and devout persons","Epicurean and Stoic philosophers"],"gospels":{"Acts":"17:16–21","1Thess":"3:1–2"},"summary":"Waiting alone at Athens for Silas and Timothy, Paul is deeply stirred to see the city full of idols. He argues in the synagogue and every day in the agora, the marketplace below the Acropolis, with whoever is there. Epicurean and Stoic philosophers take him up: some call him a \"babbler,\" others think he preaches foreign gods, because he speaks of Jesus and the resurrection. Curious for something new, they bring him to the Areopagus to explain himself.","journey":"About 300 miles, probably by sea in about a week: from Beroea down to the coast near Pydna or Dion, south along the shore of Thessaly, through the Euripus channel inside Euboea, round Cape Sounion to the Piraeus and up to Athens. Some think he went by land, about 250 miles.","route":[[22.2,40.52],[22.58,40.37],[22.75,40.05],[23.05,39.45],[23.25,39.05],[23.55,38.75],[23.6,38.46],[23.95,38.2],[24.05,37.68],[23.8,37.8],[23.63,37.94],[23.7225,37.9715]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 17:16–17","text":"Now while Paul was waiting for them at Athens, his spirit was provoked within him as he saw that the city was full of idols. So he argued in the synagogue with the Jews and the devout persons, and in the market place every day with those who chanced to be there."},{"ref":"Acts 17:18","text":"Some also of the Epicurean and Stoic philosophers met him. And some said, \"What would this babbler say?\" Others said, \"He seems to be a preacher of foreign divinities\"—because he preached Jesus and the resurrection."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"The Athenian agora from the Areopagus","artist":"Photograph by Jakub Hałun","year":"2024","medium":"Photograph","location":"Athens, Greece","description":"Looking down from the Areopagus over the excavated agora, with the Doric temple of Hephaestus standing on its hill at the left and modern Athens spreading to the mountains beyond.","significance":"The agora was the marketplace where Paul argued daily with \"those who chanced to be there\" (Acts 17:17) and met the Stoic and Epicurean philosophers. The Stoics took their name from the Painted Stoa on its north side.","symbolism":"The temple of Hephaestus, already about 500 years old when Paul came, is one of the many shrines that filled the city he found \"full of idols.\"","impact":"The agora has been excavated by the American School of Classical Studies since 1931.","img":"img/athens0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Jakub Hałun, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_Hephaisteion_and_the_Agora,_Athens,_Greece,_20240531_1216_9593.jpg"}],"tags":["teaching"],"orderNote":"1 Thessalonians 3:1–2 says Timothy was sent back from Athens to Thessalonica, so Timothy seems to have joined Paul briefly at Athens; Acts mentions only their arrival later at Corinth (18:5)."},
  {"id":"areopagus","harmony":"Acts 17:22–34","lane":"paul","place":"athens","date":"c. AD 50","title":"Paul's speech on the Areopagus","people":["Paul","The Areopagus council","Dionysius the Areopagite","Damaris"],"gospels":{"Acts":"17:22–34"},"summary":"Standing before the Areopagus, Athens' ancient council, Paul begins from an altar he has seen inscribed \"To an unknown god.\" The God they worship as unknown, he says, made the world and everything in it, does not live in shrines made by hands, and is not far from any of us; he even quotes their own poets. Now God calls all people to repent, for he will judge the world by a man he has appointed and raised from the dead. At the word resurrection some mock and others want to hear more. A few believe, among them Dionysius, a member of the council, and a woman named Damaris.","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 17:22–23","text":"So Paul, standing in the middle of the Areopagus, said: \"Men of Athens, I perceive that in every way you are very religious. For as I passed along, and observed the objects of your worship, I found also an altar with this inscription, 'To an unknown god.' What therefore you worship as unknown, this I proclaim to you."},{"ref":"Acts 17:28","text":"for 'In him we live and move and have our being'; as even some of your poets have said, 'For we are indeed his offspring.'"},{"ref":"Acts 17:31","text":"because he has fixed a day on which he will judge the world in righteousness by a man whom he has appointed, and of this he has given assurance to all men by raising him from the dead.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Isaiah 42:5","note":"God the Creator \"who gives breath to the people upon it and spirit to those who walk in it.\""},{"ref":"1 Kings 8:27","note":"Solomon: \"Heaven and the highest heaven cannot contain you; how much less this house which I have built!\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"Saint Paul Preaching in Athens","artist":"Raphael","year":"1515–16","medium":"Bodycolour on paper mounted on canvas (tapestry cartoon)","location":"Victoria and Albert Museum, London (Royal Collection, on loan)","description":"Paul stands on a step with both arms raised, preaching to a crowd of Athenians. Some listen intently, some frown or argue; at the right a man and a woman, Dionysius and Damaris, step forward. A statue of Mars stands in a round temple behind them.","significance":"One of seven surviving cartoons Raphael made for tapestries commissioned by Pope Leo X for the Sistine Chapel, showing the acts of Peter and Paul.","symbolism":"Paul is placed above and apart, like a classical orator; the statue of Mars (Ares) names the hill of the Areopagus, and the pagan temple shows the idols that stirred his spirit.","impact":"The cartoons have been among the most copied compositions in European art; artists studied them for centuries as models of storytelling.","img":"img/areopagus0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:V&A_-_Raphael,_St_Paul_Preaching_in_Athens_(1515).jpg"},{"kind":"Artifact","title":"Bronze plaque of Paul's speech on the Areopagus","artist":"Photograph by Jebulon","year":"2016","medium":"Photograph","location":"Areopagus Hill, Athens","description":"A bronze plaque set into the rock at the foot of the Areopagus steps, inscribed with the Greek text of Paul's speech from Acts 17:22–31.","significance":"It marks the traditional place of the speech, the first recorded Christian sermon to Greek philosophers.","symbolism":"The words \"to an unknown god\" are still read in the language in which they were spoken.","impact":"The hill is a stop for nearly every Christian pilgrim to Athens.","img":"img/areopagus1.jpg","credit":"Photo: Jebulon, Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plaque_Areopagus_Hill_Preach_St_Paul_in_Athens,_Greece.jpg"}],"tags":["sermon"],"orderNote":"\"Areopagus\" can mean the Hill of Ares below the Acropolis or the council named after it, which in Roman times met in the Royal Stoa in the agora; Acts may mean either. The two quotations are usually traced to Epimenides of Crete and Aratus of Cilicia (Phaenomena 5). Tradition makes Dionysius the first bishop of Athens."},
  {"id":"aquilapriscilla","harmony":"Acts 18:1–4","lane":"paul","place":"corinth","from":"athens","date":"c. AD 50","title":"Corinth: tentmaking with Aquila and Priscilla","people":["Paul","Aquila","Priscilla (Prisca)"],"gospels":{"Acts":"18:1–4","1Cor":"2:1–5"},"summary":"Paul moves on to Corinth, the busy Roman capital of Achaia between two seas. There he meets Aquila, a Jew from Pontus, and his wife Priscilla, recently arrived from Italy because the emperor Claudius had expelled the Jews from Rome. They share his trade, tentmaking or leatherwork, so he lives and works with them. Every sabbath he argues in the synagogue, persuading Jews and Greeks. The couple became lifelong co-workers of Paul.","stay":"Paul lodged and worked with Aquila and Priscilla, supporting himself by his trade (Acts 18:3; 1 Corinthians 9:15–18).","journey":"About 50 miles by land, two to three days: west along the Sacred Way to Eleusis, through Megara, along the cliff road of the Scironian Rocks and across the Isthmus to Corinth.","route":[[23.7225,37.9715],[23.54,38.04],[23.35,38.0],[23.2,37.97],[23.05,37.93],[22.97,37.92],[22.879,37.906]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 18:2–3","text":"And he found a Jew named Aquila, a native of Pontus, lately come from Italy with his wife Priscilla, because Claudius had commanded all the Jews to leave Rome. And he went to see them; and because he was of the same trade he stayed with them, and they worked, for by trade they were tentmakers."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"Bust of the emperor Claudius","artist":"Photograph by Marie-Lan Nguyen","year":"2011","medium":"Photograph of a marble bust (AD 41–54)","location":"National Archaeological Museum, Naples","description":"A marble portrait of Claudius from his own reign, crowned with a wreath and wearing a toga.","significance":"Claudius (emperor AD 41–54) expelled the Jews from Rome, which brought Aquila and Priscilla to Corinth (Acts 18:2). He is also the emperor whose letter at Delphi names Gallio.","symbolism":"The wreath marks imperial honor; Acts sets the spread of the gospel against the decrees of this Caesar (17:7).","impact":"Acts names Claudius twice, for the famine of 11:28 and the expulsion of 18:2; the Roman historian Suetonius also records the expulsion.","img":"img/aquilapriscilla0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Marie-Lan Nguyen, CC BY 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Claudius_crop.jpg"}],"tags":[],"orderNote":"The Roman historian Suetonius says Claudius expelled the Jews from Rome because of riots \"at the instigation of Chrestus\" (probably disputes about Christ); the fifth-century Orosius dates the edict to AD 49. Paul later describes his arrival at Corinth \"in weakness and in much fear and trembling\" (1 Corinthians 2:3)."},
  {"id":"corinthvision","harmony":"Acts 18:5–11","lane":"paul","place":"corinth","date":"c. AD 50–51","title":"\"I have many people in this city\": the vision at Corinth","people":["Paul","Silas","Timothy","Titius Justus","Crispus, ruler of the synagogue","The Lord (in a vision)"],"gospels":{"Acts":"18:5–11","1Thess":"3:6","1Cor":"1:14"},"summary":"When Silas and Timothy arrive from Macedonia, bringing good news and a gift, Paul gives himself wholly to preaching. Opposed in the synagogue, he shakes out his garments and turns to the Gentiles, moving next door to the house of Titius Justus. Crispus, the ruler of the synagogue, believes with his whole household, and many Corinthians are baptized. One night the Lord speaks to Paul in a vision: \"Do not be afraid... for I am with you.\" He stays a year and a half, and from Corinth writes 1 and 2 Thessalonians, the earliest of his letters to survive.","stay":"A year and six months, teaching the word of God (Acts 18:11).","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 18:6","text":"And when they opposed and reviled him, he shook out his garments and said to them, \"Your blood be upon your heads! I am innocent. From now on I will go to the Gentiles.\""},{"ref":"Acts 18:9–10","text":"And the Lord said to Paul one night in a vision, \"Do not be afraid, but speak and do not be silent; for I am with you, and no man shall attack you to harm you; for I have many people in this city.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Jeremiah 1:8","note":"The Lord to the young prophet: \"Be not afraid of them, for I am with you to deliver you.\""},{"ref":"Ezekiel 33:4","note":"The watchman's warning: whoever does not heed it, \"his blood shall be upon his own head.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"Saint Paul at his Writing-Desk","artist":"Rembrandt","year":"c. 1629–30","medium":"Oil on panel","location":"Germanisches Nationalmuseum, Nuremberg","description":"An old, white-bearded Paul sits half-turned from his desk, pen in hand, lost in thought. Light falls from the right across his face and the papers; a sword hangs on the wall behind him.","significance":"An early work from Rembrandt's Leiden years. From Corinth Paul wrote 1 and 2 Thessalonians, the first of the letters that fill so much of the New Testament.","symbolism":"The sword is Paul's traditional attribute, the instrument of his martyrdom; the pen is the work he is known by.","impact":"Rembrandt returned to Paul again and again; this panel shows the young painter's mastery of light and inward stillness.","img":"img/corinthvision0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Paul_at_his_Writing-Desk,_by_Rembrandt.jpg"}],"tags":["teaching"],"orderNote":"1 Thessalonians 3:6 tells of Timothy's return with good news. Paul names Crispus among the few he baptized himself (1 Corinthians 1:14). An inscription found near the theater names an aedile Erastus, possibly the city treasurer Erastus of Romans 16:23, written from Corinth a few years later."},
  {"id":"gallio","harmony":"Acts 18:12–17","lane":"rome","place":"corinth","date":"AD 51–52","title":"Before Gallio's tribunal","people":["Gallio, proconsul of Achaia","Paul","Sosthenes, ruler of the synagogue","The Jews of Corinth"],"gospels":{"Acts":"18:12–17"},"summary":"When Gallio becomes proconsul of Achaia, Paul's opponents bring him before the tribunal, the bema in the forum, charging him with persuading people to worship God against the law. Before Paul can even speak, Gallio throws the case out: this is a matter of words and names and their own law, not a crime. The crowd then beats Sosthenes, the ruler of the synagogue, in front of the tribunal, and Gallio pays no attention. Rome's refusal to judge left Paul free to preach.","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 18:12–13","text":"But when Gallio was proconsul of Achaia, the Jews made a united attack upon Paul and brought him before the tribunal, saying, \"This man is persuading men to worship God contrary to the law.\""},{"ref":"Acts 18:14–15","text":"But when Paul was about to open his mouth, Gallio said to the Jews, \"If it were a matter of wrongdoing or vicious crime, I should have reason to bear with you, O Jews; but since it is a matter of questions about words and names and your own law, see to it yourselves; I refuse to be a judge of these things.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"The Gallio inscription","artist":"Photograph by Gérard","year":"2018","medium":"Photograph of a carved limestone fragment (c. AD 52)","location":"Archaeological Museum of Delphi, Greece","description":"A fragment of a Greek inscription from Delphi; the outlined word in the middle line is the name of Gallio.","significance":"The inscription records a letter of the emperor Claudius to Delphi, naming \"Junius Gallio... proconsul\" of Achaia and dated by Claudius' 26th acclamation as emperor. It fixes Gallio's year of office at AD 51–52 and so gives the anchor date for Paul's 18 months in Corinth.","symbolism":"A worn fragment of stone that lets historians put a date on a scene in Acts.","impact":"First published in 1905 from fragments found at Delphi, it is the most important single artifact for the chronology of Paul's life.","img":"img/gallio0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Gérard, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Delphes_Gallion.jpg"},{"kind":"Artifact","title":"The bema in the forum of Corinth","artist":"Photograph by George E. Koronaios","year":"2019","medium":"Photograph","location":"Ancient Corinth, Greece","description":"The stone platform of the rostra, or bema, rises from the middle of the Roman forum at Corinth, with fragments of marble columns at its edge.","significance":"The bema was the tribunal from which Roman officials spoke and gave judgment. It is usually identified with the \"tribunal\" before which Paul was brought to Gallio (Acts 18:12).","symbolism":"A raised seat of Roman justice, where Rome declined to judge the gospel.","impact":"Remains of a Byzantine church built over the bema show that Christians long remembered it as the site of Paul's trial.","img":"img/gallio1.jpg","credit":"Photo: George E. Koronaios, Public domain (CC0), via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_rostra_(Bema)_in_the_Roman_forum_of_the_Ancient_Corinth_on_6_April_2019.jpg"}],"tags":["conflict"],"orderNote":"Gallio, brother of the philosopher Seneca, is named in a letter of Claudius inscribed at Delphi, which places his proconsulship in AD 51–52 (or 52–53). It is the firmest fixed date in Paul's life. A Sosthenes is co-sender of 1 Corinthians (1:1); whether he is the same man is uncertain."},
  {"id":"cenchreae","harmony":"Acts 18:18","lane":"paul","place":"cenchreae","from":"corinth","date":"c. AD 51–52","title":"The vow at Cenchreae","people":["Paul","Priscilla","Aquila"],"gospels":{"Acts":"18:18","Rom":"16:1"},"summary":"After many more days in Corinth, Paul takes leave of the brethren and sets out for Syria with Priscilla and Aquila. At Cenchreae, Corinth's eastern port on the Saronic Gulf, he has his hair cut because of a vow. Luke does not say what the vow was. Cenchreae soon had its own church, whose deaconess Phoebe carried Paul's letter to the Romans.","journey":"About 7 miles by land, two or three hours, east across the isthmus from Corinth to its Aegean harbor.","route":[[22.879,37.906],[22.93,37.895],[22.99,37.88]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 18:18","text":"After this Paul stayed many days longer, and then took leave of the brethren and sailed for Syria, and with him Priscilla and Aquila. At Cenchreae he cut his hair, for he had a vow."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Numbers 6:18","note":"At the end of a Nazirite vow, \"the Nazirite shall shave his consecrated head.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"The sunken harbor of Cenchreae","artist":"Photograph by Heinz Schmitz","year":"1994","medium":"Photograph","location":"Kechries, Corinthia, Greece","description":"Ancient walls and harbor works run out into the clear, still water of the Saronic Gulf at Kechries, the site of Cenchreae.","significance":"From this port Paul sailed for Syria after his vow (Acts 18:18). Part of the harbor sank in later earthquakes; underwater excavations found remains including a sanctuary of Isis later turned into a church.","symbolism":"The quiet harbor faces east, toward Ephesus and Syria, the way Paul sailed.","impact":"Paul commends to Rome \"our sister Phoebe, a deaconess of the church at Cenchreae\" (Romans 16:1), probably the bearer of his letter.","img":"img/cenchreae0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Heinz Schmitz, CC BY-SA 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kenchreai.jpg"}],"tags":["prayer"],"orderNote":"The Greek could mean that Aquila, not Paul, cut his hair, but most take it of Paul. It may have been a Nazirite-type vow, though the hair of a Nazirite vow was normally shaved at the Temple, so some think of a private vow of thanksgiving."},
  {"id":"ephesusfirst","harmony":"Acts 18:19–21","lane":"paul","place":"ephesus","from":"cenchreae","date":"c. AD 52","title":"A first, brief visit to Ephesus","people":["Paul","Priscilla","Aquila","The Jews of Ephesus"],"gospels":{"Acts":"18:19–21"},"summary":"Sailing across the Aegean, Paul lands at Ephesus, the great city of Asia that the Spirit had kept him from on the way out. He leaves Priscilla and Aquila there and goes into the synagogue to reason with the Jews. They ask him to stay longer, but he declines, promising, \"I will return to you if God wills.\" He keeps that promise on his next journey, staying more than two years.","journey":"About 250 miles by sea, perhaps a week: out of the Saronic Gulf past Cape Sounion and east through the Cyclades by Mykonos, north of Ikaria and through the strait of Samos to the harbor of Ephesus.","route":[[22.99,37.88],[23.3,37.75],[23.75,37.65],[24.1,37.6],[24.6,37.55],[25.1,37.5],[25.5,37.45],[26.1,37.6],[26.6,37.68],[27.05,37.7],[27.25,37.9],[27.341,37.939]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 18:19","text":"And they came to Ephesus, and he left them there; but he himself went into the synagogue and argued with the Jews."},{"ref":"Acts 18:20–21","text":"When they asked him to stay for a longer period, he declined; but on taking leave of them he said, \"I will return to you if God wills,\" and he set sail from Ephesus."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"The Arcadiane, the harbor street of Ephesus","artist":"Photograph by Bernard Gagnon","year":"2014","medium":"Photograph","location":"Ephesus, near Selçuk, Turkey","description":"Seen from the great theater, a broad marble-paved street lined with column bases runs straight toward the long-silted harbor.","significance":"Arriving by sea from Cenchreae, Paul would have come up from the harbor along this route into the city (Acts 18:19). The colonnaded street was rebuilt by the emperor Arcadius around AD 400, over the earlier harbor road.","symbolism":"The road from the sea into the city: Paul's first, brief entry into a city where he would later spend more than two years.","impact":"The harbor now lies several miles inland, cut off by the silt of the Cayster river.","img":"img/ephesusfirst0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Bernard Gagnon, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ephesus_-_Arcadiane_Street_01.jpg"}],"tags":["teaching"],"orderNote":"Later manuscripts (the Western text, followed by the King James Version) add that Paul said, \"I must by all means keep this feast that comes in Jerusalem,\" giving a reason for his haste; the RSV follows the older manuscripts, which lack it."},
  {"id":"caesareajerusalem","harmony":"Acts 18:22","lane":"paul","place":"jerusalemchurch","from":"ephesus","date":"c. AD 52","title":"Landing at Caesarea and greeting the church","people":["Paul","The church at Jerusalem"],"gospels":{"Acts":"18:22"},"summary":"Paul sails from Ephesus to Caesarea, the Roman port of Judea. From there, Acts says only, he \"went up and greeted the church.\" Most take this to mean the mother church at Jerusalem: in Judea one always goes \"up\" to Jerusalem and \"down\" from it. It was Paul's fourth visit to Jerusalem since his conversion. Luke passes over it in a single verse.","journey":"About 600 miles by sea, two to three weeks: south past Samos, Cos and Rhodes, then across the open sea south of Cyprus to Caesarea; then about 65 miles by land up through the hills to Jerusalem, three days.","route":[[27.341,37.939],[27.15,37.65],[27.29,36.95],[27.8,36.65],[28.22,36.44],[29.3,36.1],[30.8,35.6],[32.3,34.7],[33.5,34.1],[34.4,33.1],[34.89,32.5],[34.93,32.1],[35.05,31.9],[35.231,31.776]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 18:22","text":"When he had landed at Caesarea, he went up and greeted the church"}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"The coast at Caesarea Maritima","artist":"Photograph by Yulia Kuprina","year":"2009","medium":"Photograph","location":"Caesarea National Park, Israel","description":"Waves break on the beach before the ruins of Caesarea, with the remains of the harbor and the old town on the point.","significance":"Herod the Great's artificial harbor made Caesarea the port of Judea and the seat of its Roman governors. Paul landed here at the end of his second journey and would later be held here two years (Acts 23–26).","symbolism":"The rough open sea shows why Herod's great breakwaters were needed on this harborless coast.","impact":"Most of Herod's harbor now lies under water and has been explored by marine archaeologists since the 1960s.","img":"img/caesareajerusalem0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Yulia Kuprina, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caesarea_Maritima.jpg"}],"tags":[],"orderNote":"Some read \"the church\" as the church at Caesarea itself, so that Paul never went to Jerusalem on this trip; the words \"went up\" and \"went down to Antioch\" fit Jerusalem better. The Western text's added vow to keep the feast in Jerusalem (18:21) supports a visit at a pilgrim festival, perhaps Passover or Pentecost of AD 52."},
  {"id":"backtoantioch","harmony":"Acts 18:22","lane":"paul","place":"antioch","from":"jerusalemchurch","date":"c. AD 52","title":"Back to Antioch: the second journey ends","people":["Paul","The church at Antioch"],"gospels":{"Acts":"18:22"},"summary":"From Jerusalem Paul goes down to Antioch in Syria, the church that had sent him out. The second journey, roughly three years and well over 2,500 miles, ends where it began. In that time churches had been planted in Philippi, Thessalonica, Beroea and Corinth, and the gospel had reached Europe. After spending some time at Antioch, Paul sets out again (18:23), and the third journey begins.","stay":"Paul spent \"some time\" at Antioch before leaving on his third journey (Acts 18:23).","journey":"About 400 miles: by land down to Caesarea and north along the coast road through Ptolemais, Tyre, Sidon and Berytus to Laodicea and Antioch, three weeks or more; or by ship from Caesarea to Seleucia, the port of Antioch.","route":[[35.231,31.776],[34.93,32.1],[34.89,32.5],[35.07,32.92],[35.2,33.27],[35.37,33.56],[35.5,33.89],[35.84,34.43],[35.85,35.0],[35.78,35.52],[36.161,36.202]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 18:22","text":"When he had landed at Caesarea, he went up and greeted the church, and then went down to Antioch."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"The Orontes at Antakya (Antioch)","artist":"Photograph by Елена Карвовская","year":"2013","medium":"Photograph","location":"Antakya, Hatay Province, Turkey","description":"The Orontes river flows between stone embankments through the center of modern Antakya at sunset.","significance":"Antioch on the Orontes was Paul's home base: the church there sent him out on each journey and welcomed him back (Acts 13:1–3; 14:26–28; 18:22).","symbolism":"The river that gave the city its name still runs through it, though little of the ancient city can be seen above ground.","impact":"Much of the old city was destroyed in the earthquakes of February 2023, ten years after this photograph.","img":"img/backtoantioch0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Елена Карвовская, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hatay_Antakya_Turkey_2013_River.jpg"}],"tags":[],"orderNote":"Some date the confrontation with Peter at Antioch (Galatians 2:11–14) to this stay rather than to the time before the Jerusalem council."},
  {"id":"galatiaphrygia","harmony":"Acts 18:23","lane":"paul","place":"phrygia","from":"antioch","date":"c. AD 52","title":"Through Galatia and Phrygia","people":["Paul"],"gospels":{"Acts":"18:23","1Cor":"16:1"},"summary":"After some time in Antioch, Paul sets out on his third journey. He goes from place to place through the region of Galatia and Phrygia, strengthening all the disciples. These are the churches he founded on his first journey and visited again on his second: Derbe, Lystra, Iconium and Antioch of Pisidia. On this journey he also begins the collection for the poor in Jerusalem, which he asked the churches of Galatia to take part in.","journey":"About 450 miles by land: north from Antioch over the Amanus pass, west along the coast to Tarsus, up through the Cilician Gates onto the plateau, then on to Derbe, Lystra, Iconium and Antioch of Pisidia. At least a month or two of walking, with stops.","route":[[36.161,36.202],[36.22,36.49],[36.2,36.85],[35.4,36.98],[34.895,36.918],[34.78,37.3],[34.55,37.45],[33.36,37.35],[32.33,37.58],[32.48,37.87],[31.8,38.1],[31.189,38.306],[31.5,39.2]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 18:23","text":"After spending some time there he departed and went from place to place through the region of Galatia and Phrygia, strengthening all the disciples."},{"ref":"1 Corinthians 16:1","text":"Now concerning the contribution for the saints: as I directed the churches of Galatia, so you also are to do."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"The Cilician Gates (Gülek Pass)","artist":"Photo: Teogomez","year":"2007","medium":"Photograph","location":"Taurus Mountains, north of Tarsus, Turkey","description":"A modern highway runs through a narrow gorge between high limestone cliffs; the road sign reads \"Gülek Boğazı,\" the Turkish name of the pass.","significance":"The Cilician Gates were the one main pass from Tarsus and the coast up through the Taurus Mountains onto the plateau of Asia Minor. Alexander the Great marched through them, and Paul most likely took this road to reach Derbe and Lystra.","symbolism":"The tight walls of the pass show why travelers went in company: Paul speaks of \"danger from robbers\" on his journeys (2 Corinthians 11:26).","impact":"The pass has carried the main road between Syria and Anatolia for some three thousand years, and still does.","img":"img/galatiaphrygia0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Puertas_Cilícias.jpg"}],"tags":["teaching"],"orderNote":"Luke gives this journey one verse. \"The region of Galatia and Phrygia\" most likely means the churches of southern Galatia from the first journey; some scholars (the North Galatian view) think Paul also went to the ethnic Galatians around Ancyra. Dates for the start of the third journey run from AD 52 to 54."},
  {"id":"apollos","harmony":"Acts 18:24–26","lane":"paul","place":"ephesus","date":"c. AD 52","title":"Apollos, Priscilla and Aquila at Ephesus","people":["Apollos","Priscilla","Aquila"],"gospels":{"Acts":"18:24–26"},"summary":"While Paul is away, a Jew named Apollos comes to Ephesus from Alexandria. He is eloquent, knows the Scriptures well and teaches accurately about Jesus, though he knows only the baptism of John. Priscilla and Aquila, Paul's fellow tentmakers whom he had left in Ephesus, hear him in the synagogue. They take him aside and explain the way of God to him more accurately.","stay":"Priscilla and Aquila had stayed on in Ephesus when Paul sailed for Syria (Acts 18:18–19); later a church met in their house there (1 Corinthians 16:19).","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 18:24–25","text":"Now a Jew named Apollos, a native of Alexandria, came to Ephesus. He was an eloquent man, well versed in the scriptures. He had been instructed in the way of the Lord; and being fervent in spirit, he spoke and taught accurately the things concerning Jesus, though he knew only the baptism of John."},{"ref":"Acts 18:26","text":"He began to speak boldly in the synagogue; but when Priscilla and Aquila heard him, they took him and expounded to him the way of God more accurately."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"Companions of Paul: Silas, Timothy, Aquila, Priscilla, Titus and Apollos","artist":"Clayton and Bell","year":"1903","medium":"Stained glass","location":"All Saints Church, Hove, England","description":"Six tall lights each show one of Paul's fellow workers standing under a canopy, named on a scroll above: from left Silas, Timothy, Aquila, Priscilla, Titus and Apollos, who holds a book of the Scriptures. Small scenes from their stories fill the panels below.","significance":"Made by Clayton and Bell, one of the leading Victorian stained-glass studios, for the large Gothic Revival church of All Saints in Hove.","symbolism":"Priscilla and Aquila stand side by side in the center, as the couple who taught Apollos; Apollos' book recalls that he was \"well versed in the scriptures.\"","impact":"Few works of art show Apollos at all; here he takes his place among the circle that carried the gospel with Paul.","img":"img/apollos0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Antiquary, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:All_Saints,_Hove_glass_15.jpg"}],"tags":["teaching"],"orderNote":"Alexandria had the largest Jewish community outside Judea and was a great center of learning. Luke does not say where Apollos first heard about Jesus. Martin Luther suggested that Apollos wrote the Letter to the Hebrews; this is only a guess."},
  {"id":"apolloscorinth","harmony":"Acts 18:27–19:1","lane":"paul","place":"corinth","from":"ephesus","date":"c. AD 52–53","title":"Apollos waters the church at Corinth","people":["Apollos"],"gospels":{"Acts":"18:27–19:1","1Cor":"3:4–6"},"summary":"Apollos wants to cross over to Achaia, and the believers at Ephesus write a letter asking the disciples there to welcome him. At Corinth he is a great help to those who had believed. He refutes the Jews in public, showing from the Scriptures that the Christ is Jesus. Later some Corinthians said \"I belong to Apollos\" against those who said \"I belong to Paul\"; Paul answers that both are only servants.","journey":"About 250 miles by sea, west from Ephesus across the Aegean past Samos and the Cyclades to Cenchreae, the eastern port of Corinth. Three to five days with good winds.","route":[[27.341,37.939],[27.1,37.88],[26.7,37.8],[26.0,37.62],[25.4,37.48],[24.95,37.45],[24.4,37.55],[23.9,37.68],[23.4,37.8],[22.99,37.88],[22.879,37.906]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 18:27–28","text":"And when he wished to cross to Achaia, the brethren encouraged him, and wrote to the disciples to receive him. When he arrived, he greatly helped those who through grace had believed, for he powerfully confuted the Jews in public, showing by the scriptures that the Christ was Jesus."},{"ref":"1 Corinthians 3:6","text":"I planted, Apollos watered, but God gave the growth."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"Papyrus 46: 1 Corinthians 2:11–3:5","artist":"Unknown Egyptian scribe","year":"c. AD 200","medium":"Ink on papyrus","location":"University of Michigan Papyrology Collection, Ann Arbor","description":"A page from the oldest surviving book of Paul's letters. Near the foot of the page, in Greek capitals, are the words of 1 Corinthians 3:4: \"I belong to Paul,\" and \"I belong to Apollos.\"","significance":"Papyrus 46 is a codex of Paul's letters copied in Egypt around AD 200, about 150 years after he wrote. Its 86 surviving leaves are divided between the Chester Beatty Library in Dublin and the University of Michigan.","symbolism":"The page number at the top (79, written in Greek letters) shows this was a bound book, not a scroll: early Christians favored the codex for their Scriptures.","impact":"Its text is a key witness for scholars reconstructing the Greek text of Paul's letters.","img":"img/apolloscorinth0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ann_Arbor,_University_of_Michigan_P.Mich.inv._6238_(Papyrus_46)_fol._79v_1_Cor_2,_11–3,_5.jpg"}],"tags":["teaching","conflict"]},
  {"id":"johnsdisciples","harmony":"Acts 19:1–7","lane":"paul","place":"ephesus","from":"phrygia","date":"c. AD 53","title":"The disciples of John at Ephesus","people":["Paul","About twelve disciples"],"gospels":{"Acts":"19:1–7"},"summary":"Paul comes down to Ephesus through the upper country. There he finds about twelve disciples who have received only John's baptism and have never heard that there is a Holy Spirit. Paul explains that John pointed to the one coming after him, Jesus. They are baptized in the name of the Lord Jesus, and when Paul lays his hands on them the Holy Spirit comes on them, and they speak in tongues and prophesy. It is a small Pentecost in Asia, like the ones in Samaria and at Cornelius' house.","journey":"About 250 miles west by land from central Phrygia, by the \"upper country\": the high road across the plateau and down the Cayster valley to Ephesus, rather than the busier Lycus and Maeander valleys. About two to three weeks on foot.","route":[[31.5,39.2],[30.55,38.75],[29.9,38.65],[29.4,38.62],[28.7,38.4],[28.2,38.25],[27.8,38.15],[27.5,38.02],[27.341,37.939]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 19:2","text":"And he said to them, \"Did you receive the Holy Spirit when you believed?\" And they said, \"No, we have never even heard that there is a Holy Spirit.\""},{"ref":"Acts 19:5–6","text":"On hearing this, they were baptized in the name of the Lord Jesus. And when Paul had laid his hands upon them, the Holy Spirit came on them; and they spoke with tongues and prophesied."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Joel 2:28","note":"\"I will pour out my spirit on all flesh; your sons and your daughters shall prophesy\": the promise Peter quoted at Pentecost."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"Paul Baptizes the Disciples at Ephesus","artist":"After Maarten van Heemskerck; published by Hieronymus Cock","year":"1553","medium":"Engraving","location":"Rijksmuseum, Amsterdam","description":"Paul, seated, pours water over the head of a kneeling man from a basin, while other men wait, pray and look up as the dove of the Holy Spirit flies in at the top right. On the floor lie open books.","significance":"One of a series of prints of the Acts of the Apostles designed by the Dutch painter Heemskerck and published in Antwerp by Hieronymus Cock, the leading print publisher of the day.","symbolism":"The Latin caption reads, \"At Ephesus Paul initiates into Christ those baptized only with John's baptism\"; the dove shows the Spirit coming on them as Paul lays on his hands.","impact":"Heemskerck, who had studied ancient sculpture in Rome, gave biblical figures the heroic, muscular bodies of Michelangelo; his print designs spread that style across northern Europe.","img":"img/johnsdisciples0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paulus_doopt_de_inwoners_van_Efeze,_RP-P-OB-7279.jpg"}],"tags":["teaching","miracle"],"orderNote":"Paul had promised to return to Ephesus \"if God wills\" (Acts 18:21). The Church has seen in the laying on of hands after baptism here a witness to the sacrament of Confirmation."},
  {"id":"tyrannus","harmony":"Acts 19:8–10","lane":"paul","place":"ephesus","date":"c. AD 53–55","title":"Two years in the hall of Tyrannus","people":["Paul","Tyrannus"],"gospels":{"Acts":"19:8–10","1Cor":"16:8–9"},"summary":"For three months Paul speaks boldly in the synagogue about the kingdom of God. When some harden their hearts and speak evil of the Way, he takes the disciples and argues daily in the lecture hall of Tyrannus. This goes on for two years, so that all who live in the province of Asia, Jews and Greeks, hear the word of the Lord. The churches of the Lycus valley, Colossae, Laodicea and Hierapolis, were likely founded in these years by Paul's helpers such as Epaphras. From Ephesus Paul also writes 1 Corinthians.","stay":"About three years in all at Ephesus (Acts 20:31), Paul's longest stay anywhere on his journeys. He worked with his own hands to support himself (Acts 20:34).","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 19:9–10","text":"but when some were stubborn and disbelieved, speaking evil of the Way before the congregation, he withdrew from them, taking the disciples with him, and argued daily in the hall of Tyrannus. This continued for two years, so that all the residents of Asia heard the word of the Lord, both Jews and Greeks."},{"ref":"1 Corinthians 16:8–9","text":"But I will stay in Ephesus until Pentecost, for a wide door for effective work has opened to me, and there are many adversaries."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"Curetes Street, Ephesus","artist":"Photo: shankar s.","year":"2014","medium":"Photograph","location":"Ephesus, near Selçuk, Turkey","description":"The marble-paved main street of Ephesus runs downhill between rows of columns, statue bases and ruined facades toward the Library of Celsus.","significance":"Curetes Street was one of the city's chief streets in Paul's day, though many of the buildings seen now are later. The hall of Tyrannus has not been found; it was likely a lecture hall or school building in the city center.","symbolism":"For two years Paul argued here daily, in the heart of a great pagan city, so that \"all the residents of Asia heard the word of the Lord.\"","impact":"Ephesus became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2015.","img":"img/tyrannus0.jpg","credit":"Photo: shankar s., CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Looking_down_the_Street_of_Curetes_(16314212482).jpg"}],"tags":["teaching","sermon","conflict"],"orderNote":"An old Western reading of Acts 19:9 adds that Paul taught \"from the fifth hour to the tenth,\" about 11 a.m. to 4 p.m., the hot midday hours when the hall stood empty. Many scholars think Paul was also imprisoned for a time at Ephesus, and that some of the \"prison letters\" (Philippians, Philemon) may come from here rather than Rome; this is debated."},
  {"id":"sceva","harmony":"Acts 19:11–17","lane":"paul","place":"ephesus","date":"c. AD 54–55","title":"Miracles at Ephesus and the sons of Sceva","people":["Paul","The seven sons of Sceva"],"gospels":{"Acts":"19:11–17"},"summary":"God works extraordinary miracles through Paul: even handkerchiefs and aprons that have touched him are carried to the sick, and they are healed and evil spirits leave them. Some wandering Jewish exorcists, the seven sons of a Jewish high priest named Sceva, try to use the name of Jesus like a spell. The evil spirit answers, \"Jesus I know, and Paul I know; but who are you?\" The possessed man leaps on them and overpowers them, and they flee naked and wounded. Fear falls on Ephesus, and the name of the Lord Jesus is praised.","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 19:11–12","text":"And God did extraordinary miracles by the hands of Paul, so that handkerchiefs or aprons were carried away from his body to the sick, and diseases left them and the evil spirits came out of them."},{"ref":"Acts 19:15","text":"But the evil spirit answered them, \"Jesus I know, and Paul I know; but who are you?\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"2 Kings 13:21","note":"A dead man touches the bones of Elisha and comes back to life: an Old Testament root of the veneration of relics."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"The Sons of Sceva Beaten by the Demon","artist":"Mathieu Elias","year":"1702","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Notre-Dame de Paris","description":"A possessed man, bare-chested, swings a chain over the sons of Sceva; one falls in terror at the front, stripped of his clothes, while women clutch a child and flee. In the background, under a domed temple, Paul appears among the sick he heals.","significance":"The \"May\" of 1702, given to Notre-Dame by the Paris goldsmiths' guild. It was shown newly restored at the Galerie des Gobelins in 2024, where this photo was taken.","symbolism":"The violence in front and Paul's calm healing behind set the true power of Jesus' name against those who tried to use it as a spell.","impact":"Elias, born in French Flanders, worked mostly in Dunkirk and Paris; this is his best-known painting.","img":"img/sceva0.jpg","credit":"Photo: GO69, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mathieu_Elias_-_Les_fils_de_Scéva_battus_par_le_démon_-_01.jpg"}],"tags":["miracle","healing","exorcism"],"orderNote":"No Jewish high priest named Sceva is known from other sources; he may have been from a high-priestly family, or the title may have been his own claim. The \"handkerchiefs and aprons\" were probably the sweat-cloths and work aprons Paul wore at his trade."},
  {"id":"magicbooks","harmony":"Acts 19:18–22","lane":"paul","place":"ephesus","date":"c. AD 55","title":"The burning of the magic books","people":["Paul","New believers of Ephesus","Timothy","Erastus"],"gospels":{"Acts":"19:18–22"},"summary":"Many who have become believers come forward and openly confess their practices. Those who had practiced magic bring their books and burn them in front of everyone. The books are worth fifty thousand pieces of silver, a fortune. \"So the word of the Lord grew and prevailed mightily.\" Paul then resolves in the Spirit to pass through Macedonia and Achaia, go to Jerusalem, and afterward see Rome; he sends Timothy and Erastus ahead into Macedonia.","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 19:19–20","text":"And a number of those who practiced magic arts brought their books together and burned them in the sight of all; and they counted the value of them and found it came to fifty thousand pieces of silver. So the word of the Lord grew and prevailed mightily."},{"ref":"Acts 19:21","text":"Now after these events Paul resolved in the Spirit to pass through Macedonia and Achaia and go to Jerusalem, saying, \"After I have been there, I must also see Rome.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Deuteronomy 18:10–12","note":"Divination, sorcery and charms are forbidden as \"an abomination to the Lord.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"The Preaching of Saint Paul at Ephesus","artist":"Eustache Le Sueur","year":"1649","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Musée du Louvre, Paris","description":"Paul, in a red mantle, stands on the steps of a classical portico and points to heaven. Around him the Ephesians bring armfuls of books; in the foreground a kneeling man feeds a fire with books and scrolls, while a scribe on the left writes down Paul's words.","significance":"Painted as the \"May\" of 1649, the large picture the Paris goldsmiths' guild gave each year to Notre-Dame Cathedral. It is often counted Le Sueur's masterpiece.","symbolism":"The upward finger and the downward fire set heaven against magic; the temple and the statue on the right stand for the pagan city that is giving up its arts.","impact":"Le Sueur, called \"the French Raphael,\" made the scene a model of French classical painting; it was widely copied and engraved.","img":"img/magicbooks0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eustache_Le_Sueur_-_The_Preaching_of_St_Paul_at_Ephesus_-_WGA12613.jpg"}],"tags":["conflict"],"orderNote":"Ephesus was famous in the ancient world for magic; spells written on papyrus were called \"Ephesian letters\" (Ephesia grammata). A piece of silver was about a day's wage, so the books were worth some fifty thousand days' wages. From this point Luke's story looks toward Rome."},
  {"id":"ephesusriot","harmony":"Acts 19:23–41","lane":"paul","place":"ephesus","date":"Spring AD 56","title":"The riot of the silversmiths","people":["Demetrius the silversmith","Gaius","Aristarchus","Alexander","The town clerk"],"gospels":{"Acts":"19:23–41","1Cor":"15:32","2Cor":"1:8–10"},"summary":"Demetrius, a silversmith who makes silver shrines of Artemis, gathers the craftsmen. Paul's preaching that gods made with hands are not gods is ruining their trade and threatens the honor of the great temple of Artemis. The crowd shouts \"Great is Artemis of the Ephesians!\" and rushes into the theater, dragging Paul's companions Gaius and Aristarchus. Paul wants to go in, but the disciples and some friendly officials of Asia hold him back. For two hours the crowd chants, until the town clerk quiets them and sends them home, warning that the courts are open and Rome may charge the city with rioting.","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 19:26","text":"And you see and hear that not only at Ephesus but almost throughout all Asia this Paul has persuaded and turned away a considerable company of people, saying that gods made with hands are not gods."},{"ref":"Acts 19:28–29","text":"When they heard this they were enraged, and cried out, \"Great is Artemis of the Ephesians!\" So the city was filled with the confusion; and they rushed together into the theater, dragging with them Gaius and Aristarchus, Macedonians who were Paul's companions in travel."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Psalm 115:4","note":"\"Their idols are silver and gold, the work of men's hands.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"The Great Theater of Ephesus","artist":"Photo: Slyronit","year":"2011 (theater 3rd century BC, enlarged in Roman times)","medium":"Photograph","location":"Ephesus, near Selçuk, Turkey","description":"The steep, curved banks of seats of the great theater rise up the slope of Mount Pion, dwarfing the visitors on the stage floor below.","significance":"This is the theater into which the mob rushed, dragging Gaius and Aristarchus, and where for two hours they shouted \"Great is Artemis of the Ephesians!\" It held about 25,000 people.","symbolism":"The town clerk's speech was made to the crowd in this space, the city's official place of assembly.","impact":"Still one of the largest ancient theaters to survive, it is used for concerts today.","img":"img/ephesusriot0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Slyronit, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Theatre_of_Ephesus_2.jpg"}],"tags":["conflict"],"orderNote":"The Temple of Artemis at Ephesus was one of the Seven Wonders of the ancient world; the theater held about 25,000 people. Paul's words about \"fighting with beasts at Ephesus\" (1 Corinthians 15:32) and his deadly peril in Asia (2 Corinthians 1:8) may refer to this time, or to other dangers there; Luke does not say. The riot is usually dated to spring AD 55 or 56, around the festival month of Artemis."},
  {"id":"macedonia3","harmony":"Acts 20:1–2","lane":"paul","place":"macedonia","from":"ephesus","date":"Summer–autumn AD 56","title":"Through Macedonia, waiting for Titus","people":["Paul","Titus","Timothy"],"gospels":{"Acts":"20:1–2","2Cor":"2:12–13; 7:5–7","Rom":"15:19"},"summary":"After the uproar Paul encourages the disciples, says goodbye and leaves for Macedonia. His letters fill in what Luke leaves out: he stops at Troas to preach, but cannot rest because Titus has not come with news from troubled Corinth. In Macedonia he is afflicted on every side until Titus arrives with good news, and Paul writes 2 Corinthians. He goes through the region giving the churches much encouragement, and perhaps reaches as far as Illyricum.","journey":"About 450 miles: north by land from Ephesus through Smyrna and Pergamum to Troas (about 200 miles), by ship past Samothrace to Neapolis (about 125 miles, two days), then along the Via Egnatia through Philippi, Amphipolis and Thessalonica. Several months with stops in the churches.","route":[[27.341,37.939],[27.14,38.42],[27.0,38.8],[27.18,39.12],[27.02,39.6],[26.4,39.7],[26.155,39.752],[25.53,40.47],[24.41,40.94],[24.287,41.013],[23.84,40.82],[23.43,40.64],[22.94,40.64],[22.5,40.2]],"scripture":[{"ref":"2 Corinthians 2:12–13","text":"When I came to Troas to preach the gospel of Christ, a door was opened for me in the Lord; but my mind could not rest because I did not find my brother Titus there. So I took leave of them and went on to Macedonia."},{"ref":"2 Corinthians 7:5–6","text":"For even when we came into Macedonia, our bodies had no rest but we were afflicted at every turn—fighting without and fear within. But God, who comforts the downcast, comforted us by the coming of Titus,"}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"Papyrus 46: 2 Corinthians 11:33–12:9","artist":"Unknown Egyptian scribe","year":"c. AD 200","medium":"Ink on papyrus","location":"University of Michigan Papyrology Collection, Ann Arbor","description":"A leaf of 2 Corinthians, the letter Paul wrote from Macedonia after Titus brought news from Corinth. This page runs from his escape over the wall of Damascus in a basket to the \"thorn in the flesh\" and the Lord's answer, \"My grace is sufficient for you.\"","significance":"Papyrus 46, copied in Egypt around AD 200, is the oldest surviving collection of Paul's letters; this leaf belongs to the part kept at the University of Michigan.","symbolism":"On this one page Paul boasts only of his weakness: \"when I am weak, then I am strong\" follows on the next lines of the letter.","impact":"2 Corinthians is the most personal of Paul's letters, and much of what is known of his journey through Macedonia comes from it.","img":"img/macedonia30.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ann_Arbor,_University_of_Michigan_P.Mich.inv._6238_(Papyrus_46)_fol._142r_-_2_Corinthians_11,33-12,9.jpg"}],"tags":["teaching"],"orderNote":"Luke covers these months in two verses; the order comes from 2 Corinthians. Romans 15:19 says Paul preached \"as far round as Illyricum\" (modern Albania and the former Yugoslavia), probably on this visit, going west along the Via Egnatia. Some date this part of the journey to AD 55–56."},
  {"id":"greece","harmony":"Acts 20:2–3","lane":"paul","place":"corinth","from":"macedonia","date":"Winter AD 56–57","title":"Three months in Greece: the Letter to the Romans","people":["Paul","Gaius","Erastus","Phoebe"],"gospels":{"Acts":"20:2–3","Rom":"15:25–26; 16:1, 23"},"summary":"Paul comes to Greece and spends three winter months there, most likely at Corinth in the house of Gaius. There he writes his Letter to the Romans, the fullest statement of his gospel, to a church he has not yet visited; Phoebe of nearby Cenchreae probably carried it. He is gathering the collection from Macedonia and Achaia for the poor in Jerusalem. As he is about to sail for Syria, he learns of a plot by some of the Jews and decides to go back through Macedonia instead.","stay":"Three months (Acts 20:3), probably as the guest of Gaius, \"who is host to me and to the whole church\" (Romans 16:23).","journey":"About 300 miles by land south from Macedonia through Thessaly, past Thermopylae and Thebes, to the Isthmus and Corinth. About two weeks on foot.","route":[[22.5,40.2],[22.42,39.64],[22.4,39.1],[22.55,38.8],[23.0,38.55],[23.32,38.32],[23.35,38.0],[23.1,37.95],[22.879,37.906]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Romans 15:25–26","text":"At present, however, I am going to Jerusalem with aid for the saints. For Macedonia and Achaia have been pleased to make some contribution for the poor among the saints at Jerusalem;"},{"ref":"Romans 16:23","text":"Gaius, who is host to me and to the whole church, greets you. Erastus, the city treasurer, and our brother Quartus, greet you."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"Saint Paul Writing His Epistles","artist":"Valentin de Boulogne (attributed)","year":"c. 1618–1620","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Museum of Fine Arts, Houston (Blaffer Foundation)","description":"Paul, bald and red-bearded in a red mantle, sits at a plain table writing with a quill, a letter open in his other hand. Books and a scroll lie around him, and the hilt of a sword rises at the right.","significance":"Valentin, a French follower of Caravaggio working in Rome, shows Paul as a real man at work rather than a distant saint. The Letter to the Romans, written in Corinth, is the longest of his epistles.","symbolism":"The sword is Paul's traditional emblem, the instrument of his martyrdom in Rome and \"the sword of the Spirit, which is the word of God\" (Ephesians 6:17).","impact":"The strong light from the left and the dark background are typical of the Caravaggesque painting that swept Rome in the 1610s.","img":"img/greece0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Valentin_de_Boulogne_-_Saint_Paul_Writing_His_Epistles_-_BF.1991.4_-_Museum_of_Fine_Arts.jpg"},{"kind":"Artifact","title":"The Erastus inscription","artist":"Unknown","year":"Mid-1st century AD","medium":"Limestone paving slab with letters once inlaid in bronze","location":"Ancient Corinth, near the theater","description":"A paving stone with large Latin letters: \"Erastus, in return for his aedileship, laid this pavement at his own expense.\" The letters were cut to hold bronze inlay, now lost.","significance":"Found in 1929 near the theater of Corinth. Many scholars identify this Erastus with \"Erastus, the city treasurer\" who sends greetings in Romans 16:23, written from Corinth; others think the titles do not match or date it later.","symbolism":"A public gift by a rich official: a glimpse of the kind of well-placed people who joined Paul's church in Corinth.","impact":"One of the very few inscriptions that may name a person from the New Testament.","img":"img/greece1.jpg","credit":"Photo: Rduncan2, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Erastus_stone_at_Theater_of_Corinth_(cropped).jpg"}],"tags":["teaching","conflict"],"orderNote":"Acts says only \"Greece\"; Corinth is inferred from Romans 16 (Gaius, Erastus the city treasurer, Phoebe of Cenchreae). Most scholars date Romans to the winter of AD 56–57, some to 55–56 or 57–58. The plot may have been to kill Paul on a pilgrim ship bound for Passover, with the collection money aboard."},
  {"id":"philippipassover","harmony":"Acts 20:3–6","lane":"paul","place":"philippi","from":"corinth","date":"Spring AD 57","title":"Back through Macedonia: Passover at Philippi","people":["Paul","Luke","Sopater","Aristarchus","Secundus","Gaius of Derbe","Timothy","Tychicus","Trophimus"],"gospels":{"Acts":"20:3–6"},"summary":"Paul turns back north through Macedonia with a band of companions from the churches: Sopater of Beroea, Aristarchus and Secundus of Thessalonica, Gaius of Derbe, Timothy, and Tychicus and Trophimus of Asia. They likely carry the collection on behalf of their churches. The others go ahead to Troas. Paul keeps the days of Unleavened Bread at Philippi, and here the \"we\" of Luke's narrative begins again: Luke, last with Paul at Philippi years before, rejoins him.","journey":"About 400 miles back by land, north through Thessaly to Thessalonica, then east along the Via Egnatia to Philippi. About three weeks.","route":[[22.879,37.906],[23.35,38.0],[23.32,38.32],[22.55,38.8],[22.42,39.64],[22.5,40.2],[22.94,40.64],[23.43,40.64],[23.84,40.82],[24.287,41.013]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 20:4–5","text":"Sopater of Beroea, the son of Pyrrhus, accompanied him; and of the Thessalonians, Aristarchus and Secundus; and Gaius of Derbe, and Timothy; and the Asians, Tychicus and Trophimus. These went on and were waiting for us at Troas,"}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"The forum of Philippi in spring","artist":"Photo: Carole Raddato","year":"2012 (forum 2nd century AD; basilica 6th century)","medium":"Photograph","location":"Philippi, Greece","description":"The paved Roman forum of Philippi in April, with the tall piers of a great 6th-century church, Basilica B, behind it and the snow-capped Pangaion range beyond.","significance":"Paul kept the Passover days here in spring AD 57, among the believers of the first church he founded in Europe. The forum seen today was rebuilt in the 2nd century over the one Paul knew.","symbolism":"Spring flowers in the ancient streets match the season of Unleavened Bread, when Luke rejoined Paul here.","impact":"Philippi has been a UNESCO World Heritage Site since 2016.","img":"img/philippipassover0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Carole Raddato, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_on_the_forum_and_the_basilica_B_with_Pangaion_Hills_in_the_background,_Philippi_(7272434092).jpg"}],"tags":["prayer"],"orderNote":"The \"we\" passages of Acts stopped at Philippi in Acts 16:17 and resume here at Acts 20:5; the traditional reading is that Luke, the author, stayed at Philippi in between. The days of Unleavened Bread fell in April AD 57."},
  {"id":"eutychus","harmony":"Acts 20:7–12","lane":"paul","place":"troas","from":"philippi","date":"April AD 57","title":"Eutychus falls from the window at Troas","people":["Paul","Eutychus","Luke"],"gospels":{"Acts":"20:7–12"},"summary":"On the first day of the week the believers at Troas gather in an upper room to break bread. Paul, leaving the next day, talks on until midnight in the room full of lamps. A young man named Eutychus, sitting in the window, sinks into a deep sleep and falls three stories; he is taken up dead. Paul goes down, embraces him and says, \"Do not be alarmed, for his life is in him.\" Then Paul goes back up, breaks bread, and talks until daybreak, and they take the boy home alive.","stay":"Seven days at Troas (Acts 20:6).","journey":"About 150 miles by sea from Neapolis, the port of Philippi, past Samothrace to Troas. Five days, against the wind; the same crossing took two days going the other way (Acts 16:11).","route":[[24.287,41.013],[24.41,40.94],[24.9,40.7],[25.53,40.47],[25.95,40.1],[26.155,39.752]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 20:7","text":"On the first day of the week, when we were gathered together to break bread, Paul talked with them, intending to depart on the morrow; and he prolonged his speech until midnight."},{"ref":"Acts 20:9–10","text":"And a young man named Eutychus was sitting in the window. He sank into a deep sleep as Paul talked still longer; and being overcome by sleep, he fell down from the third story and was taken up dead. But Paul went down and bent over him, and embracing him said, \"Do not be alarmed, for his life is in him.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"1 Kings 17:21","note":"Elijah stretches himself upon the widow's dead son three times, and the child revives."},{"ref":"2 Kings 4:34","note":"Elisha lies upon the Shunammite's son, mouth to mouth, and the child's flesh becomes warm."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"Saint Paul Raising Eutychus","artist":"Jacques-François Courtin","year":"Early 18th century","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Cathédrale Saint-Étienne, Toulouse","description":"By lamplight, Paul bends over the limp, half-naked body of Eutychus and lays his hand on the boy's chest. A man holds the boy up from behind, while the believers around them pray, stare and raise their hands in alarm.","significance":"A large altarpiece-style canvas in Toulouse Cathedral by Courtin, a member of the Royal Academy of Painting in Paris; it is listed among France's protected historic objects.","symbolism":"The burning lamps recall Luke's detail, \"there were many lights in the upper chamber\"; the man praying with joined hands looks up as Paul says, \"his life is in him.\"","impact":"One of the few large paintings of this rarely painted scene, the only raising of the dead by Paul told in Acts.","img":"img/eutychus0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Didier Descouens, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cathédrale_Saint-Étienne_de_Toulouse_-_Saint_Paul_ressuscitant_Eutyque_par_Jacques_François_Courtin_PM31001406.jpg"},{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"Saint Paul Restoring Eutychus to Life","artist":"Taddeo Zuccari","year":"c. 1550–1566","medium":"Pen and brown ink, brush and brown wash, highlighted with white","location":"Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York","description":"In a crowded hall, the young Eutychus lies limp on the floor while two men lift him. Paul, at the center, raises his hand, and the believers press around to see.","significance":"A drawing by the Roman Mannerist Taddeo Zuccari, who painted scenes from the life of Paul in the Frangipani Chapel of San Marcello al Corso in Rome.","symbolism":"The dead weight of the boy's body, held up by others, contrasts with Paul's open, raised hand.","impact":"Zuccari's drawings were prized by collectors and studied by later artists.","img":"img/eutychus1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Paul_Restoring_Eutychus_to_Life_MET_DP812361.jpg"}],"tags":["raising","miracle"],"orderNote":"This is one of the earliest descriptions of Christians gathering on Sunday, \"the first day of the week,\" for the breaking of bread, the Eucharist. Luke, who was present, says Eutychus was \"taken up dead\"; a few interpreters think he only seemed dead."},
  {"id":"assoswalk","harmony":"Acts 20:13–14","lane":"paul","place":"assos","from":"troas","date":"April AD 57","title":"Paul walks to Assos","people":["Paul","Luke and companions"],"gospels":{"Acts":"20:13–14"},"summary":"The others board the ship at Troas and sail for Assos, but Paul chooses to go on foot. The ship must round Cape Lectum, while the road cuts straight across the neck of land. Paul meets them at Assos, they take him aboard, and they sail on to Mitylene on the island of Lesbos. Luke does not say why Paul wanted to walk alone.","journey":"About 20 to 30 miles south by the Roman road, a long day's walk, while the ship sailed around Cape Lectum.","route":[[26.155,39.752],[26.2,39.66],[26.27,39.57],[26.34,39.49]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 20:13–14","text":"But going ahead to the ship, we set sail for Assos, intending to take Paul aboard there; for so he had arranged, intending himself to go by land. And when he met us at Assos, we took him on board and came to Mitylene."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"The Temple of Athena at Assos","artist":"Photo: Carole Raddato","year":"Temple c. 530 BC","medium":"Photograph","location":"Assos (Behramkale), Turkey","description":"Re-erected Doric columns of the temple of Athena stand on the summit of the acropolis of Assos, with the sea and the island of Lesbos lost in the mist beyond.","significance":"The temple, built about 530 BC, crowned the city where Paul joined the ship after walking from Troas. From the summit one looks south over the harbor toward Lesbos, the next stop.","symbolism":"The old pagan temple above and the sea below frame the moment Paul stepped aboard for the last voyage of his journey.","impact":"It is the only Archaic Doric temple in Asia Minor; parts of its sculpture are in the Louvre, Boston and Istanbul.","img":"img/assoswalk0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Carole Raddato, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_of_Athena,_Assos,_Turkey.jpg"}],"tags":[],"orderNote":"Assos was a harbor city on a high volcanic hill crowned by a temple of Athena; Aristotle lived there for a few years in the 340s BC."},
  {"id":"miletusfarewell","harmony":"Acts 20:15–38","lane":"paul","place":"miletus","from":"assos","date":"Late April AD 57","title":"Farewell to the Ephesian elders at Miletus","people":["Paul","The elders of Ephesus"],"gospels":{"Acts":"20:15–38"},"summary":"Paul is hurrying to reach Jerusalem by Pentecost, so the ship sails past Ephesus. From Miletus he sends for the elders of the Ephesian church. He reminds them how he served among them with tears and trials, and says he is going to Jerusalem bound in the Spirit, knowing that prison and afflictions await him. He charges them to shepherd the flock, the church of God which he obtained with his own blood, and warns of fierce wolves to come. Then he kneels and prays with them all, and they weep and embrace him, grieving most because they will not see his face again.","stay":"The elders came about 30 miles from Ephesus, so Paul likely waited at Miletus three or four days.","journey":"About 150 miles by sea over four days, hugging the coast: Assos to Mitylene on Lesbos, down the strait off Chios, across to Samos, and through the narrows by Trogyllium to Miletus.","route":[[26.34,39.49],[26.45,39.33],[26.55,39.11],[26.42,38.8],[26.3,38.55],[26.24,38.4],[26.35,38.2],[26.6,37.9],[26.85,37.72],[26.94,37.69],[27.05,37.64],[27.278,37.53]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 20:24","text":"But I do not account my life of any value nor as precious to myself, if only I may accomplish my course and the ministry which I received from the Lord Jesus, to testify to the gospel of the grace of God."},{"ref":"Acts 20:28","text":"Take heed to yourselves and to all the flock, in which the Holy Spirit has made you guardians, to feed the church of the Lord which he obtained with his own blood."},{"ref":"Acts 20:35","text":"In all things I have shown you that by so toiling one must help the weak, remembering the words of the Lord Jesus, how he said, 'It is more blessed to give than to receive.'"}],"echoes":[{"ref":"1 Samuel 12:3","note":"Samuel's farewell: \"Whose ox have I taken? Or whose ass have I taken?\" Like Paul, he calls the people to witness that he took nothing from them."},{"ref":"Ezekiel 34:2–5","note":"The Lord's word against shepherds who feed themselves and leave the flock to the wild beasts."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"The theater of Miletus","artist":"Photo: Tomisti","year":"Theater 4th century BC, rebuilt in Roman times","medium":"Photograph","location":"Miletus (Balat), Turkey","description":"The great curved seating bank and stage of the theater of Miletus rise out of the plain, with a later Byzantine fortress on top.","significance":"The theater faced one of the city's harbors, near where Paul's ship would have put in. It was enlarged in Roman times to seat some 15,000 people. The coastline has since moved miles away.","symbolism":"The empty harbor plain recalls the parting: \"And they brought him to the ship.\"","impact":"One of the largest and best-preserved theaters of Asia Minor; an inscription on its seats reserves places for \"Jews who are also God-fearers.\"","img":"img/miletusfarewell0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Tomisti, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Miletus_theatre_01.jpg"},{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"Paul Takes Leave of the Elders","artist":"Jan Luyken","year":"1712","medium":"Etching","location":"Rijksmuseum, Amsterdam","description":"On the shore by a fortified harbor, Paul clasps hands with the elders of Ephesus, his other hand on his heart. Behind them a small boat waits to take him out to the ship at anchor.","significance":"From the Bible illustrations of the Dutch poet and etcher Jan Luyken, who made several thousand etchings for books.","symbolism":"The waiting ship gives the end of the scene: \"And they brought him to the ship.\"","impact":"Luyken's Bible etchings were reprinted for generations in Dutch family Bibles.","img":"img/miletusfarewell1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paulus_neemt_afscheid_van_de_ouderlingen,_RP-P-OB-46.090.jpg"}],"tags":["sermon","prayer","prophecy"],"orderNote":"This is the only speech in Acts given to Christians, and the one most like Paul's letters. \"It is more blessed to give than to receive\" is a saying of Jesus found nowhere in the Gospels. Some manuscripts read \"the church of God\" in Acts 20:28, others \"the church of the Lord.\" The later stop at Trogyllium (Acts 20:15) is in some manuscripts only."},
  {"id":"tyrebeach","harmony":"Acts 21:1–6","lane":"paul","place":"tyre","from":"miletus","date":"May AD 57","title":"A week at Tyre and farewell on the beach","people":["Paul","Luke","The disciples of Tyre"],"gospels":{"Acts":"21:1–6"},"summary":"The ship runs straight to Cos, then Rhodes, then Patara on the Lycian coast. There Paul's party finds a ship crossing to Phoenicia, sails past Cyprus on the left, and lands at Tyre, where the ship unloads its cargo. They seek out the disciples and stay seven days. Through the Spirit the disciples tell Paul not to go up to Jerusalem. When the week is over, the whole church, with wives and children, walks them out of the city, and they kneel on the beach to pray and say goodbye.","stay":"Seven days at Tyre (Acts 21:4).","journey":"About 550 miles by sea: island-hopping along the coast of Asia to Cos, Rhodes and Patara (about 150 miles, three days), then in a larger merchant ship across the open sea south of Cyprus to Tyre (about 400 miles, some five days).","route":[[27.278,37.53],[27.2,37.25],[27.29,36.95],[27.6,36.7],[28.22,36.44],[28.8,36.3],[29.32,36.26],[30.4,35.6],[31.5,35.0],[32.5,34.4],[33.4,34.2],[34.4,33.6],[35.195,33.27]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 21:4","text":"And having sought out the disciples, we stayed there for seven days. Through the Spirit they told Paul not to go on to Jerusalem."},{"ref":"Acts 21:5","text":"And when our days there were ended, we departed and went on our journey; and they all, with wives and children, brought us on our way till we were outside the city; and kneeling down on the beach we prayed and bade one another farewell."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"Paul Prays on the Shore Outside Tyre","artist":"Jan Luyken","year":"c. 1700","medium":"Etching","location":"Rijksmuseum, Amsterdam","description":"Paul stands with joined hands on the beach, praying, while men, women and children of the church of Tyre kneel around him. The ship waits offshore, and the city lies along the coast behind.","significance":"From Jan Luyken's Bible etchings; the caption reads \"Paul's prayer on the shore outside Tyre, Acts 21:5.\"","symbolism":"The families with small children follow Luke's detail: \"they all, with wives and children, brought us on our way.\"","impact":"Luyken's plates made rarely painted scenes of Acts familiar in Dutch Bibles for two centuries.","img":"img/tyrebeach0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paulus_bidt_bij_het_strand_van_Tyrus_Paulus_gebed_aan_den_oever_buijten_Tirus_(titel_op_object),_RP-P-OB-44.779.jpg"},{"kind":"Artifact","title":"The Roman road and arch at Al-Bass, Tyre","artist":"Photo: RomanDeckert","year":"2019 (arch 2nd century AD)","medium":"Photograph","location":"Al-Bass archaeological site, Tyre, Lebanon","description":"A long Roman road paved with stone runs toward a monumental arch, with the ruins of the aqueduct on the right.","significance":"The road led into Tyre from the mainland; the arch is later than Paul, probably from the time of Hadrian, but the road follows the old way into the city. The site is part of a UNESCO World Heritage Site.","symbolism":"This is the kind of road the church of Tyre walked with Paul \"till we were outside the city\" on the way to the beach.","impact":"Tyre's Roman ruins are among the largest in Lebanon.","img":"img/tyrebeach1.jpg","credit":"Photo: RomanDeckert, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TyreAlBass_RomanTriumphalArch-Aqueduct_RomanDeckert19112019.jpg"}],"tags":["prayer","prophecy"],"orderNote":"The church at Tyre likely began among believers scattered after Stephen's death (Acts 11:19). The Spirit's warnings are about what awaits Paul; Paul himself understood that the Spirit was leading him on to Jerusalem (Acts 20:22)."},
  {"id":"ptolemaisstop","harmony":"Acts 21:7","lane":"paul","place":"ptolemais","from":"tyre","date":"May AD 57","title":"One day at Ptolemais","people":["Paul","Luke","The brethren of Ptolemais"],"gospels":{"Acts":"21:7"},"summary":"The ship finishes its voyage down the coast from Tyre at Ptolemais, the old port of Acco. Paul and his companions greet the believers there and stay with them for one day. Even in this small harbor town on the way to Jerusalem there is already a Christian community.","stay":"One day (Acts 21:7).","journey":"About 25 miles south by sea along the Phoenician coast, less than a day.","route":[[35.195,33.27],[35.15,33.12],[35.09,33.0],[35.07,32.92]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 21:7","text":"When we had finished the voyage from Tyre, we arrived at Ptolemais; and we greeted the brethren and stayed with them for one day."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"The old port of Acre","artist":"Photo: Mary Madigan","year":"2006","medium":"Photograph","location":"Acre (Akko), Israel","description":"Small boats in Acre's harbor beside the ruined stones of the old sea wall, with the bay curving south toward Haifa and Mount Carmel.","significance":"Acre's harbor has been in use since Canaanite times; it was the Roman Ptolemais where Paul stayed a day, and later the main port of the Crusaders.","symbolism":"The small harbor matches Luke's brief note: they greeted the brethren and stayed one day.","impact":"The Old City of Acre is a UNESCO World Heritage Site.","img":"img/ptolemaisstop0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Mary Madigan, CC BY 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Akko._View_of_the_port_(494794255).jpg"}],"tags":[]},
  {"id":"philipsdaughters","harmony":"Acts 21:8–9","lane":"paul","place":"caesarea","from":"ptolemais","date":"May AD 57","title":"At the house of Philip the evangelist","people":["Paul","Luke","Philip the evangelist","Philip's four daughters"],"gospels":{"Acts":"21:8–9"},"summary":"The next day they come to Caesarea and stay in the house of Philip the evangelist, one of the seven chosen with Stephen, who had preached in Samaria and baptized the Ethiopian. More than twenty years earlier he had settled in Caesarea. Philip has four unmarried daughters who prophesy. Luke probably heard from Philip much of what he later wrote about the early church in Jerusalem and Samaria.","stay":"\"Some days\" in Philip's house (Acts 21:8, 10).","journey":"About 40 miles south along the coast road around Mount Carmel, one or two days on foot.","route":[[35.07,32.92],[35.0,32.83],[34.96,32.72],[34.93,32.6],[34.89,32.5]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 21:8–9","text":"On the morrow we departed and came to Caesarea; and we entered the house of Philip the evangelist, who was one of the seven, and stayed with him. And he had four unmarried daughters, who prophesied."}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Joel 2:28","note":"\"Your sons and your daughters shall prophesy\": fulfilled in Philip's household."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"The harbor of Caesarea Maritima","artist":"Photo: Immanuel Giel","year":"2018 (harbor c. 22–10 BC)","medium":"Photograph","location":"Caesarea National Park, Israel","description":"The remains of Herod's harbor at Caesarea along the shore, where Paul's party arrived to stay in the house of Philip.","significance":"Herod the Great built the harbor and city between about 22 and 10 BC. Philip the evangelist had lived here for about twenty years when Paul came.","symbolism":"Caesarea, Rome's capital in Judea, was where the gospel first went to the Gentiles in Cornelius' house, and where Paul would spend two years in prison.","impact":"Underwater archaeology since the 1960s has shown that Herod's harbor was built with Roman concrete that set under the sea.","img":"img/philipsdaughters0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Immanuel Giel, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caesarea_Maritima_Harbour_01.jpg"}],"tags":["prophecy"],"orderNote":"Philip came to Caesarea after baptizing the Ethiopian (Acts 8:40). Early tradition (Papias, Polycrates) says Philip and his daughters later lived at Hierapolis in Asia; some ancient writers confused him with Philip the apostle."},
  {"id":"agabus","harmony":"Acts 21:10–14","lane":"paul","place":"caesarea","date":"May AD 57","title":"Agabus binds himself with Paul's belt","people":["Paul","Agabus","Luke","The believers of Caesarea"],"gospels":{"Acts":"21:10–14"},"summary":"A prophet named Agabus comes down from Judea, the same who had foretold the great famine years before. He takes Paul's belt, binds his own hands and feet, and says the Holy Spirit declares that the Jews in Jerusalem will bind the owner of this belt and hand him over to the Gentiles. Luke and the others beg Paul not to go up. Paul answers that he is ready not only to be bound but to die at Jerusalem for the name of the Lord Jesus. They fall silent and say, \"The will of the Lord be done.\"","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 21:11","text":"And coming to us he took Paul's girdle and bound his own feet and hands, and said, \"Thus says the Holy Spirit, 'So shall the Jews at Jerusalem bind the man who owns this girdle and deliver him into the hands of the Gentiles.'\""},{"ref":"Acts 21:13–14","text":"Then Paul answered, \"What are you doing, weeping and breaking my heart? For I am ready not only to be imprisoned but even to die at Jerusalem for the name of the Lord Jesus.\" And when he would not be persuaded, we ceased and said, \"The will of the Lord be done.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Jeremiah 13:1–11","note":"Jeremiah's loincloth: like the old prophets, Agabus speaks with an acted sign."},{"ref":"Isaiah 20:2–4","note":"Isaiah walks stripped and barefoot as a sign of Egypt's captivity."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"The Prophet Agabus Foretelling Saint Paul's Sufferings in Jerusalem","artist":"Louis Chéron","year":"1687","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Notre-Dame de Paris","description":"At the center Agabus sits with a band bound around his wrist and ankle, raising his hand toward the dove of the Holy Spirit above. An old, white-bearded apostle in a red mantle, most likely Paul, gestures toward him; a disciple at his feet hides his face, and the women of the house weep.","significance":"One of the \"Mays\" of Notre-Dame, painted for the cathedral in 1687. Newly restored, it was shown in the 2024 exhibition of Notre-Dame's restored paintings at the Galerie des Gobelins in Paris, where this photo was taken.","symbolism":"The dove marks the prophecy as the Holy Spirit's word: \"Thus says the Holy Spirit.\" The bound hands and feet show in advance the chains Paul will wear in Jerusalem and Rome.","impact":"Chéron, a Huguenot, later left France for London, where he helped found an academy of drawing.","img":"img/agabus0.jpg","credit":"Photo: GO69, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis_Chéron_-_Le_prophète_Agabus_prédisant_à_saint_Paul_ses_souffrances_à_Jérusalem_-_01.jpg"}],"tags":["prophecy"],"orderNote":"Agabus also foretold the famine under Claudius (Acts 11:28). In the event, the Romans bound Paul to rescue him from a Jewish mob (Acts 21:33), so the prophecy came true in substance. \"The will of the Lord be done\" echoes Jesus in Gethsemane."},
  {"id":"uptojerusalem","harmony":"Acts 21:15–16","lane":"paul","place":"jerusalemchurch","from":"caesarea","date":"May AD 57, before Pentecost","title":"Up to Jerusalem","people":["Paul","Luke","Disciples from Caesarea","Mnason of Cyprus"],"gospels":{"Acts":"21:15–16"},"summary":"After these days Paul and his companions get ready and go up to Jerusalem, carrying the collection from the Gentile churches. Some disciples from Caesarea go with them and bring them to the house of Mnason of Cyprus, an early disciple, where they will lodge. It is Paul's fifth and last visit to Jerusalem as a free man.","journey":"About 65 miles: south along the coastal plain to Antipatris, then up through Lydda and the ascent of Beth-horon into the hills to Jerusalem. Two to three days, possibly with horses or mules.","route":[[34.89,32.5],[34.9,32.3],[34.93,32.1],[34.895,31.951],[35.05,31.88],[35.13,31.86],[35.231,31.776]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 21:15–16","text":"After these days we made ready and went up to Jerusalem. And some of the disciples from Caesarea went with us, bringing us to the house of Mnason of Cyprus, an early disciple, with whom we should lodge."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"The ascent to Beth-horon","artist":"American Colony Photo Department (Matson Collection)","year":"c. 1900","medium":"Photograph","location":"Library of Congress, Washington","description":"A rider on a donkey comes down a stony track through the terraced hills northwest of Jerusalem, on the old ascent from the coastal plain past Beth-horon. (One half of a stereoscopic photograph.)","significance":"From Lydda the main road up to Jerusalem climbed the ridge of Beth-horon; this was the likely way for travelers coming from Caesarea, as Paul did.","symbolism":"Going \"up\" to Jerusalem was a real climb: from the sea to about 2,500 feet.","impact":"The American Colony photographers documented the land before modern roads and towns changed it.","img":"img/uptojerusalem0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tibna,_north_west_of_Jerusalem,_etc._The_ascent_to_Beth-Horon._LOC_matpc.05135.jpg"}],"tags":[],"orderNote":"The Greek can mean they brought Paul to Mnason's house in Jerusalem, or that Mnason lived in a village on the way; the Western text says the latter. Paul hoped to arrive by Pentecost (Acts 20:16), most likely late May AD 57."},
  {"id":"meetjames","harmony":"Acts 21:17–26","lane":"paul","place":"jerusalemchurch","date":"Pentecost, AD 57","title":"Paul meets James and the elders","people":["Paul","James","The elders","Luke","Mnason of Cyprus"],"gospels":{"Acts":"21:17–26","Rom":"15:25–31"},"summary":"Paul reaches Jerusalem with the collection from the Gentile churches, and the brethren receive him gladly. The next day he tells James and all the elders what God has done among the Gentiles, and they glorify God. But thousands of Jewish believers have heard that Paul tells Jews to give up the law of Moses. So the elders ask him to join four men under a vow, pay their costs and go with them to the Temple, to show that he still keeps the law.","stay":"They lodged with Mnason of Cyprus, an early disciple (Acts 21:16).","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 21:18–19","text":"On the following day Paul went in with us to James; and all the elders were present. After greeting them, he related one by one the things that God had done among the Gentiles through his ministry."},{"ref":"Acts 21:26","text":"Then Paul took the men, and the next day he purified himself with them and went into the temple, to give notice when the days of purification would be fulfilled and the offering presented for every one of them."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"St James the Brother of the Lord","artist":"Stephanos Tzangarolas","year":"c. 1688–1710","medium":"Tempera on panel (icon)","location":"Benaki Museum, Athens","description":"James stands full length on a gold ground in the richly embroidered vestments of a bishop, holding an open scroll with Greek writing and raising his right hand. The Greek title names him \"James the Brother of God\" (that is, of the Lord).","significance":"An icon by Stephanos Tzangarolas, a priest-monk and painter of the Cretan school in the late 17th century, who signed it at the foot. Eastern tradition honors James as the first bishop of Jerusalem, the leader Paul meets in Acts 21.","symbolism":"The bishop's robes, embroidered with tiny figures of saints, show James as head of the mother church in Jerusalem; the scroll recalls the ancient Liturgy of St James that bears his name.","impact":"James 'the Just' was remembered for his strict piety; Josephus records his death by stoning in AD 62, soon after Paul reached Rome.","img":"img/meetjames0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tzangarolas_Stephanos_-_St_James_the_Brother_of_the_Lord_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg"}],"tags":[],"orderNote":"Paul hurried to reach Jerusalem by Pentecost (Acts 20:16), which fixes the season; most date the year AD 57, some AD 56 or 58. Acts does not mention the collection here, but Paul's letters show it was the purpose of the visit (Romans 15:25–31; Acts 24:17), and Romans 15:31 shows he feared the believers might not accept it."},
  {"id":"templeriot","harmony":"Acts 21:27–36","lane":"paul","place":"temple","date":"Pentecost, AD 57","title":"The riot in the Temple and Paul's arrest","people":["Paul","Jews from Asia","Claudius Lysias (the tribune)","Roman soldiers"],"gospels":{"Acts":"21:27–36"},"summary":"Near the end of the seven days of purification, Jews from Asia see Paul in the Temple and stir up the crowd. They had seen him in the city with Trophimus, a Gentile from Ephesus, and suppose Paul brought him past the barrier into the inner courts. The crowd drags Paul out, the gates are shut, and they begin to beat him. The Roman tribune runs down with soldiers from the fortress, binds Paul with two chains and has him carried up the steps above the mob shouting \"Away with him!\"","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 21:28","text":"crying out, \"Men of Israel, help! This is the man who is teaching men everywhere against the people and the law and this place; moreover he also brought Greeks into the temple, and he has defiled this holy place.\""},{"ref":"Acts 21:30","text":"Then all the city was aroused, and the people ran together; they seized Paul and dragged him out of the temple, and at once the gates were shut."},{"ref":"Acts 21:31–32","text":"And as they were trying to kill him, word came to the tribune of the cohort that all Jerusalem was in confusion. He at once took soldiers and centurions, and ran down to them; and when they saw the tribune and the soldiers, they stopped beating Paul."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"The Temple warning inscription","artist":"Unknown (Herodian Temple)","year":"1st century BC – 1st century AD","medium":"Limestone, Greek inscription","location":"Istanbul Archaeology Museums","description":"A limestone block with seven lines of Greek capitals. It reads: \"No foreigner is to enter within the balustrade and enclosure around the Temple. Whoever is caught will have himself to blame for his death, which follows.\"","significance":"Found complete near the Temple Mount in 1871 by Charles Clermont-Ganneau. Signs like this stood on the barrier around the inner courts; the charge against Paul was that he had brought a Greek past them (Acts 21:28–29).","symbolism":"Paul himself later wrote that Christ \"has broken down the dividing wall of hostility\" between Jew and Gentile (Ephesians 2:14), perhaps with this barrier in mind.","impact":"One of the few objects that survive from Herod's Temple; Josephus describes the same warning in Greek and Latin (Jewish War 5.5.2).","img":"img/templeriot0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Yong Woo Park, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_Warning_Inscription_from_Jerusalem_Istanbul_Archaeology_Museums.jpg"}],"tags":["conflict"],"orderNote":"A stone barrier (the soreg) kept Gentiles out of the inner courts; Greek inscriptions on it warned that any foreigner who passed would have himself to blame for the death that followed. Two of these inscriptions have been found (1871 and 1935)."},
  {"id":"barracks","harmony":"Acts 21:37–22:29","lane":"paul","place":"antonia","date":"Pentecost, AD 57","title":"Paul's speech from the barracks steps","people":["Paul","Claudius Lysias (the tribune)","The crowd","A centurion"],"gospels":{"Acts":"21:37–22:29"},"summary":"The tribune takes Paul for an Egyptian rebel, but Paul answers in Greek that he is a Jew of Tarsus and asks to speak. Standing on the steps, he addresses the crowd in Hebrew (Aramaic) and tells of his zeal for the law, the light on the Damascus road, Ananias, and the Lord's command to go to the Gentiles. At the word \"Gentiles\" the crowd erupts again. The tribune orders him examined by scourging, but Paul asks the centurion whether it is lawful to scourge a Roman citizen who has not been condemned.","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 21:39","text":"Paul replied, \"I am a Jew, from Tarsus in Cilicia, a citizen of no mean city; I beg you, let me speak to the people.\""},{"ref":"Acts 22:21","text":"And he said to me, 'Depart; for I will send you far away to the Gentiles.'\""},{"ref":"Acts 22:28","text":"The tribune answered, \"I bought this citizenship for a large sum.\" Paul said, \"But I was born a citizen.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"Saint Paul Rescued from the Multitude","artist":"Gustave Doré","year":"1866","medium":"Wood engraving","location":"From La Grande Bible de Tours (1866)","description":"Roman soldiers with spears force their way through a raging crowd and lift Paul bodily up the steps of the fortress, while the mob below shake their fists and a figure at the top of the stair raises his arm.","significance":"One of more than 200 plates in Doré's illustrated Bible, among the most reproduced Bible pictures of the 19th century.","symbolism":"Paul is carried above the crowd \"because of the violence of the crowd\" (Acts 21:35): he is saved by the very power that has made him a prisoner.","impact":"Doré's Bible appeared in many languages and editions and shaped how a whole generation pictured the Bible.","img":"img/barracks0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paul_Addresses_the_Crowd_After_His_Arrest_by_Gustave_Doré.jpg"},{"kind":"Artifact","title":"The Antonia Fortress (model)","artist":"Holyland Model of Jerusalem, after Michael Avi-Yonah","year":"1966 (photo 2016)","medium":"Scale model (1:50), stone","location":"Israel Museum, Jerusalem","description":"The Antonia Fortress in the model of Jerusalem: a walled block of pale stone with four tall corner towers and battlements.","significance":"The model reconstructs Jerusalem before AD 70 from Josephus and archaeology. From this fortress the tribune ran down with his soldiers, and on its steps Paul spoke to the crowd.","symbolism":"Its towers looked straight down into the Temple courts: Roman power watching over the house of God.","impact":"The model, built at the Holyland Hotel and moved to the Israel Museum in 2006, is the most familiar picture of Herodian Jerusalem.","img":"img/barracks1.jpg","credit":"Photo: Oren Rozen, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Israel_Museum_290416_Model_of_Jerusalem_03.jpg"}],"tags":["sermon","conflict"],"orderNote":"This is the second of three accounts of Paul's conversion in Acts (9:1–19, 22:3–21, 26:9–18); they differ in detail, for example over who heard the voice."},
  {"id":"paulcouncil","harmony":"Acts 22:30–23:11","lane":"paul","place":"temple","date":"Pentecost, AD 57","title":"Paul before the Sanhedrin","people":["Paul","Ananias the high priest","Pharisees","Sadducees","Claudius Lysias (the tribune)"],"gospels":{"Acts":"22:30–23:11"},"summary":"To learn the charge, the tribune brings Paul before the chief priests and the whole council. The high priest Ananias orders him struck on the mouth, and Paul flares back at him, not knowing he is the high priest. Seeing that the council is divided, Paul cries out that he is a Pharisee on trial for the hope of the resurrection, and Pharisees and Sadducees fall to quarreling. The soldiers pull him out by force. That night the Lord stands by him and tells him he must bear witness also at Rome.","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 23:6","text":"But when Paul perceived that one part were Sadducees and the other Pharisees, he cried out in the council, \"Brethren, I am a Pharisee, a son of Pharisees; with respect to the hope and the resurrection of the dead I am on trial.\""},{"ref":"Acts 23:11","text":"The following night the Lord stood by him and said, \"Take courage, for as you have testified about me at Jerusalem, so you must bear witness also at Rome.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"Paul before the High Council","artist":"Christoph Anton Mayr","year":"1768","medium":"Ceiling fresco","location":"Parish Church of Sts Peter and Paul, Söll, Tyrol (Austria)","description":"Paul, in a green robe, stands on the steps of a great hall beside a Roman officer and points to heaven, while the elders of the council gesture and argue on either side of him.","significance":"Part of a ceiling cycle of the lives of Peter and Paul, the church's patrons, painted in the late Baroque style of the Tyrol.","symbolism":"The council is split into two arguing groups, as Luke tells it: Pharisees and Sadducees divided over the resurrection, and Paul between them pointing upward.","impact":"Mayr painted the whole ceiling cycle of the church, a rich example of Tyrolean Rococo.","img":"img/paulcouncil0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Wolfgang Sauber, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St.Peter_und_Paul_in_Söll_-_Deckenfresko_Peter_und_Paul_1b_Paulus_vor_dem_Hohen_Rat.jpg"}],"tags":["conflict","appearance"],"orderNote":"Acts does not say where the council met; Lysias \"brought Paul down\" from the fortress, so it was near the Temple, perhaps in the council chamber by the Temple courts (the Chamber of Hewn Stone of later Jewish tradition). This Ananias, son of Nedebaeus, was high priest about AD 47–59 and was known for his violence (Josephus, Antiquities 20.9.2)."},
  {"id":"plot","harmony":"Acts 23:12–22","lane":"paul","place":"antonia","date":"Pentecost, AD 57","title":"The plot against Paul's life","people":["Paul","Paul's nephew","More than forty conspirators","Claudius Lysias (the tribune)"],"gospels":{"Acts":"23:12–22"},"summary":"More than forty men bind themselves by an oath neither to eat nor drink until they have killed Paul. They ask the chief priests to have him brought down to the council again, so they can ambush him on the way. Paul's sister's son hears of it, gets into the barracks and tells Paul, who sends him to the tribune. The tribune listens in private and tells the young man to say nothing.","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 23:12","text":"When it was day, the Jews made a plot and bound themselves by an oath neither to eat nor drink till they had killed Paul."},{"ref":"Acts 23:16","text":"Now the son of Paul's sister heard of their ambush; so he went and entered the barracks and told Paul."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"The Antonia Fortress, the barracks (model)","artist":"Holyland Model of Jerusalem","year":"1966 (photo 2008)","medium":"Scale model (1:50), stone","location":"Israel Museum, Jerusalem","description":"The model of the Antonia Fortress seen from above: its high walls and corner towers rise directly behind the colonnades of the Temple court, with stairs leading down into it.","significance":"Paul was held in this fortress, the \"barracks\" of Acts, and it was here that his nephew found him and warned him of the plot.","symbolism":"The walls that held Paul captive also protected him from the men who had sworn to kill him.","impact":"Josephus says the fortress was like a city in size, with courts, baths and quarters for a whole cohort.","img":"img/plot0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Ariely, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Antonia_Fortress.jpg"}],"tags":["conflict"],"orderNote":"This is the only mention of Paul's family in Jerusalem; nothing more is known of his sister or her son."},
  {"id":"nightride","harmony":"Acts 23:23–35","lane":"rome","place":"caesarea","from":"antonia","date":"Summer, AD 57","title":"The night ride to Caesarea","people":["Claudius Lysias (the tribune)","Paul","Two centurions","470 soldiers, horsemen and spearmen","Felix the governor"],"gospels":{"Acts":"23:23–35"},"summary":"The tribune Claudius Lysias acts at once. At nine at night he sends Paul out under a guard of two hundred soldiers, seventy horsemen and two hundred spearmen, with a mount for Paul to ride. He writes to the governor Felix that he rescued a Roman citizen and found no charge deserving death or chains. The foot soldiers go as far as Antipatris, and the horsemen take Paul on to Caesarea. Felix keeps him under guard in Herod's praetorium until his accusers come.","stay":"Felix ordered him to be guarded in Herod's praetorium (Acts 23:35), the former palace of Herod the Great on the promontory at Caesarea.","journey":"About 60–65 miles. By night from Jerusalem north through the hills by Gophna and down to Antipatris (about 40 miles), then the next day across the open coastal plain to Caesarea (about 25 miles) with the horsemen only.","route":[[35.2335,31.7803],[35.22,31.84],[35.215,31.96],[35.1,32.03],[34.93,32.105],[34.92,32.3],[34.89,32.5]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 23:23–24","text":"Then he called two of the centurions and said, \"At the third hour of the night get ready two hundred soldiers with seventy horsemen and two hundred spearmen to go as far as Caesarea. Also provide mounts for Paul to ride, and bring him safely to Felix the governor.\""},{"ref":"Acts 23:31","text":"So the soldiers, according to their instructions, took Paul and brought him by night to Antipatris."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"Tel Afek, the site of Antipatris","artist":"Photo: Avishai Teicher","year":"Modern photograph","medium":"Photograph","location":"Yarkon National Park (Tel Afek), Israel","description":"The square walls of the later Ottoman fort at Tel Afek, built over the mound of ancient Aphek, which Herod the Great rebuilt as Antipatris.","significance":"Herod named the town for his father, Antipater. It stood where the hill road from Jerusalem met the coastal highway; here the foot soldiers turned back and the horsemen took Paul on to Caesarea.","symbolism":"A crossroads town on the edge of the hill country: beyond it the open plain, safe from ambush.","impact":"Excavations have uncovered a stretch of Roman paved road and shops from the town of Paul's day.","img":"img/nightride0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Avishai Teicher (PikiWiki Israel), CC BY 2.5, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PikiWiki_Israel_51795_antipatris_fortress.jpg"}],"tags":[],"orderNote":"Forty miles in one night is a hard march; some think the infantry turned back early or that the march took longer than one night. The route by Beth-horon and Lydda is also possible."},
  {"id":"felix","harmony":"Acts 24:1–23","lane":"paul","place":"caesarea","date":"Summer, AD 57","title":"Paul before Felix","people":["Paul","Felix the governor","Ananias the high priest","Tertullus","The elders"],"gospels":{"Acts":"24:1–23"},"summary":"Five days later the high priest Ananias comes down with elders and an orator named Tertullus, who flatters Felix and calls Paul a pest and a ringleader of the sect of the Nazarenes. Paul answers that he came up to worship only twelve days before, bringing alms to his nation, and stirred up no crowd. He confesses that he serves the God of his fathers according to the Way, in the hope of the resurrection. Felix, who knows the Way well, puts the case off until Lysias comes, and gives Paul some liberty and leave for his friends to care for him.","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 24:14–15","text":"But this I admit to you, that according to the Way, which they call a sect, I worship the God of our fathers, believing everything laid down by the law or written in the prophets, having a hope in God which these themselves accept, that there will be a resurrection of both the just and the unjust."},{"ref":"Acts 24:16","text":"So I always take pains to have a clear conscience toward God and toward men."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"Paul before Felix","artist":"William Hogarth","year":"1748","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Lincoln's Inn, London","description":"Paul, in chains, points upward as he speaks before the governor Felix, crowned with laurel, and his wife Drusilla. The orator Tertullus stands at the left with his brief, the old high priest Ananias sits by the governor, and on the floor lies the letter of Claudius Lysias to Felix.","significance":"Painted for the hall of Lincoln's Inn, one of London's Inns of Court, as a picture of a just case pleaded before a judge.","symbolism":"Felix looks uneasily at the prisoner as Paul speaks of \"justice and self-control and future judgment\": the prisoner judges the judge.","impact":"Hogarth's only large painting for a legal setting; his engraving of it was widely sold, and he also made a mocking 'burlesque' version of the same scene.","img":"img/felix0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hogarth_-_Paul_Before_Felix,_1747-1751.jpg"}],"tags":["conflict","teaching"],"orderNote":"Antonius Felix, a freedman, governed Judea about AD 52–59 (Tacitus, Histories 5.9, says he \"exercised the power of a king with the mind of a slave\")."},
  {"id":"felixyears","harmony":"Acts 24:24–27","lane":"rome","place":"caesarea","date":"AD 57–59","title":"Felix, Drusilla, and two years in prison","people":["Felix the governor","Drusilla","Paul","Porcius Festus"],"gospels":{"Acts":"24:24–27"},"summary":"Some days later Felix comes with his wife Drusilla, a Jewish princess, the daughter of Herod Agrippa I, and sends for Paul to hear him speak about faith in Christ. When Paul argues about justice, self-control and the judgment to come, Felix is alarmed and sends him away. He sends for him often, hoping for a bribe. Two years pass; then Felix is recalled, and to do the Jews a favor he leaves Paul in prison.","stay":"Two years under guard at Caesarea (Acts 24:27).","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 24:25","text":"And as he argued about justice and self-control and future judgment, Felix was alarmed and said, \"Go away for the present; when I have an opportunity I will summon you.\""},{"ref":"Acts 24:27","text":"But when two years had elapsed, Felix was succeeded by Porcius Festus; and desiring to do the Jews a favor, Felix left Paul in prison."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"Herod's Promontory Palace, Caesarea","artist":"Photo: Deror Avi","year":"2011","medium":"Photograph","location":"Caesarea National Park, Israel","description":"The paved floors and low walls of Herod's palace on its point at Caesarea, by the sea, with the old harbor and the later Crusader walls in the distance.","significance":"Built by Herod the Great about 20 BC, the palace became the residence of the Roman governors, the \"praetorium of Herod\" where Paul was kept for two years (Acts 23:35).","symbolism":"A king's pleasure palace turned into a governor's prison: here Paul waited while Felix waited for a bribe.","impact":"A stone found nearby in 1961 bears the name of Pontius Pilate, one of the governors who lived here.","img":"img/felixyears0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Deror Avi, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herods_Promontory_Palace_P1080624.JPG"}],"tags":["teaching"],"orderNote":"Some scholars think Paul wrote one or more of his prison letters (Philippians, Colossians, Philemon, Ephesians) during these two years at Caesarea; most place them in Rome, and some in an earlier imprisonment at Ephesus."},
  {"id":"festus","harmony":"Acts 25:1–12","lane":"paul","place":"caesarea","date":"AD 59","title":"Before Festus: \"I appeal to Caesar\"","people":["Paul","Porcius Festus","The chief priests","The Jews from Jerusalem"],"gospels":{"Acts":"25:1–12"},"summary":"Three days after he arrives, the new governor Porcius Festus goes up to Jerusalem, where the chief priests ask him to send Paul there, planning an ambush on the road. Festus hears the case at Caesarea instead. The charges cannot be proved, and Festus, wishing to please the Jews, asks Paul if he will be tried in Jerusalem. Paul refuses: he stands before Caesar's tribunal and appeals to Caesar. As a Roman citizen he has that right, and Festus must send him to Rome.","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 25:10–11","text":"But Paul said, \"I am standing before Caesar's tribunal, where I ought to be tried; to the Jews I have done no wrong, as you know very well. If then I am a wrongdoer, and have committed anything for which I deserve to die, I do not seek to escape death; but if there is nothing in their charges against me, no one can give me up to them. I appeal to Caesar.\""},{"ref":"Acts 25:12","text":"Then Festus, when he had conferred with his council, answered, \"You have appealed to Caesar; to Caesar you shall go.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"Bust of the emperor Nero","artist":"Roman sculptor","year":"1st century AD","medium":"Marble","location":"Glyptothek, Munich","description":"A marble head of Nero, with his heavy jaw and full face and the rows of curled locks over the brow that he made fashionable.","significance":"Nero was the Caesar to whom Paul appealed (reigned AD 54–68). In AD 59 he was still in the early years of his reign, before the fire of Rome and his persecution of the Christians in AD 64.","symbolism":"Paul trusted Roman justice; tradition holds that he was later beheaded under the same emperor.","impact":"Few portraits of Nero survive, since many were destroyed after his memory was condemned by the Senate.","img":"img/festus0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Yair Haklai, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Nero_(GL_321).jpg"}],"tags":["conflict"],"orderNote":"The year Festus replaced Felix is debated (between AD 55 and 60); AD 59 is the date most often given, partly because new coinage of the province appears that year. The emperor to whom Paul appealed was Nero (reigned AD 54–68)."},
  {"id":"agrippa","harmony":"Acts 25:13–27","lane":"herod","place":"caesarea","date":"AD 59","title":"King Agrippa and Bernice come to Caesarea","people":["Herod Agrippa II","Bernice","Porcius Festus","Paul"],"gospels":{"Acts":"25:13–27"},"summary":"King Agrippa II, the last ruler of the Herod family, comes with his sister Bernice to welcome the new governor. Festus lays Paul's case before him: the charges are only disputes about their religion and \"about one Jesus, who was dead, but whom Paul asserted to be alive.\" Agrippa wants to hear the man himself. The next day the king and Bernice enter the audience hall with great pomp, with the tribunes and leading men of the city, and Paul is brought in. Festus needs something to write to the emperor.","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 25:13","text":"Now when some days had passed, Agrippa the king and Bernice arrived at Caesarea to welcome Festus."},{"ref":"Acts 25:23","text":"So on the morrow Agrippa and Bernice came with great pomp, and they entered the audience hall with the military tribunes and the prominent men of the city. Then by command of Festus Paul was brought in."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"Paul before Festus, King Agrippa and Queen Bernice","artist":"Hendrick Bloemaert","year":"1635","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Centraal Museum, Utrecht","description":"Paul, in a red cloak, raises his hand as he speaks to King Agrippa, in a turban and ermine-trimmed robe with a scepter, and Bernice, crowned and dressed in yellow silk. The governor Festus sits on the dais beneath a canopy marked SPQR.","significance":"A work of the Utrecht school, by the son of Abraham Bloemaert, showing the grand hearing of Acts 25:23.","symbolism":"The \"great pomp\" of the king and his sister is set against the barefoot prisoner; the letters SPQR over the governor's seat recall that the case now belongs to Rome.","impact":"Bloemaert's large history paintings were made for Utrecht's Catholic and civic patrons in the decades of the Dutch Republic.","img":"img/agrippa0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paul_and_King_Agrippa_by_Hendrick_Bloemaert_Centraal_Museum_2570.jpg"}],"tags":[],"orderNote":"Agrippa II, son of the Herod Agrippa of Acts 12, ruled territories north and east of Galilee and had charge of the Temple and the choice of high priests. His sister Bernice later became the mistress of Titus."},
  {"id":"agrippaspeech","harmony":"Acts 26:1–32","lane":"paul","place":"caesarea","date":"AD 59","title":"Paul's defense before Agrippa","people":["Paul","Herod Agrippa II","Bernice","Porcius Festus"],"gospels":{"Acts":"26:1–32"},"summary":"Stretching out his hand, Paul tells the king of his life as a strict Pharisee, his persecution of the saints, and the light brighter than the sun on the road to Damascus, where the risen Jesus sent him to open the eyes of the Gentiles. He says he was not disobedient to the heavenly vision. Festus shouts that Paul is mad; Paul appeals to the king, who knows the prophets. Agrippa answers half in jest, and the hearers agree that Paul has done nothing to deserve death. He could have been set free if he had not appealed to Caesar.","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 26:19","text":"\"Wherefore, O King Agrippa, I was not disobedient to the heavenly vision,"},{"ref":"Acts 26:28–29","text":"And Agrippa said to Paul, \"In a short time you think to make me a Christian!\" And Paul said, \"Whether short or long, I would to God that not only you but also all who hear me this day might become such as I am—except for these chains.\""},{"ref":"Acts 26:32","text":"And Agrippa said to Festus, \"This man could have been set free if he had not appealed to Caesar.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"St Paul before Agrippa","artist":"Sir James Thornhill","year":"c. 1710","medium":"Oil sketch","location":"Yale Center for British Art, New Haven","description":"Within a frame shaped like a panel of the dome, Paul raises his hands as he speaks to Agrippa and Bernice, seated high on a dais among their court; a scribe takes down his words and soldiers stand behind him.","significance":"A design for one of the eight scenes from the life of St Paul that Thornhill painted in grisaille inside the dome of St Paul's Cathedral, London (1716–19).","symbolism":"Paul's gesture is that of a classical orator: the prisoner speaks as the true king in the room.","impact":"Thornhill's dome cycle is one of the great works of English Baroque painting, seen by millions of visitors to the cathedral.","img":"img/agrippaspeech0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:James_Thornhill_-_St._Paul_before_Agrippa_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg"},{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"Saint Paul Speaking before King Agrippa","artist":"Giorgio Vasari","year":"1573","medium":"Pen and brown ink, brown wash","location":"Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York","description":"In a classical hall with statues in niches, Paul points upward as he defends himself before the king, Bernice and the governor seated on a dais. The Latin caption reads \"He defends himself before the king and the Roman governor.\"","significance":"A late drawing by Vasari, painter, architect and the first historian of Renaissance art.","symbolism":"Paul points past the seated rulers to heaven: \"I was not disobedient to the heavenly vision.\"","impact":"Vasari's Lives of the Artists (1550, 1568) made him the founder of art history.","img":"img/agrippaspeech1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Paul_Speaking_before_King_Agrippa_(Acts_26)_MET_DP812210.jpg"}],"tags":["sermon"],"orderNote":"The third account of Paul's conversion in Acts. Agrippa's words in 26:28 are hard to translate: the King James Version's \"Almost thou persuadest me to be a Christian\" is now usually read as a wry question, \"In so short a time you think to make me a Christian?\""},
  {"id":"sidon","harmony":"Acts 27:1–3","lane":"paul","place":"sidon","from":"caesarea","date":"Late summer, AD 59","title":"Setting sail for Rome: Sidon","people":["Paul","Julius the centurion","Luke","Aristarchus","Other prisoners"],"gospels":{"Acts":"27:1–3"},"summary":"Paul and some other prisoners are handed over to Julius, a centurion of the Augustan Cohort. They board a ship of Adramyttium bound for the ports of Asia, with Luke (\"we\") and Aristarchus of Thessalonica. The next day they put in at Sidon, and Julius kindly lets Paul go to his friends to be cared for. Luke's account of this voyage is the most detailed description of a sea journey to survive from the ancient world.","journey":"About 80 miles north by sea, a day's sail along the coast of Samaria, around Mount Carmel and past Ptolemais and Tyre.","route":[[34.89,32.5],[34.87,32.62],[34.9,32.75],[34.93,32.845],[35.04,32.95],[35.08,33.1],[35.16,33.27],[35.3,33.46],[35.37,33.56]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 27:1","text":"And when it was decided that we should sail for Italy, they delivered Paul and some other prisoners to a centurion of the Augustan Cohort, named Julius."},{"ref":"Acts 27:3","text":"The next day we put in at Sidon; and Julius treated Paul kindly, and gave him leave to go to his friends and be cared for."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"The Sea Castle at Sidon","artist":"Photo: Vyacheslav Argenberg","year":"2008","medium":"Photograph","location":"Sidon (Saida), Lebanon","description":"A tower of the Crusader Sea Castle rising from the water of Sidon's harbor. Ancient column drums are laid crosswise in its walls, reused from older buildings.","significance":"The castle was built in 1228, but the harbor is the ancient one where Paul's ship put in on its first day out of Caesarea.","symbolism":"A harbor of friends: the centurion Julius let Paul go ashore to be cared for.","impact":"Sidon was one of the great Phoenician cities, often named with Tyre in the Bible.","img":"img/sidon0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Vyacheslav Argenberg, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sidon_Sea_Castle,_Sidon,_Lebanon.jpg"}],"tags":[]},
  {"id":"myra","harmony":"Acts 27:4–6","lane":"paul","place":"myra","from":"sidon","date":"Early autumn, AD 59","title":"Under the lee of Cyprus to Myra","people":["Paul","Julius the centurion","Luke","Aristarchus"],"gospels":{"Acts":"27:4–6"},"summary":"Against the westerly winds of summer, the ship keeps to the sheltered side of Cyprus, passing to the east and north of the island. It then works west along the coast of Cilicia and Pamphylia to Myra in Lycia. There Julius finds a grain ship of Alexandria bound for Italy and puts the prisoners on board. In all, 276 people sailed on her (Acts 27:37).","journey":"About 430 miles by sea, north past the east end of Cyprus, through the channel between Cyprus and Cilicia, then west along the coast of Pamphylia and Lycia. Some manuscripts add that the crossing took fifteen days.","route":[[35.37,33.56],[35.25,33.8],[35.05,34.4],[34.75,35.4],[34.45,35.8],[33.8,35.85],[33.0,35.75],[32.3,35.95],[31.4,36.25],[30.6,36.15],[30.45,36.1],[30.15,36.15],[29.96,36.2],[29.98,36.26]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 27:5–6","text":"And when we had sailed across the sea which is off Cilicia and Pamphylia, we came to Myra in Lycia. There the centurion found a ship of Alexandria sailing for Italy, and put us on board."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"The rock tombs of Myra","artist":"Photo: Saffron Blaze","year":"4th century BC (modern photograph)","medium":"Photograph","location":"Demre (ancient Myra), Turkey","description":"Lycian tombs carved like little temple fronts, with columns and pediments, cut one above another into the face of a cliff.","significance":"The tombs date from the 4th century BC and were already old when Paul's ship put in at Myra's port, where Julius found an Alexandrian grain ship bound for Italy.","symbolism":"The cliff of the dead above the harbor: Paul passed on to a voyage in which all would be saved.","impact":"Myra later became famous as the see of St Nicholas, bishop there in the 4th century.","img":"img/myra0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Saffron Blaze, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Myra_Rock_Tombs.jpg"}],"tags":[],"orderNote":"Egyptian grain ships often sailed north to Lycia before heading west, because of the steady northwest winds; Myra was a regular port of call for the Alexandrian fleet."},
  {"id":"fairhavens","harmony":"Acts 27:7–12","lane":"paul","place":"fairhavens","from":"myra","date":"October, AD 59","title":"Fair Havens and Paul's warning","people":["Paul","Julius the centurion","The captain and the owner of the ship","Luke"],"gospels":{"Acts":"27:7–12"},"summary":"Against the wind, the ship makes slow progress to off Cnidus, then turns south under the lee of Crete, around Cape Salmone, and coasts with difficulty to Fair Havens. It is late in the season: the Fast of the Day of Atonement is past, and sailing is dangerous. Paul warns that the voyage will bring loss of cargo, ship and lives. But the centurion trusts the captain and the owner, and the majority decide to try for Phoenix, a better harbor further west, to winter there.","journey":"About 375 miles by sea, slowly west past Rhodes to the waters off Cnidus, then south around Cape Salmone at the east end of Crete and west along its south coast.","route":[[29.98,36.26],[29.95,36.18],[29.6,36.12],[29.1,36.22],[28.6,36.45],[27.9,36.6],[27.4,36.6],[27.3,36.15],[26.75,35.7],[26.45,35.3],[26.35,34.95],[25.9,34.9],[25.4,34.92],[24.95,34.88],[24.78,34.92]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 27:7–8","text":"We sailed slowly for a number of days, and arrived with difficulty off Cnidus, and as the wind did not allow us to go on, we sailed under the lee of Crete off Salmone. Coasting along it with difficulty, we came to a place called Fair Havens, near which was the city of Lasea."},{"ref":"Acts 27:9–10","text":"As much time had been lost, and the voyage was already dangerous because the fast had already gone by, Paul advised them, saying, \"Sirs, I perceive that the voyage will be with injury and much loss, not only of the cargo and the ship, but also of our lives.\""}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"Kali Limenes (Fair Havens), Crete","artist":"Photo: Olaf Tausch","year":"2012","medium":"Photograph","location":"Kali Limenes, south coast of Crete, Greece","description":"The shore at Kali Limenes on Crete's south coast: a long beach under bare hills, with small rocky islets offshore.","significance":"The place keeps Luke's name, \"Fair Havens.\" The bay is sheltered from the northwest but open to the east and south, which is why the sailors wanted a better harbor for the winter.","symbolism":"The safe harbor they chose to leave: Paul's warning was ignored here.","impact":"A chapel of St Paul stands nearby; the Greek Church remembers the apostle's stop here.","img":"img/fairhavens0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Olaf Tausch, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kali_Limenes_04.jpg"}],"tags":["prophecy"],"orderNote":"Ancient writers counted the seas dangerous from mid-September and closed from about November 11 to March 10 (Vegetius). In AD 59 the Day of Atonement fell on October 5, which is one reason that year is favored."},
  {"id":"tempest","harmony":"Acts 27:13–26","lane":"paul","place":"adria","from":"fairhavens","date":"October–November, AD 59","title":"The storm at sea","people":["Paul","The sailors","Julius the centurion","Luke","An angel"],"gospels":{"Acts":"27:13–26"},"summary":"A gentle south wind tempts them out, but soon a violent northeaster (in Greek, Eurakylon) sweeps down from the mountains of Crete. The ship cannot face it and is driven before it. Under the lee of the small island of Cauda they haul in the boat, undergird the hull with ropes, and lower the gear for fear of the Syrtis sandbanks off Africa; then they throw the cargo and the tackle overboard. For many days neither sun nor stars appear, and all hope is abandoned. Then Paul stands up among them: an angel has told him he must stand before Caesar, and God has granted him the lives of all who sail with him.","journey":"The drift is a reconstruction, not a recorded course. From Fair Havens the ship was blown southwest to Cauda, about 40 miles, and then drove west for fourteen days across \"the sea of Adria\". James Smith (1848) worked out that a ship lying to in such a gale drifts about 36 nautical miles a day, slightly north of west, which would carry her the roughly 550 miles from Cauda to Malta in about thirteen days. The line on the map follows that estimate; the true path is unknown.","route":[[24.78,34.92],[24.62,34.9],[24.4,34.85],[24.15,34.77],[23.5,34.85],[22.0,35.05],[20.5,35.25],[19.0,35.45]],"routeUncertain":true,"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 27:14–15","text":"But soon a tempestuous wind, called the northeaster, struck down from the land; and when the ship was caught and could not face the wind, we gave way to it and were driven."},{"ref":"Acts 27:20","text":"And when neither sun nor stars appeared for many a day, and no small tempest lay on us, all hope of our being saved was at last abandoned."},{"ref":"Acts 27:23–24","text":"For this very night there stood by me an angel of the God to whom I belong and whom I worship, and he said, 'Do not be afraid, Paul; you must stand before Caesar; and lo, God has granted you all those who sail with you.'"}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Jonah 1:4–5","note":"Jonah's ship is caught in \"a mighty tempest\" and the sailors throw the cargo into the sea; but where Jonah was fleeing from God, Paul is sent by him."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"Gavdos (Cauda)","artist":"Photo: Artemis Katsadoura","year":"2011","medium":"Photograph","location":"Gavdos, south of Crete, Greece","description":"A sand beach on Gavdos, with wind-whipped surf and faint mountains on the horizon.","significance":"Luke's Cauda, about 25 miles south of Crete. In its brief shelter the crew hauled in the ship's boat and passed ropes under the hull before the storm drove them out to sea.","symbolism":"The last land they saw for fourteen days.","impact":"Gavdos is the southernmost inhabited point of Europe.","img":"img/tempest0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Artemis Katsadoura, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gavdos_Island.jpg"}],"tags":["appearance","prophecy"],"orderNote":"The northeaster (Greek Eurakylon) is still a known winter wind in the eastern Mediterranean, the gregale. Cauda is the island of Gavdos, south of Crete."},
  {"id":"shipwreck","harmony":"Acts 27:27–44","lane":"paul","place":"malta","from":"adria","date":"November, AD 59","title":"The shipwreck on Malta","people":["Paul","The sailors","The soldiers","Julius the centurion","Luke","276 people on board"],"gospels":{"Acts":"27:27–44"},"summary":"On the fourteenth night the sailors sense land; soundings show twenty fathoms, then fifteen, and they drop four anchors from the stern and pray for day. Some sailors try to escape in the boat, but Paul warns the centurion and the soldiers cut it loose. At dawn Paul urges all to eat, takes bread, gives thanks and breaks it, and they are encouraged. They make for a beach in a bay, strike a shoal, and the stern breaks up in the surf. The soldiers plan to kill the prisoners, but the centurion, wishing to save Paul, stops them, and all 276 come safe to land, swimming or on planks.","journey":"The last stretch of the drift (see the storm above): the line on the map is a reconstruction ending at St Paul's Bay, the traditional site of the wreck.","route":[[19.0,35.45],[17.5,35.65],[16.0,35.85],[14.9,35.97],[14.5,35.98],[14.4,35.95]],"routeUncertain":true,"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 27:35","text":"And when he had said this, he took bread, and giving thanks to God in the presence of all he broke it and began to eat."},{"ref":"Acts 27:41","text":"But striking a shoal they ran the vessel aground; the bow stuck and remained immovable, and the stern was broken up by the surf."},{"ref":"Acts 27:44","text":"and the rest on planks or on pieces of the ship. And so it was that all escaped to land."}],"art":[{"kind":"Descriptive","title":"St Paul Shipwrecked on Malta","artist":"Francesco Bartolozzi, after Benjamin West","year":"1791","medium":"Etching and engraving","location":"Wellcome Collection, London","description":"A tall, crowded scene below a rocky cliff: survivors are carried and helped up from the shore, with the wreck in the surf at the lower left, while islanders bring baskets and firewood down from above. At the center Paul, haloed, shakes the viper from his hand into the fire.","significance":"A print after Benjamin West's great altarpiece, The Preservation of St Paul after Shipwreck at Malta (1789), painted for the chapel of the Royal Hospital for Seamen at Greenwich.","symbolism":"Painted for a chapel of old sailors, it shows a shipwreck in which every life was saved.","impact":"West, an American who became President of the Royal Academy in London, painted the huge altarpiece, which still hangs in the chapel of the Old Royal Naval College.","img":"img/shipwreck0.jpg","credit":"Wellcome Collection, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St._Paul_shipwrecked_on_Malta._Etching_by_F._Bartolozzi,_179_Wellcome_V0014670.jpg"},{"kind":"Artifact","title":"St Paul's Islands, Malta","artist":"Photo: Samuele","year":"2007","medium":"Photograph","location":"St Paul's Islands, St Paul's Bay, Malta","description":"The rocky islet of St Paul's Islands, with a ruined fort and a tall statue of St Paul (seen here under repair), and Gozo in the distance.","significance":"The islets lie at the mouth of St Paul's Bay. A shallow channel between them and the mainland is the traditional \"place where two seas met\" where the ship ran aground.","symbolism":"The statue faces the sea that brought Paul, and with him the faith, to Malta.","impact":"The statue was set up in 1845. Malta keeps the feast of St Paul's Shipwreck as a national holiday on February 10.","img":"img/shipwreck1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Malta_-_St._Paul_Islands_-_St._Paul_statue.jpg"}],"tags":[],"orderNote":"Most scholars accept Malta (Luke's \"Melite\") and St Paul's Bay, where the depths and the sandbar between the mainland and St Paul's Islands match Luke's account. Other proposals include St Thomas Bay and Mellieha Bay on Malta, Mljet in the Adriatic and Cephalonia in Greece; none has won wide acceptance."},
  {"id":"viper","harmony":"Acts 28:1–6","lane":"paul","place":"malta","date":"November, AD 59","title":"The viper on Malta","people":["Paul","The islanders","Luke"],"gospels":{"Acts":"28:1–6"},"summary":"The islanders show the castaways unusual kindness and light a fire, for it is raining and cold. As Paul puts a bundle of sticks on the fire, a viper driven out by the heat fastens on his hand. The islanders say he must be a murderer whom Justice will not let live. Paul shakes the creature off into the fire and suffers no harm, and when nothing happens to him they change their minds and say he is a god.","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 28:1–2","text":"After we had escaped, we then learned that the island was called Malta. And the natives showed us unusual kindness, for they kindled a fire and welcomed us all, because it had begun to rain and was cold."},{"ref":"Acts 28:3","text":"Paul had gathered a bundle of sticks and put them on the fire, when a viper came out because of the heat and fastened on his hand."},{"ref":"Acts 28:5–6","text":"He, however, shook off the creature into the fire and suffered no harm. They waited, expecting him to swell up or suddenly fall down dead; but when they had waited a long time and saw no misfortune come to him, they changed their minds and said that he was a god."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"St Paul on Malta","artist":"Adam Elsheimer","year":"c. 1600","medium":"Oil on copper","location":"National Gallery, London","description":"On a stormy night the castaways come up out of the raging sea, strip off their wet clothes and gather around the fires. At the left, beside the fire, Paul shakes the viper from his hand; a beacon burns on the headland and the moon breaks through the clouds.","significance":"A tiny painting by the German master who worked in Rome, famous for his night scenes lit by several sources at once: fire, beacon and moon.","symbolism":"The fire that warms the castaways also drives out the serpent, and Paul is unharmed, a sign of God's protection.","impact":"Elsheimer's small coppers deeply influenced Rubens and Rembrandt.","img":"img/viper0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adam_Elsheimer_-_St_Paul_at_Malta_-_WGA7504.jpg"},{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"Saint Paul Shipwrecked on Malta","artist":"Laurent de La Hyre","year":"1630","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"Birmingham Museum of Art, Alabama","description":"Paul, in blue and rose robes, bends over the fire with the viper hanging from his hand, while islanders lean in to watch. Behind them, survivors are helped ashore from the stormy sea.","significance":"An early work by one of the founders of French classical painting.","symbolism":"The islanders lean in, waiting for him to fall dead; in Luke's story they turn in a moment from \"murderer\" to \"a god.\"","impact":"La Hyre went on to become a founding member of the French Royal Academy of Painting (1648).","img":"img/viper1.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Laurent_de_La_Hyre's_Saint_Paul_Shipwrecked_on_Malta.jpg"}],"tags":["miracle"],"orderNote":"Malta has no venomous snakes today; some think vipers have died out as the woods were cleared, others that the snake was a harmless kind the islanders feared. Maltese tradition honors a grotto at Rabat (St Paul's Grotto) as the place where Paul stayed."},
  {"id":"publius","harmony":"Acts 28:7–10","lane":"paul","place":"malta","date":"Winter, AD 59–60","title":"Paul heals the father of Publius","people":["Paul","Publius, the chief man of the island","The father of Publius","The islanders","Luke"],"gospels":{"Acts":"28:7–10"},"summary":"Publius, the chief man of the island, takes them in and entertains them for three days. His father lies sick with fever and dysentery; Paul goes in, prays, lays his hands on him and heals him. Then the rest of the sick on the island come and are cured. When the travelers sail, the islanders load them with gifts and with everything they need.","stay":"Three days with Publius; three months on the island in all (Acts 28:7, 11).","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 28:8","text":"It happened that the father of Publius lay sick with fever and dysentery; and Paul visited him and prayed, and putting his hands on him healed him."},{"ref":"Acts 28:9–10","text":"And when this had taken place, the rest of the people on the island who had diseases also came and were cured. They presented many gifts to us; and when we sailed, they put on board whatever we needed."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"St Paul heals the father of Publius","artist":"Mathis Gerung","year":"c. 1530–32","medium":"Illumination on parchment","location":"Bavarian State Library, Munich (Ottheinrich Bible, Cgm 8010)","description":"A wide miniature: Paul, barefoot and haloed, reaches out to the old man lying sick in a curtained bed, while Publius and a companion, in 16th-century dress, look on from the left.","significance":"From the Ottheinrich Bible, the oldest surviving illustrated manuscript of the New Testament in German, finished for the Elector Ottheinrich.","symbolism":"Hospitality returned: the man who took in the castaways receives healing in his own house.","impact":"The manuscript was split up during the Thirty Years' War; its parts were reunited in Munich in 2008.","img":"img/publius0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ottheinrich_Folio263r_Act28B.jpg"},{"kind":"Artifact","title":"St Paul's Grotto, Rabat","artist":"Photo: Simon Burchell","year":"2025","medium":"Photograph","location":"Rabat, Malta","description":"A rock-cut cave beneath the church of St Paul in Rabat, with a marble statue of the apostle.","significance":"Maltese tradition holds that Paul lived and preached here during his three months on the island.","symbolism":"The cave is a place of welcome, remembering the islanders' \"unusual kindness.\"","impact":"Popes John Paul II (1990) and Benedict XVI (2010) prayed here on their visits to Malta.","img":"img/publius1.jpg","credit":"Photo: Simon Burchell, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St._Paul's_Grotto,_Rabat_02.jpg"}],"tags":["healing","miracle"],"orderNote":"\"Chief man\" (Greek protos) is attested as the title of Malta's Roman official in inscriptions. Maltese tradition makes Publius the island's first bishop and places his estate at San Pawl Milqi, near St Paul's Bay."},
  {"id":"syracuse","harmony":"Acts 28:11–12","lane":"paul","place":"syracuse","from":"malta","date":"Early spring, AD 60","title":"On to Syracuse","people":["Paul","Julius the centurion","Luke","Aristarchus"],"gospels":{"Acts":"28:11–12"},"summary":"After three months, when the sailing season reopens, they set sail in another Alexandrian ship that had wintered at the island. Its figurehead is the Twin Brothers, Castor and Pollux, the protectors of sailors. They put in at Syracuse in Sicily and stay three days.","journey":"About 95 miles by sea, north from Malta past Cape Passero, the southeast tip of Sicily, a day's sail with a fair wind.","route":[[14.4,35.95],[14.45,36.0],[14.7,36.25],[15.05,36.58],[15.25,36.68],[15.33,36.95],[15.29,37.07]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 28:11","text":"After three months we set sail in a ship which had wintered in the island, a ship of Alexandria, with the Twin Brothers as figurehead."},{"ref":"Acts 28:12","text":"Putting in at Syracuse, we stayed there for three days."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"The catacombs of San Giovanni, Syracuse","artist":"Photo: Bernard Gagnon","year":"2024","medium":"Photograph","location":"Syracuse, Sicily","description":"A long underground gallery cut in the rock, lined with burial niches and lit from openings above.","significance":"Early Christian catacombs of the 3rd–5th centuries, among the largest outside Rome. A tradition of Syracuse says Paul preached during his three days here, at the crypt of St Marcian above the catacombs.","symbolism":"The tombs of a church that grew from small beginnings in a harbor city.","impact":"Syracuse had a Christian community very early; its bishops were among the first in Sicily.","img":"img/syracuse0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Bernard Gagnon, CC BY 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Catacombs_of_San_Giovanni_Evangelista_(Syracuse)_01.jpg"}],"tags":[]},
  {"id":"puteoli","harmony":"Acts 28:13–14","lane":"paul","place":"puteoli","from":"syracuse","date":"Spring, AD 60","title":"Rhegium and Puteoli","people":["Paul","Julius the centurion","Luke","The brethren at Puteoli"],"gospels":{"Acts":"28:13–14"},"summary":"Tacking against the wind, they work up the coast to Rhegium at the toe of Italy and wait a day. Then a south wind springs up and carries them through the Strait of Messina and up the coast, and on the second day they reach Puteoli on the Bay of Naples, the great port of the grain fleet. There they find Christians and are invited to stay a week. From here Paul goes on to Rome by road.","stay":"Seven days with the brethren at Puteoli (Acts 28:14).","journey":"About 310 miles by sea: some 80 miles up the east coast of Sicily to Rhegium, then about 230 miles through the Strait of Messina and north along the coast of Italy to Puteoli in two days.","route":[[15.29,37.07],[15.33,37.25],[15.22,37.5],[15.35,37.85],[15.55,38.02],[15.63,38.11],[15.62,38.26],[15.55,38.45],[15.7,38.68],[15.85,39.2],[15.45,39.8],[14.8,40.15],[14.15,40.5],[14.1,40.65],[14.12,40.78],[14.12,40.82]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 28:13–14","text":"And from there we made a circuit and arrived at Rhegium; and after one day a south wind sprang up, and on the second day we came to Puteoli. There we found brethren, and were invited to stay with them for seven days. And so we came to Rome."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"The Roman market at Puteoli","artist":"Photo: Miguel Hermoso Cuesta","year":"2013","medium":"Photograph","location":"Pozzuoli, Italy","description":"Three tall marble columns and the round central shrine of the Roman market hall (macellum) of Puteoli, long called the Temple of Serapis, below the level of the modern town.","significance":"The market served the busy port where the Alexandrian grain ships unloaded and where Paul found Christians and stayed a week.","symbolism":"Rome's food came through this port, and so did Paul with his message.","impact":"Holes bored by sea creatures high on the columns show how the ground here has sunk and risen again; geologists still study it.","img":"img/puteoli0.jpg","credit":"Photo: Miguel Hermoso Cuesta, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Macellum_de_Pozzuoli_01.JPG"}],"tags":[]},
  {"id":"threetaverns","harmony":"Acts 28:15","lane":"paul","place":"appiiforum","from":"puteoli","date":"Spring, AD 60","title":"The brethren meet Paul on the Appian Way","people":["Paul","Julius the centurion","Luke","The brethren from Rome"],"gospels":{"Acts":"28:15","Rom":"1:11–13"},"summary":"News runs ahead of them, and Christians from Rome come out along the Appian Way to meet Paul, some as far as the Forum of Appius, others to Three Taverns. Three years earlier he had written to them that he longed to see them. On seeing them, Paul thanks God and takes courage.","journey":"About 100 miles on foot, five days or so: north from Puteoli to Capua, then on the Appian Way along the coast by Sinuessa, Minturnae, Formiae and Tarracina to the Forum of Appius at the edge of the Pontine marshes.","route":[[14.12,40.82],[14.2,40.92],[14.25,41.08],[13.95,41.15],[13.83,41.16],[13.77,41.24],[13.61,41.26],[13.43,41.36],[13.25,41.29],[13.1,41.43]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 28:15","text":"And the brethren there, when they heard of us, came as far as the Forum of Appius and Three Taverns to meet us. On seeing them Paul thanked God and took courage."}],"art":[{"kind":"Artifact","title":"The Appian Way","artist":"Photo: Nicolò Musmeci","year":"2005","medium":"Photograph","location":"Via Appia Antica, near Rome","description":"The stone-paved Appian Way runs straight between umbrella pines and cypresses past a ruined medieval tower, Torre Selci, in the countryside south of Rome.","significance":"Built from 312 BC, the \"queen of roads\" led from Rome to Capua and on to Brundisium. Paul walked its last stretch from Capua to the city, and the brethren came out along it to meet him.","symbolism":"The road of the empire becomes the road of the gospel.","impact":"Christians later buried their dead in catacombs along this road; the Quo Vadis tradition of Peter is set on it too.","img":"img/threetaverns0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Appia_antica_2-7-05_048.jpg"}],"tags":[],"orderNote":"The road beyond Tarracina crossed the marshes; travelers often took a canal boat by night to the Forum of Appius, as the poet Horace did (Satires 1.5)."},
  {"id":"romejews","harmony":"Acts 28:16–29","lane":"paul","place":"rome","from":"appiiforum","date":"Spring, AD 60","title":"Paul in Rome meets the Jewish leaders","people":["Paul","The soldier who guarded him","The leaders of the Jews in Rome"],"gospels":{"Acts":"28:16–29"},"summary":"In Rome Paul is allowed to live by himself, chained to a soldier who guards him. After three days he calls together the leaders of the Jews and tells them he is in chains \"because of the hope of Israel.\" On an appointed day many come to his lodging, and from morning till evening he tries to convince them about Jesus from the law and the prophets. Some are convinced and some disbelieve. Paul quotes Isaiah on hearts grown dull, and says that this salvation has been sent to the Gentiles: they will listen.","journey":"About 40 miles (43 Roman miles) up the Appian Way from the Forum of Appius, past Three Taverns and Aricia, two or three days on foot, entering Rome from the south.","route":[[13.1,41.43],[12.95,41.52],[12.85,41.6],[12.67,41.72],[12.58,41.8],[12.4964,41.9028]],"scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 28:16","text":"And when we came into Rome, Paul was allowed to stay by himself, with the soldier that guarded him."},{"ref":"Acts 28:23","text":"When they had appointed a day for him, they came to him at his lodging in great numbers. And he expounded the matter to them from morning till evening, testifying to the kingdom of God and trying to convince them about Jesus both from the law of Moses and from the prophets."},{"ref":"Acts 28:28","text":"Let it be known to you then that this salvation of God has been sent to the Gentiles; they will listen.\""}],"echoes":[{"ref":"Isaiah 6:9–10","note":"\"Hear and hear, but do not understand\": Paul's last words in Acts quote the call of Isaiah, as Jesus did in the parable of the sower."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"The Apostle Paul Preaching on the Ruins","artist":"Giovanni Paolo Pannini","year":"1744","medium":"Oil on canvas","location":"State Hermitage Museum, Saint Petersburg","description":"Among broken columns, a ruined arch and a temple portico, Paul, in a yellow robe, stands on the fallen stones and preaches to a mixed group of listeners: women and children, workmen and Roman soldiers.","significance":"Pannini was the great painter of Roman ruins and imaginary views (capriccios); he often set a Bible scene among the monuments.","symbolism":"The ruins of pagan Rome frame the preacher of the kingdom that would outlast it.","impact":"Pannini's views were prized by Grand Tour travelers and spread the image of Rome across Europe.","img":"img/romejews0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giovanni_Paolo_Pannini_-_Apostle_Paul_Preaching_on_the_Ruins_-_WGA16977.jpg"}],"tags":["teaching"]},
  {"id":"hiredhouse","harmony":"Acts 28:30–31","lane":"paul","place":"rome","date":"AD 60–62","title":"Two years under guard in Rome","people":["Paul","His visitors","The praetorian guard"],"gospels":{"Acts":"28:30–31","Phil":"1:12–14"},"summary":"Paul lives two whole years in Rome at his own expense, under guard, and welcomes all who come to him. He preaches the kingdom of God and teaches about the Lord Jesus Christ \"quite openly and unhindered.\" With this word Acts ends, the gospel having come from Jerusalem to the capital of the world. Paul's letters to the Philippians, Colossians, Ephesians and Philemon are traditionally dated to these years; in Philippians he writes that his imprisonment has made Christ known throughout the praetorian guard.","stay":"Two whole years in his own rented lodging (Acts 28:30).","scripture":[{"ref":"Acts 28:30–31","text":"And he lived there two whole years at his own expense, and welcomed all who came to him, preaching the kingdom of God and teaching about the Lord Jesus Christ quite openly and unhindered."}],"art":[{"kind":"Masterpiece","title":"The Apostle Paul in Prison","artist":"Rembrandt","year":"1627","medium":"Oil on panel","location":"Staatsgalerie, Stuttgart","description":"Paul sits on his straw bed in a bare cell, lit by a shaft of light from a barred window, pausing as he writes, with his sword and books beside him.","significance":"One of Rembrandt's earliest masterpieces, painted at about 21. It shows Paul as the writer of letters from captivity.","symbolism":"The sword is the sign of his martyrdom and of \"the sword of the Spirit, which is the word of God\" (Ephesians 6:17); the light falls on his thought, not on his chains.","impact":"Rembrandt returned to Paul again and again; this panel already shows his mastery of light and inner life.","img":"img/hiredhouse0.jpg","credit":"Public domain, via Wikimedia Commons","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rembrandt_St._Paul_in_Prison.jpg"}],"tags":["teaching"],"orderNote":"Acts does not say what came next. Early tradition (1 Clement 5, the Pastoral Letters, Eusebius) holds that Paul was released, travelled further, perhaps to Spain, and was later imprisoned again and beheaded under Nero about AD 64–67, at the place honored by the Basilica of St Paul Outside the Walls. Others think the trial ended in his death at the close of these two years."}
];
